              \"message\": {\r\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n                    \"content\": \"The running jump is a fundamental and exciting movement in parkour, often seen in various media. It emphasizes the combination of speed and strength, allowing practitioners to execute jumps with maximum power. The technique involves focusing on the takeoff point rather than being overly concerned with the landing, which is a common mistake among beginners. This approach not only enhances performance but also increases safety by ensuring a proper initiation of the jump.nnWhen practicing the running jump, it is crucial to prioritize where the feet land during takeoff. Beginners often look down towards their landing targets too early, which can lead to miscalculations in their jump. Instead, they should ensure their foot firmly contacts the ground at full speed and then shift their gaze towards the landing area, allowing for better spatial awareness and landing technique. It\'s also important to practice using both legs to facilitate balanced development and skill.nnA well-executed running jump guarantees a seamless transition from speed to height, enabling parkour enthusiasts to navigate obstacles with agility. The recommendation to practice on both legs ensures that individuals are well-rounded in their abilities. Ultimately, by concentrating on the takeoff first and addressing the landing afterward, practitioners can significantly improve the effectiveness and safety of their jumps.nn**Key Points:**n1. The running jump combines speed and strength, focusing primarily on the takeoff point to maximize power.n2. Beginners often make the mistake of looking at the landing too early, which can negatively affect their performance.n3. Practicing the running jump on both legs promotes balanced skill development and enhances overall jump execution.\",\r\n                    \"refusal\": null\r\n                },\r\n                \"logprobs\": null,\r\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\r\n            }\r\n        ],\r\n        \"usage\": {\r\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1524,\r\n            \"completion_tokens\": 310,\r\n            \"total_tokens\": 1834,\r\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\r\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"audio_tokens\": 0\r\n            },\r\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\r\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"audio_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"accepted_prediction_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0\r\n            }\r\n        },\r\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0aa8d3e20b\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"response\": {\r\n        \"code\": 200,\r\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"cookies\": [\r\n        {\r\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\r\n            \"value\": \"q3KlygyOE6vz3cT788QOogzYwUo7uAmyRV6nr9atfQs-1735745014-1.0.1.1-OpIzzGY8p8wpt_ktkP3eLd_h3VpAoD9mK1PN36cvn03a.wPx34twl_PnzrQ6PVjrTq3xyvaowtz8VyM9WQC6GA\",\r\n            \"expires\": 1735746814,\r\n            \"path\": \"/\",\r\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\r\n            \"port\": null,\r\n            \"host_only\": false\r\n        },\r\n        {\r\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\r\n            \"value\": \"hvOCzmRbs4vjab9TzFL8j39i4RLWfaMYrWyqLk.tW08-1735745014026-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\r\n            \"expires\": null,\r\n            \"path\": \"/\",\r\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\r\n            \"port\": null,\r\n            \"host_only\": false\r\n        }\r\n    ],\r\n    \"filename\": null,\r\n    \"http_response\": {\r\n        \"data\": null,\r\n        \"headers\": null,\r\n        \"status\": null\r\n    }\r\n}'),(24142,55195,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(24143,55195,'video_transcript_chatgpt','The running jump is a fundamental and exciting movement in parkour, often seen in various media. It emphasizes the combination of speed and strength, allowing practitioners to execute jumps with maximum power. The technique involves focusing on the takeoff point rather than being overly concerned with the landing, which is a common mistake among beginners. This approach not only enhances performance but also increases safety by ensuring a proper initiation of the jump.\r\n\r\nWhen practicing the running jump, it is crucial to prioritize where the feet land during takeoff. Beginners often look down towards their landing targets too early, which can lead to miscalculations in their jump. Instead, they should ensure their foot firmly contacts the ground at full speed and then shift their gaze towards the landing area, allowing for better spatial awareness and landing technique. It\'s also important to practice using both legs to facilitate balanced development and skill.\r\n\r\nA well-executed running jump guarantees a seamless transition from speed to height, enabling parkour enthusiasts to navigate obstacles with agility. The recommendation to practice on both legs ensures that individuals are well-rounded in their abilities. Ultimately, by concentrating on the takeoff first and addressing the landing afterward, practitioners can significantly improve the effectiveness and safety of their jumps.\r\n\r\n**Key Points:**\r\n1. The running jump combines speed and strength, focusing primarily on the takeoff point to maximize power.\r\n2. Beginners often make the mistake of looking at the landing too early, which can negatively affect their performance.\r\n3. Practicing the running jump on both legs promotes balanced skill development and enhances overall jump execution.'),(24144,55195,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(24145,55195,'svg_id',''),(24146,55195,'_svg_id','field_67160c975525b'),(24147,55195,'svg_colour',''),(24148,55195,'_svg_colour','field_67161465e3b58'),(24149,55195,'extra_variables',''),(24150,55195,'_extra_variables','field_671615de0e542'),(24151,55195,'notes',''),(24152,55195,'