  \"body\": {\r\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-Akum5WsCNEP0vlEGHXKo2rJ9DwUWB\",\r\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\r\n        \"created\": 1735744693,\r\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\r\n        \"choices\": [\r\n            {\r\n                \"index\": 0,\r\n                \"message\": {\r\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n                    \"content\": \"The crane position is a unique landing technique that involves resting one foot on a target while the other leg dangles down. This method can sometimes carry a stigma because some may perceive it as less optimal than landing with both feet simultaneously. However, there are practical reasons for using the crane position, such as when the landing area is small or at a specific angle that doesn\'t allow for a two-foot landing.nnPracticing the crane can help improve landing techniques and body control. It\'s recommended to start from lower heights and incrementally increase the difficulty level by choosing walls that are either level or slightly elevated. Landing one foot at a time can reduce the impact on joints, especially when transitioning from elevated surfaces, making it easier to develop this skill without causing injury.nnTo increase proficiency, practicing the crane from both sides is essential. This helps in developing balanced strength and control, ensuring that the body adapts equally to both sides of the movement. As one grows more comfortable with the crane technique, they can challenge themselves with greater heights or different angles to improve their overall mobility and landing skills. nnKey points:n1. The crane position is a practical landing technique that may be used when a two-foot landing is not feasible.n2. Starting with lower heights and practicing on a suitable angle can help ease into the crane technique and minimize injury risk.n3. Practicing from both sides aids in achieving balance and control, allowing for progression to more challenging landing scenarios.\",\r\n                    \"refusal\": null\r\n                },\r\n                \"logprobs\": null,\r\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\r\n            }\r\n        ],\r\n        \"usage\": {\r\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 2462,\r\n            \"completion_tokens\": 289,\r\n            \"total_tokens\": 2751,\r\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\r\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"audio_tokens\": 0\r\n            },\r\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\r\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"audio_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"accepted_prediction_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0\r\n            }\r\n        },\r\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0aa8d3e20b\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"response\": {\r\n        \"code\": 200,\r\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"cookies\": [\r\n        {\r\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\r\n            \"value\": \"9TIu.GGjgb.mlYTaYOgkoQ8RRepDZprchPz2_1vxkDU-1735744697-1.0.1.1-dTQmto7u.w9QYCe6i404CdXN6xI93hRvpExury.af_C8Te2lyDKg8pziecIDr6LLyy1ZJbK39grTlDH0.fXURQ\",\r\n            \"expires\": 1735746497,\r\n            \"path\": \"/\",\r\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\r\n            \"port\": null,\r\n            \"host_only\": false\r\n        },\r\n        {\r\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\r\n            \"value\": \"j698dV1HRE8pvpiDETjDxxxDQ_iZ28P2d4R44vDBwTQ-1735744697912-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\r\n            \"expires\": null,\r\n            \"path\": \"/\",\r\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\r\n            \"port\": null,\r\n            \"host_only\": false\r\n        }\r\n    ],\r\n    \"filename\": null,\r\n    \"http_response\": {\r\n        \"data\": null,\r\n        \"headers\": null,\r\n        \"status\": null\r\n    }\r\n}'),(28656,55242,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(28657,55242,'video_transcript_chatgpt','The crane position is a unique landing technique that involves resting one foot on a target while the other leg dangles down. This method can sometimes carry a stigma because some may perceive it as less optimal than landing with both feet simultaneously. However, there are practical reasons for using the crane position, such as when the landing area is small or at a specific angle that doesn\'t allow for a two-foot landing.\r\n\r\nPracticing the crane can help improve landing techniques and body control. It\'s recommended to start from lower heights and incrementally increase the difficulty level by choosing walls that are either level or slightly elevated. Landing one foot at a time can reduce the impact on joints, especially when transitioning from elevated surfaces, making it easier to develop this skill without causing injury.\r\n\r\nTo increase proficiency, practicing the crane from both sides is essential. This helps in developing balanced strength and control, ensuring that the body adapts equally to both sides of the movement. As one grows more comfortable with the crane technique, they can challenge themselves with greater heights or different angles to improve their overall mobility and landing skills. \r\n\r\nKey points:\r\n1. The crane position is a practical landing technique that may be used when a two-foot landing is not feasible.\r\n2. Starting with lower heights and practicing on a suitable angle can help ease into the crane technique and minimize injury risk.\r\n3. Practicing from both sides aids in achieving balance and control, allowing for progression to more challenging landing scenarios.'),(28658,55242,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(28659,55242,'svg_id',''),(28660,55242,'_svg_id','field_67160c975525b'),(28661,55242,'svg_colour',''),(28662,55242,'_svg_colour','field_67161465e3b58'),(28663,55242,'extra_variables',''),(28664,55242,'_extra_variables','field_671615de0e542'),(28665,55242,'notes',''),(28666,55242,'_notes','field_6749f5664