  \"id\": \"chatcmpl-Ab9ujaDkVqgABnIFD9k1QkHztcchH\",\r\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\r\n        \"created\": 1733419609,\r\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\r\n        \"choices\": [\r\n            {\r\n                \"index\": 0,\r\n                \"message\": {\r\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n                    \"content\": \"The discussion focuses on a technique known as the \"double tap,\" which is particularly useful for movements involving a hand descent or an upward pull on bars or scaffolding. This maneuver typically involves transitioning from a lower to a higher bar and is demonstrated through various methods, including the single hand descent. Learning this technique requires practicing solid engagement of the upper body muscles, especially when performing the ascent and descent with one hand while stabilizing with the other.nnTo effectively execute the single hand descent, one must maintain both arms in a pulling position at the top of the movement before switching grips. This process begins with both feet off the ground, emphasizing the need for core stability and strength in the arms. It’s crucial to engage the muscles fully while transferring weight between arms, ensuring that the pull from one side is supported by the other, rather than relying solely on one arm during the transition.nnAs aspiring practitioners progress, they can practice smaller and larger gaps to build their confidence and strength. Spaces like basketball hoops or scaffolding often provide the necessary structure for training this explosive movement. Ultimately, mastering the double tap not only enhances the ability to move between bars but also contributes to developing overall upper body strength and explosive power, essential for various climbing techniques and exercises, such as muscle-ups.nnKey Points:n1. The double tap technique involves transitioning from a lower bar to a higher one, requiring strong upper body engagement and stability.n2. Practicing the single hand descent involves using both arms effectively while ensuring the body remains suspended off the ground to maintain control.n3. This training approach can be applied in various environments, enhancing overall power and strength beneficial for climbing and other upper body movements.\",\r\n                    \"refusal\": null\r\n                },\r\n                \"logprobs\": null,\r\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\r\n            }\r\n        ],\r\n        \"usage\": {\r\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 2685,\r\n            \"completion_tokens\": 334,\r\n            \"total_tokens\": 3019,\r\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\r\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"audio_tokens\": 0\r\n            },\r\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\r\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"audio_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"accepted_prediction_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0\r\n            }\r\n        },\r\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0705bf87c0\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"response\": {\r\n        \"code\": 200,\r\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"cookies\": [\r\n        {\r\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\r\n            \"value\": \".XnGvdlM_.XRrbNZpJ1r22TyXUx_46zluWezHiEy98M-1733419616-1.0.1.1-3Dj2xr0RE2vn83L9npoMkLpmXlp_.xJdHfLzJWqVpqNp27fyzndBcQIKRtKboerY6euUIKiHXWlTkGSjSVUtEA\",\r\n            \"expires\": 1733421416,\r\n            \"path\": \"/\",\r\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\r\n            \"port\": null,\r\n            \"host_only\": false\r\n        },\r\n        {\r\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\r\n            \"value\": \"vkoMGnBZzH1xpeWx9SGmN2y4e2hAOUtsqUTPVWPACiM-1733419616037-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\r\n            \"expires\": null,\r\n            \"path\": \"/\",\r\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\r\n            \"port\": null,\r\n            \"host_only\": false\r\n        }\r\n    ],\r\n    \"filename\": null,\r\n    \"http_response\": {\r\n        \"data\": null,\r\n        \"headers\": null,\r\n        \"status\": null\r\n    }\r\n}'),(31778,55269,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(31779,55269,'video_transcript_chatgpt','The discussion focuses on a technique known as the \"double tap,\" which is particularly useful for movements involving a hand descent or an upward pull on bars or scaffolding. This maneuver typically involves transitioning from a lower to a higher bar and is demonstrated through various methods, including the single hand descent. Learning this technique requires practicing solid engagement of the upper body muscles, especially when performing the ascent and descent with one hand while stabilizing with the other.\r\n\r\nTo effectively execute the single hand descent, one must maintain both arms in a pulling position at the top of the movement before switching grips. This process begins with both feet off the ground, emphasizing the need for core stability and strength in the arms. It’s crucial to engage the muscles fully while transferring weight between arms, ensuring that the pull from one side is supported by the other, rather than relying solely on one arm during the transition.\r\n\r\nAs aspiring practitioners progress, they can practice smaller and larger gaps to build their confidence and strength. Spaces like basketball hoops or scaffolding often provide the necessary structure for training this explosive movement. Ultimately, mastering the double tap not only enhances the ability to move between bars but also contributes to developing overall upper body strength and explosive power, essential for various climbing techniques and exercises, such as muscle-ups.\r\n\r\nKey Points:\r\n1. The double tap technique involves transitioning from a lower bar to a higher one, requiring strong upper body engagement and stability.\r\n2. Practicing the single hand descent involves using both arms effectively while ensuring the body remains suspended off the ground to maintain control.\r\n3. This training approach can be applied in various environments, enhancing overall power and strength beneficial for climbing and other upper body movements.'),(31780,55269,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(31781,55269,'svg_id',''),(31782,55269,'_svg_id','field_67160c975525b'),(31783,55269,'svg_colour',''),(31784,55269,'_svg_colour','field_67161465e3b58'),(31785,55269,'extra_variables',''),(31786,55269,'_extra_variables','field_671615de0e542'),(31787,55269,'notes',''),(31788,55269,'_notes','field_6749f56647b9e'),(31789,55269,'enable_youtube_api',''),(31790,55269,'_enable_youtube_api','field_6721ddb5e8fdf'),(31791,55269,'enable_youtube_filter',''),(31792,55269,'_enable_youtube_filter','field_67225378495f0'),(31793,55269,'wordpress_filter_name','tutorial_filter'),(31794,55269,'_wordpress_filter_name','field_6722538b495f1'),(31795,55269,'enable_youtube_chatgpt',''),(31796,55269,'_enable_youtube_chatgpt','field_6721f22d49ffe'),(31797,55269,'youtube_chatgpt_prompt','Summarise the following description into three paragraphs :'),(31798,55269,'_youtube_chatgpt_prompt','field_67222f1d7722b'),(31799,55269,'enable_use_as_excerpt',''),(31800,55269,'_enable_use_as_excerpt','field_67225a0b50411'),(31801,55269,'enable_transcript',''),(31802,55269,'_enable_transcript','field_6722088670e84'),(31803,55269,'enable_transcript_chatgpt',''),(31804,55269,'_enable_transcript_chatgpt','field_67223080dbaa5'),(31805,55269,'transcript_chatgpt_prompt','primarily focus on the single hand descent. Summarise the following transcript into three paragraphs and summarise with three key points. Never mention the speaker or the transcript. I want this to sound like I wrote the result.'),(31806,55269,'_transcript_chatgpt_prompt','field_672214208bf31'),(31807,55269,'rank_math_seo_score','21'),(31808,55269,'video_filename',''),(31809,55269,'_video_filename','field_677cead91f698