id\": \"chatcmpl-Ab9vovw2fv7W76XnqOAMgc8aTB1Ss\",\r\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\r\n        \"created\": 1733419676,\r\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\r\n        \"choices\": [\r\n            {\r\n                \"index\": 0,\r\n                \"message\": {\r\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\r\n                    \"content\": \"The double-handed catch, often referred to as the \"double tap,\" is a unique movement primarily used in climbing and upper body exercises. It involves transitioning from a lower bar to a higher one and is usually performed on structures like scaffolding or frames. In this exercise, one must understand that the motion requires substantial strength and coordination, involving both arms working together in an explosive manner to effectively execute the catch.nnTo practice the double tap, it is essential to first build strength with one-arm movements before transitioning to using both hands. This includes training the ability to pull oneself up while also effectively contracting the core and legs for propulsion. The goal is to master fluid movements, which may initially incorporate smaller gaps before progressing to wider distances between bars. Finding the right structures, such as playground equipment or scaffolding, is crucial for effective practice.nnOverall, the double tap not only enhances upper body explosive power but also translates into improved skills for climbing techniques and muscle-ups. It serves as a valuable training method for athletes looking to develop strength in climbing and other upper body activities. Practicing this movement supports greater proficiency in dynamic upper body exercises, positioning athletes for success in various physical challenges.nn**Key Points:**n1. The double tap is an explosive movement crucial for transitioning between bars in climbing exercises.n2. Building strength through one-arm exercises is a foundational step before progressing to the double tap with both hands.n3. Effective practice environments, such as playgrounds and scaffolding, are essential for mastering this dynamic movement.\",\r\n                    \"refusal\": null\r\n                },\r\n                \"logprobs\": null,\r\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\r\n            }\r\n        ],\r\n        \"usage\": {\r\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 2684,\r\n            \"completion_tokens\": 302,\r\n            \"total_tokens\": 2986,\r\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\r\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"audio_tokens\": 0\r\n            },\r\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\r\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"audio_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"accepted_prediction_tokens\": 0,\r\n                \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0\r\n            }\r\n        },\r\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0705bf87c0\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"response\": {\r\n        \"code\": 200,\r\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"cookies\": [\r\n        {\r\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\r\n            \"value\": \"FG2ry8eYLNXyE2zDFU43E0hM2CRSlICJtUBouZDYHG4-1733419681-1.0.1.1-EANEyprG2TzByz2fHGAA..e0GC9Pot9uyl0fC.itbJzhY3NWIkmYzjPzpogUFElZ86zuRmaS3C8nVOdxOPlL8g\",\r\n            \"expires\": 1733421481,\r\n            \"path\": \"/\",\r\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\r\n            \"port\": null,\r\n            \"host_only\": false\r\n        },\r\n        {\r\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\r\n            \"value\": \"fHdZG6wQDTexNYtPWnDz3XhdTDDx_0tz.Un7fESBFZQ-1733419681044-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\r\n            \"expires\": null,\r\n            \"path\": \"/\",\r\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\r\n            \"port\": null,\r\n            \"host_only\": false\r\n        }\r\n    ],\r\n    \"filename\": null,\r\n    \"http_response\": {\r\n        \"data\": null,\r\n        \"headers\": null,\r\n        \"status\": null\r\n    }\r\n}'),(31919,55270,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(31920,55270,'video_transcript_chatgpt','The double-handed catch, often referred to as the \"double tap,\" is a unique movement primarily used in climbing and upper body exercises. It involves transitioning from a lower bar to a higher one and is usually performed on structures like scaffolding or frames. In this exercise, one must understand that the motion requires substantial strength and coordination, involving both arms working together in an explosive manner to effectively execute the catch.\r\n\r\nTo practice the double tap, it is essential to first build strength with one-arm movements before transitioning to using both hands. This includes training the ability to pull oneself up while also effectively contracting the core and legs for propulsion. The goal is to master fluid movements, which may initially incorporate smaller gaps before progressing to wider distances between bars. Finding the right structures, such as playground equipment or scaffolding, is crucial for effective practice.\r\n\r\nOverall, the double tap not only enhances upper body explosive power but also translates into improved skills for climbing techniques and muscle-ups. It serves as a valuable training method for athletes looking to develop strength in climbing and other upper body activities. Practicing this movement supports greater proficiency in dynamic upper body exercises, positioning athletes for success in various physical challenges.\r\n\r\n**Key Points:**\r\n1. The double tap is an explosive movement crucial for transitioning between bars in climbing exercises.\r\n2. Building strength through one-arm exercises is a foundational step before progressing to the double tap with both hands.\r\n3. Effective practice environments, such as playgrounds and scaffolding, are essential for mastering this dynamic movement.'),(31921,55270,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(31922,55270,'svg_id',''),(3