riables_2_svg','field_619b573a0adcd_field_619b5546d4ea4'),(32827,55279,'variables_3_orientation','same'),(32828,55279,'_variables_3_orientation','field_61a2509ea7d6e_field_61a24f7b3f0a1'),(32829,55279,'variables_3_description','Finish facing the same direction as your starting direction.'),(32830,55279,'_variables_3_description','field_61a2509ea7d6e_field_61a24f7b3f39a'),(32831,55279,'variables_3_svg','<svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M12,2L16,6H13V13.85L19.53,17.61L21,15.03L22.5,20.5L17,21.96L18.53,19.35L12,15.58L5.47,19.35L7,21.96L1.5,20.5L3,15.03L4.47,17.61L11,13.85V6H8L12,2Z\"/></svg>'),(32832,55279,'_variables_3_svg','field_61a2509ea7d6e_field_61a24f7b3f5d6'),(32833,55279,'variables','a:4:{i:0;s:6:\"height\";i:1;s:5:\"width\";i:2;s:7:\"landing\";i:3;s:11:\"orientation\";}'),(32834,55279,'_variables','field_619509f88af8c'),(32835,55279,'json_import_data',' '),(32836,55279,'_json_import_data','field_602e58a3bbfcd'),(32837,55279,'json_import_svg',' '),(32838,55279,'_json_import_svg','field_60608139eb262'),(32839,55279,'json_export_data','null'),(32840,55279,'_json_export_data','field_602e273c8cee6'),(32841,55279,'json_export_svg','null'),(32842,55279,'_json_export_svg','field_6060811aeb261'),(32843,55279,'specific_location','Canada Water MuscleUp Scaffolding'),(32844,55279,'_specific_location','field_61b85c2e2b6a2'),(32845,55279,'_thumbnail_id','3434'),(32846,55279,'filmed','zinc-200'),(32847,55279,'_filmed','field_61d6b047b127d'),(32848,55279,'media','2'),(32849,55279,'_media','field_61b879f94dc1b'),(32850,55279,'_mepr_unauthorized_message_type','default'),(32851,55279,'_mepr_unauthorized_message',''),(32852,55279,'_mepr_unauth_login','default'),(32853,55279,'_mepr_unauth_excerpt_type','default'),(32854,55279,'_mepr_unauth_excerpt_size','100'),(32855,55279,'total_watch_seconds','1078'),(32856,55279,'_total_watch_seconds','field_6256bfd87b1e7'),(32857,55279,'media_0_videoId','_2g9XgPf5Bg'),(32858,55279,'_media_0_videoId','field_619a7fe0a22a1'),(32859,55279,'media_0_video_details','{\"kind\":\"youtube#videoListResponse\",\"etag\":\"qQfBCX-Bs0ysWFrdtNi1XjaXt_U\",\"items\":[{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"etag\":\"dcNBaV_2b-SiNuorP7WIgSxyv4E\",\"id\":\"_2g9XgPf5Bg\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2021-09-27T23:00:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"Step Vault Basics - Parkour Tutorials - 001\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on Step Vaults for Beginnersnud83eudd77 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 001nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnn#Vaulting #Step-Vault #Parkournn##u00a0Step Vault 1 - Basicsn---n### IntronnThis is called the **Step Vault** or also known as the Safety VaultnThe step vault\'s primary purpose is to allow maximum confidence overcoming an obstacle, nMoving both safely and securely.nn### The TL;DRnnIt is usually the vault that is taught first to beginners because of it\'s simplicity. nHowever this vault is hugely versatile and can have a huge range of variations.nn### The goalnnThe key is control and security, so the more confidence you feel with this vault, the better because it will always be your fall-back vault. nAdvanced practitioners will also use this vault, especially when they\'re feeling uncomfortable or want maximum reliability. So nailing this down to perfection is in your best interest.nnn### PrerequisitesnnYou do not need any previous experience and very little movement ability to complete this vault at its basic level.nHowever, having the mobility to be able to get a foot and hand on the obstacle will be nessesary.nn### Getting startednn1. General Joint Mobility should be encouraged. It\'ll loosen up the joints and help with being able to reach better ranges of motion under controln2. Thorough warmups should always be done. Unless you are a top-level athlete who monitors their performance, you should start slow but gradually move into going hard on the warmups.n3. Shoulders and arms with QM / Diving Monkeys / Pushupsn4. Hip mobility with squats / lunges / burpees / jumps.nnnnnn## Basicsnn### The setupnnYou need to find a wall roughly thigh to hip height. This is the general height for starting vaults.nnWe are going to do everything with as much control as possible, which means nice and slow.nnn### Both Hands & FootnnStart with both hands and a single foot to create space under you.nYou want them a good distance apart to create a large space.nn### Hand positionnnStart with placing both hands on the wall first. Hands facing forward, have a good grip, flat, with the whole palm on the wall.nThe pressure should be in the middle of the hand to ball of thumb.nn### FootnnPlace one of your feet onto the wall, with the ball of the foot. Facing forwards. nn### Foot trajectorynnTry to place the foot up and over the wall, not straight at it. This is to prevent clipping the foot on the corner. As your accuracy improves, this can be refined, but for the beginning, over exaggerate the movement.nn### HipsnnPush your hips up high behind you. nAgain, you are trying to create space, so less mobility means higher hips.nn### Inside legnnThis is the Tricky part - Remove the inside arm and push the back leg through the gap between the stabilising hand and foot. nn### LandingnnPlace the ball of that foot down on the ground, controlled and slow.nn### DirectionnnMove forward in a straight line, removing the hand/foot on the wall behind you.nn### Cue 1 - straightnnStraight line - For now, remember that you are travelling in a straight line, so pick a point in front of you and head towards it.nn### Cue 2 - spacennThe amount of space under you is key. Either you have good mobility, or you create more space to get the trailing leg through.nn### Cue 3 - weight distributionnnAt the beginning, try to move your weight so that it\'s more towards your arms.nn---n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_2g9XgPf5Bg/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_2g9XgPf5Bg/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_2g9XgPf5Bg/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_2g9XgPf5Bg/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_2g9XgPf5Bg/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"tags\":[\"Coaching Parkour\",\"How to Learn Parkour\",\"Improve Parkour\",\"Learn to Coach Parkour\",\"Learn to Parkour\",\"Learning Parkour\",\"London Parkour\",\"Movement Tutorials\",\"Parkour Demonstration\",\"Parkour Education\",\"Parkour for Advanced\",\"Parkour for Beginners\",\"Parkour for Coaches\",\"Parkour School\",\"Parkour Tips\",\"Parkour Tutorials\",\"Parkour\",\"Practical Movement\",\"Teaching Parkour\",\"Training Parkour\"],\"categoryId\":\"17\",\"liveBroadcastContent\":\"none\",\"defaultLanguage\":\"en-GB\",\"localized\":{\"title\":\"Step Vault Basics - Parkour Tutorials - 001\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on Step Vaults for Beginnersnud83eudd77 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 001nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnn#Vaulting #Step-Vault #Parkournn##u00a0Step Vault 1 - Basicsn---n### IntronnThis is called the **Step Vault** or also known as the Safety VaultnThe step vault\'s primary purpose is to allow maximum confidence overcoming an obstacle, nMoving both safely and securely.nn### The TL;DRnnIt is usually the vault that is taught first to beginners because of it\'s simplicity. nHowever this vault is hugely versatile and can have a huge range of variations.nn### The goalnnThe key is control and security, so the more confidence you feel with this vault, the better because it will always be your fall-back vault. nAdvanced practitioners will also use this vault, especially when they\'re feeling uncomfortable or want maximum reliability. So nailing this down to perfection is in your best interest.nnn### PrerequisitesnnYou do not need any previous experience and very little movement ability to complete this vault at its basic level.nHowever, having the mobility to be able to get a foot and hand on the obstacle will be nessesary.nn### Getting startednn1. General Joint Mobility should be encouraged. It\'ll loosen up the joints and help with being able to reach better ranges of motion under controln2. Thorough warmups should always be done. Unless you are a top-level athlete who monitors their performance, you should start slow but gradually move into going hard on the warmups.n3. Shoulders and arms with QM / Diving Monkeys / Pushupsn4. Hip mobility with squats / lunges / burpees / jumps.nnnnnn## Basicsnn### The setupnnYou need to find a wall roughly thigh to hip height. This is the general height for starting vaults.nnWe are going to do everything with as much control as possible, which means nice and slow.nnn### Both Hands & FootnnStart with both hands and a single foot to create space under you.nYou want them a good distance apart to create a large space.nn### Hand positionnnStart with placing both hands on the wall first. Hands facing forward, have a good grip, flat, with the whole palm on the wall.nThe pressure should be in the middle of the hand to ball of thumb.nn### FootnnPlace one of your feet onto the wall, with the ball of the foot. Facing forwards. nn### Foot trajectorynnTry to place the foot up and over the wall, not straight at it. This is to prevent clipping the foot on the corner. As your accuracy improves, this can be refined, but for the beginning, over exaggerate the movement.nn### HipsnnPush your hips up high behind you. nAgain, you are trying to create space, so less mobility means higher hips.nn### Inside legnnThis is the Tricky part - Remove the inside arm and push the back leg through the gap between the stabilising hand and foot. nn### LandingnnPlace the ball of that foot down on the ground, controlled and slow.nn### DirectionnnMove forward in a straight line, removing the hand/foot on the wall behind you.nn### Cue 1 - straightnnStraight line - For now, remember that you are travelling in a straight line, so pick a point in front of you and head towards it.nn### Cue 2 - spacennThe amount of space under you is key. Either you have good mobility, or you create more space to get the trailing leg through.nn### Cue 3 - weight distributionnnAt the beginning, try to move your weight so that it\'s more towards your arms.nn---n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\"},\"defaultAudioLanguage\":\"en-GB\"},\"contentDetails\":{\"duration\":\"PT6M44S\",\"dimension\":\"2d\",\"definition\":\"hd\",\"caption\":\"false\",\"licensedContent\":false,\"contentRating\":{},\"projection\":\"rectangular\"}}],\"pageInfo\":{\"totalResults\":1,\"resultsPerPage\":1}}'),(32860,55279,'_media_0_video_details','field_620b62afe64c7'),(32861,55279,'media_1_videoId','8LR6aA2JzsY'),(32862,55279,'_media_1_videoId','field_619a7fe0a22a1'),(32863,55279,'media_1_video_details','{\"kind\":\"youtube#videoListResponse\",\"etag\":\"P11YaASJ9cOvevV0IXQ9im1fpds\",\"items\":[{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"etag\":\"P4s9hWKkZLv0P6BVMiunMhsPPu4\",\"id\":\"8LR6aA2JzsY\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2021-10-05T10:30:01Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"Step Vault Progressions - Parkour Tutorials - 002\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on Step Vault Progressions for Beginnersnud83eudd77 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 002nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnn#Vaults #StepVault #Parkournn## Variationsn---nOrdered by difficulty, in this video we are going to look at over a dozen variations on the step vault. nn### Beginner 1 - Both handsnnThis is the one done in the basics video. Use two hands and one foot to get yourself over an obstacle.nn### Beginner 2 - One handnnThis is the standard step vault that everyone will recognise - the one hand, one foot version.nSame as the two foot one, but without using the inside hand on the wall at all. nYou may need a little hop to get the foot up on the wall now.nn### Beginner 3 - floornnThis is safer, but harder. Do the one foot, one handed version on the floor. This will help with confidence, mobility and balance.nn### Beginner 4 - bad sidennThe one handed version of the vault but performed with the less other side.nThis is a variation that you should always do to maximise versatility - sometimes the environment will dictate that you can only do a move on one side - so best get comfortable using either.nn### Beginner 5 - momentumnnStart with one step back and move into the step vault with a constant speed (not fast). Once happy, switch to the other side.nNext take two steps and repeat. Keep increasing steps as you feel comfortable with the amount of momentum generated.nHowever - be aware of wasting the momentum by pausing or slowing before the wall.nn### Intermediate 1 - Different surfacesnnStart investigating the use of different surfaces. I\'m talking about shapes and materials. From concrete brick to metal railings and everything else in between.nYou want to start being able to distinguish how different materials are going to behave and react. Wood, metal, types of brick; all have to be treated slightly differently.nAs an addition to this one, you can use the weather to your advantage and use it as another variable to change the surfaces. The same obstacle in the wet, cold, hot, dry, icy, all make things different.nFor instance, we know that wet wood will no-doubt be slippery. Crumbly dry brick with dust may also need care...nn### Intermediate 2 - ChainingnnStart to practice chaining multiple step vaults together with two or three steps in between. nLearning how to set yourself up with the correct hand and foot placement from one to another will help create flow.nThis will also get you to start understanding the importance of being  accurate with those positions.nn### Intermediate 3 - ConsistencynnThis one can be misunderstood for going fast. What we are looking for is a consistent movement speed, which can be slow, but has less fluctuations of that speed.nFor instance, the entry and exit of the vault are usually easier and quicker than the middle part of getting over the wall. Try to remain active and contracted the whole way, without any relaxing of the muscles at any point.nFilm yourself and watch it to see when any of the \"sticking\" points are. nn### Intermediate 4 - Slow and ControllednnPerform the step vault as slow as you can and then hold the position where the back leg has passed through, but not touched the ground yet.nExtend the leg straight, hold your balance for 10seconds and reverse the movement to the beginning again.nn### Advanced 1 - SpeednnThe \"Speed Step\" is a slightly different variation of the step vault and deserves its own video. However, performing the normal step vault at speed is a good skill to acquire.nSlowly practice increasing your speed from walking pace to jogging into / out of it, to having the confidence of going at sprinting speed.nn### Advanced 2 - FlownnGoing fast, over multiple obstacles, of different types, without planning your foot placement and keep a consistent speed is good practice for when the terrain and environment is unknown.nBeing able to adapt to what is coming up without preparation will make you more capable and practical.nn### Advanced 3 - HeightnnThis is obviously dependent of your own height, but try to use you arms to pull you into a much higher step vault. nHelpful on higher railings and the beginnings of climb-ups.nn### Advanced 4 - IsometricnnThe same as the intermediate variation of the slow and controlled, however, this is at height and on a railing. A goal post, top of a swing set, scaffolding or anything else. nThis will really tax your muscular control, adrenaline and fear. A great way of fine-tuning those positions and body awareness.nn### Advanced 5 - Embrace the weirdnnThe last variation is to go out and challenge yourself to find any weird and strange obstacles that you don\'t know whether you can step vault or not.nCan you step vault through small gaps? At slanted angles? With backpacks on? Barefoot? In the dark? Up high? Underground? On a chain? A moving gate? The list is endless... Try challenge yourself with everything.n---n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"tags\":[\"Coaching Parkour\",\"How to Learn Parkour\",\"Improve Parkour\",\"Learn to Coach Parkour\",\"Learn to Parkour\",\"Learning Parkour\",\"London Parkour\",\"Movement Tutorials\",\"Parkour Demonstration\",\"Parkour Education\",\"Parkour for Advanced\",\"Parkour for Beginners\",\"Parkour for Coaches\",\"Parkour School\",\"Parkour Tips\",\"Parkour Tutorials\",\"Parkour\",\"Practical Movement\",\"Teaching Parkour\",\"Training Parkour\"],\"categoryId\":\"17\",\"liveBroadcastContent\":\"none\",\"defaultLanguage\":\"en-GB\",\"localized\":{\"title\":\"Step Vault Progressions - Parkour Tutorials - 002\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on Step Vault Progressions for Beginnersnud83eudd77 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 002nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnn#Vaults #StepVault #Parkournn## Variationsn---nOrdered by difficulty, in this video we are going to look at over a dozen variations on the step vault. nn### Beginner 1 - Both handsnnThis is the one done in the basics video. Use two hands and one foot to get yourself over an obstacle.nn### Beginner 2 - One handnnThis is the standard step vault that everyone will recognise - the one hand, one foot version.nSame as the two foot one, but without using the inside hand on the wall at all. nYou may need a little hop to get the foot up on the wall now.nn### Beginner 3 - floornnThis is safer, but harder. Do the one foot, one handed version on the floor. This will help with confidence, mobility and balance.nn### Beginner 4 - bad sidennThe one handed version of the vault but performed with the less other side.nThis is a variation that you should always do to maximise versatility - sometimes the environment will dictate that you can only do a move on one side - so best get comfortable using either.nn### Beginner 5 - momentumnnStart with one step back and move into the step vault with a constant speed (not fast). Once happy, switch to the other side.nNext take two steps and repeat. Keep increasing steps as you feel comfortable with the amount of momentum generated.nHowever - be aware of wasting the momentum by pausing or slowing before the wall.nn### Intermediate 1 - Different surfacesnnStart investigating the use of different surfaces. I\'m talking about shapes and materials. From concrete brick to metal railings and everything else in between.nYou want to start being able to distinguish how different materials are going to behave and react. Wood, metal, types of brick; all have to be treated slightly differently.nAs an addition to this one, you can use the weather to your advantage and use it as another variable to change the surfaces. The same obstacle in the wet, cold, hot, dry, icy, all make things different.nFor instance, we know that wet wood will no-doubt be slippery. Crumbly dry brick with dust may also need care...nn### Intermediate 2 - ChainingnnStart to practice chaining multiple step vaults together with two or three steps in between. nLearning how to set yourself up with the correct hand and foot placement from one to another will help create flow.nThis will also get you to start understanding the importance of being  accurate with those positions.nn### Intermediate 3 - ConsistencynnThis one can be misunderstood for going fast. What we are looking for is a consistent movement speed, which can be slow, but has less fluctuations of that speed.nFor instance, the entry and exit of the vault are usually easier and quicker than the middle part of getting over the wall. Try to remain active and contracted the whole way, without any relaxing of the muscles at any point.nFilm yourself and watch it to see when any of the \"sticking\" points are. nn### Intermediate 4 - Slow and ControllednnPerform the step vault as slow as you can and then hold the position where the back leg has passed through, but not touched the ground yet.nExtend the leg straight, hold your balance for 10seconds and reverse the movement to the beginning again.nn### Advanced 1 - SpeednnThe \"Speed Step\" is a slightly different variation of the step vault and deserves its own video. However, performing the normal step vault at speed is a good skill to acquire.nSlowly practice increasing your speed from walking pace to jogging into / out of it, to having the confidence of going at sprinting speed.nn### Advanced 2 - FlownnGoing fast, over multiple obstacles, of different types, without planning your foot placement and keep a consistent speed is good practice for when the terrain and environment is unknown.nBeing able to adapt to what is coming up without preparation will make you more capable and practical.nn### Advanced 3 - HeightnnThis is obviously dependent of your own height, but try to use you arms to pull you into a much higher step vault. nHelpful on higher railings and the beginnings of climb-ups.nn### Advanced 4 - IsometricnnThe same as the intermediate variation of the slow and controlled, however, this is at height and on a railing. A goal post, top of a swing set, scaffolding or anything else. nThis will really tax your muscular control, adrenaline and fear. A great way of fine-tuning those positions and body awareness.nn### Advanced 5 - Embrace the weirdnnThe last variation is to go out and challenge yourself to find any weird and strange obstacles that you don\'t know whether you can step vault or not.nCan you step vault through small gaps? At slanted angles? With backpacks on? Barefoot? In the dark? Up high? Underground? On a chain? A moving gate? The list is endless... Try challenge yourself with everything.n---n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\"},\"defaultAudioLanguage\":\"en-GB\"},\"contentDetails\":{\"duration\":\"PT11M14S\",\"dimension\":\"2d\",\"definition\":\"hd\",\"caption\":\"false\",\"licensedContent\":false,\"contentRating\":{},\"projection\":\"rectangular\"}}],\"pageInfo\":{\"totalResults\":1,\"resultsPerPage\":1}}'),(32864,55279,'_media_1_video_details','field_620b62afe64c7'),(32865,55279,'_edit_lock','1702633359:1'),(32866,55279,'video_type','syllabus'),(32867,55279,'_video_type','field_67160a62e1ea2'),(32868,55279,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(32869,55279,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(32870,55279,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(32871,55279,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(32872,55280,'_edit_last','1'),(32873,55280,'award_level','9'),(32874,55280,'_award_level','field_619a7081cdd24'),(32875,55280,'filming_location','Canada Water'),(32876,55280,'_filming_location','field_619b630057212'),(32877,55280,'filming_notes','Muscleup bar.'),(32878,55280,'_filming_notes','field_619b541e49dfd'),(32879,55280,'videoId',''),(32880,55280,'_videoId','field_619a7fe0a22a1'),(32881,55280,'variables_0_height','full'),(32882,55280,'_variables_0_height','field_61950a338af8d_field_619507b0f7d82'),(32883,55280,'variables_0_more_or_less','more'),(32884,55280,'_variables_0_more_or_less','field_61950a338af8d_field_6195