lds. This position allows for a stronger grip, ensuring that as you climb, you\'re not only hanging on but actively engaging with the surface. The goal is to get as much of your hand over the hold as possible, locking your fingers in place to create a secure connection. If you do happen to start slipping, rather than losing your grip completely, this technique helps to control descents, allowing you to gradually slide down your fingers instead of falling off the hold.nnIn summary, focusing on your hand positioning is a small yet pivotal adjustment that can enhance your climbing experience. By keeping your knuckles bent and gripping firmly, you not only improve your climbing efficiency but also increase your safety. So, next time you\'re on the wall, remember to check in with your grip—it\'s the details that often make the biggest difference! nn- Focus on bending your knuckles while gripping holds for increased control.n- Engage fully with the surface by getting your hand over the hold.n- Remember that a solid grip allows for safer descents in case of slips.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 335,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 299,\n            \"total_tokens\": 634,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"rogmOgOP4p_279ypnSQbm.LJL9hl6G6Au9NKnRIEalg-1730718176-1.0.1.1-_1ocMRHMmBNKiJ996ELYeSRXyPn76GwxL9XxMXCOPpOshfExMCbq_6Vu8mMZ90Z3eOk8.98Y6Ln9TFio.9nabA\",\n            \"expires\": 1730719976,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"2hforq8n3HdiJKDvW7slBgRAdzaUzYmZBWhJ3uNX7xM-1730718176957-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(74178,55818,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(74179,55818,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(74180,55818,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(74181,55818,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(74182,55818,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(74183,55819,'_thumbnail_id','14042'),(74184,55819,'video_id','zEhl_e0rl-U'),(74185,55819,'video_type','tutorial'),(74186,55819,'order_position','6'),(74187,55819,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"eQqO8Gz_q6iVE7q0kQoOg_bjBlc\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUUGMzT01DNmlBMm5tSnNKNXluT2JxbC4xMkVGQjNCMUM1N0RFNEUx\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-06-25T08:44:53Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Cat Leap #07 - Arm Preparedness Parkour Tutorial. [021]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Cat Leap (Beginner) in Parkour - Arm Preparedness.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Cat Leap Series. Episode #07nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Climbing #ArmPreparedness #---nnCat-Leap Arm Preparedness.nnWith the sudden jolt of trying to stop your entire bodyweight from sliding off the wall, the shoulders, lats, long-head bicep, pecs and many other muscles need to get used to starting in a fully contracted position at the top and slowly extending the arms into a straight arm position.nThis is more of a conditioning exercise to get you prepared for holding your bodyweight. As the cat-leap distance grows, angle of trajectory changes (downwards Cat-Leap) and the landing position alters, you need to be ready to hold the bodyweight with increasing downward force.nn1. Being ready for the pull on the muscles is imperative. Therefore you need to be contracted straight away. Being relaxed in the arms means that the top portion of your range-of-motion of the arm contraction is being missed and not utilised (which is arguably the strongest position of your pulling chain). This also means you won\'t go straight into the fully extended position instantly. u26a0EXTERNAL rotation at the shoulder joint, contracting the lats and long-head of the bicep will give you a maximum strength position on the pulling chain. I\'ll do videos on this at some point going into this, but for now, think that your elbows should be twisted inwards and next to your ribcage.nn2. At the bottom position, do not \'hang\' on your tendons. Ensure your muscles are still engaged the whole way down to bottom, and are still switched on AT the bottom.nn3. Increase distance and size as you feel confident that your strength is able to take the impact.nn4. As always, both sides take off!nnn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Climbing}}{{Series:Cat Leaps Tips}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/zEhl_e0rl-U/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/zEhl_e0rl-U/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/zEhl_e0rl-U/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/zEhl_e0rl-U/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/zEhl_e0rl-U/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTPc3OMC6iA2nmJsJ5ynObql\",\"position\":6,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"zEhl_e0rl-U\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":6}'),(74188,55819,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Arm Jumps'),(74189,55819,'authId','Auth1'),(74190,55819,'searchId','Tutorials - Arm Jumps'),(74191,55819,'filterId','none'),(74192,55819,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(74193,55819,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Arm Jumps'),(74194,55819,'_edit_last','1'),(74195,55819,'video_transcript_chatgpt','If you’ve ever found yourself landing from a jump and feeling overwhelmed by the impact, this tutorial is for you. We’ll focus on a technique that helps you transition smoothly into a straight-arm position after landing, particularly when executing a cat leap. By mastering this skill, you can significantly reduce the strain on your arms and maintain better control during your jumps.\n\nTo start, practice landing in a stable position before gently lowering your arms into a straight posture. The key here is to avoid plopping into the straight-arm position; instead, you want to transition slowly and with purpose. Think of it as easing into the position rather than dropping suddenly. This controlled movement helps distribute the impact more evenly and keeps you from being thrown off balance. The goal is to become accustomed to this gradual motion so that it feels natural when you leap.\n\nIn summary, learning to land smoothly and shift into a straight-arm position will benefit your jumping technique immensely. By avoiding abrupt movements that can lead to fall-offs, you’ll find that controlling your landings becomes easier and more instinctive. So take your time practicing this transition, and you’ll notice a difference in your jumping as you gain confidence in your technique.\n\nHighlights:\n- Focus on gradually transitioning into a straight-arm position after landing.\n- Avoid sudden movements to maintain control and stability.\n- Practice regularly to make the technique feel effortless.'),(74196,55819,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(74197,55819,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"today we\'re going to have a look at\",\n        \"start\": 0.75,\n        \"duration\": 5.939\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"landing in the position but then going\",\n        \"start\": 2.84,\n        \"duration\": 5.92\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"down into straight-arm\",\n        \"start\": 6.689,\n        \"duration\": 3.901\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"now the reason we\'re gonna do this is\",\n        \"start\": 8.76,\n        \"duration\": 5.76\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"because if you do a cat leap and it\'s\",\n        \"start\": 10.59,\n        \"duration\": 6.089\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"there\'s a lot of force behind it and\",\n        \"start\": 14.52,\n        \"duration\": 4.589\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"it\'s a big jump and essentially you\",\n        \"start\": 16.679,\n        \"duration\": 5.16\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"can\'t take all of the impact with your\",\n        \"start\": 19.109,\n        \"duration\": 4.98\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"arms bank constantly we want to get used\",\n        \"start\": 21.839,\n        \"duration\": 5.041\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"to slowly bringing the arms to the\",\n        \"start\": 24.089,\n        \"duration\": 4.981\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"straight position not just dropping into\",\n        \"start\": 26.88,\n        \"duration\": 4.5\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"it because that sudden jerk is then\",\n        \"start\": 29.07,\n        \"duration\": 4.38\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"gonna allow you to fall off and\",\n        \"start\": 31.38,\n        \"duration\": 4.08\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"obviously we don\'t want that alright so\",\n        \"start\": 33.45,\n        \"duration\": 6.5\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"slow down into a straight up\",\n        \"start\": 35.46,\n        \"duration\": 4.49\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"okay that\'s it\",\n        \"start\": 51.66,\n        \"duration\": 3.26\n    }\n]'),(74198,55819,'_video_transcript_json','field_67221da62655b'),(74199,55819,'video_transcript_srt','1\n00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000\ntoday we\'re going to have a look at\n\n2\n00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,000\nlanding in the position but then going\n\n3\n00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000\ndown into straight-arm\n\n4\n00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000\nnow the reason we\'re gonna do this is\n\n5\n00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000\nbecause if you do a cat leap and it\'s\n\n6\n00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000\nthere\'s a lot of force behind it and\n\n7\n00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000\nit\'s a big jump and essentially you\n\n8\n00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000\ncan\'t take all of the impact with your\n\n9\n00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000\narms bank constantly we want to get used\n\n10\n00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000\nto slowly bringing the arms to the\n\n11\n00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000\nstraight position not just dropping into\n\n12\n00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000\nit because that sudden jerk is then\n\n13\n00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,000\ngonna allow you to fall off and\n\n14\n00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,000\nobviously we don\'t want that alright so\n\n15\n00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000\nslow down into a straight up\n\n16\n00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000\nokay that\'s it\n\n'),(74200,55819,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(74201,55819,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n     