warmed up properly—this promotes safety and reduces the risk of injury. Incorporating exercises that develop shoulder and arm strength as well as hip mobility, such as push-ups, burpees, and lunges, will set a solid foundation. When you\'re ready to practice, find a wall that is about thigh to hip height. Begin by placing both hands firmly on the wall and positioning one foot on top. Focus on pushing your hips high to create space as you move your back leg through. Timing and control are crucial here; land on the ball of your foot and step down smoothly. Initially, aim to travel in a straight line, ensuring your weight is distributed correctly towards your arms as you vault.nnThe key takeaway with the step vault is to prioritize space and control. As you build confidence, you can refine your technique and explore various advanced forms of this fundamental vault. Remember, practice makes perfect, so keep working on your skills, and soon you\'ll be vaulting over obstacles with ease and style. Enjoy the journey of improvement, and remember that the more comfortable you become, the more dynamic your movements can get.nn- Always prioritize a thorough warm-up to prevent injury and improve mobility.n- Start with a wall at a comfortable height, focusing on control and creating space as you practice.n- Remember to distribute your weight towards your arms to facilitate the movement and improve your technique.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1403,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 375,\n            \"total_tokens\": 1778,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"tG9mtjPFUDDxfMAnM4RhdcpLRE3tRSR7FrEewn8qKL8-1730723083-1.0.1.1-YX3Y34sZJHJ08b70_4xGUeG9dAX.y8toApjWbupxp17vxEiXbLvOlTxqL_VZhorqYq0B55yndCxZek8.LB1dfg\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724883,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"fLrfg8zQUyswrN2ZMwXZbPVexwWg5PVwoPmDbAeD1ZM-1730723083681-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(74299,55823,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(74300,55823,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(74301,55823,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(74302,55823,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(74303,55823,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(74304,55824,'_thumbnail_id','14601'),(74305,55824,'video_id','8LR6aA2JzsY'),(74306,55824,'video_type','tutorial'),(74307,55824,'order_position','1'),(74308,55824,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"yJjNuRGiYX1i3SY6u1scW7DfVYk\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUUGl1a0xaRXFZaUM2cFlkMUxSNWUxOC4wMTcyMDhGQUE4NTIzM0Y5\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2021-09-28T10:07:12Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"Step Vault Progressions - Parkour Tutorials - 002\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on Step Vault Progressions for Beginnersnud83eudd77 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 002nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnn#Vaults #StepVault #Parkournn## Variationsn---nOrdered by difficulty, in this video we are going to look at over a dozen variations on the step vault. nn### Beginner 1 - Both handsnnThis is the one done in the basics video. Use two hands and one foot to get yourself over an obstacle.nn### Beginner 2 - One handnnThis is the standard step vault that everyone will recognise - the one hand, one foot version.nSame as the two foot one, but without using the inside hand on the wall at all. nYou may need a little hop to get the foot up on the wall now.nn### Beginner 3 - floornnThis is safer, but harder. Do the one foot, one handed version on the floor. This will help with confidence, mobility and balance.nn### Beginner 4 - bad sidennThe one handed version of the vault but performed with the less other side.nThis is a variation that you should always do to maximise versatility - sometimes the environment will dictate that you can only do a move on one side - so best get comfortable using either.nn### Beginner 5 - momentumnnStart with one step back and move into the step vault with a constant speed (not fast). Once happy, switch to the other side.nNext take two steps and repeat. Keep increasing steps as you feel comfortable with the amount of momentum generated.nHowever - be aware of wasting the momentum by pausing or slowing before the wall.nn### Intermediate 1 - Different surfacesnnStart investigating the use of different surfaces. I\'m talking about shapes and materials. From concrete brick to metal railings and everything else in between.nYou want to start being able to distinguish how different materials are going to behave and react. Wood, metal, types of brick; all have to be treated slightly differently.nAs an addition to this one, you can use the weather to your advantage and use it as another variable to change the surfaces. The same obstacle in the wet, cold, hot, dry, icy, all make things different.nFor instance, we know that wet wood will no-doubt be slippery. Crumbly dry brick with dust may also need care...nn### Intermediate 2 - ChainingnnStart to practice chaining multiple step vaults together with two or three steps in between. nLearning how to set yourself up with the correct hand and foot placement from one to another will help create flow.nThis will also get you to start understanding the importance of being  accurate with those positions.nn### Intermediate 3 - ConsistencynnThis one can be misunderstood for going fast. What we are looking for is a consistent movement speed, which can be slow, but has less fluctuations of that speed.nFor instance, the entry and exit of the vault are usually easier and quicker than the middle part of getting over the wall. Try to remain active and contracted the whole way, without any relaxing of the muscles at any point.nFilm yourself and watch it to see when any of the \"sticking\" points are. nn### Intermediate 4 - Slow and ControllednnPerform the step vault as slow as you can and then hold the position where the back leg has passed through, but not touched the ground yet.nExtend the leg straight, hold your balance for 10seconds and reverse the movement to the beginning again.nn### Advanced 1 - SpeednnThe \"Speed Step\" is a slightly different variation of the step vault and deserves its own video. However, performing the normal step vault at speed is a good skill to acquire.nSlowly practice increasing your speed from walking pace to jogging into / out of it, to having the confidence of going at sprinting speed.nn### Advanced 2 - FlownnGoing fast, over multiple obstacles, of different types, without planning your foot placement and keep a consistent speed is good practice for when the terrain and environment is unknown.nBeing able to adapt to what is coming up without preparation will make you more capable and practical.nn### Advanced 3 - HeightnnThis is obviously dependent of your own height, but try to use you arms to pull you into a much higher step vault. nHelpful on higher railings and the beginnings of climb-ups.nn### Advanced 4 - IsometricnnThe same as the intermediate variation of the slow and controlled, however, this is at height and on a railing. A goal post, top of a swing set, scaffolding or anything else. nThis will really tax your muscular control, adrenaline and fear. A great way of fine-tuning those positions and body awareness.nn### Advanced 5 - Embrace the weirdnnThe last variation is to go out and challenge yourself to find any weird and strange obstacles that you don\'t know whether you can step vault or not.nCan you step vault through small gaps? At slanted angles? With backpacks on? Barefoot? In the dark? Up high? Underground? On a chain? A moving gate? The list is endless... Try challenge yourself with everything.n---n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8LR6aA2JzsY/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTPiukLZEqYiC6pYd1LR5e18\",\"position\":1,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"8LR6aA2JzsY\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":1}'),(74309,55824,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Step Vault New'),(74310,55824,'authId','Auth1'),(74311,55824,'searchId','Tutorials - Step Vaults New'),(74312,55824,'filterId','none'),(74313,55824,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(74314,55824,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Step Vaults New'),(74315,55824,'_edit_last','1'),(74316,55824,'video_transcript_chatgpt','In this tutorial, we will explore the world of step vaults, ranging from beginner to advanced techniques. Step vaults are essential for anyone looking to enhance their parkour skills, as they enable you to navigate obstacles with ease and confidence. Whether you\'re starting out or refining your skill set, it’s crucial to practice variations that suit your current ability level, ensuring you build a solid foundation while progressively challenging yourself.\n\nTo kick things off, begin with the basics: the two-handed step vault using both hands and one foot to lift yourself over a wall. As you grow comfortable, transition to the one-handed variation, practicing on the floor for safety. Gradually introduce movements with your non-dominant side to maximize versatility. For those ready to advance, incorporating momentum by stepping back and then vaulting can help you gain speed. Explore different surfaces and materials to understand how various conditions affect your technique, allowing you to adapt effectively. Chain together multiple vaults to improve flow and precision, while always maintaining consistency in your speed and control.\n\nAs you progress to advanced techniques, focus on increasing your speed, finding your flow over different obstacles, and building your confidence in height variations. Work on isometric holds at higher elevations to enhance your muscular control and body awareness. Lastly, embrace the unconventional by challenging yourself with unique obstacles, which can foster creativity and adaptability in your movements. Overall, the key takeaway is to practice regularly, be patient with your progress, and most importantly, have fun while you develop your parkour skills.\n\n- Start with basic step vaults, progressing from two-handed to one-handed variations, and practice on the floor for safety.\n- Experiment with different surfaces and continuous chaining to enhance your flow and consistency.\n- Embrace unique obstacles and focus o