5,000 --> 00:01:468,000\nfurther down so it gets higher and\n\n217\n00:00:527,000 --> 00:01:470,000\nhigher\n\n218\n00:00:528,000 --> 00:01:472,000\njump step through\n\n219\n00:00:530,000 --> 00:01:474,000\nif you can get to something\n\n220\n00:00:532,000 --> 00:01:476,000\nroughly head height that would be a good\n\n221\n00:00:534,000 --> 00:01:477,000\nstart so being able to\n\n222\n00:00:537,000 --> 00:01:478,000\npush\n\n223\n00:00:538,000 --> 00:01:480,000\nstep\n\n224\n00:00:539,000 --> 00:01:483,000\nover\n\n225\n00:00:540,000 --> 00:01:484,000\nso advanced number four is an isometric\n\n226\n00:00:543,000 --> 00:01:486,000\nhold\n\n227\n00:00:544,000 --> 00:01:489,000\nthis is the same as the intermediate\n\n228\n00:00:547,000 --> 00:01:492,000\nvariation of the slow and controlled one\n\n229\n00:00:550,000 --> 00:01:495,000\nhowever this is now at height on a\n\n230\n00:00:552,000 --> 00:01:497,000\nrailing so the a goal post the top of a\n\n231\n00:00:555,000 --> 00:01:499,000\nswing set some scaffolding or anything\n\n232\n00:00:558,000 --> 00:01:501,000\nelse like that that\'s what you want to\n\n233\n00:00:560,000 --> 00:01:503,000\nfind\n\n234\n00:00:561,000 --> 00:01:505,000\nthis will really tax your muscular\n\n235\n00:00:563,000 --> 00:01:508,000\ncontrol your adrenaline and fear and a\n\n236\n00:00:566,000 --> 00:01:510,000\ngreat way of fine tuning those positions\n\n237\n00:00:568,000 --> 00:01:511,000\nand body awareness\n\n238\n00:00:570,000 --> 00:01:513,000\nso same as before you\'re going to use\n\n239\n00:00:572,000 --> 00:01:516,000\nthe railing you\'re going to get up on\n\n240\n00:00:573,000 --> 00:01:517,000\ntop you\'re going to bring the other leg\n\n241\n00:00:576,000 --> 00:01:522,000\nthrough\n\n242\n00:00:578,000 --> 00:01:524,000\nhold that position for 5-10 seconds\n\n243\n00:00:582,000 --> 00:01:525,000\nand then slowly\n\n244\n00:00:585,000 --> 00:01:529,000\nbring it back\n\n245\n00:00:586,000 --> 00:01:530,000\nand come down don\'t forget to do both\n\n246\n00:00:589,000 --> 00:01:531,000\nsides\n\n247\n00:00:590,000 --> 00:01:533,000\nso same thing\n\n248\n00:00:592,000 --> 00:01:537,000\nyou\'re going to get up onto the railing\n\n249\n00:00:594,000 --> 00:01:538,000\nfind your way up that back leg comes\n\n250\n00:00:597,000 --> 00:01:540,000\nthrough\n\n251\n00:00:598,000 --> 00:01:543,000\npoint the leg\n\n252\n00:00:600,000 --> 00:01:543,000\nhold that position\n\n253\n00:00:604,000 --> 00:01:547,000\nslowly\n\n254\n00:00:606,000 --> 00:01:551,000\ncome back\n\n255\n00:00:607,000 --> 00:01:552,000\nadvance number five embrace the weird\n\n256\n00:00:611,000 --> 00:01:555,000\nthe last variation is to go out and\n\n257\n00:00:613,000 --> 00:01:557,000\nchallenge yourself to find any weird and\n\n258\n00:00:615,000 --> 00:01:559,000\nstrange obstacles that you don\'t know\n\n259\n00:00:617,000 --> 00:01:561,000\nwhether you can step vault or not\n\n260\n00:00:619,000 --> 00:01:562,000\nfor example trees and branches can be\n\n261\n00:00:622,000 --> 00:01:565,000\ngood\n\n262\n00:00:623,000 --> 00:01:568,000\ncan you do non-flat fences\n\n263\n00:00:626,000 --> 00:01:570,000\ncan you step vault through small gaps a\n\n264\n00:00:628,000 --> 00:01:573,000\nslanted angle with backpacks on a\n\n265\n00:00:630,000 --> 00:01:574,000\nbarefoot in the dark up high underground\n\n266\n00:00:633,000 --> 00:01:576,000\non a chain\n\n267\n00:00:635,000 --> 00:01:579,000\na moving gate the list is endless\n\n268\n00:00:637,000 --> 00:01:580,000\ntry and challenge yourself with\n\n269\n00:00:639,000 --> 00:01:583,000\neverything and anything\n\n270\n00:00:641,000 --> 00:01:585,000\nso that\'s it for this video\n\n271\n00:00:643,000 --> 00:01:587,000\ntry and just go out and have some fun\n\n272\n00:00:645,000 --> 00:01:589,000\nand do some parkour don\'t worry about\n\n273\n00:00:648,000 --> 00:01:593,000\ncommenting subscribing or liking or any\n\n274\n00:00:650,000 --> 00:01:595,000\nof that stuff just go and try and get\n\n275\n00:00:653,000 --> 00:01:598,000\nbetter at doing parkour and getting more\n\n276\n00:00:656,000 --> 00:01:612,000\npractical and functional\n\n277\n00:00:658,000 --> 00:01:611,000\n[Music]\n\n278\n00:00:673,000 --> 00:01:615,000\nyou\n\n'),(74321,55824,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(74322,55824,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-APqQWWLFakpyHAbJH1mLvyzLhjwTJ\",\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730723092,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"In this tutorial, we will explore the world of step vaults, ranging from beginner to advanced techniques. Step vaults are essential for anyone looking to enhance their parkour skills, as they enable you to navigate obstacles with ease and confidence. Whether you\'re starting out or refining your skill set, it’s crucial to practice variations that suit your current ability level, ensuring you build a solid foundation while progressively challenging yourself.nnTo kick things off, begin with the basics: the two-handed step vault using both hands and one foot to lift yourself over a wall. As you grow comfortable, transition to the one-handed variation, practicing on the floor for safety. Gradually introduce movements with your non-dominant side to maximize versatility. For those ready to advance, incorporating momentum by stepping back and then vaulting can help you gain speed. Explore different surfaces and materials to understand how various conditions affect your technique, allowing you to adapt effectively. Chain together multiple vaults to improve flow and precision, while always maintaining consistency in your speed and control.nnAs you progress to advanced techniques, focus on increasing your speed, finding your flow over different obstacles, and building your confidence in height variations. Work on isometric holds at higher elevations to enhance your muscular control and body awareness. Lastly, embrace the unconventional by challenging yourself with unique obstacles, which can foster creativity and adaptability in your movements. Overall, the key takeaway is to practice regularly, be patient with your progress, and most importantly, have fun while you develop your parkour skills.nn- Start with basic step vaults, progressing from two-handed to one-handed variations, and practice on the floor for safety.n- Experiment with different surfaces and continuous chaining to enhance your flow and consistency.n- Embrace unique obstacles and focus on speed and height to challenge yourself and improve confidence in your movements.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 2072,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 364,\n            \"total_tokens\": 2436,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"3bX58D9sAv3BNtldGarpO83zUbE9nAUV9x4zi3ODip8-1730723097-1.0.1.1-jSmMrv6BH0._CR9whyjX_ALKFct99IFoDpzi3Dd40LgV4iDLTKjHK7TTqN6qjzMCP3YaUFYEKhfC97v8_Gcwaw\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724897,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"BmHbyi6YpN1wKEMpJlWCeqt88JHBVmh41zasQ87GrvQ-1730723097192-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(74323,55824,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(74324,55824,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(74325,55824,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(74326,55824,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(74327,55824,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(74328,55825,'_thumbnail_id','14603'),(74329,55825,'video_id','hKDhQaYRke4'),(74330,55825,'video_type','tutorial'),(74331,55825,'order_position','2'),(74332,55825,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"CUEBCcpJUTknWXSCx7_E-SEKj6I\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUUGl1a0xaRXFZaUM2cFlkMUxSNWUxOC4yODlGNEE0NkRGMEEzMEQy\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2021-09-28T10:01:35Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"Step Vault Common Faults - Parkour Tutorials - 003\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on Step Vault Common Faults for Beginnersnud83eudd77 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 003nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnn#Vaults #StepVault #Parkournn## Common Faultsn---nIn this video we\'ll discuss and demonstrate some of the common faults made when starting out with the step vault.nnYou can use this as a troubleshooting guide to help any difficulties you may be having.nnn### Fault 1 - Steepled FingersnnThis happens If you cannot create the correct body structure to create enough space under yourself. nnWithout thinking about it, the fingers are sometimes steepled up to increase the torso height.nnThis leaves the fingers susceptible to stubbing them and too much body weight on the finger tendons. Not to mention over bending if they\'re unable to hold you up.nnThis can also be an indicator of strength / mobility issues. And it also means that you\'ll have difficulty when you want to move onto vaulting railings and thinner obstacles.nn#### FixnnHave flat hands and increasing hip height or mobility.nnn### Fault 2 - Weight Distribution towards the legnnMoving too much towards the leg usually means you\'re feeling uncomfortable taking your bodyweight on the arms and upper-body. nn#### FixnnStart practicing on the floor to create the structure for confidence.nnYou can also practice using two arms to get into the position. Do NOT move your feet and hands once they\'re placed on the wall.nnn### Fault 3 - Foot & Hand too closennWith the foot and hand being too close, you will have to create enough space under yourself by raising hips far too high. Alternatively, you\'ll have to have great mobility. nn#### FixnnIf this is the case,  spread the feet/hands out more and make visible marks on the wall as targets.nnn#### Fault 4 - Space underneathnnIf the space underneath is still too small, it means your mobility needs work or your feet and hands are too close.nn#### FixnnSpend more time at the bottom of a squat position. Try practicing deep lunges, crane positions and sideways monkeys.nnn### Fault 5 - Knee collapsennThe knee can collapse and all weight placed onto the hands because you don\'t want to raise your hips higher (which, in turn, leans them forwards - which won\'t feel comfortable.)nn#### FixnnGet the knee pointing upwards and use both the arms and leg to push the bodyweight higher.nnSometimes it\'s hard to even realise what the problem is, so film yourself to spot the mistake.nnMobility work will also help to open up the hips. Deep squat position and pushing knees out will start to work this.nnn### Fault 6  - Foot facing outwardsnnThe foot position is key to where the hips will be facing and where the weight will be distributed. If the foot is pointed at an angle, the hips will have difficulty getting high enough.nnKeep the foot facing fowards, pressure towards the ball of the foot and keep it stable, active, tense, and solid.nn### Fault 7 - Wrong motivationsnnHaving the right motivations is key. nnObtaining clean and refined technique is what will help you become better when other variables are introduced. (Usually speed, flow, chaining, height, variations, etc...) However, most advanced practitioners usually wish they had spent time refining the basics when they first started - don\'t fall into the trap of introducing more complicated things before being able to do the simple ones.nn### FixnnCreate clear objectives and goals for yourself to benchmark your progress. Write down what you can do and what you struggle with.  Identifying, highlighting and fixing those weaknesses will pay tenfold in the future.nn### Fault 8 - RotationnnYou are rotating around the arm and moving in a curve, rather than going in a straight line. This means you are using the arm as a pivot point too much.nnThis is not to say that you can\'t move in this way, it\'s just that it should come as a later variation - and when the situation arises. Nailing down the straight line should be the priority.nn### FixnnDraw a line perpendicular to the wall on both sides with chalk. Try to take off and land alond the line.nnn### Fault 9 - Two Foot LandingsnnUnless the environment dictates a two-foot landing, or you are landing in a precision, you should be able to keep the momentum going in the same direction. nnA two foot landing will stop the momentum.nn#### FixnnLand one foot only and then step to the next, moving in a straight line.nnn### Fault 10 - GranularitynnIf you are unable to do the current movement then the step between the previous version and the one you\'re currently trying to do is too great.nn#### FixnnFind a mid-way point on the difficulty. Take a regression step back try something slightly different and then retry.nn---n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hKDhQaYRke4/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hKDhQaYRke4/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hKDhQaYRke4/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hKDhQaYRke4/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hKDhQaYRke4/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTPiukLZEqYiC6pYd1LR5e18\",\"position\":2,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"hKDhQaYRke4\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":2}'),(74333,55825,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Step Vault New'),(74334,55825,'authId','Auth1'),(74335,55825,'searchId','Tutorials - Step Vaults New'),(74336,55825,'filterId','none'),(74337,55825,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(74338,55825,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Step Vaults New'),(74339,55825,'_edit_last','1'),(74340,55825,'video_transcript_chatgpt','When venturing into the world of parkour, the step vault can feel a bit tricky, especially for beginners. Common mistakes can quickly hinder your progress and lead to frustration. By understanding and addressing these mistakes, like improper hand and foot placement or ineffective weight distribution, you can develop a solid foundation for executing the step vault smoothly and confidently. This tutorial will guide you through key improvements that can enhance your technique and keep your training sessions productive.\n\nTo start, focus on maintaining correct hand placement. Steepled fingers are a sign of inadequate body structure and may lead to stubbing or straining your tendons. Keep your hands flat on the ground, which will help distribute your weight effectively when transitioning into the vault. Next, mind your weight distribution; lean too much on your legs, and you’ll feel unsteady. Begin practicing on the floor to build comfort and confidence in shifting body weight from your arms. Additionally, ensure your feet and hands aren’t too close together. Spreading them apart will allow for better mobility and a smoother movement through the vault. Throughout this process, enhancing your overall mobility with exercises like deep squats and lunges will further improve your performance.\n\nIn conclusion, refining your approach to the step vault is all about small adjustments leading to significant improvements. Focus on your body structure, weight distribution, and hand and foot positioning to create a solid foundation for your technique. Remember, practice makes perfect, and embracing these tips will not only make your vaults more effective but also boost your overall parkour abilities. \n\n- Flatten your hands and avoid steepled fingers to prevent injury.\n- Lean into your arms while keeping feet and hands adequately spaced for better execution.\n- Work on mobility through exercises to enhance your overall performance in the step vault.'),(74341,55825,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(74342,55825,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"in this video we\'ll discuss and\",\n        \"start\": 0.48,\n        \"duration\": 3.76\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"demonstrate some of the common faults\",\n        \"start\": 2.24,\n        \"duration\": 3.76\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"made when starting out with the step\",\n        \"start\": 4.24,\n        \"duration\": 4\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"vault\",\n        \"start\": 6,\n        \"duration\": 2.24\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"[Music]\",\n        \"start\": 13.23,\n        \"duration\": 5.73\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"fault number one is steepled fingers so\",\n        \"start\": 15.44,\n 