uide you through key improvements that can enhance your technique and keep your training sessions productive.nnTo start, focus on maintaining correct hand placement. Steepled fingers are a sign of inadequate body structure and may lead to stubbing or straining your tendons. Keep your hands flat on the ground, which will help distribute your weight effectively when transitioning into the vault. Next, mind your weight distribution; lean too much on your legs, and you’ll feel unsteady. Begin practicing on the floor to build comfort and confidence in shifting body weight from your arms. Additionally, ensure your feet and hands aren’t too close together. Spreading them apart will allow for better mobility and a smoother movement through the vault. Throughout this process, enhancing your overall mobility with exercises like deep squats and lunges will further improve your performance.nnIn conclusion, refining your approach to the step vault is all about small adjustments leading to significant improvements. Focus on your body structure, weight distribution, and hand and foot positioning to create a solid foundation for your technique. Remember, practice makes perfect, and embracing these tips will not only make your vaults more effective but also boost your overall parkour abilities. nn- Flatten your hands and avoid steepled fingers to prevent injury.n- Lean into your arms while keeping feet and hands adequately spaced for better execution.n- Work on mobility through exercises to enhance your overall performance in the step vault.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1608,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 353,\n            \"total_tokens\": 1961,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"mtFA9uFraCZZT3tqiB0nw2qosS5IFMLtHrvLTPEv8ro-1730723091-1.0.1.1-Nl19coNEyC43dZSHTlaCSdsWA9LSOaAjfWoUske1PIwQHosOBlmRMNXTpMfLvi8Tfsn8Tsh1aQAB1Py0ToezPw\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724891,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"rVfzQrg5g2jOJXqMuBmxA_IeR_Hevusm6u417CJhmog-1730723091009-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(74347,55825,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(74348,55825,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(74349,55825,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(74350,55825,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(74351,55825,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(74352,55826,'_thumbnail_id','14605'),(74353,55826,'video_id','PaRVcCoGrFQ'),(74354,55826,'video_type','tutorial'),(74355,55826,'order_position','3'),(74356,55826,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"y6xIG-6DtO1aP-TgZELbjMVGEo4\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUUGl1a0xaRXFZaUM2cFlkMUxSNWUxOC4wOTA3OTZBNzVEMTUzOTMy\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2021-09-28T10:08:14Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"Step Vault Technical Details - Parkour Tutorials - 004\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on Step Vault Technical Details for Beginnersnud83eudd77 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 004nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnn#Vaults #StepVault #Parkourn---n### ud83euddbeBiomechanicsnnParkour movements are very complex biomechanically because they go through multiple phases and change constantly. In this video, we\'re going to try to dissect the movement down into its component parts.nnFirstly, The step-vault is a Contralateral movement - Meaning that opposite limbs are used.nnWe can break down this vault into three steps:n1. Entry - This is generally a short concentric (shorten), then eccentric (lengthening of muscles) to get onto the wall.  n2. Hold - Isometric hold to create a body structure that will allow us to balance on one hand / foot.n3. Exit - Lastly, a concentric off the back limbs and eccentric on the front leg.nThe main muscle groups will depend on situation and environment, but you will find that the triceps / deltoids for arms, quads / hip-flexors for legs.nn### ud83euddceu200du2642ufe0f R.O.M. And mobilitynnThe Step Vault doesn\'t require a huge amount of range to be effective, which is one of the reasons for its low requirements to perform. nHowever, having better range of motion gives you more adaptability to smaller spaces and awkward scenarios.nMobility is the range of motion (flexibilty) of the limbs while having power and control. nIf you cant get into the correct positions, then mobility is usually the issue.nnn### u2699ufe0f Torque / TensionnnThe pushing action requires an internal torque (tension) on the shoulder. No rotation is required though.  The legs will also have an internal tension for stability.nRemember, it matters what muscles are doing the work, not what LOOKS like it doing the work. Feel which muscles are working and contracting.nn### ud83cudff9 TrajectorynnThe initial idea is to move in a straight line with minimal up/down motion. \"Efficiency\" really just means less wild, unnecessary, movement and using the power generated to do the job needed.nThe position of the head is also a good indication of tension, contraction and efficiency. Try to minimalise the head moving up and down.nn### ud83dudc63 Body PositionnnHip stability and control from the lower abs/erectors/glutes play a large part in positioning. Hips should always be working to be facing forwards and driving towards the straight line path. nA strong and stable body structure will give you confidence in performing. nThere should always be tension within the whole movement. Try not to release at any stage. A contract-release-contract action will take more time and loose power and control.nn### ud83eudec1 BreathingnnIf you have issues holding the body structure / shape, then try holding your breath during the holding-phase. This will create inter-abdominal pressure and spinal stability.nSimilar to power lifters, both the concentric and eccentric  phase needs full stability. You can breathe out on the eccentric part of the movement (exiting the vault).nThis will also help remove the contract-release-contract mistake. nn### ud83euddd8u200du2642ufe0f Recovery worknnMake sure you spend time on recovery work. The primary muscle groups to focus on for stretching (which is always done after the training), are:nn1.  Triceps - from the the arms are opening on the push. n2.  Deltoids - from stabilisationn3.  Forearms - from hold the body position while the hand is pulled backwards.n4.  Calfs - From holding the foot position on the ball of the foot.n5.  Quads - From the stabilisation of the body in the isometric position.nnYou can also use Myofacial Release with a foam roller or ball to also focus on these areas. nn### u2696ufe0f ImbalancesnnMake sure you spend MORE time on the weaker side. Otherwise over time you\'ll build up more and more imbalances within your body. nAt best, this can lead to being less functional within certain situations and at worst it can cause postural issues and difficulty on hard vaults when more stress is applied. n(For instance, a lot of people start to notice these with cat-passes, climbups and muscle-ups - where the stronger, more dominant side of the body makes it really hard to stay straight and unilateral. ) nnn### ud83eudd15 Common injuriesnnAn uncommon topic for people to acknowledge and talk about, but it\'s wise to be aware of the most common mistakes and injuries made.nn1.  Clipping the foot.nThe foot clips the wall because it hasn\'t been raised high enough and the person falls forward over the wall. Injury to the shin and wrist (from catching themselves on the floor) are the most common.n    n2.  Hand slips off front of the obstacle.nMisjudgement of the hand position accuracy, usually from going too fast or with reduced concentration can cause the person to slip and go head-first over the obstacle.n    n3.  Catching your knee.nLastly, this comes from not raising the hips up high enough and not judging the required amount of space and mobility needed to get the back leg through. n---n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PaRVcCoGrFQ/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PaRVcCoGrFQ/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PaRVcCoGrFQ/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PaRVcCoGrFQ/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PaRVcCoGrFQ/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTPiukLZEqYiC6pYd1LR5e18\",\"position\":3,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"PaRVcCoGrFQ\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":3}'),(74357,55826,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Step Vault New'),(74358,55826,'authId','Auth1'),(74359,55826,'searchId','Tutorials - Step Vaults New'),(74360,55826,'filterId','none'),(74361,55826,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(74362,55826,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Step Vaults New'),(74363,55826,'_edit_last','1'),(74364,55826,'video_transcript_chatgpt','In this tutorial, we’ll delve into the step vault, a dynamic and essential movement in parkour. The step vault involves transitioning your body over an obstacle, showcasing not just agility but also a variety of muscle engagements. Understanding the biomechanics—how various muscle groups work together during the movement—can significantly enhance your technique and efficiency. By examining the movement’s three phases and key concepts like mobility, torque chains, and recovery, you can build your confidence and skill in performing this movement effectively.\n\nTo start, let’s break down the movement into three key steps: the entry phase, the isometric hold, and the exit phase. The entry involves a brief muscle contraction followed by lengthening, while the hold focuses on maintaining body structure to navigate with your back leg. The exit combines both eccentric and concentric movements, resulting in a powerful descent. Throughout this process, it’s critical to maintain internal tension in your shoulders and legs to ensure stability. The goal is to keep your body aligned in a straight line, minimizing unnecessary movements and focusing on core engagement for hip stability. Remember to breathe correctly, holding your breath during crucial phases for added stability and strength.\n\nIn conclusion, practicing the step vault effectively requires dedication to both strength and technique. Focus on maintaining tension throughout the entire movement, supplementing your training with stretching and conditioning exercises to address muscle imbalances. Always be mindful of common mistakes to prevent injuries, such as catching your knee or misjudging hand placements. With these guiding principles, you\'ll not only improve your step vault but also enjoy your journey in parkour.\n\n- Break down the step vault into three phases: entry, hold, and exit for clarity in execution.\n- Maintain tension in your muscles throughout the movement for better control and stability.\n- Incorporate recovery and conditioning exercises to enhance strength and flexibility, addressing any muscle imbalances.'),(74365,55826,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(74366,55826,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"hey everybody we are looking at\",\n        \"start\": 0,\n        \"duration\": 4\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"technical details today in this fourth\",\n        \"start\": 2.159,\n        \"duration\": 3.6\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"video of the step vault series we\'re\",\n        \"start\": 4,\n        \"duration\": 3.44\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"going to be looking at everything from\",\n        \"start\": 5.759,\n        \"duration\": 4.721\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"biomechanics mobility flexibility we\'re\",\n        \"start\": 7.44,\n        \"duration\": 5.68\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"going to be looking at torque chains and\",\n        \"start\": 10.48,\n        \"duration\": 4.96\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"a lot of other bits and pieces to do\",\n        \"start\": 13.12,\n        \"duration\": 5.12\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"with the step vault\",\n        \"start\": 15.44,\n        \"duration\": 2.8\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"[Music]\",\n        \"start\": 22.77,\n        \"duration\": 4.669\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"part one is biomechanics in this video\",\n        \"start\": 24.56,\n        \"duration\": 4.879\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"we\'re going to go and dissect the\",\n        \"start\": 27.439,\n        \"duration\": 4.881\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"movement down into its component parts\",\n        \"start\": 29.439,\n        \"duration\": 4.481\n    },