sjudgment of the hand position\n\n316\n00:00:797,000 --> 00:01:743,000\naccuracy usually from either going too\n\n317\n00:00:800,000 --> 00:01:745,000\nfast or with reduced concentration again\n\n318\n00:00:804,000 --> 00:01:749,000\ncan cause the person to slip and\n\n319\n00:00:806,000 --> 00:01:750,000\nbasically go head first this time\n\n320\n00:00:809,000 --> 00:01:752,000\nover the obstacle\n\n321\n00:00:810,000 --> 00:01:754,000\nthis happens more on things like\n\n322\n00:00:812,000 --> 00:01:757,000\nrailings and thinner walls because\n\n323\n00:00:814,000 --> 00:01:761,000\nobviously your accuracy has to improve\n\n324\n00:00:817,000 --> 00:01:764,000\nnumber three is catching your knee\n\n325\n00:00:821,000 --> 00:01:766,000\nlastly this comes from not raising the\n\n326\n00:00:824,000 --> 00:01:768,000\nhips up high enough and not judging the\n\n327\n00:00:827,000 --> 00:01:771,000\nrequired amount of space and mobility\n\n328\n00:00:829,000 --> 00:01:773,000\nneeded to get the back leg through\n\n329\n00:00:832,000 --> 00:01:776,000\nso as you come up here\n\n330\n00:00:834,000 --> 00:01:779,000\nthis is basically catching the knee\n\n331\n00:00:836,000 --> 00:01:781,000\nright here as you bring it through\n\n332\n00:00:840,000 --> 00:01:784,000\nback knee clips the corner of the wall\n\n333\n00:00:841,000 --> 00:01:786,000\nand usually causes either a bruise or a\n\n334\n00:00:844,000 --> 00:01:788,000\ncut on the knee itself at worst\n\n335\n00:00:847,000 --> 00:01:792,000\nif you\'re coming up and you catch your\n\n336\n00:00:848,000 --> 00:01:794,000\nknee again this is you going forwards\n\n337\n00:00:852,000 --> 00:01:796,000\nso that\'s it for this video\n\n338\n00:00:855,000 --> 00:01:799,000\ntry and just go out and have some fun\n\n339\n00:00:857,000 --> 00:01:801,000\nand do some parkour don\'t worry about\n\n340\n00:00:859,000 --> 00:01:804,000\ncommenting subscribing or liking or any\n\n341\n00:00:862,000 --> 00:01:807,000\nof that stuff just go and try and get\n\n342\n00:00:864,000 --> 00:01:808,000\nbetter at doing parkour and getting more\n\n343\n00:00:867,000 --> 00:01:822,000\npractical and functional\n\n344\n00:00:869,000 --> 00:01:822,000\n[Music]\n\n345\n00:00:883,000 --> 00:01:825,000\nyou\n\n'),(74369,55826,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(74370,55826,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-APqQcSgvFHrgitNySwygjECl6Ng1e\",\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730723098,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"In this tutorial, we’ll delve into the step vault, a dynamic and essential movement in parkour. The step vault involves transitioning your body over an obstacle, showcasing not just agility but also a variety of muscle engagements. Understanding the biomechanics—how various muscle groups work together during the movement—can significantly enhance your technique and efficiency. By examining the movement’s three phases and key concepts like mobility, torque chains, and recovery, you can build your confidence and skill in performing this movement effectively.nnTo start, let’s break down the movement into three key steps: the entry phase, the isometric hold, and the exit phase. The entry involves a brief muscle contraction followed by lengthening, while the hold focuses on maintaining body structure to navigate with your back leg. The exit combines both eccentric and concentric movements, resulting in a powerful descent. Throughout this process, it’s critical to maintain internal tension in your shoulders and legs to ensure stability. The goal is to keep your body aligned in a straight line, minimizing unnecessary movements and focusing on core engagement for hip stability. Remember to breathe correctly, holding your breath during crucial phases for added stability and strength.nnIn conclusion, practicing the step vault effectively requires dedication to both strength and technique. Focus on maintaining tension throughout the entire movement, supplementing your training with stretching and conditioning exercises to address muscle imbalances. Always be mindful of common mistakes to prevent injuries, such as catching your knee or misjudging hand placements. With these guiding principles, you\'ll not only improve your step vault but also enjoy your journey in parkour.nn- Break down the step vault into three phases: entry, hold, and exit for clarity in execution.n- Maintain tension in your muscles throughout the movement for better control and stability.n- Incorporate recovery and conditioning exercises to enhance strength and flexibility, addressing any muscle imbalances.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 2796,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 369,\n            \"total_tokens\": 3165,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"A7p.HQVbQ9GlqbJE44z_bzDONrFVOWGJo7y0eyNNlcs-1730723107-1.0.1.1-2kHKYr.M1ysilBx7LgwL0UkQaLtrpSrjBMEy8JyU0bU0h9h0LjxW2ay7WGfnsGMNGPSXbuoBOlt4HBG_InFZtw\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724907,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"zba2isVVGS8Q2jqf8LED6iln1BcAfgZhis66sq30P.k-1730723107843-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(74371,55826,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(74372,55826,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(74373,55826,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(74374,55826,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(74375,55826,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(74376,55827,'_thumbnail_id','14607'),(74377,55827,'video_id','G_-mMmQYy90'),(74378,55827,'video_type','tutorial'),(74379,55827,'order_position','4'),(74380,55827,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"-S-oU_3jyFNtQsnBX1WMl9BpdCk\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUUGl1a0xaRXFZaUM2cFlkMUxSNWUxOC41MjE1MkI0OTQ2QzJGNzNG\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2021-09-28T10:08:01Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"Step Vault Variations - Parkour Tutorials - 005\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on Speed Step and Reverse Stepnud83eudd77 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 005nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnn#Vaults #StepVault #Parkourn---n##   Variationsnn### Speed stepnnThe variation that is used more frequently by more advanced practitioners because it requires a lot more momentum. nnEssentially, the foot leads the vault, rather than the hand. nnThe primary reason for this is because the leg muscles are a lot bigger and stronger than the upper body muscles of the arms. This means they can take a lot more impact and control the momentum much better.nnBy jumping into the step vault, with the foot landing first, you can be sure that you\'re able to take the increased force through the leg.nnSecondly, it means that the head is not leading the movement and this can be a lot more dangerous and non recommended in a number of scenarios that i\'ll demonstrate later in the video.nnLastly, with this added control, you\'ll be able to move faster with more confidence.nnn### HowTonnFirstly, you want to aim for a jump onto the wall, with a single foot landing slightly to the side and in-front of you. nnIt\'s important to note that the following leg now does not trail behind. Instead it will also lead ahead, but without making contact with the wall.nAs the foot lands on the wall, using the ball of the foot, the second leg should already be ahead of the wall.nThe opposite hand will then land last and help to keep positioning and direction correct. It\'s there to stabilise you and take some of the remaining downward body weight onto the wall.nn### DownwardsnnAs you can see, this method requires the momentum and power to almost completely jump over the wall already and starts to look like a dash vault rather than a step vault.nnNot only does the speed step allow you to move much quicker over the wall, it also allows for the ability to go downwards. nnTraditionally, in this position of a level-to-level wall, a normal step vault, with the hand leading first, would require the arm to take a lot of impact and the head to go first. As I mentioned earlier, this is one of those situations where you really don\'t want to be going fast, downwards with the head first. nnThe speed step is much more useful vault here. With the foot leading and taking the impact, you can safely do the step vault downwards.nnThere is an extra added benefit of this movement in a specific scenario - where you have multiple downward levels. nnIt\'s much easier to chain speed steps together, staying on the same side and foot, hopping down as if you are doing multiple one-leg pistols, strides or cranes. Note that you can also alternate sides as well.  Because the leg is taking the impact you can slow yourself down and regulate the momentum much easier if needed.nnnnnn### Reverse Step VaultnnAs the name implies, the reverse step vault is performing the exact same movement, but in reverse.nnThis isn\'t as common or as useful as the speed step, but a good tool to have when you\'re working on flowing routes. nnThe reason this is helpful is because you don\'t need to turn around to perform a forwards based vault. By being able to accurately step vault backwards allows you to land from a previous vault and instantly perform a second wthout breaking the flow.nnLet me show you an example here:nnImagine you\'ve done a vault over the wall, landed two feet. But to continue the flow, you\'ll have to either turn or explosively return backwards, like this:nn### LearningnnThe thing about the reverse step vault is that you\'re blind to the obstacle and getting the foot up onto the wall will be a tricky thing to do. It\'s going to require more spacial awareness from feeling where the wall is with your hand.nnTo start training this, you can do the following things:nn### 1. Forwards First.nnDo a normal step vault, and without leaving the hand / foot position, reverse the movement.nn### 2. Use a low / wide obstacle.nnStart with your hand on the obstacle and you jump up onto the wall. Then step through and backwards. You can slowly increase the height as you feel more comfortable.nn###u00a03. Go slow, with no jump.nnTry without any momentum and slowly perform the reverse step vault without jumping into the position.nn###u00a03. JumpnnWhen you reversing backwards, jump much higher than the wall and come down onto it. The last thing you want is to clip you heel on the wall and go backwards. Jump up, over and down.nnThis will also give you more time to position the foot for the landing.nn### 4. HandnnAlso rely more heavily on the arm with your bodyweight towards it. This means that there is less impact coming down onto the foot AND you\'re not having to commit your balance and position on correctly guessing the position of your landing.nnIf you misjudge, then the arm will more likely catch you rather than commiting all of your body to the leg and foot before landing.nn---n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/G_-mMmQYy90/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/G_-mMmQYy90/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/G_-mMmQYy90/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/G_-mMmQYy90/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/G_-mMmQYy90/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTPiukLZEqYiC6pYd1LR5e18\",\"position\":4,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"G_-mMmQYy90\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":4}'),(74381,55827,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Step Vault New'),(74382,55827,'authId','Auth1'),(74383,55827,'searchId','Tutorials - Step Vaults New'),(74384,55827,'filterId','none'),(74385,55827,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(74386,55827,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Step Vaults New'),(74387,55827,'_edit_last','1'),(74388,55827,'video_transcript_chatgpt','In this tutorial, we\'ll explore two exciting variations of the step vault: the speed step and the reverse step vault. Both techniques enhance your parkour skills and improve your ability to navigate obstacles with fluidity and control. The speed step is particularly popular among advanced practitioners, as it allows for greater momentum and a safer way to land while moving quickly. Conversely, the reverse step vault can help maintain the flow when transitioning between movements, making it a valuable skill to develop.\n\nLet’s start with the speed step. This variation emphasizes the foot leading the vault, allowing your stronger leg muscles to absorb impact rather than relying on your upper body. To perform the speed step, aim to jump quickly onto the wall with one foot landing slightly in front of you while the opposite leg helps to stabilize your position above the wall. It’s crucial to land on the ball of your foot to ensure a secure grip and proper momentum. As you gain confidence, you can transition smoothly between multiple steps or even adjust sides more easily. Just remember to focus on your speed and control so that you can safely navigate forwards and downwards without risking injury.\n\nNow, let’s discuss the reverse step vault. While less common, it is an excellent tool for linking movements without turning around. To master this technique, you can start by performing a normal step vault and then reversing the movement while keeping your foot and hand in place. Work with low obstacles to practice feeling your way to the wall without relying too heavily on vision. As you become comfortable, experiment with higher jumps to give yourself more time to position your foot correctly upon landing. Most importantly, ensure your hand remains flat and your weight distributed properly to balance your body and avoid misjudging your landings.\n\nIn summary, practicing the speed step and reverse step vault can significantly enhance your parkour skills and boost your confidence when navigating obstacles. These variations not only improve your momentum control but also allow for a smoother transition between movements, making them valuable additions to your skill set. \n\n- The speed step uses momentum and foot-first landing for better impact absorption.\n- Maintain stability by landing on the ball of your foot and ensuring proper body alignment.\n- The reverse step vault helps maintain flow when transitioning between movements without turning around.'),(74389,55827,'_video_transcr