cuff2fuff2euff24uff2fuff2euff30uff21uff32uff2buff2fuff35uff32nnnu2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550u2550nn{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}}\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/0NckwJYXz-8/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/0NckwJYXz-8/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/0NckwJYXz-8/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/0NckwJYXz-8/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/0NckwJYXz-8/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTNfKYNDCLKycuLtrbrOU3T6\",\"position\":6,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"0NckwJYXz-8\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":6}'),(74738,55842,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Precisions'),(74739,55842,'authId','Auth1'),(74740,55842,'searchId','Tutorials - Precisions'),(74741,55842,'filterId','none'),(74742,55842,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(74743,55842,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Precisions'),(74744,55842,'_edit_last','1'),(74745,55842,'video_transcript_chatgpt','When it comes to jumping, understanding the difference between direction and trajectory can significantly enhance your performance. Many newcomers focus solely on aiming straight at their target without considering how they approach it. This can lead to less effective jumps, causing them to overshoot or fall awkwardly on landing. By grasping the nuances of trajectory and adjusting your approach, you can improve not only your jumps but also your overall landing technique.\n\nTo start, visualize your jump in terms of direction and trajectory. Your direction is the straight line from your takeoff point to your target—this remains consistent regardless of how high or low you jump. However, your trajectory is where the magic happens. If you jump with a shallow trajectory, you may find yourself going straight toward the target but lacking the height you need to land effectively. In contrast, a steeper trajectory allows you to come down onto the target, increasing your chances of landing correctly and remaining balanced. Think of this as creating an arch with your jump, where you ascend higher before descending onto the target, allowing your feet to \"stick\" rather than causing you to fall forward.\n\nIn summary, mastering the jump is all about finding that right balance between direction and trajectory. While you want to aim directly at your target, be sure to incorporate a higher trajectory to secure a more stable landing. With practice, you’ll notice how these adjustments can transform your jump technique and enhance your overall athleticism.\n\nHighlights:\n- Focus on both direction and trajectory for better jump performance.\n- A shallow trajectory may lead to falling forward upon landing; aim for a higher arc.\n- A well-executed high trajectory allows for smoother landings and better stability.'),(74746,55842,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(74747,55842,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"Direction and trajectory these are two\",\n        \"start\": 0.599,\n        \"duration\": 6.121\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"different concepts and when you\'re doing\",\n        \"start\": 2.919,\n        \"duration\": 4.961\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"a\",\n        \"start\": 6.72,\n        \"duration\": 4.959\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"jump I see that the direction will be\",\n        \"start\": 7.88,\n        \"duration\": 5.839\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"towards the target okay so I\'m going\",\n        \"start\": 11.679,\