at.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730718025,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"When tackling maximum distance workouts, one key area to focus on is stabilizing your spine and lower back. This is particularly important when you\'re pushing yourself to the limits, as a stable core prevents unnecessary movement that might waste energy or lead to injury. An effective technique some athletes use is breath control, which can provide the necessary support and stability for your body during intense exercises.nnThe concept is simple: when you take a deep breath, your lungs and chest expand, creating internal pressure that compresses your lower back and stabilizes it against excessive movement. This action is akin to what you see in powerlifting, where weightlifters hold their breath at crucial moments to maintain a strong core and prevent flexing. As you prepare to perform a lift or jump, aligning your breath with the movement can enhance your stability. So, as you get set, take a deep breath, and when you reach the peak of your jump or the final push of your lift, exhale fully. This method not only supports your back but also helps you maintain posture and generate power.nnIncorporating breath control into your workout routine can be a game changer for your performance and safety. By adding this simple step, you\'ll create a more structured approach to your movements, allowing you to tackle max distance tasks with confidence. Remember, the blend of proper body mechanics and breathwork will go a long way in ensuring your spine remains stable and your energy is efficiently directed.nnHighlights:n- Breath control can stabilize your lower back during intense workouts.n- A deep breath compresses the lower back, preventing unwanted movement.n- Coordinating breathing with your movements enhances performance and safety.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 623,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 327,\n            \"total_tokens\": 950,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"T0mKoeGazQ4qMegZrd7E9K6t_3j_mbZQexFxlsvWCxM-1730718032-1.0.1.1-VpUwyVSWiLZX2pPcE194aeLpb.Qh.WsqwAohPmjaVuhUHhxS1ohuLkUgEe7eJmtMhdLVcxYSeCsNUumawZBSOQ\",\n            \"expires\": 1730719832,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"SJqoYIDBBSn1bxu4JfKZ2.8CbU.bJdmRFvHLC0WyMrE-1730718032604-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(74826,55845,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(74827,55845,'video_transcript_chatgpt','When tackling maximum distance workouts, one key area to focus on is stabilizing your spine and lower back. This is particularly important when you\'re pushing yourself to the limits, as a stable core prevents unnecessary movement that might waste energy or lead to injury. An effective technique some athletes use is breath control, which can provide the necessary support and stability for your body during intense exercises.\n\nThe concept is simple: when you take a deep breath, your lungs and chest expand, creating internal pressure that compresses your lower back and stabilizes it against excessive movement. This action is akin to what you see in powerlifting, where weightlifters hold their breath at crucial moments to maintain a strong core and prevent flexing. As you prepare to perform a lift or jump, aligning your breath with the movement can enhance your stability. So, as you get set, take a deep breath, and when you reach the peak of your jump or the final push of your lift, exhale fully. This method not only supports your back but also helps you maintain posture and generate power.\n\nIncorporating breath control into your workout routine can be a game changer for your performance and safety. By adding this simple step, you\'ll create a more structured approach to your movements, allowing you to tackle max distance tasks with confidence. Remember, the blend of proper body mechanics and breathwork will go a long way in ensuring your spine remains stable and your energy is efficiently directed.\n\nHighlights:\n- Breath control can stabilize your lower back during intense workouts.\n- A deep breath compresses the lower back, preventing unwanted movement.\n- Coordinating breathing with your movements enhances performance and safety.'),(74828,55845,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(74829,55845,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(74830,55845,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(74831,55845,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(74832,55845,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(74833,55846,'_thumbnail_id','14439'),(74834,55846,'video_id','x2vveClVo1Q'),(74835,55846,'video_type','tutorial'),(74836,55846,'order_position','10'),(74837,55846,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\