ny flexion of the back when jumping of landing. A strong torso and \'core\' will help to protect any impact to the discs and causing issues.nnThere should be INTERNAL TORQUE (IT) on your body to maximise support of the body and landings. This means : Hamstrings, External Obliques, Teres Major (mid back), Front Deltoid and Pec.n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Jumping}}{{Series:Landing Tips}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PrU1U9BgzvE/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PrU1U9BgzvE/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PrU1U9BgzvE/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PrU1U9BgzvE/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/PrU1U9BgzvE/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTPduVso9pgecvVBsZePiAaP\",\"position\":6,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"PrU1U9BgzvE\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":6}'),(75486,55871,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Landings'),(75487,55871,'authId','Auth1'),(75488,55871,'searchId','Tutorials - Landings'),(75489,55871,'filterId','none'),(75490,55871,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(75491,55871,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Landings'),(75492,55871,'_edit_last','1'),(75493,55871,'video_transcript_chatgpt','When it comes to proper movement mechanics, especially if you\'re looking to build strength or prevent injury, understanding how to engage your core is essential. This tutorial will focus on how to maintain the right body positioning during exercises, specifically emphasizing the importance of activating your core and external obliques. This approach ensures that your lower back is supported and minimizes the risk of strain while allowing for optimal performance.\n\nStart by positioning yourself so that your hips are pushed back, ensuring that your heels are elevated rather than flat on the ground. As you get into this stance, actively engage your abdominal muscles, particularly your external obliques. It\'s crucial to resist the urge to let your knees drive forward, which can often lead to unnecessary stress on your knees and lower back. Instead, consciously work to keep your knees in line with your toes while focusing on contracting your obliques. If it\'s challenging initially, that’s perfectly normal! With practice, you\'ll improve your control and stability.\n\nIn summary, prioritizing hip positioning and engaging your core will make a significant difference in your workout routine. By continuously pushing your hips back and activating your external obliques, you not only support your lower back but also enhance the effectiveness of your exercises. Remember, proper form leads to better results and reduced risk of injury, allowing you to stay active and healthy.\n\nHighlights:  \n1. Push your hips back and keep your heels elevated to maintain proper positioning.  \n2. Actively engage your external obliques to support your lower back and avoid strain.  \n3. Focus on keeping your knees in line with your toes to ensure safe and effective movement.'),(75494,55871,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(75495,55871,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"hey guys so as I said in the previous\",\n        \"start\": 4.64,\n        \"duration\": 4.959\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"video the idea being that we\'re going to\",\n        \"start\": 7.29,\n        \"duration\": 3.9\n    },\n    {\n        \"