eoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":13}'),(75654,55878,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Landings'),(75655,55878,'authId','Auth1'),(75656,55878,'searchId','Tutorials - Landings'),(75657,55878,'filterId','none'),(75658,55878,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(75659,55878,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Landings'),(75660,55878,'_edit_last','1'),(75661,55878,'video_transcript_chatgpt','If you\'re looking for a fun yet challenging way to engage your leg muscles and improve your stability, trying the chair position against a wall might just be the perfect fit. This exercise not only helps to strengthen your lower body but also teaches you how to balance more effectively, which can be useful in various activities. By utilizing the wall for support, you can focus on form and engagement without worrying too much about losing your balance.\n\nTo get started, find a wall and come into a chair position with your thighs parallel to the ground and your shins perpendicular to it. Your knees should be at a 90-degree angle, and your heels will be doing most of the work initially. To enhance the effectiveness of the exercise, contract your lower back and place your hands on the wall for added stability. As you maintain this position, shift your weight onto the balls of your feet while ensuring that your knees remain at that 90-degree angle. This shift engages the muscles throughout your lower body and helps position you for a strong landing when you push back from the chair stance.\n\nIn conclusion, practicing the chair position against the wall is a great way to strengthen your legs and improve overall body awareness. Whether you use it as part of your workout routine or just for a bit of fun, you\'re working on crucial muscle engagement and balance skills. So next time you\'re looking for a quick exercise challenge, remember this position and give it a try—your legs will thank you!\n\nHighlights:\n- Use a wall for support to master the chair position more easily.\n- Focus on shifting your weight onto the balls of your feet to engage your muscles.\n- Keeping knees at a 90-degree angle helps with stability and effective muscle activation.'),(75662,55878,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(75663,55878,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"okay here\'s a fun one but I know that\",\n        \"start\": 4.04,\n        \"duration\": 5.92\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"you\'ll probably hate butt chair position\",\n        \"start\": 6.839,\n        \"duration\": 6.18\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"against the wall so if we come into the\",\n        \"start\": 9.96,\n        \"duration\": 7.649\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"chair position so thighs are going to be\",\n        \"start\": 13.019,\n        \"duration\": 7.141\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"parallel to the ground shins are\",\n        \"start\": 17.609,\n        \"duration\": 4.861\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"parallel to the wall we\'re going at 90\",\n        \"start\": 20.16,\n        \"duration\": 4.35\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"degrees from here you\'re probably going\",\n        \"start\": 22.47,\n        \"duration\": 4.049\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"to feel that your heels are doing the\",\n        \"start\": 24.51,\n        \"duration\": 5.099\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"work what I want you to do is contract\",\n        \"start\": 26.519,\n        \"duration\": 5.85\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"your lower back you can put your hands\",\n        \"start\": 29.609,\n        \"duration\": 3.54\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"on the wall\",\n        \"start\": 32.369,\n        \"duration\": 3.001\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"keep your feet where they are and you\'re\",\n        \"start\": 33.149,\n        \"duration\": 4.59\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"gonna push your weight trying to keep\",\n 