 it, rather than straight at it. Think of an arch up, over and down.nnFor help with this, you can put a small obstacle between yourself and the landing to force you to jump higher, over the obstacle, then onto the landing.nnThe straight-at-it technique is one I see a lot with new students because they\'re not used to the mechanics and technique of jumping. So start off small and gradually build up, keeping quality of landing in mind.nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Jumping}}{{Series:Landing Tips}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/oyPNPLJKQ5U/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/oyPNPLJKQ5U/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/oyPNPLJKQ5U/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/oyPNPLJKQ5U/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/oyPNPLJKQ5U/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTPduVso9pgecvVBsZePiAaP\",\"position\":17,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"oyPNPLJKQ5U\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":17}'),(75750,55882,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Landings'),(75751,55882,'authId','Auth1'),(75752,55882,'searchId','Tutorials - Landings'),(75753,55882,'filterId','none'),(75754,55882,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(75755,55882,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Landings'),(75756,55882,'_edit_last','1'),(75757,55882,'video_transcript_chatgpt','Understanding trajectory is key to successfully navigating obstacles, whether you’re in a parkour class or a challenging outdoor park. Many people make the mistake of approaching obstacles too directly, resulting in an awkward hit that can lead to falls or clipped feet. By adjusting your approach and focusing on how you leap and land, you can increase your chances of a clean obstacle crossing and make your movements feel more fluid.\n\nInstead of charging straight at the obstacle, think about your jump as a combination of rising and descending. Instead of merely approaching the obstacle, emphasize your ascent by leaping up and over it rather than just skimming its surface. This upward motion allows you to create the space you need to land safely without risking a stumble or injury on the way down. When you focus on coming down onto the obstacle, rather than just hitting it at an angle, you give yourself the chance to land more securely in a balanced position, ensuring you don\'t clip your feet or lose your momentum.\n\nA successful approach to tackling obstacles comes down to your trajectory. By consciously thinking about jumping high and landing smoothly, you can enhance your performance and enjoyment. The key takeaway is to focus on the upward movement followed by a downward landing, which helps avoid missteps and keeps you in control. With practice, you’ll find that a well-executed trajectory not only looks impressive, but it also enhances your overall safety and confidence.\n\n- Approach obstacles by emphasizing a high jump before landing.\n- Focus on coming down onto the obstacle, rather than hitting it at an angle.\n- A smooth trajectory enhances your control and reduces the risk of clipping your feet.'),(75758,55882,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(75759,55882,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"okay so let\'s have a look at trajectory\",\n        \"start\": 8.179,\n        \"duration\": 7.061\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"so sometimes I see people going straight\",\n       