tart they tend to drive the foot up towards the foot placement, barely missing the front edge by millimeters. With lower motor-control ability, the risk of clipping the foot is higher, so to mitigate this, the foot needs to raise over and above the wall, then return down to it.nnThis also allows us more time to control the positioning of the foot and ensure we\'re secure before vaulting.n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Series:Step Vault Tips}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bUw-cYzKaHk/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bUw-cYzKaHk/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bUw-cYzKaHk/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bUw-cYzKaHk/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bUw-cYzKaHk/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMlXqDokEh3bNO1BW9LroT7\",\"position\":5,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"bUw-cYzKaHk\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":5}'),(75894,55888,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Quickstart Step Vaults'),(75895,55888,'authId','Auth1'),(75896,55888,'searchId','Tutorials - Quickstart Step Vaults'),(75897,55888,'filterId','none'),(75898,55888,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(75899,55888,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Step Vault'),(75900,55888,'_edit_last','1'),(75901,55888,'video_transcript_chatgpt','When practicing a step vault, it\'s common to make mistakes that can hinder your progress and increase the risk of injury. One frequent error is positioning your foot too directly at the box, which can lead to tripping or not clearing the surface properly. This tutorial will guide you through the correct technique to ensure a smoother, safer vaulting experience. \n\nThe key to mastering the step vault lies in your foot placement and the trajectory of your movement. Instead of bringing your foot straight up to the box, aim to lift it higher than the intended surface. Start by placing your hands on the box and focusing on driving your knee upward, creating a powerful yet controlled motion. Once your foot is at a sufficient height, allow it to come down gently onto the box. This technique minimizes the chances of tripping and helps you transition into a step-through movement more effortlessly. As you gain confidence with this approach, you can gradually adjust to a more straightforward motion toward the box. \n\nIn summary, practicing the step vault with an emphasis on height and controlled foot placement can dramatically improve both your technique and safety. Remember, the goal is to clear the obstacle before landing on it, which can help you develop a smoother, more confident approach. Embrace the learning process, take your time, and soon you\'ll be vaulting with ease and precision.\n\n- Focus on lifting your foot higher than the box to prevent tripping. \n- Use hand placements for balance and guidance during your vault. \n- Practice consistently to build confidence and improve your technique.'),(75902,55888,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(75903,55888,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"all right so this is fault number two\",\n        \"start\": 0,\n        \"duration\": 5.79\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"sometimes I see people trying to get\",\n        \"start\": 3.29,\n        \"duration\": 4.93\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"onto the step vault and this is to do\",\n        \"start\"