e ground first\n\n15\n00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:51,000\nokay so when you\'re on the bar good\n\n16\n00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:54,000\nposition good and now I shift my weight\n\n17\n00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000\nover okay what you don\'t want to do is\n\n18\n00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000\nwhen you\'re on here you\'re just going to\n\n19\n00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000\nstep\n\n'),(76264,55903,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(76265,55903,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-APp8IYcujMzOGhmDe0GceEUe64gTC\",\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730718118,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"Walking, especially on a narrow surface like a balance beam or a log, can be a fun yet challenging skill to master. It requires not only physical balance but also mental focus to ensure your steps are both stable and confident. The key is to utilize your body’s natural balance by engaging your limbs and shifting your weight strategically. This tutorial will guide you through the essential steps needed to walk effectively and safely on a narrow surface.nnTo begin, position one foot firmly on the balance beam while keeping your other foot lifted and ready to move. As you prepare to step, avoid the temptation to simply shift your weight entirely onto the forward foot. Instead, extend that foot in front of you slightly, ensuring it’s placed in a secure and stable position. Take a moment to feel how the footing beneath you reacts, ensuring it’s not slippery or unstable before fully transferring your weight onto it. This cautious approach allows you to maintain your balance, thereby minimizing the risk of falling. Remember, it’s all about testing the ground first, so your movements build on a solid foundation.nnIn summary, walking on a narrow surface is about finding balance through careful weight distribution and deliberate foot placement. By placing one foot confidently before shifting your weight and testing the stability of each step, you can make significant progress towards mastering this skill. Over time, with practice, you’ll find walking on a beam or similar surface becomes a seamless and enjoyable experience.nn- Focus on placing one foot firmly on the surface before shifting your weight.n- Test the stability of the ground with the extended foot before full weight transfer.n- Practice regularly to build confidence and improve your balance skills.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 355,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 327,\n            \"total_tokens\": 682,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"8cxWqIZCQQ6ei_KP1PbXi1FuPRO8gLn3TOXtjGH79zQ-1730718122-1.0.1.1-6L9gARdRw4eVn8BTODA8KDlSq5rCKYKoFEIc.K.tdgEnTpHE0tWyhvW2ftORoJmtb5zMQZdzGt4yYvdPupXwEA\",\n            \"expires\": 1730719922,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"5wfsBhJMyP1wrhHghJvl5I93X7GleTrvFGyCO5LD1V4-1730718122452-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76266,55903,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76267,55903,'video_transcript_chatgpt','Walking, especially on a narrow surface like a balance beam or a log, can be a fun yet challenging skill to master. It requires not only physical balance but also mental focus to ensure your steps are both stable and confident. The key is to utilize your body’s natural balance by engaging your limbs and shifting your weight strategically. This tutorial will guide you through the essential steps needed to walk effectively and safely on a narrow surface.\n\nTo begin, position one foot firmly on the balance beam while keeping your other foot lifted and ready to move. As you prepare to step, avoid the temptation to simply shift your weight entirely onto the forward foot. Instead, extend that foot in front of you slightly, ensuring it’s placed in a secure and stable position. Take a moment to feel how the footing beneath you reacts, ensuring it’s not slippery or unstable before fully transferring your weight onto it. This cautious approach allows you to maintain your balance, thereby minimizing the risk of falling. Remember, it’s all about testing the ground first, so your movements build on a solid foundation.\n\nIn summary, walking on a narrow surface is about finding balance through careful weight distribution and deliberate foot placement. By placing one foot confidently before shifting your weight and testing the stability of each step, you can make significant progress towards mastering this skill. Over time, with practice, you’ll find walking on a beam or similar surface becomes a seamless and enjoyable experience.\n\n- Focus on placing one foot firmly on the surface before shifting your weight.\n- Test the stability of the ground with the extended foot before full weight transfer.\n- Practice regularly to build confidence and improve your balance skills.'),(76268,55903,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76269,55903,'_edit_lock','1730717500:1'),(76270,55903,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76271,55903,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76272,55903,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76273,55903,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76274,55904,'_thumbnail_id','14178'),(76275,55904,'video_id','V7LGkLqR2CE'),(76276,55904,'video_type','tutorial'),(76277,55904,'order_position','0'),(76278,55904,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"x3ez0wFmafl-4gmYW1thrfZP2wI\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC5BRjJDODk5REM0NjkzMUIy\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #01 - Introduction Parkour Tutorial. [076]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Beginner) in Parkour - Introduction.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #01nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnPulling Position.nnThe best position to do this exercise would be standing since it would closely replicate how you would be on a bar pullup. However, this would require the band to be tied up very high, so using your knees might be the only option.nnIf you are using your knees, try to still engage your abs and squeeze your glutes to align your hips correctly. Essentially, we want to be as close to the bodyweight pull-up position as we can possibly be.nnn### Scaling the difficulty up or down.nnThere are a few different ways (that I have used in the past) to adjust the difficulty of this exercise.nnYou can shorten or lengthen the band by wrapping it over the pull-up bar or the bar you are pulling on more or fewer times.nChanging the thickness of the band by using a different one.nUse multiple bands at the same time to increase the resistance.nA combination of all of the above.nnn### Easier at the top, harder at the bottom.nnJust because the way the band works, you will always find the beginning part of the pull easier than at the end, with a gradual increase.nnThis is not the exact way that a bodyweight pull-up works since that will be usually harder at the start and end, with the middle section being a little easier. The reason for this is because the middle section is the part your arms would have had the most use and will be at their strongest.nnn### The accuracy of resistance.nIf you wish to have a much more accurate reading of the amount of tension (and stress on your body) that the bands are giving you, then a simple hooked weighing scale can come in useful.nnYou can hook the scale onto the band and pull it down to the lowest point. Record the amount of weight that you are pulling as a way to gauge your progress.nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/V7LGkLqR2CE/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/V7LGkLqR2CE/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/V7LGkLqR2CE/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/V7LGkLqR2CE/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/V7LGkLqR2CE/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\