, ensure you’re in the proper starting position: grab the pull-up bar with your hands shoulder-width apart and begin in a dead hang. From this relaxed position, engage your muscles and pull yourself up, making sure to use your full range of motion. It\'s important to go beyond just getting your chin over the bar; the pull-up concludes when your arms are fully engaged, typically when they are parallel to the ground. As you practice, you can adjust the difficulty by varying the number of repetitions and sets, tailoring your workout to focus on strength, endurance, or muscle building. Remember, while the pull-up effectively works your upper body, it doesn’t fully engage your entire body, so consider incorporating additional exercises for a well-rounded fitness routine.nnIn summary, the bodyweight pull-up is a fantastic exercise for building strength and improving functionality, provided you focus on executing it correctly. Pay attention to your form and keep working on your range of motion; these elements are crucial for maximizing your training benefits. As you make progress, you\'ll find that you can adapt the pull-up to fit different training goals, making it a versatile addition to your workout regimen. nn- Start with a dead hang and engage your muscles to pull yourself up, ensuring full range of motion.n- Adjust the number of reps and sets to match your fitness goals, whether it\'s strength training, endurance, or muscle gain. n- Always focus on maintaining proper form to maximize effectiveness and prevent injury.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1471,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 379,\n            \"total_tokens\": 1850,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"9V4mTJFHb5BnSsvYoc91P3L0HWFs7d4LWUDczh4wQ18-1730722265-1.0.1.1-ODV9AseAbfPWWeiJYGO7Hql12ob89t.8BQ4C6Kh7wf0FFlJfkd1k70hng3vDpYLcyUzZCtO81xr4P2VIU0Vcsw\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724065,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"u40WbNv0WjxEWf5uLbh9ZFRiNWltCUO4vvCLUsNDldA-1730722265301-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76523,55912,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76524,55912,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76525,55912,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76526,55912,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76527,55912,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76528,55913,'_thumbnail_id','14196'),(76529,55913,'video_id','baJEiH9PABM'),(76530,55913,'video_type','tutorial'),(76531,55913,'order_position','9'),(76532,55913,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"O0onOFnmUGUcZsFDXRkwaNXi1vg\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC5DMkU4NTY1QUFGQTYwMDE3\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #10 - Intermediate 2 Parkour Tutorial. [085]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Intermediate) in Parkour - Intermediate 2.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #10nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnIt\'s a simple concept, do the same exercise but slower. A hugely underrated technique, it allows you to add another way of making the pull-up harder.nnRequiring a more strength to do the pull since the amount of power you can use is reduced because the speed is much slower.nnn### Changing TimenThere are two things happening when you make things slower. The first is fairly obvious to understand. You\'re taking longer to pull, so more energy is needed to do the work. This makes it harder and harder over time.nnFor the second, you need to know a very rough, basic, but simple formula...nnPower = Strength + Speed.nnYou can be more powerful by getting stronger, becoming faster, or a combination of the two...nnTherefore, if you have to go at a slower speed (because you have to take longer to do the pull), the amount of strength required to have the same amount of power will have to be more to compensate...nnAgain, this makes things harder for you.nnn### Focus on the techniquenWe can now put our technique under the microscope. By slowing everything down you\'ll be able to see exactly what parts you are struggling with and what to train more on.nnI highly recommend filming yourself too. Going slow and filming will allow you to analyse exactly how you are performing.nnn### ControlnYour brain sends signals to your muscles. Those messages to \'PULLLLLLL\' will get weaker over time as you get tired.nnThe pathways that those signals get sent down are called the neuro-muscular pathways and they can get fatigued as well. The more it\'s continuously used, the weaker the signal will become.nnSending that signal continuously will require a lot more control over your body and mind because of this.  You must be sending the right signal to the right muscles over a prolonged period... Which is harder. But the practice gives more reinforcement of the message.nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:intermediate}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/baJEiH9PABM/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/baJEiH9PABM/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/baJEiH9PABM/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/baJEiH9PABM/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/baJEiH9PABM/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":9,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"baJEiH9PABM\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":9}'),(76533,55913,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(76534,55913,'authId','Auth1'),(76535,55913,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(76536,55913,'filterId','none'),(76537,55913,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(76538,55913,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(76539,55913,'_edit_last','1'),(76540,55913,'video_transcript_chatgpt','If you\'re comfortable with body weight pull-ups and looking to challenge yourself further, slowing down your pull-up can be an excellent next step. This technique not only increases the intensity of your workout but also allows you to focus on your mechanics and form, revealing areas of strength and weakness in your pull-up. By extending the duration of each rep, you can optimize your training and track your progress effectively.\n\nTo practice slower-speed pull-ups, begin by finding a pull-up bar and assume a dead hang position. Instead of pulling yourself up as quickly as possible, aim to complete the movement over two to three seconds. From the bottom position, pull yourself up, taking that time to reach the top before returning to the starting position. This deliberate slowdown emphasizes control and muscle engagement, enabling you to identify which parts of the pull-up are most challenging—usually the beginning and end of the movement. Adjust the tempo according to your ability; starting with a shorter time can help you progressively increase the difficulty as you build strength and confidence.\n\nIn summary, using time as a variable in your pull-up workouts is a simple yet effective way to enhance your training. Slower pull-ups cultivate better control and strength, helping you to pinpoint your weaker areas for targeted improvement. As you continue to refine your form and increase the difficulty, you\'ll become more adept not just at pull-ups but also at adjusting your training style to suit different fitness goals. \n\n- Slowing down your pull-ups helps improve control and strength.\n- Start with a manageable tempo and gradually increase the time to enhance difficulty.\n- Use this technique to identify and develop your weaker points in pull-up mechanics.'),(76541,55913,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76542,55913,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"so the second option for intermediates\",\n        \"start\": 0.06,\n        \"duration\": 4.86\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"we\'re going to be looking at how we can\",\n        \"start\": 2.58,\n        \"duration\": 5.069\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"make this slightly harder by increasing\",\n        \"start\": 4.92,\n        \"duration\": 4.889\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"the amount of time it takes for you to\",\n      