djusted based on your fitness level by changing the number of repetitions, sets, pause points along the pull-up path, and the length of each pause. For example, if you’re just beginning, try doing two pull-ups with a two-second hold at two points. As you progress, move on to more repetitions, longer holds, or more pause points to increase difficulty. Additionally, consider recording yourself while performing the exercise—this can help you identify your weaker areas and monitor your form throughout.nnTo wrap up, incorporating isometric pauses into your pull-up routine can dramatically enhance your strength and control, particularly in the more challenging segments of the movement. While this technique focuses more on muscular endurance and less on pure strength or speed, it is invaluable for building a well-rounded pulling ability. By adjusting the variables outlined, you can continuously challenge yourself and avoid plateaus in your training.nnHighlights:n- Incorporate isometric holds at various points during your pull-up to build strength and endurance.n- Adjust the difficulty by modifying repetitions, sets, pause points, and hold durations.n- Record your performance to identify weaker areas and improve your technique.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1106,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 363,\n            \"total_tokens\": 1469,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"mxUHAFzArvNI0CXILkkkXhynJkxqhBINIvnOwOR59LU-1730722278-1.0.1.1-xRBBtHsdQOR9ve.jq_VYRnT_qMffuLbpXhkJ4Gh12mnLdWTxXzlRiiYjkx0QAOEorBGxGgihNHD8RYUobjzwig\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724078,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"8H4Mlm4Lu4R9elZ9CML2w727BF6t1x_od8KrM.13EBs-1730722278460-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76571,55914,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76572,55914,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76573,55914,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76574,55914,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76575,55914,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76576,55915,'_thumbnail_id','14200'),(76577,55915,'video_id','QxH6PXb3kIA'),(76578,55915,'video_type','tutorial'),(76579,55915,'order_position','11'),(76580,55915,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"tKodY1HcQKu3BJxU4gc7RhdNcbQ\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC5DQ0MyQ0Y4Mzg0M0VGOEYw\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #12 - Intermediate 4 Parkour Tutorial. [087]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Intermediate) in Parkour - Intermediate 4.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #12nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnThe full range of motion, regardless of what you are doing, is about going to the limit of what your body was designed to do.nnThis episode will look at what that means on the pull-up and why it\'s important.nnn### Full Range on a PullupnThe key here is that we are trying to do pulling. That\'s simple enough to understand. However, when it comes to the pull-up, I mostly see people stopping their pull once they get to their neck.nnThe trouble with this is that you could actually carry on pulling if you had the power, it\'s just that from the neck onwards, this is where most will be weakest and therefore miss it.nnLet\'s get out of that habit. Yes, it\'s harder, but ultimately it will be much more useful when transformed into a muscle up.nnn### Bar is in the way.nBecause everyone\'s ribcage sticks out from their body it will become harder to navigate around the bar. Using a little momentum we can generate more power to curve around the bar and get higher.nnObviously, using momentum and speed means that it\'s taking away from strength slightly, but to do this slowly, and all strength-based is very, very difficult. Something beyond the intermediate level of technical ability.nnn### KippingnThis is a term used to describe the \'bounce\' at the bottom of the pull-up to help do the next rep.nnEssentially, you use the elastic properties of your muscle and tendons to stretch to their limits with speed from the drop of the pull-up and let them \'ping\' you back like an elastic band.nnI\'m sure you can figure out the problem here... Swinging your body weight into the absolute limits of your muscle length and tendon length means that they are under tremendous stress and stretching. Which will very often lead to muscle strains, tears, tendon damage or worse.nnDon\'t do it. It\'s not helpful, not worth it, not functional and leads to injury.n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:intermediate}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/QxH6PXb3kIA/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/QxH6PXb3kIA/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/QxH6PXb3kIA/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/QxH6PXb3kIA/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/QxH6PXb3kIA/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":11,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"QxH6PXb3kIA\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":11}'),(76581,55915,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(76582,55915,'authId','Auth1'),(76583,55915,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(76584,55915,'filterId','none'),(76585,55915,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(76586,55915,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(76587,55915,'_edit_last','1'),(76588,55915,'video_transcript_chatgpt','Pull-ups are a fundamental exercise for building upper body strength, but many people struggle to achieve the full range of motion required for optimal effectiveness. This tutorial addresses an advanced technique to help you master true full range pull-ups, emphasizing the importance of effectively engaging your muscles from the bottom of the movement to a higher point where your forearms become parallel to the ground. By making this transition, you’ll not only enhance your pulling strength but also prepare yourself for more complex movements like muscle-ups and climb-ups.\n\nTo start, focus on how to pull yourself higher by incorporating speed and a slight curve around the bar. Begin from a dead hang position and use a bit of momentum to propel your chest forward, rounding your back to lift yourself up. Instead of stopping your upward motion when your chin passes the bar, aim to lift your chest until your forearms reach parallel to the ground. This requires practicing the full range of motion, making sure that you’re not just relying on your sheer strength. Be cautious about kipping, as it can lead to poor habits by using elasticity instead of muscle strength. Keep your form clean and focus on engaging your muscles throughout the entire movement, from the dead hang to the top.\n\nRemember, mastering this technique is a journey and might feel challenging at first, especially if you\'re used to only doing partial pull-ups. However, the benefits of developing a true full range of motion will become evident as you progress, leading to greater power and speed in your pull-ups. Establishing a solid foundation now will pave the way for more advanced movements later on, solidifying your overall functional fitness. So, be patient and practice consistently, and you’ll see marked improvements in no time!\n\nHighlights:\n- Focus on achieving a full range of motion by pulling up until your forearms are parallel to the ground.\n- Incorporate slight momentum and a rounding motion to lift your chest higher rather than just relying on chin-ups.\n- Practice consistently and avoid kipping to ensure you\'re building strength effectively for future more complex movements.'),(76589,55915,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76590,55915,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"welcome to intermediate number four with\",\n        \"start\": 0.089,\n        \"duration\": 4.77\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"this particular option what we\'re going\",\n        \"start\": 2.82,\n        \"duration\": 4.38\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"to be looking at is changing the\",\n        \"start\": 4.859,\n        \"duration\": 4.8