 us struggle with grip fatigue and transition between movements, understanding the mechanics behind the false grip is essential for building the strength and technique needed for more complex exercises.nnTo get started, approach the bar and instead of a traditional grip, extend your hands so that your fingers and wrists create a welcoming overhang over the bar. Your fingers should bend backward as you position yourself. In this grip, your wrists take on the burden of your body weight—think of it as if you\'re climbing over a flat surface, with your hands supporting your weight at the pivot point of your wrists, instead of only your fingers. When performing pull-ups in the false grip, maintain a dead hang and avoid bouncing or kipping; control is key to mastering this grip. As you pull up, remember to keep your wrists engaged, as this will not only create a stronger pull but also prepare your body for those muscle up transitions later on.nnMastering the false grip can be challenging at first, and it\'s normal for the position to feel a little uncomfortable as you train your body to adapt. You may experience some soreness since the muscles aren\'t used to this wrist positioning. Nevertheless, it’s important to differentiate between good discomfort from exertion and potential injury. Ultimately, incorporating the false grip into your training will bolster your pull-up ability and lay the groundwork for more advanced gymnastic maneuvers. As you develop your skill in this grip, you’ll find it improves functionality in various movements, taking your training to new heights.nn- To engage the false grip effectively, focus on positioning the wrists as the primary support rather than relying solely on your fingers.n- Control your movements to avoid unnecessary bouncing; aim for a steady, measured pace for each repetition.n- Embrace the initial discomfort as part of the learning process, and stay mindful of your form to avoid any injuries.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1749,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 417,\n            \"total_tokens\": 2166,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"6hAwt0RiGfL5rMvdsnvLM_blpaNvrrcmkgg4WquE4q4-1730721623-1.0.1.1-ITKUnD60GpvRQX7drp8rLavy7QjdwYVNy2qJmVY_v4Alyl8podw8B6Nu0.SX2QOPPDrW3C_itEVQTDDrHaSqrw\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723423,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"TV14tDeKHdq0M8ULd13Xw1ufKOmiMIqz0lSurSzJb_o-1730721623137-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76643,55917,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76644,55917,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76645,55917,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76646,55917,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76647,55917,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76648,55918,'_thumbnail_id','14206'),(76649,55918,'video_id','mU003YBiGkk'),(76650,55918,'video_type','tutorial'),(76651,55918,'order_position','14'),(76652,55918,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"1hx1bWmLQlQ7otSEQAYewJDyjnk\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC45NzUwQkI1M0UxNThBMkU0\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #15 - Advanced 3 Parkour Tutorial. [090]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Advanced) in Parkour - Advanced 3.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #15nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnHow do we correct a strength difference in the left or right arm to be more equal? And how do we add more variation to your strength training at the more difficult end of the scale?nnWell, that\'s where the legendary one-arm pull-up comes in.nnn### Belts and Rope and Rings and Assistance BandsnSomething I failed to mention in the video is that you don\'t just have to use an assistance band. Actually, many people use a heavy-duty leather belt, gymnastic rings, a towel or thick rope.nnThe advantage of the belt, towel, rope, etc is that it doesn\'t stretch and you can more accurately determine your progress.nnn### Weakest sidenSimply, don\'t do more work on your stronger arm.nnWhy? Well, you\'ll never get both arms balanced to be equally as strong otherwise. You want your weaker side to catch up to the stronger, but if your stronger side keeps getting even stronger, it\'ll never catch up.nnn### Making it hardernBy moving your hand down the band/rope/belt/towel you are going to increase the difficulty of the exercise.nnYou may want to make a pen mark on what your current hand position is, so that your next session will return to the same spot or lower.nnn### Grip strengthnGrrrrrrrr! Crushing your enemies with your bare hands. Well, by doing the one-arm pull-up you will be increasing your grip strength too.nnGrip strength is absolutely essential for many parkour, climbing, bouldering, movement exercises and techniques. Want to be able to hold onto that wall for longer? Well, guess what will probably fail first?n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:advanced}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/mU003YBiGkk/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/mU003YBiGkk/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/mU003YBiGkk/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/mU003YBiGkk/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/mU003YBiGkk/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":14,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"mU003YBiGkk\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":14}'),(76653,55918,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(76654,55918,'authId','Auth1'),(76655,55918,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(76656,55918,'filterId','none'),(76657,55918,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(76658,55918,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(76659,55918,'_edit_last','1'),(76660,55918,'video_transcript_chatgpt','If you\'re looking to take your pull-up game to the next level, the one-arm pull-up is an impressive and challenging exercise to master. This tutorial will guide you through the process of performing one-arm pull-ups using a resistance band for assistance. Ideal for those aiming to balance strength between both sides of the body, this method allows you to progressively build the strength necessary for a true one-arm pull-up while also improving your grip strength.\n\nTo start, you\'ll need to position one hand on the pull-up bar and the other hand on a resistance band. The height of the band determines the difficulty: gripping it higher makes the exercise easier. Begin with your weaker arm—if your left arm is weaker, grab the bar with your left hand and the band at the top. Lower into a dead hang, then pull yourself up as high as you can. Repeat this for your stronger side, ensuring that both hands are at the same height on the band, which prevents creating a bigger strength imbalance. To progress, you can gradually lower the band’s position making each subsequent pull-up more challenging until you can perform a one-arm pull-up without assistance.\n\nIn summary, the one-arm pull-up is a fantastic way to enhance your upper body strength while also working on grip strength. By consistently challenging your weaker side, you’ll promote symmetry in your muscle development. Remember to track your progress by marking the band at your hand positions—I recommend using lines or knots. This not only helps you visualize your improvement but ensures that you are gradually working towards a true one-arm pull-up. With patience and persistence, you\'ll find yourself moving closer to this remarkable feat.\n\n- Start with the band height that makes the exercise manageable and switch to a lower height as you gain strength.\n- Always match the starting hand position of your dominant side with your weaker side to avoid strength imbalances.\n- Record your progress with marks on the band to visually measure improvements in strength and technique.'),(76661,55918,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76662,55918,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"welcome to advanced option number three\",\n        \"start\": 0.12,\n        \"duration\": 5.01\n    },\n    