2,000 --> 00:01:137,000\nharder if again you can do this with the\n\n71\n00:00:195,000 --> 00:01:139,000\ngreen band you just put on the blue band\n\n72\n00:00:198,000 --> 00:01:142,000\nand then once you can do that you go to\n\n73\n00:00:199,000 --> 00:01:143,000\nthe red band so eventually we\'re trying\n\n74\n00:00:202,000 --> 00:01:146,000\nto get to the point where we don\'t need\n\n75\n00:00:204,000 --> 00:01:149,000\nany of the bands and we can do a true\n\n76\n00:00:206,000 --> 00:01:152,000\none-arm pull-up without the need of any\n\n77\n00:00:209,000 --> 00:01:155,000\nother assistance so the really really\n\n78\n00:00:213,000 --> 00:01:158,000\ngreat thing about this is that it is a\n\n79\n00:00:215,000 --> 00:01:159,000\nunilateral exercise which means we can\n\n80\n00:00:218,000 --> 00:01:162,000\nhave a look at what our left and what\n\n81\n00:00:220,000 --> 00:01:165,000\nour right side is doing and it means\n\n82\n00:00:222,000 --> 00:01:167,000\nthat we can fix the weaker side and we\n\n83\n00:00:225,000 --> 00:01:170,000\ncan look at trying to make that weaker\n\n84\n00:00:228,000 --> 00:01:173,000\nside stronger to get closer to match the\n\n85\n00:00:231,000 --> 00:01:175,000\nstronger side one of the other things\n\n86\n00:00:233,000 --> 00:01:177,000\nthat we\'re doing is we\'re also going to\n\n87\n00:00:235,000 --> 00:01:179,000\nbe working our grip strength this is a\n\n88\n00:00:237,000 --> 00:01:180,000\nsmall byproduct of this type of training\n\n89\n00:00:240,000 --> 00:01:183,000\nbut you\'re going to find that you\'re\n\n90\n00:00:241,000 --> 00:01:185,000\nreally going to be gripping hard on the\n\n91\n00:00:244,000 --> 00:01:189,000\nband to really try and help yourself\n\n92\n00:00:246,000 --> 00:01:191,000\npull up so a little by-product is that\n\n93\n00:00:249,000 --> 00:01:193,000\nyour grip strength is also going to be\n\n94\n00:00:251,000 --> 00:01:196,000\nworking as well on the flip side of this\n\n95\n00:00:254,000 --> 00:01:200,000\nit\'s a little bit more tricky to\n\n96\n00:00:256,000 --> 00:01:202,000\naccurately gauge where the progress is\n\n97\n00:00:260,000 --> 00:01:204,000\nhappening in terms of where your hand is\n\n98\n00:00:262,000 --> 00:01:206,000\ngoing on the band now you can mark the\n\n99\n00:00:265,000 --> 00:01:209,000\nband you could draw lines\n\n100\n00:00:267,000 --> 00:01:212,000\nmake sure that the band is always in\n\n101\n00:00:269,000 --> 00:01:214,000\nexactly the same position always with\n\n102\n00:00:272,000 --> 00:01:217,000\nthe same knot at the top and you\'re\n\n103\n00:00:275,000 --> 00:01:219,000\nalways going to place your hand at the\n\n104\n00:00:277,000 --> 00:01:221,000\nexact same position and you know that\n\n105\n00:00:280,000 --> 00:01:224,000\nwhen you\'re progressing you\'re getting\n\n106\n00:00:281,000 --> 00:01:227,000\nstronger that your hand can go down\n\n107\n00:00:284,000 --> 00:01:230,000\nthose notches and down those lines also\n\n108\n00:00:288,000 --> 00:01:232,000\nfunctionally in the real world\n\n109\n00:00:291,000 --> 00:01:234,000\nthis is not going to be an exercise that\n\n110\n00:00:292,000 --> 00:01:237,000\nyou\'re too much and you will not really\n\n111\n00:00:295,000 --> 00:01:240,000\nfind it in parkour that much either\n\n112\n00:00:298,000 --> 00:01:242,000\ndoesn\'t mean that it shouldn\'t be done\n\n113\n00:00:300,000 --> 00:01:244,000\nit just means that it is something is a\n\n114\n00:00:302,000 --> 00:01:246,000\nlittle bit more removed to what you\n\n115\n00:00:305,000 --> 00:01:248,000\nwould normally find when you\'re doing\n\n116\n00:00:306,000 --> 00:01:250,000\nparkour and when you\'re doing actual\n\n117\n00:00:308,000 --> 00:01:254,000\nfunctional movement as I also mentioned\n\n118\n00:00:311,000 --> 00:01:257,000\nearlier it is very hard to get that\n\n119\n00:00:314,000 --> 00:01:260,000\ntop-end range of motion when you\'re\n\n120\n00:00:318,000 --> 00:01:263,000\ndoing the one-arm so trying to get into\n\n121\n00:00:321,000 --> 00:01:265,000\nthe position where your forearm is\n\n122\n00:00:323,000 --> 00:01:267,000\nstraight is it going to be very very\n\n123\n00:00:325,000 --> 00:01:269,000\ndifficult and it\'s something that you\n\n124\n00:00:328,000 --> 00:01:273,000\nwant if you can aim for it and you can\n\n125\n00:00:329,000 --> 00:01:275,000\nget to it then fantastic that is where\n\n126\n00:00:333,000 --> 00:01:278,000\nyou can you just keep going to that but\n\n127\n00:00:335,000 --> 00:01:281,000\nfor most people getting a full range\n\n128\n00:00:339,000 --> 00:01:284,000\nmotion one-arm pull-up is gonna be a\n\n129\n00:00:342,000 --> 00:01:287,000\nvery very very difficult thing to try\n\n130\n00:00:344,000 --> 00:01:288,000\nand accomplish so as you can see with\n\n131\n00:00:347,000 --> 00:01:291,000\nthe advanced option number three this is\n\n132\n00:00:349,000 --> 00:01:293,000\na great way of getting to the point\n\n133\n00:00:351,000 --> 00:01:295,000\nwhere we can iron out those\n\n134\n00:00:353,000 --> 00:01:297,000\ninconsistencies between our weak and our\n\n135\n00:00:356,000 --> 00:01:300,000\nstrong side it\'s a very scalable\n\n136\n00:00:358,000 --> 00:01:301,000\nexercise as long as we gauge and measure\n\n137\n00:00:361,000 --> 00:01:305,000\nit correctly\n\n138\n00:00:362,000 --> 00:01:307,000\nbut this is an awesome advanced option\n\n139\n00:00:365,000 --> 00:01:310,000\nfor those that want to try to get their\n\n140\n00:00:367,000 --> 00:01:309,000\nweaker side stronger\n\n'),(76665,55918,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(76666,55918,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-APq2q9doJr2pDcAUxfmQUmoCKzZuT\",\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730721624,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"If you\'re looking to take your pull-up game to the next level, the one-arm pull-up is an impressive and challenging exercise to master. This tutorial will guide you through the process of performing one-arm pull-ups using a resistance band for assistance. Ideal for those aiming to balance strength between both sides of the body, this method allows you to progressively build the strength necessary for a true one-arm pull-up while also improving your grip strength.nnTo start, you\'ll need to position one hand on the pull-up bar and the other hand on a resistance band. The height of the band determines the difficulty: gripping it higher makes the exercise easier. Begin with your weaker arm—if your left arm is weaker, grab the bar with your left hand and the band at the top. Lower into a dead hang, then pull yourself up as high as you can. Repeat this for your stronger side, ensuring that both hands are at the same height on the band, which prevents creating a bigger strength imbalance. To progress, you can gradually lower the band’s position making each subsequent pull-up more challenging until you can perform a one-arm pull-up without assistance.nnIn summary, the one-arm pull-up is a fantastic way to enhance your upper body strength while also working on grip strength. By consistently challenging your weaker side, you’ll promote symmetry in your muscle development. Remember to track your progress by marking the band at your hand positions—I recommend using lines or knots. This not only helps you visualize your improvement but ensures that you are gradually working towards a true one-arm pull-up. With patience and persistence, you\'ll find yourself moving closer to this remarkable feat.nn- Start with the band height that makes the exercise manageable and switch to a lower height as you gain strength.n- Always match the starting hand position of your dominant side with your weaker side to avoid strength imbalances.n- Record your progress with marks on the band to visually measure improvements in strength and technique.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1463,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 388,\n            \"total_tokens\": 1851,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"_JQ2KEuEA9V9_mDhdTG82vuIOCdzA2IhdJiaLWpfhd0-1730721630-1.0.1.1-Ot2LKbNpBEStr_h83DL8YGrtCd22lMQAiSm9lsOVWIvKn6yWCAnRJJtyuMHuUqbZboaJcxoM0tXhNBotZN931A\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723430,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"j6WHh3MFsQewUDBYXL1K_YgJRFGpVpoOIhjEnsw43jY-1730721630425-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76667,55918,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76668,55918,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76669,55918,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76670,55918,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76671,55918,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76672,55919,'_thumbnail_id','14208'),(76673,55919,'video_id','fMd5O1s17vU'),(76674,55919,'video_type','tutorial'),(76675,55919,'order_position','15'),(76676,55919,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"YAcIzkj41Z9zHl99YSRDUXCtkBU\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC4zRjM0MkVCRTg0MkYyQTM0\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #16 - Programming 1 Parkour Tutorial. [091]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Advanced) in Parkour - Programming 1.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #16nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnLet\'s get into the nuts and bolts of how to create a training plan for yourself or others.nnThis is what we call \'Programming\'. It will tell you exactly what work you should be doing on any given training day.nnIn this video, I will show you step one. Finding out what your current ability is.nnn### The 1RMnThe 1RM stands for \'One Repetition Maximum\' and it is simply the weight that you can only do a single repetition of the exercise of when completely fresh.nnSo, as an example, this is usually applied to weightlifting and weight training. A squat 1RM would be the maximum weight you could squat only once. Same for the deadlift... How much could you pick off the floor only once?nnThis 1RM number is then used as a benchmark for your current ability.nnIf you train for a few weeks and check your 1RM again, and you find it has increased, then you know your training is making you stronger.nnn### The 3RM and 5RMnIt should be noted that you could also use your three or five repetition maximum just as well as the 1RM as a way to gauge your progress.nnThe benefit of the 3RM or 5RM is that you will be measuring your progress closer to what your actual training program will be. (You\'ll see the program in the next few videos).nnn### Band resistancesnI\'ve taken the resistance values straight from the website of the manufacturer of my bands. This tells me how much each band will ROUGHLY be helping me and making me lighter.nnFor details on where to get the bands, check out the links in episode 2, the prerequisites.nnIf you want more accurate readings of the resistance of the bands, check out episode 4 (Beginner Option 1), Where I\'ve linked to some fish scales that will help with this.nnn### RESTnOne of the biggest mistakes is not resting enough when testing the 1RM. The point is to test how much you could pull when absolutely fresh and at your best.nnHowever, don\'t rest too much either. You don\'t want to start cooling down and becoming unprepared for the pull.n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:advanced}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fMd5O1s17vU/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fMd5O1s17vU/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fMd5O1s17vU/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fMd5O1s17vU/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fMd5O1s17vU/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":15,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"fMd5O1s17vU\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":15}'),(76677,55919,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(76678,55919,'authId','Auth1'),(76679,55919,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(76680,55919,'filterId','none'),(76681,55919,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(76682,55919,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(76683,55919,'_edit_last','1'),(76684,55919,'video_transcript_chatgpt','Understanding your own ability level is a crucial step in structuring an effective training session. By assessing your performance and setting up a clear scale, you can tailor your workouts to match your strength and enhance your progress. This tutorial will guide you through the process of establishing a personalized training scale based on your body weight and the types of resistance bands available, ensuring you get the most out of your workouts.\n\nStart by determining your current body weight and mark it as the center point of your scale. For instance, if you weigh 65 kilograms, that becomes the baseline. Next, familiarize yourself with the different resistance bands: the red band typically reduces your weight by about 14 kilograms, the blue band by 23 kilograms, and the green band by 30 kilograms. This means that if you\'re using the green band, you would effectively be performing pull-ups as if you weigh only 35 kilograms. To build the upper portion of your scale, you can add weights incrementally using a weight belt, starting from your body weight and going up in manageable chunks.\n\nOnce your scale is set up, it’s time to find your one-repetition maximum (1 RM), which reflects your true strength level. Begin with the lightest band and gradually work your way up, trying to perform a single repetition. If you find a band too easy, skip to the next one; if it feels too hard, reduce the weight slightly. The goal is to identify the point at which you can just manage one rep—this will help you identify where you currently sit on your scale. With your 1 RM established, you can easily start crafting a training program that targets your goals while steadily pushing your strength.\n\nHighlights:\n- Create a personalized scale based on your body weight and resistance bands to assess your current ability.\n- Use the right bands to understand how much you\'re effectively lifting when performing pull-ups.\n- Establish your one-repetition maximum (1 RM) to create a tailored training program to help you progress effectively.'),(76685,55919,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76686,55919,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"well done for getting through all of the\",\n        \"start\": 0.089,\n        \"duration\": 3.33\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"beginner intermediate and advanced\",\n        \"start\": 1.86,\n        \"duration\": 4.019\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"options in the series now we\'re going to\",\n        \"start\": 3.419,\n        \"duration\": 4.68\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"have a look at the actual programming\",\n        \"start\": 5.879,\n        \"duration\": 5.01\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"behind your training session how your\",\n        \"start\": 8.099,\n        \"duration\": 3.87\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"training session is going to be\",\n        \"start\": 10.889,\n        \"duration\": 3.54\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"structured how many sets how many reps\",\n        \"start\": 11.969,\n        \"duration\": 4.65\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"what you\'re actually looking at doing\",\n        \"start\": 14.429,\n        \"duration\": 5.52\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"when you go out to train this episode\",\n        \"start\": 16.619,\n        \"duration\": 4.981\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"we\'re going to have a look at actually\",\n        \"start\": 19.949,\n        \"duration\": 4.351\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"finding your ability level and placing\",\n        \"start\": 21.6,\n        \"duration\": 4.74\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"it on a scale that you\'re going t