                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"Understanding your own ability level is a crucial step in structuring an effective training session. By assessing your performance and setting up a clear scale, you can tailor your workouts to match your strength and enhance your progress. This tutorial will guide you through the process of establishing a personalized training scale based on your body weight and the types of resistance bands available, ensuring you get the most out of your workouts.nnStart by determining your current body weight and mark it as the center point of your scale. For instance, if you weigh 65 kilograms, that becomes the baseline. Next, familiarize yourself with the different resistance bands: the red band typically reduces your weight by about 14 kilograms, the blue band by 23 kilograms, and the green band by 30 kilograms. This means that if you\'re using the green band, you would effectively be performing pull-ups as if you weigh only 35 kilograms. To build the upper portion of your scale, you can add weights incrementally using a weight belt, starting from your body weight and going up in manageable chunks.nnOnce your scale is set up, it’s time to find your one-repetition maximum (1 RM), which reflects your true strength level. Begin with the lightest band and gradually work your way up, trying to perform a single repetition. If you find a band too easy, skip to the next one; if it feels too hard, reduce the weight slightly. The goal is to identify the point at which you can just manage one rep—this will help you identify where you currently sit on your scale. With your 1 RM established, you can easily start crafting a training program that targets your goals while steadily pushing your strength.nnHighlights:n- Create a personalized scale based on your body weight and resistance bands to assess your current ability.n- Use the right bands to understand how much you\'re effectively lifting when performing pull-ups.n- Establish your one-repetition maximum (1 RM) to create a tailored training program to help you progress effectively.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1706,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 398,\n            \"total_tokens\": 2104,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"DXEOB8KGulJW0S8hkFlPEBBqYudAkt2chLUZSAgsRiU-1730722355-1.0.1.1-_yUCnSFEPAJ_5cDCS_hFSFGqYnL5QvT8LXVynkwxCa12euSMdoAQWAyNCvbPlbH1IMfnlItkcR1QNpsosWsH0g\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724155,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"FJdc4jHMQnalikz_gdJw5IIDzNb8hpOKNbJ_Gjn8Q7E-1730722355131-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76691,55919,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76692,55919,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76693,55919,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76694,55919,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76695,55919,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76696,55920,'_thumbnail_id','14210'),(76697,55920,'video_id','KvclevcVF7k'),(76698,55920,'video_type','tutorial'),(76699,55920,'order_position','16'),(76700,55920,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"geedOqlsub3-a7zEH9Xxie2l3jE\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC5GM0Q3M0MzMzY5NTJFNTdE\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #17 - Programming 2 Parkour Tutorial. [092]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Advanced) in Parkour - Programming 2.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #17nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnNow we have a way to measure our current ability step two is to now work out what we have to do to actually get stronger.nnLet\'s talk about your \'working weight\' and how much you need to do.nnn### 5RM Work SetsnWe are going to do five repetitions in our actual training plan for each set. So we need to know what we are able to currently do. Try to only increase each attempt by 1 to 1.5kg each go.nnThis is similar to the 1RM testing in the last episode, except we are doing 5\'s instead.nnn### Why 5nWell, simply put, if you do singles, doubles or triples, you are doing pure strength training. If you do more than twenty, you are doing endurance.nnFives are a happy medium that develops are little of everything (but mostly strength).nnThe image below shows a rough guide to what different repetitions will do. (I know it looks a little complicated and has lots of crazy-long terms, all you really need to see is the top line.)nnn### Check the quality of worknIf the quality of your pull-up decreases as you get towards the end of the five repetitions, then you need to stop and count the last GOOD five as your 5RM.nnYou\'re only as good as what you put into the work. Don\'t bother cutting corners because it\'s only you who will suffer!nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:advanced}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/KvclevcVF7k/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/KvclevcVF7k/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/KvclevcVF7k/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/KvclevcVF7k/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/KvclevcVF7k/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":16,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"KvclevcVF7k\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":16}'),(76701,55920,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(76702,55920,'authId','Auth1'),(76703,55920,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(76704,55920,'filterId','none'),(76705,55920,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(76706,55920,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(76707,55920,'_edit_last','1'),(76708,55920,'video_transcript_chatgpt','In this tutorial, we’ll explore how to progressively determine your five-rep max (5 RM) using a structured approach while maintaining quality during each set. This focuses on gradually increasing weight and understanding when your body has reached its limit. By developing this awareness, you can enhance your strength training without risking poor form or injury.\n\nTo begin, start with a lighter weight where you can comfortably perform five pull-ups without compromise. Begin with a resistance band that provides enough assistance, allowing you to perform full-range movements. Once you’re stable with the green band, you can incrementally increase the weight by 1 kilogram or even half a kilogram, moving to a more challenging belt, like the blue one. It\'s crucial to monitor the quality of your repetitions—if you notice any degradation in your form or struggle with the final reps, that’s a sign to reassess and establish your current work set based on what you can execute while keeping the pull-ups clean and effective.\n\nAs you continue your training, always prioritize rest between sets. Allow yourself adequate recovery time so that you can approach each attempt with fresh strength. Staying warm during your rest period is important, too, to maintain muscle readiness. Remember, the goal is to find a challenging weight while ensuring that your technique remains sharp and safe.\n\nTo recap, focus on maintaining quality during each set while progressively increasing weights. Pay close attention to how your body responds—when your form declines, it’s time to set your work target. Finally, remember to rest adequately between attempts, ensuring you stay as capable as possible for each set.\n\n- Start with lighter weights and focus on perfecting your form during five pull-ups.\n- Incrementally increase resistance while always monitoring your movement quality.\n- Ensure adequate rest between sets to optimize your performance during weight increases.'),(76709,55920,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76710,55920,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"welcome back to programming number two\",\n        \"start\": 0.21,\n        \"duration\": 5.22\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"with this episode we are going to have a\",\n        \"start\": 2.61,\n        \"duration\": 6.18\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"look at trying to n