": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"In this tutorial, we’ll explore how to progressively determine your five-rep max (5 RM) using a structured approach while maintaining quality during each set. This focuses on gradually increasing weight and understanding when your body has reached its limit. By developing this awareness, you can enhance your strength training without risking poor form or injury.nnTo begin, start with a lighter weight where you can comfortably perform five pull-ups without compromise. Begin with a resistance band that provides enough assistance, allowing you to perform full-range movements. Once you’re stable with the green band, you can incrementally increase the weight by 1 kilogram or even half a kilogram, moving to a more challenging belt, like the blue one. It\'s crucial to monitor the quality of your repetitions—if you notice any degradation in your form or struggle with the final reps, that’s a sign to reassess and establish your current work set based on what you can execute while keeping the pull-ups clean and effective.nnAs you continue your training, always prioritize rest between sets. Allow yourself adequate recovery time so that you can approach each attempt with fresh strength. Staying warm during your rest period is important, too, to maintain muscle readiness. Remember, the goal is to find a challenging weight while ensuring that your technique remains sharp and safe.nnTo recap, focus on maintaining quality during each set while progressively increasing weights. Pay close attention to how your body responds—when your form declines, it’s time to set your work target. Finally, remember to rest adequately between attempts, ensuring you stay as capable as possible for each set.nn- Start with lighter weights and focus on perfecting your form during five pull-ups.n- Incrementally increase resistance while always monitoring your movement quality.n- Ensure adequate rest between sets to optimize your performance during weight increases.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 1123,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 358,\n            \"total_tokens\": 1481,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"iChVGmMClxW_OzPvHCC7ezze.nmEu7jTU1CDtx.QvmE-1730722362-1.0.1.1-_swXwUe0ycTMMy2BhSngHvh3Yw4k..sq.341cIzw4FWpyHc8aqINBSR8G55exgq_gFoP0m6mMjc2r6hQSLpurw\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724162,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"j9HakLiO5PQwwWZAeD54SgsjsmdORYatbYf4dEWQVq0-1730722362588-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76715,55920,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76716,55920,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76717,55920,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76718,55920,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76719,55920,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76720,55921,'_thumbnail_id','14212'),(76721,55921,'video_id','KcI2Cu8TiPg'),(76722,55921,'video_type','tutorial'),(76723,55921,'order_position','17'),(76724,55921,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"rRZ5g6KEAF-MzyZ_LPQ13BBi7wQ\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC4yMDhBMkNBNjRDMjQxQTg1\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #18 - Programming 3 Parkour Tutorial. [093]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Advanced) in Parkour - Programming 3.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #18nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnThis is an important episode for you. Let\'s look at how many reps and sets we should do in our training, how often we should be repeating it and how to record it down.nn**NOTE. My maths in the video is slightly wrong!**nApologies. The values of the volume in the video are slightly wrong. With the adjusted weight, I multiplied them by 3 in my head, rather than 5.nnSo instead the 43kg x 5 rep would = 215. NOT 213.nnThis is the same for the other adjusted values.nnn### 3 sets of 5 RepsnThe program consists of doing five consecutive repetitions, three times. The reason for this is because we don\'t want to do a high amount of volume, we want to do a low volume, but with a high intensity with heavier weights.nnThis way, we will be focusing a little more towards the strength development, rather than endurance.nnRemember though, the 5 reps should be HARD. As hard as you can bare them.nnn### Repeat every 48 to 72 hoursnThe body needs to go through three stages:nn- Training. This breaks down the muscle in your body.n- Fatigue. The body feels the effects of the training and tries to protect itself.n- Recovery. As fatigue sets in, the body starts the recovery stage to heal.nnIf you don\'t let the body recover properly your next training session will be working within the second/third stage and then the there will be even more fatigue to recover from.nnSlowly, the fatigue will catch up and you won\'t be able to train, losing out on significant progress.nnTherefore, you need to wait until you\'re recovered. This is between 48-72 hours after the training.nnn### Small IncrementsnEven if you\'re a beast, you should increase by very small amounts. Sure, the training will go on for longer, but that\'s the point.nnA slow, linear increase in strength is what we\'re aiming for, NOT an exponential explosion that leads to plateaus and depression.nnThink smart and long-term.nnn### Why write it downnI know writing down your numbers can be tedious, but recording your progress is absolutely essential. Here is a list of reasons to convince you:nn- You have 100%, concrete, written-down proof of your increase in strength.n- You can look back on your progress historically.n- Checks for stalling and plateaus become easier.n- By recording your volume you can accurately change the program to different reps & sets but with the same amount of volume.n- Only amateurs go training without a plan and record progress.n- Stop being lazy.n n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u