lups'),(76818,55925,'filterId','none'),(76819,55925,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(76820,55925,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(76821,55925,'_edit_last','1'),(76822,55925,'video_transcript_chatgpt','When it comes to strength training for advanced practitioners, the focus shifts from simply increasing weight to honing in on technique and the details of each lift. Unlike beginners, who often see more noticeable gains, advanced athletes need to recognize that their strength ramps up in smaller increments. This requires them to pay closer attention to their form, full range of motion, and specific mechanics during exercises. By ensuring that each movement is performed excellently, advanced practitioners can continue shifting their strength upwards, even if the progress feels less dramatic.\n\nAs an advanced practitioner prepares for their workout, it’s important to integrate joint mobility and a thorough warm-up routine. Start with dynamic stretches for major muscle groups, including biceps and triceps. Then, engage in specific pull-up warm-ups using a resistance band to maintain focus on technique before progressing to bodyweight pull-ups. This is where you can transition into work sets, typically working with weight on a belt while performing low-rep sets—aiming for three sets of four repetitions. This approach not only increases weight slightly but also optimizes performance in that anaerobic strength zone, where work is done at 80-100% of the practitioner’s maximum effort.\n\nIn conclusion, advanced strength training demands a systematic approach, balancing strength gains with precise technique. By emphasizing small adjustments and thoughtful progression, it’s possible to continue growing stronger while mastering the mechanics of each exercise. Remember, just because weight increases are subtler doesn’t mean workouts should become less challenging; rather, they should focus on perfecting every detail for long-lasting functional strength.\n\n- Focus on form and technique to maximize strength gains in advanced training.\n- Include a comprehensive warm-up that targets joint mobility and specific muscle groups.\n- Use lower rep ranges (e.g., 4 reps) with increased weights to optimize anaerobic strength without sacrificing form.'),(76823,55925,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76824,55925,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"this is example number three we\'re going\",\n        \"start\": 0.06,\n        \"duration\": 4.199\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"to be looking at the program of an\",\n        \"start\": 1.979,\n        \"duration\": 5.851\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"advanced practitioner specifically for\",\n        \"start\": 4.259,\n        \"duration\": 5.88\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"their strength training and making sure\",\n        \"start\": 7.83,\n        \"duration\": 4.41\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"that that advanced practitioner is still\",\n        \"start\": 10.139,\n        \"duration\": 4.231\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"progressing and getting stronger we\'re\",\n        \"start\": 12.24,\n        \"duration\": 4.02\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"still working on our strength for an\",\n        \"start\": 14.37,\n        \"duration\": 4.29\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"advanced practitioner still on that 80\",\n        \"start\": 16.26,\n        \"duration\": 5.79\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"to 100% anaerobic type training where\",\n        \"start\": 18.66,\n        \"duration\": 6.99\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"we\'re doing low reps high sets however\",\n        \"start\": 22.05,\n        \"duration\": 6.719\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"for an advanced practitioner you need to\",\n        \"start\": 25.65,\n        \"duration\": 6.359\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"realize that the increments will have\",\n        \"start\": 28.769,\n        \"duration\": 5.731