n00:00:225,000 --> 00:01:169,000\nbecomes second nature and it becomes\n\n88\n00:00:227,000 --> 00:01:171,000\nvery very easy for you to do that one or\n\n89\n00:00:229,000 --> 00:01:174,000\ntwo so muscular endurance training\n\n90\n00:00:231,000 --> 00:01:177,000\nespecially in parkour is something we\n\n91\n00:00:234,000 --> 00:01:177,000\nheavily rely on\n\n92\n00:00:243,000 --> 00:01:185,000\nyou\n\n'),(76851,55926,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(76852,55926,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-APqCVr76SrqLu3J4fTFDr5zhcLcfP\",\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730722223,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"Muscular endurance training focuses on increasing your ability to perform repeated movements over time, rather than solely increasing strength. This type of training is particularly useful for activities that require sustained physical exertion, like parkour or other dynamic sports. By emphasizing higher repetitions with lower weights, you build the stamina your muscles need to excel in prolonged activities. In this tutorial, we’ll break down how to effectively incorporate muscular endurance training into your routine, ensuring you get the most out of each workout.nnThe key to muscular endurance lies in adjusting your reps and sets. Start with a proper warm-up that includes two sets of four repetitions to prepare your muscles. For your main workout, aim for about five sets of eight to ten repetitions. This totals to around 50 repetitions for your work sets, so be sure to lower the weight to accommodate this higher volume. A critical aspect of this training is managing your rest periods. Instead of resting fully between sets like in strength training, limit your rest time to about three to four minutes. This keeps your heart rate elevated and enhances muscular endurance, making each set feel a bit more challenging. To further intensify your workout, consider adding an additional exercise targeting different muscle groups during your rest, such as bodyweight squats or lunges. nnIn summary, muscular endurance training is all about higher volumes and shared recovery. The fundamental takeaway is that by focusing on more repetitions with less weight and shorter rest periods, you condition your muscles to work harder for longer durations. This kind of training not only improves your performance in endurance-based activities but also reinforces proper movement techniques, making them second nature in applications like parkour. By consistently practicing these strategies, you will enhance your physical capabilities and make your activities feel easier and more natural.nnKey takeaways: n- Focus on 8-10 reps for 5 sets with reduced weight to enhance muscular endurance.n- Limit rest periods to 3-4 minutes to maintain intensity and challenge your muscles.n- Incorporate different exercises during rest to elevate your training and build functional strength.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 965,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 408,\n            \"total_tokens\": 1373,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"RYnMuS3cHhJh17encNc6oYLEu3o9DBYvYwyT5N959T4-1730722228-1.0.1.1-YRaCbTXWureCb8WvnW_ekgHUIK2laOPzmq0VzMBBoXLl7JiK1b6zuPgD8wGpZCJv2u2CSM__8vIEzfrVm0nPjw\",\n            \"expires\": 1730724028,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"QoGdLTBP74KzxL4uQ09JfxnkARqbiDARG13BKhzuDhY-1730722228805-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76853,55926,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76854,55926,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76855,55926,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76856,55926,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76857,55926,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76858,55927,'_thumbnail_id','14224'),(76859,55927,'video_id','hLSoVB6Ixno'),(76860,55927,'video_type','tutorial'),(76861,55927,'order_position','23'),(76862,55927,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"HscqDbbQjCB-8-AqRRtJRKBIzLM\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC41Mzk2QTAxMTkzNDk4MDhF\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #24 - Example 5 Parkour Tutorial. [099]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Advanced) in Parkour - Example 5.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #24nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnWhen most people say the word endurance they\'re usually referring to \'cardiovascular endurance\', which works the stamina of your heart and lungs.nnRather than waiting for your muscles to fatigue, we\'re looking at your overall body performance by seeing how long you can go and how much you can do.nnn### Aerobic over AnaerobicnWith endurance training we want to use roughly around 40% of our maximum strength but with many, many, many reps...nnIt all comes down to the type of energy in your body we want to try to use to keep you going. Essentially, we want oxygen to be your source of fuel.nnTo get to this stage, we need to use less hard intensity and heavy weights (anaerobic) and switch to light/medium intensity with lighter weight/stress.nnn### WarmupnNothing crazy here. You just want to do something lighter that will warm you up but not fatigue you.nnSome simple movement work, balancing or light rowing on the row machine will work well.nnn### AMRAPnAs Many Repetitions As Possible. Is a tool for focusing on endurance or muscular endurance.nnHeavily used within Crossfit, the AMRAP can take many forms. You can change the length of times used (both for each set and total amount of time for the training) to make it very versatile. You can combine it with multiple exercises to mix different muscle groups or movements too.nnThis is also a good way to finish your other types of training too as a \'finisher\' exercise.nnn### Assistance worknFinished? Not quite... Once you\'ve done the endurance portion of the training this is a good time to do a little strength training on other parts of your body.nnYou abs, obliques, lower back and \'core\' should always get a bit of attention. So focus a little time on those muscle groups.nnn### Why do endurancenSo why do endurance? Well, simply, it means you\'ll be able to have more stamina with the other training you do. You\'ll be able to go for longer and have more tolerance to any lactic acid build-up.nnIt\'s also very good more the mechanics and technical practice too. All of those reps build in the muscle-memory and gets the neuro-muscular pathways shouting loud and clear.nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:intermediate}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hLSoVB6Ixno/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hLSoVB6Ixno/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hLSoVB6Ixno/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hLSoVB6Ixno/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/hLSoVB6Ixno/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":23,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"hLSoVB6Ixno\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":23}'),(76863,55927,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(76864,55927,'authId','Auth1'),(76865,55927,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(76866,55927,'filterId','none'),(76867,55927,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(76868,55927,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(76869,55927,'_edit_last','1'),(76870,55927,'video_transcript_chatgpt','Endurance training is a critical component of any fitness regimen, particularly if you’re looking to improve your overall stamina for activities like parkour. Unlike strength training, which focuses on lifting heavy weights for fewer repetitions, endurance training involves lifting lighter weights with many repetitions. This approach not only enhances your aerobic capacity but also allows you to sustain higher levels of activity for extended periods. In this tutorial, we will explore how to effectively structure your endurance training sessions, ensuring you maximize your performance while minimizing fatigue.\n\nTo get started, it’s important to warm up properly. Instead of jumping into challenging exercises right away, focus on the mechanics of your movements using lighter bands or resistance options, like a blue or green band. This way, you can avoid unnecessary fatigue and prepare your muscles for the workout to come. Once you’re warmed up, the main part of your endurance training will use an \"AMRAP\" format, which stands for “as many repetitions as possible” within a set time frame, typically one minute. After this minute of high-intensity work, you’ll rest for one minute before repeating. This cycle can be done for as long as you like—20 minutes, for instance—with the goal of maximizing the number of high-quality repetitions you can perform, while gradually increasing the overall volume in future sessions.\n\nBy consistently tracking the total number of repetitions you complete, you can set tangible goals for your endurance training. For instance, if you manage 200 repetitions in one session, aim for 201 the next time. This progressive approach not only helps you build your endurance but also allows you to see measurable improvements over time. Incorporating endurance training into your routine will facilitate better performance in activities like parkour, where being able to handle oxygen deprivation and lactic acid buildup is vital for success. Remember, the goal here is to enhance your ability to perform for longer periods, making endurance training an invaluable tool for any serious athlete.\n\n- Focus on light weights and high repetitions to build endurance.\n- Use the AMRAP format for structured, timed endurance workouts.\n- Track your repetitions to set progressive goals and improve stamina over time.'),(76871,55927,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76872,55927,'video_tr