 way\n\n212\n00:00:577,000 --> 00:01:523,000\nfrom the mid-back and the lower back all\n\n213\n00:00:580,000 --> 00:01:525,000\nthe way around your side up to under the\n\n214\n00:00:583,000 --> 00:01:527,000\nshoulder and connecting and it all\n\n215\n00:00:585,000 --> 00:01:529,000\nconnect all the way up to your elbow you\n\n216\n00:00:588,000 --> 00:01:533,000\ncan also see that the bicep is\n\n217\n00:00:590,000 --> 00:01:536,000\nhighlighted here and the bicep is gonna\n\n218\n00:00:593,000 --> 00:01:539,000\nbe the long head of the bicep the outer\n\n219\n00:00:596,000 --> 00:01:542,000\none and this is going to be also helping\n\n220\n00:00:600,000 --> 00:01:545,000\naround the front in blue you can see\n\n221\n00:00:602,000 --> 00:01:548,000\nhere these are the abdominals and the\n\n222\n00:00:605,000 --> 00:01:550,000\nobliques server side muscles these are\n\n223\n00:00:608,000 --> 00:01:553,000\ngoing to be used for stabilizing when\n\n224\n00:00:611,000 --> 00:01:554,000\nyou do the pull as well\n\n225\n00:00:620,000 --> 00:01:562,000\nyou\n\n'),(76947,55930,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(76948,55930,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-APq34l9muhUv6tAufSEGQ9bMkzqex\",\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730721638,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"Understanding the biomechanics of exercises like pull-ups can greatly enhance your performance and protect against injury. When performing a pull-up, it’s essential to know which muscles are engaged and how to utilize them effectively. This tutorial will guide you through the primary muscle groups involved, how to achieve better technique, and additional tips to optimize your form.nnWhen you initiate a pull-up, your primary muscle groups at work are the latissimus dorsi (the lats) and the biceps. The lats, located along your back, are the major players in pulling your body upward, while the long head of your bicep assists in this motion. To maximize efficiency, focus on pulling your elbows down towards your sides instead of just thinking of pulling with your arms. This engagement helps you emphasize your lats and take pressure off your biceps. Additionally, as you pull up, you should maintain tension by externally rotating outwards, which means your elbows should come in while pushing outward against the bar.nnIncorporating stabilizer muscles and breath control is also crucial for a successful pull-up. Engaging your core and contracting your abs will help stabilize your spine, allowing for a controlled motion without risking injury. Remember to hold your breath while pulling up to create intra-abdominal pressure, which further supports your spine, and then exhale when you reach the top of the movement. Implementing these techniques will not only help optimize your pull-up but will also contribute to better overall strength and control during your workouts.nnHighlights: n- Engage your lats by focusing on pulling your elbows down rather than just pulling with your arms.n- Maintain stability by contracting your core muscles and engaging your abs during the exercise.n- Control your breathing by holding it during the pull and exhaling at the top for better spine support.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 2197,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 360,\n            \"total_tokens\": 2557,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"2xbk4ZVdtymofe0TwWuup1HeftA2.UVq6J19nokn.2w-1730721646-1.0.1.1-8ZUy7F0yKlZ1k6GcQNLgAm0emrrUsfAW5gncDKzb15kaBF7lgqnDlM70Yyr1spPoAIU5XhGIkFxs4tF3zid6OA\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723446,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"_xs_vO_HXbVBeqzBWZ.jKaunWwPmSnOcPO867vBuRck-1730721646010-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(76949,55930,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(76950,55930,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(76951,55930,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(76952,55930,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(76953,55930,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(76954,55931,'_thumbnail_id','14232'),(76955,55931,'video_id','5-fhpLiRHTo'),(76956,55931,'video_type','tutorial'),(76957,55931,'order_position','27'),(76958,55931,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"RL9oAuyUmgR4fs1f-52Qf8kY570\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC40NzZCMERDMjVEN0RFRThB\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #28 - Coaching Basics Parkour Tutorial. [103]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Intermediate) in Parkour - Coaching Basics.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #28nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnCoaching can be tough. It\'s a skill, just like any other, that needs to be practised and constantly improved, analysed and refined.nnTeaching the pull-up, even though it seems like a simple action, can have a lot of problems. In this video, I try to cover the basic things you should keep in mind and remember to do whenever instructing others.nnn### RULE 1 is to Do no harm.nIt seems like a simple rule, but in reality, can be a little tricky. \'Harm\' can be both short-term and long-term. By teaching someone incorrect technique or not correcting bad habits will harm them much more overall. This falls under Rule 1.nnBy not educating your students correctly, but promoting poor mechanics or making them do more weight than they should, again will hurt them in the future. It\'s your duty, as the coach, to educate yourself, correct yourself when wrong, call out any bull-shit from your students, keep up-to-date and do everything you can to pass on your experience and knowledge.nnn### What is the goalnIf there is a clear target for the student(s) to work towards, they will be much more motivated to achieve it. Making difficult but realistic and achievable goals requires planning, but the more experience you get, the easier it will become.nnHaving goals also has the added benefit of tracking progress. Once a goal is achieved, you can then move onto the next.nnn### Fun yet FocusednThis really comes down to planning and experience. Ask yourself, would YOU enjoy this session and have fun? How about the unfit office-working first-timer beginner who is very shy, knows no-one and hasn\'t ever done pull-ups before? What about the veteran, international parkour performer who is much better than you?nnYep, you need to make teaching EVERYONE fun but in a way that they remain focused on the goal and get something out of your session. With pull-ups, this can be tricky because it\'s not a very \'glamorous\' exercise. However, it can be done... you just need to put in the work to think about how.nnn### TimenBe aware that improvements in strength require constant exposure to the training for long periods of time. Anyone expecting to improve in one session will be disappointed.nnConsistency is key here. Making your students aware that they have to keep training to make a difference is something you have to do as the coach.n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:intermediate}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/5-fhpLiRHTo/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/5-fhpLiRHTo/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/5-fhpLiRHTo/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/5-fhpLiRHTo/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/5-fhpLiRHTo/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":27,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"5-fhpLiRHTo\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":27}'),(76959,55931,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(76960,55931,'authId','Auth1'),(76961,55931,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(76962,55931,'filterId','none'),(76963,55931,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(76964,55931,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(76965,55931,'_edit_last','1'),(76966,55931,'video_transcript_chatgpt','Incorporating effective training strategies into your coaching sessions is crucial for achieving the desired outcomes while keeping students safe and engaged. A key part of this process involves ensuring that students master basic techniques and mechanics before any additional loads or advanced skills are introduced. By solidifying their foundational skills, you not only decrease the risk of injury but also enhance their overall confidence and enjoyment during training sessions. The core principle here is that without proper technique, pushing students towards heavier loads or more complex moves can backfire, leading to frustration and injury.\n\nOnce the mechanics are in place, it’s essential to communicate clear goals so students understand their purpose in the training. Developing personalized objectives based on individual skill levels can keep students motivated and invested in their progression. No matter the size of the class, it\'s important to start from simpler tasks before scaling up to more challenging ones. If students see a clear pathway for growth—from beginner to advanced levels—they\'re likely to feel more accomplished and engaged. Incorporating fun elements into strength and conditioning, such as friendly competition or group challenges, can also enhance their experience by making the exercises more enjoyable, breaking the monotony that often comes with such training.\n\nAs you wrap up each session, remind students that strength training is a gradual process that requires time and consistent effort to yield visible results. Recognizing that significant improvements might not come immediately can help foster patience and perseverance among your students. By continuously revisiting their goals, celebrating small wins, and maintaining an engaging atmosphere, you create a supportive environment where students can thrive. Most importantly, they will leave each session reaffirmed in their abilities and eager to tackle new challenges in the future.\n\nKey takeaways include ensuring students master technique before adding difficulty, providing clear and personalized goals to maintain motivation, and creating a fun, engaging environment that encourages consistent effort and growth.'),(76967,55931,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(76968,55931,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"this is the first coaching video we\'re\",\n        \"start\": 0.089,\n        \"duration\": 4.621\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"going to look at the basics of how to\",\n        \"start\": 2.669,\n        \"duration\": 4.891\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"incorporate this type of training into\",\n        \"start\": 4.71,\n        \"duration\": 4.71\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"your sessions and the things you want to\",\n        \"start\": 7.56,\n        \"duration\": 3.63\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"be thinking about with your students\",\n        \"start\": 9.42,\n        \"duration\": 5.46\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"first things first you will always make\",\n        \"start\": 11.19,\n        \"duration\": 6.39\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"sure that the technical aspects the\",\n        \"start\": 14.88,\n        \"duration\": 5.12\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"technique and the mechanics are\",\n        \"start\": 17.58,\n        \"duration\": 4.949\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"absolutely perfect with your students\",\n        \"start\": 20,\n        \"duration\": 5.619\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"before you move into anything else you\",\n        \"start\": 22.529,\n        \"duration\": 5.431\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"don\'t want your students to be trying\",\n        \"start\": 25.619,\n        \"duration\": 5.25\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"things that are too heavy or just not\",\n        \"start\": 27.96,\n        \"duration\": 5.73\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"ri