rating outrageous goals into your programming. This doesn\'t mean you should throw caution to the wind, but rather that you should design workouts that challenge both mental and physical limits. For example, consider implementing a program where students perform one pull-up every minute for an hour. While this amounts to just 60 pull-ups, the physical and psychological endurance required to maintain focus over the entire hour can be remarkably taxing. The idea is to realize that you or your students might surprise yourselves with what you can achieve when faced with a daunting challenge.nnUltimately, pushing limits not only fosters resilience but also prepares you for real-life scenarios. By training under demanding conditions, you’re building strength and endurance that translate into practical skills. Think of it like this: if you\'ve completed an intense challenge—like doing 1,000 pull-ups over time—you\'ll have a newfound confidence in your abilities when faced with unexpected physical demands in life. Whether it’s saving yourself in a sticky situation or simply feeling empowered through fitness, the true value of ambitious training goals lies in becoming better equipped for whatever comes your way.nn- Set challenging goals, like an hour of pull-ups, to push physical and mental limits. n- Focus on endurance and mental resilience rather than just the numbers of reps.n- Real-world application of intense training prepares you for unexpected situations.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 916,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 348,\n            \"total_tokens\": 1264,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"42W6WBy5_0Ry6n0bD21ruwLcr3M07F3Vls4mTqZYqqQ-1730722153-1.0.1.1-Esot4rIyU23bEb_jphb6ik7rEIWnb59VZU0bD1yG94A7wChVMii99VcHPJeX7QGT0lXQYeBM1Zm5UoGb7J.aOA\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723953,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"dqUq4wT1wLpmjzBliABPTuFCE8kL8Q.Ou.MW46KH2JI-1730722153396-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(77022,55933,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(77023,55933,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(77024,55933,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(77025,55933,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(77026,55933,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(77027,55934,'_thumbnail_id','14238'),(77028,55934,'video_id','AMZu29CQdKY'),(77029,55934,'video_type','tutorial'),(77030,55934,'order_position','30'),(77031,55934,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"6O_kwSK1AjwqiXNYrbwKvlZQasw\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC5DQUNERDQ2NkIzRUQxNTY1\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #31 - Common Faults 1 Parkour Tutorial. [106]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Advanced) in Parkour - Common Faults 1.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #31nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnThe next two episodes will cover a load of common mistakes that happen a lot that we should correct.nnThis first episode shows you the first three problems that you should keep an eye out for with yourself or your students.nnn### Common Fault 1 is Unique AnatomynOne \'gotcha\' that can trip coaches up is that they don\'t realise that people are built differently. This happens a lot with squat coaches and leg angles & hip positions.nnThere are a lot of variations of bone lengths, joint positions and orientations, etc... which means that you an I will do things slightly differently. Factoring in previous injuries and imbalances too, you can find that a student may be doing the correct mechanics, but it just looks wrong.nnSo, always ensure that the mechanics are correct (the right muscles being used in the right way and in the right position), but what it looks like can change.nnAs a quick reference, look at the images below from Paul Grilley that compares the anatomy of different skeletons. Notice the joint orientations especially.nnn### Film YourselfnnWith the advent of mobile-phone cameras, filming yourself has become very, very easy. It allows you to have first-hand feedback on what you are doing and how your body is working.nnAlso, it serves as a good history of your progress too. Nothing beats seeing side-by-side what you were doing six months ago, compared to today.nnHaving a mirror works well too, but actually takes your focus away from the exercise itself. Use a mirror if you don\'t have access to a camera, but filming is a much better tool.nnn### Common Fault 2 is Full Range Of MotionnThis is super, super, super important.nnBelieve me, if you train without doing a full range of motion, not only will you have weaknesses but when you get to the point that your training is plateauing and you realise that you\'ve shot yourself in the foot by not doing it.nnYou now have to de-program all of your training and basically start from scratch and spend longer getting out of your existing habits before you can build strength again.nnn### Common Fault 3 is Using the wrong muscle groupsnThe third fault is a fairly simple one to spot and correct by filming yourself or watching your students. Think about the biggest muscles in the chain doing the most work.nnWhenever you do an exercise, make sure the correct muscles are being used in the correct way, in the correct order. Sometimes it can be hard to correct, but it just takes time and effort.nnThis also means that if you are cheating the exercise by trying to utilise other forces like kipping, momentum and swinging or kicking with the legs and jerking up or jumping into the pull-up. All of these methods help you up, yes. But it won\'t make you stronger. Don\'t cheat. n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit.  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:intermediate}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/AMZu29CQdKY/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/AMZu29CQdKY/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/AMZu29CQdKY/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/AMZu29CQdKY/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/AMZu29CQdKY/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":30,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"AMZu29CQdKY\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":30}'),(77032,55934,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(77033,55934,'authId','Auth1'),(77034,55934,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(77035,55934,'filterId','none'),(77036,55934,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(77037,55934,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(77038,55934,'_edit_last','1'),(77039,55934,'video_transcript_chatgpt','Understanding common faults in exercise techniques, such as pull-ups, is essential for both coaches and individuals looking to improve their training. Many times, students may unknowingly develop bad habits that hinder their performance and progression. By identifying these faults and understanding each person’s unique anatomy, trainers can effectively tailor their guidance to help everyone achieve their fitness goals. This tutorial will discuss common pitfalls and provide practical guidance on how to address them for effective training.\n\nTo begin with, it\'s important to assess whether students are executing the pull-up with the correct technique. Consider creating a mental checklist to evaluate their form and identify any mechanical faults. Remember that each individual has unique anatomical characteristics that influence their exercise execution, from the length of their bones to the orientation of their joints. This means some people may naturally perform a pull-up with their elbows tucked in while others may have their elbows bent outward. As a coach, recognize these differences and focus on whether they are performing the exercise without discomfort and within a full range of motion. Filming oneself can be a useful tool for self-assessment, allowing individuals to observe their own form and make necessary adjustments for improvement.\n\nLastly, emphasize the importance of full range of motion and using the correct muscle groups during pull-ups. Many students might be tempted to cut corners or rely too heavily on their biceps instead of engaging the lats fully. Encourage everyone to start with the proper technique from the outset, as bad habits can lead to weaknesses and might require significant effort to unlearn later on. Use simple techniques like a pause at the bottom of the pull-up or restricting movement with a wall to eliminate momentum. Consistently reinforcing correct form will build a strong foundation for strength and technique.\n\nTo recap, here are three key highlights: \n\n- Assess each individual\'s form while considering their unique anatomical differences and focus on correcting mechanical faults.\n- Use self-filming as a helpful method to evaluate and adjust your technique for improved performance.\n- Reinforce the necessity of full range of motion and proper muscle engagement to avoid developing harmful habits.'),(77040,55934,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(77041,55934,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"we\'ve covered a lot of ground but now\",\n        \"start\": 0.06,\n        \"duration\": 4.08\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"we\'re gonna have a look at some of the\",\n        \"start\": 2.55,\n        \"duration\": 4.62\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"common faults that you\'ll see amongst\",\n        \"start\": 4.14,\n        \"duration\": 6.03\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"your students all ones that you may\",\n        \"start\": 7.17,\n        \"duration\": 4.98\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"potentially be doing yourself but didn\'t\",\n        \"start\": 10.17,\n        \"duration\": 3.69\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"realize you were doing we\'re gonna go\",\n        \"start\": 12.15,\n        \"duration\": 4.049\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"through all the common faults and see if\",\n        \"start\": 13.86,\n        \"duration\": 3.33\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"we can correct\",\n        \"start\": 16.199,\n        \"duration\": 3.84\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"so the first common fault that we\'re\",\n    