0:01:602,000\ngreat way of getting visual feedback of\n\n236\n00:00:659,000 --> 00:01:602,000\nwhat you\'re exactly doing\n\n237\n00:00:668,000 --> 00:01:610,000\nyou\n\n'),(77068,55935,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(77069,55935,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-APqBYmSD3c5TGSc1kkTOwZgjdVskd\",\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730722164,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"Improving your fitness, whether you’re an athlete, a coach, or a fitness enthusiast, relies heavily on maintaining good technique in your training. Many common mistakes can derail your progress, often linked to poor motivation or harmful training methods. Understanding these pitfalls not only helps avoid injuries but also enhances performance, ensuring that you build strength effectively and sustainably.nnOne of the most critical mistakes is allowing motivation rooted in ego to compromise technique. This often manifests as individuals pushing themselves to lift heavier weights or complete more repetitions without focusing on form. Such practices lead not only to injury but also to plateaus in progress. To truly advance, it\'s essential to prioritize quality over quantity, ensuring that every movement is performed with precision. Additionally, as fatigue sets in during workouts, sticking to proper technique remains crucial; if you\'re too tired to maintain form, it\'s time to pause and reset. This principle isn’t just about physical fatigue; if your mind isn\'t in the right place, stepping back can help prevent injuries stemming from distraction or lack of focus.nnAnother area to pay attention to is the danger of performing exercises like circular pull-ups, often seen in CrossFit environments. While these may enable athletes to complete more repetitions in competitions, they obscure the essence of proper strength training and risk straining ligaments or muscles. Real-world applications of exercises should emphasize correct movement patterns instead of relying on momentum. Overall, by reinforcing the importance of technique, being mindful of fatigue, and eliminating risky practices, both coaches and athletes can create a safer and more effective training environment.nn- Prioritize proper technique over lifting heavier weights or completing more reps.n- Monitor fatigue and mental focus—stop if you can’t maintain good form.n- Avoid exercises that use momentum, like circular pull-ups, to ensure effective strength training.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 2183,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 354,\n            \"total_tokens\": 2537,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 2048\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"phRiT2kKnoA4RoWljruLqrBbEP9LjJrjge7kgzOuZmY-1730722171-1.0.1.1-bCsx338WAmpEr587EJ1rGAf8GOYwwV52UCOSPlwAe08.ccA2AHbqcMCEztMKMnE8JCQgZclKz_r8jcFfVvDf1A\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723971,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"RvSlAUhl2MO7FIROYF5THkT.gZhW3piKUJiF.3fdcyA-1730722171020-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(77070,55935,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(77071,55935,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(77072,55935,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(77073,55935,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(77074,55935,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(77075,55936,'_thumbnail_id','14242'),(77076,55936,'video_id','bgFuvVtOz68'),(77077,55936,'video_type','tutorial'),(77078,55936,'order_position','32'),(77079,55936,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"FFlfqF0GF0qKQHhZMOkgYYVaNUk\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTW1yQ1ZzX2JPb3RRTGpWZHE1ekdoVC4xMkVGQjNCMUM1N0RFNEUx\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-08-19T07:32:10Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Pullup #33 - Recovery Parkour Tutorial. [108]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Pullup (Advanced) in Parkour - Recovery.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Pullup Series. Episode #33nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Strengthening #Pullup #---nnOnce you\'ve finished your training, it\'s not the time to pack up and go home quite yet. This is where you should get some good stretching to aid that recovery.nnIn this episode, I will focus on the primary muscles that would have been used the most in your upper-body training.nnn### ForearmsnThe forearm muscles are primarily used to grip the bar and hold on. We can stretch these out by bending the wrist back and bringing the arm across our body.nnRemember to do both sides, always. Spend enough time to feel a real difference. I\'m not going to add in timings here because it\'s really down to your anatomy.nnNote that I\'m also going to link to more stretches on youtube below.nnn### Upper armnThe upper arm can be stretched by trying to increase the distance between your shoulder and the elbow. Bring the arm across your body, hook your other arm behind the elbow and start pulling the shoulder back.nnYou shouldn\'t be rotating at the waist or moving around. Also, your arm shouldn\'t be around your neck and choking you either.nnn### Shoulder and chestnThere are so many stretches for the shoulder and chest, it\'s crazy. The one I find the most effective is by using a wall or the floor to lay my arm along, perpendicular to my body (sticking out at shoulder height) and then rolling my body back onto it. This uses my bodyweight to help the stretch.nnn### The LatsnThat big primary muscle group for the pulling action. This needs a lot of love and stretching too. Again, I\'m going to focus on two that I find the most effective.nnFirstly, you want to use a wall and place both hands in front of you onto it. Next, you are going to twist 90 degrees one way and move your hands on the wall so one is above and higher than the other. You should now feel that the outer side of your body (from the top arm running down) should be stretching and targetting the lats.nnSecondly, you could use a mobility band on the bar. Grab it with one hand and try to move back as far as you can, stretching the band. Now step backwards with the same side leg and place your knee on the floor. That side should now be getting the stretch. To target the Lats a little more you can add some twisting too.nnn### The AbsnThe abdominal muscles are notoriously tricky to target a stretch. Especially since opening the up and elongating them usually means that your lower back vertebrae get crunched together.nnSo, when you do this particular lying down stretch ensure that you engage your glute (bum) muscles to put your hips into a good position so that the discs in your back don\'t get crunched.nnn### Other StretchesnThere is a world of online tutorials and videos on stretching every part of your body. It\'s worth doing your due diligence to research up good sources of information. Find out who the leaders of the industry are and why. See what they offer and if it works for you. If not, keep searching!n--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit.  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000  uff2cuff2fuff2euff24uff2fuff2euff30uff21uff32uff2buff2fuff35uff32nnnnThis video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bgFuvVtOz68/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bgFuvVtOz68/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bgFuvVtOz68/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bgFuvVtOz68/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bgFuvVtOz68/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":32,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"bgFuvVtOz68\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":32}'),(77080,55936,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(77081,55936,'authId','Auth1'),(77082,55936,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(77083,55936,'filterId','none'),(77084,55936,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(77085,55936,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(77086,55936,'_edit_last','1'),(77087,55936,'video_transcript_chatgpt','After any intense training session, recovery is essential to ensure optimal performance and prevent injury. This tutorial will guide you through some efficient stretching techniques and myofascial release strategies specifically designed to relieve post-training muscle tension, especially after upper-body workouts. Knowing which muscle groups you\'ve pushed during your training will help you tailor these recovery techniques to better soothe sore muscles and promote healing.\n\nTo start, focus on your forearms. You can stretch them effectively by extending your arm straight in front of you, grasping your palm, and gently pulling it back to feel a nice stretch. Remember to perform this stretch on both sides for balanced recovery. Next, target your shoulders by bringing your arm across your body and using your other arm to pull it gently. The key is to keep your shoulder back and your elbow high to maintain proper alignment and maximize the stretch. You can also stretch your chest and biceps by lying on your chest with one arm extended out to the side and using your other arm to help push your body over. This counterbalance will deepen the stretch in your pectoral muscles.\n\nAdditionally, don’t neglect your lats, which play a crucial role in upper-body workouts. Use a wall or a sturdy pole to place your hands against, turning your hands 90 degrees and twisting through your body. A resistance band can also be useful: with one arm through the band, step back, and let your body weight stretch the latissimus dorsi. Finally, to gently stretch your abs, lie on the floor and arch your back while looking up. Although it can be challenging to stretch the abs deeply, this motion will help release tension in that area. Prioritizing these recovery exercises will help you bounce back faster and keep you in peak condition for your next session.\n\nIn summary, incorporating targeted stretches and relief techniques into your post-training routine will keep your muscles healthy and enhance your performance in future workouts. Listen to your body, focus on the areas you’ve worked, and give yourself the care needed to recover effectively. \n\n- Start with stretches for your forearms to alleviate lactic acid buildup.\n- Perform shoulder stretches with proper alignment to maximize benefits.\n- Use resistance bands for effective lats stretching and maintain a focus on overall recovery.'),(77088,55936,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(77089,55936,'video_transcript_json','[\n    {\n        \"text\": \"so let\'s have a look at some of the\",\n        \"start\": 0.03,\n        \"duration\": 5.16\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"recovery work that we need to do after\",\n        \"start\": 1.89,\n        \"duration\": 5.28\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"our training sessions this is going to\",\n        \"start\": 5.19,\n        \"duration\": 4.85\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"incorporate some stretching and some\",\n        \"start\": 7.17,\n        \"duration\": 6.12\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"myofascial release so as you\'re probably\",\n        \"start\": 10.04,\n        \"duration\": 5.59\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"now aware this is a lot of upper-body\",\n        \"start\": 13.29,\n        \"duration\": 5.7\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"work and we need to now do some\",\n        \"start\": 15.63,\n        \"duration\": 6.18\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"stretching on those muscles post\",\n        \"start\": 18.99,\n        \"duration\": 5.4\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"training so we\'re going to have a look\",\n        \"start\": 21.81,\n        \"duration\": 4.59\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"at some of the muscle groups that we\'ve\",\n        \"start\": 24.39,\n        \"duration\": 4.049\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"used you should know what these are by\",\n        \"start\": 26.4,\n        \"duration\": 4.59\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"now and quite honestly if you\'ve been\",\n        \"start\": 28.439,\n        \"duration\": 4.441\n    },\n    {\n        \"text\": \"doing the training 