upsnA very small difference from the pull-up is changing the grip. Instead of the pronated grip, we\'ll switch to using a supinated grip (palm facing towards you).nnJust by changing this small part will have ripple effects onto the specific muscles you use to do the work. Notably, you\'ll notice a lot more work happening on the pecs and biceps.nn*Note. What I said in the video that the lats being used less is wrong. You will still heavily use the Lats as well in the chin-up.nnn### Embrace the weirdnFinding weird grips and positions to do a pull-up or chin-up is a very good way of changing the amount of work specific muscles have to do. Having to pull to the left more might need you to engage you left obliques or something else.nnThe up-side is that you become more versatile and functional.nnTry tree-branches, scaffolding, overhangs, bouldering challenges, rocks, ledges, window sills, etc.. lots to try.nnn### SwingupsnA technique used by gymnasts a lot, but very useful for us too. It requires a smaller amount of strength, but more momentum.nnWhat you should try to do is swing your hips up and over the bar on the far side. Give it a good swing and drive with your knees as high as you can. As you bring the knees up, start pulling to get your hips as high as possible.nnThis can be a fun addition to pulling training as well as a general movement skill.nnn### Skin the catnHa! I know, a very weird (and a little morbid) name for an exercise. The skin-the-cat allows you to try and pull your bodyweight at some of the far-ends of your mobility.nnStart hanging off the bar in a dead-hang and then try to pull your feet up to touch the bar. Instead of stopping at the bar, you will now try to keep going through the gap between your arms and the bar.nnStart with only going a little way and pull back. Note that the return pull is usually the hardest because your arms are in a weaker position if you don\'t have mobility there.nnn### CalisthenicsnThis is here to give you some other ideas of things you can do to add to your bar training. There are infinite numbers of variations and ways to be on the bar.nnA simple youtube search on \'Calisthenics\' or \'Barstarzz\' will give you many results.nnCheck out one of Frank Medrano\'s videos and his callisthenics routine...nnn### MuscleupsnHere we are. The muscle-up is one of the most sought-after parkour movements by practitioners. It\'s a very tough movement, requiring a lot of strength and mobility. This is one that you will have to work hard towards and will take dedication.nnHowever, it\'s totally worth it. Being a super-functional movement, it basically says that you can scale obstacles without the use of your legs. So if you can\'t use your feet to help, you are still able to climb over.nnI highly recommend that the muscle-up be one of your primary goals of getting stronger.n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Strengthening}}{{Series:Pullups}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_hAl4VM1Pro/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_hAl4VM1Pro/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_hAl4VM1Pro/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_hAl4VM1Pro/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/_hAl4VM1Pro/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTMmrCVs_bOotQLjVdq5zGhT\",\"position\":35,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"_hAl4VM1Pro\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":35}'),(77152,55939,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Pullups'),(77153,55939,'authId','Auth1'),(77154,55939,'searchId','Tutorials - Pullups'),(77155,55939,'filterId','none'),(77156,55939,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(77157,55939,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Pullups'),(77158,55939,'_edit_last','1'),(77159,55939,'video_transcript_chatgpt','If you\'re looking to enhance your upper body strength and functional movement, exploring different types of pull-ups can significantly boost your fitness routine. Traditional pull-ups and chin-ups are excellent for building back and arm muscles, but they can also be adapted to better suit activities like parkour where grip and climbing techniques are essential. In this tutorial, we will delve into various pull-up variations and their benefits, making your workouts much more versatile and engaging.\n\nTo get started, you want to familiarize yourself with the basic grip. The traditional pull-up grip involves your palms facing away, which helps in developing strength through the lats and back. However, switching to a chin-up grip, where your palms face towards you, allows your biceps and chest to take on more of the load. Once comfortable with these, you can experiment with alternative grips, such as placing your hands in different positions on the bar to mimic real-world climbing scenarios. Additionally, techniques like the swing-up and skin-the-cat provide a more dynamic approach to pull-ups, integrating a playful aspect while also working on your strength.\n\nIn conclusion, mastering a variety of pull-up styles will not only make your workouts more enjoyable but will also translate into improved performance in functional movements like climbing and parkour. Incorporating exercises like the muscle-up into your regimen can serve as an advanced goal, as it requires a strong transition from pulling to pushing. So, whether you\'re a beginner or looking to level up your training, exploring these different pull-up variations can really enhance your overall physical capabilities. \n\nHighlights:\n- Experiment with traditional pull-ups and chin-ups to build strength effectively.\n- Incorporate variations like the swing-up and skin-the-cat to add fun and challenges to your training.\n- Aim for advanced exercises like the muscle-up to enhance your overall functional strength.'),(77160,55939,'_