o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"When it comes to climbing, proper foot placement is key to making your ascent smooth and efficient. In this tutorial, we’ll focus on elevating your lower foot while ensuring your top foot is positioned correctly. This technique not only helps you maintain balance but also allows you to reach higher holds, which is especially helpful when tackling more challenging routes. nnTo start, practice getting your top foot positioned correctly as you did in your previous training. Next, shift your attention to your lower foot. The goal is to bring this foot up higher so that you can step up more comfortably. Focus on bringing your lower foot to a higher hold while still keeping your balance. It’s essential to repeat this motion until it feels natural, so take your time, and don’t forget to practice on both sides to develop balance and strength equally.nnIn summary, working on your foot placement can significantly enhance your climbing skills. By bringing your lower foot up higher and reinforcing the top foot\'s position, you can improve your reach and balance. Keep practicing this technique, and you’ll notice a positive impact on your climbing performance, making your climbs more enjoyable and efficient.nnHighlights:n- Focus on proper foot placement for a smoother climb.n- Practice bringing your lower foot up to a higher hold.n- Don\'t forget to practice techniques on both sides for balanced development.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 345,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 265,\n            \"total_tokens\": 610,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"AxZ0.lU49L.QC_O2Z3hNXdJDdN.9UyCO8wNP.oUrVTc-1730721419-1.0.1.1-0ZyUUNmJUVBOVx_.Hq4Q5jF.0fAKBiAbNnL5z4o00h49tnrKujkAul1h7jWx1YWBjasqvLfYuzlC7vORKnpoqA\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723219,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"khJ_fArFNT61u.0K7D7zvI_U9XjmEj3lNRaqj3V3o3E-1730721419221-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(78190,55980,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(78191,55980,'video_transcript_chatgpt','When it comes to climbing, proper foot placement is key to making your ascent smooth and efficient. In this tutorial, we’ll focus on elevating your lower foot while ensuring your top foot is positioned correctly. This technique not only helps you maintain balance but also allows you to reach higher holds, which is especially helpful when tackling more challenging routes. \n\nTo start, practice getting your top foot positioned correctly as you did in your previous training. Next, shift your attention to your lower foot. The goal is to bring this foot up higher so that you can step up more comfortably. Focus on bringing your lower foot to a higher hold while still keeping your balance. It’s essential to repeat this motion until it feels natural, so take your time, and don’t forget to practice on both sides to develop balance and strength equally.\n\nIn summary, working on your foot placement can significantly enhance your climbing skills. By bringing your lower foot up higher and reinforcing the top foot\'s position, you can improve your reach and balance. Keep practicing this technique, and you’ll notice a positive impact on your climbing performance, making your climbs more enjoyable and efficient.\n\nHighlights:\n- Focus on proper foot placement for a smoother climb.\n- Practice bringing your lower foot up to a higher hold.\n- Don\'t forget to practice techniques on both sides for balanced development.'),(78192,55980,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(78193,55980,'_edit_lock','1730716580:1'),(78194,55980,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(78195,55980,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(78196,55980,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(78197,55980,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(78198,55981,'_thumbnail_id','14000'),(78199,55981,'video_id','6bcipsfs0v4'),(78200,55981,'video_type','tutorial'),(78201,55981,'order_position','3'),(78202,55981,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"HuIzXaGSvVITdcy0VxCE-MOb0Ms\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTktJVUhMckdzRTkwTXU5VXJZaERxTC5EQUE1NTFDRjcwMDg0NEMz\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-06-25T09:08:34Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Climbup #04 - Overgrip Negatives Parkour Tutorial. [043]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Climbup (Beginner) in Parkour - Overgrip Negatives.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Climbup Series. Episode #04nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Climbing #OvergripNegatives #---nnClimbups - Overgrip Negatives.nnWithout getting into the pulling action yet, you should always practice technique and mechanics first to make sure you do the action correctly. So, today we will do the climbup in reverse as what we call a negative.nnDo it in stages. Make sure you go to your belly first, bring both elbows back at the same time, then slowly let yourself down.nn1. The negative allows us to just try and resist gravity and not have to focus on the power of the pulling chain yet. However, you should make sure that you control the entire way down and should have NO dropping or fast parts. The hardest section will be the transition from a push to a pull (when the elbow reaches horizontally). You\'ll feel stress on the elbow.nn2. Make sure the foot has traction when you come down. Bend the knees and keep the feet as high as possible at all times!nn3. Your trajectory should be ud83dudc47straight down. You should not go backwards and away from the wall. This is where you\'ll come off if the hands cannot take the strain.nn4. Go slow, take your time. Keep repeating. Even if this is hardly, don\'t worry, I\'ll have more alternatives to help later!nnWarning u26a0u26a0Beware of the dreaded drop! Don\'t let yourself just drop into a full arm hang. This usually means you\'ll be \'hanging\' on your tendons and not using the muscles to slow you. The can cause shoulder issues, so go slow!nnn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Climbing}}{{Series:Climbups Tips}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/6bcipsfs0v4/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/6bcipsfs0v4/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/6bcipsfs0v4/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https: