> 00:00:29,000\ncorrect but it\'s going to put enough\n\n11\n00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000\nstress on that transition so we know\n\n12\n00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000\nwe\'re working and we\'re working hard\n\n13\n00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000\nokay so you just keep doing this until\n\n14\n00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000\nyou can nice and slowly transition from\n\n15\n00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000\ntop to bottom without any kind of little\n\n16\n00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000\ndrops or fast movement all right\n\n'),(78261,55983,'_video_transcript_srt','field_67224b69b74d7'),(78262,55983,'video_transcript_chatgpt_json','{\n    \"headers\": {},\n    \"body\": {\n        \"id\": \"chatcmpl-APpzPGlnJmkD5CPPw0FvDARX59msu\",\n        \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n        \"created\": 1730721411,\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18\",\n        \"choices\": [\n            {\n                \"index\": 0,\n                \"message\": {\n                    \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                    \"content\": \"Transitioning from a pole while moving into the push position can be a tricky skill to master, but with some focused practice, you can make it feel more natural and controlled. Many people struggle with this movement, often rushing through it or losing form. This tutorial will guide you through a technique that emphasizes control over speed, helping you build strength and confidence during the transition.nnThe main idea here is to practice a negative movement, which essentially means you\'ll be focusing on the downward phase of the transition. Start at the top position, and rather than dropping down quickly, take your time as you lower yourself. This slow descent is crucial—it allows you to maintain proper biomechanics while placing enough stress on your muscles to promote strength development. By doing this consistently, you’ll work on the transition from the top to the bottom smoothly, avoiding any abrupt movements or drops that could interfere with your stability and control.nnIn conclusion, mastering the transition from a pole to the push position involves intentionally practicing slow and controlled movements. This approach not only reinforces good mechanics but also enhances muscle engagement and strength. With patience and dedication, you’ll be able to move fluidly through this transition, making your technique much more polished and effective. Keep practicing, and remember that gradual improvement will lead to long-term results.nn- Focus on a slow and controlled downward movement to improve the transition.n- Practicing negatives helps maintain proper biomechanics and builds strength.n- Consistency is key—repeat the practice until you effortlessly transition from top to bottom.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 329,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 299,\n            \"total_tokens\": 628,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"tXHG3CjEg_FPJ9J.95PgrpjD7iaLkzn3kPRq1TFEiAs-1730721414-1.0.1.1-wq_uMS2Ra0JI7ONCY7XoO9R1N6dExQuUU3E9NKRlw.2BVpSUNlVHdDISQSfwuSQgmI.MLK8APe8_eP_WxsdD6Q\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723214,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"dys1EUHTvF7dXl9nQl_vEZ.rON5mPNpDwtjthBGNx9o-1730721414918-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(78263,55983,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(78264,55983,'video_transcript_chatgpt','Transitioning from a pole while moving into the push position can be a tricky skill to master, but with some focused practice, you can make it feel more natural and controlled. Many people struggle with this movement, often rushing through it or losing form. This tutorial will guide you through a technique that emphasizes control over speed, helping you build strength and confidence during the transition.\n\nThe main idea here is to practice a negative movement, which essentially means you\'ll be focusing on the downward phase of the transition. Start at the top position, and rather than dropping down quickly, take your time as you lower yourself. This slow descent is crucial—it allows you to maintain proper biomechanics while placing enough stress on your muscles to promote strength development. By doing this consistently, you’ll work on the transition from the top to the bottom smoothly, avoiding any abrupt movements or drops that could interfere with your stability and control.\n\nIn conclusion, mastering the transition from a pole to the push position involves intentionally practicing slow and controlled movements. This approach not only reinforces good mechanics but also enhances muscle engagement and strength. With patience and dedication, you’ll be able to move fluidly through this transition, making your technique much more polished and effective. Keep practicing, and remember that gradual improvement will lead to long-term results.\n\n- Focus on a slow and controlled downward movement to improve the transition.\n- Practicing negatives helps maintain proper biomechanics and builds strength.\n- Consistency is key—repeat the practice until you effortlessly transition from top to bottom.'),(78265,55983,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(78266,55983,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(78267,55983,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(78268,55983,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(78269,55983,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(78270,55984,'_thumbnail_id','14006'),(78271,55984,'video_id','SHsGl08OGng'),(78272,55984,'video_type','tutorial'),(78273,55984,'order_position','6'),(78274,55984,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"f-0O78wqecKnuGIegHdWxAiRD4c\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTktJVUhMckdzRTkwTXU5VXJZaERxTC5EMEEwRUY5M0RDRTU3NDJC\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-06-25T09:08:34Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Climbup #07 - Negative Front Grip Parkour Tutorial. [046]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Climbup (Beginner) in Parkour - Negative Front Grip.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Climbup Series. Episode #07nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Climbing #NegativeFrontGrip #---nnClimbups - Negative Front GripnnStarting to get a little spicier today... Now we are forcing ourselves to only use the front of the wall.nn1. If you keep automatically changing to Overgrip then find a flat top wall where there ISN\'T an overgrip to possibly use.nn2. Slow Transitions as always. Control all the way.nn3. In a few days I\'ll show you how to make all of these exercises scalable, so don\'t worry too much if you find it too hard.nn4. Repeat the stages of going onto your belly, and making sure BOTH elbows are going down at the same speed at the same time.nnAbove all else, have fun! nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing 