 It’s important to focus on transitioning progressively from the top to the bottom, ensuring that you’re fully engaging your muscles and not rushing through the exercise. Practicing this approach will help you get accustomed to using a normal grip effectively as you perform negatives.nnAs you incorporate this technique into your routine, remember that mastering the control and form is key. Taking your time with each movement will not only yield better results but also prevent injuries. So, keep practicing this grip variation, and you\'ll build both strength and confidence in your technique.nnHighlights: n- Use a front grip instead of a back edge for better control.n- Focus on transitioning smoothly from the top to the bottom.n- Emphasize control and form to enhance strength and prevent injuries.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 336,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 265,\n            \"total_tokens\": 601,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"xYHEONmtN14JQZutKsURpS6prGfWsI2lq.suC2D0VKo-1730721372-1.0.1.1-FncaP7_x3ysKajNMZdHfq1KE52YoSxm_EOHiq0y9sSfOIC89RaY7gjeuVzAdY5aY2JEtIO3nCmrqUUSHblVGcQ\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723172,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"RjsDOaLX2A.jIEKUmRp3i0lfNq66neoMyQx915SdunM-1730721372027-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(78287,55984,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(78288,55984,'video_transcript_chatgpt','In this tutorial, we’ll explore how to perform a negative grip exercise using a more conventional grip technique. This variation can be particularly useful when you don’t have the typical back edge for support. Learning to control your movements with this grip not only helps build strength but also enhances your overall technique in the exercise. \n\nTo start, take a position where you\'re facing a wall, but instead of holding onto the back edge, grab the front edge directly. This shift will challenge your stability and grip strength. Begin from a high position, then lower yourself slowly while maintaining control throughout the movement. It’s important to focus on transitioning progressively from the top to the bottom, ensuring that you’re fully engaging your muscles and not rushing through the exercise. Practicing this approach will help you get accustomed to using a normal grip effectively as you perform negatives.\n\nAs you incorporate this technique into your routine, remember that mastering the control and form is key. Taking your time with each movement will not only yield better results but also prevent injuries. So, keep practicing this grip variation, and you\'ll build both strength and confidence in your technique.\n\nHighlights: \n- Use a front grip instead of a back edge for better control.\n- Focus on transitioning smoothly from the top to the bottom.\n- Emphasize control and form to enhance strength and prevent injuries.'),(78289,55984,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','fiel