hether it\'s in climbing or a similar physical task, practice and form are key. This tutorial addresses a technique that involves pulling your body upwards efficiently. It’s designed for those who are looking to enhance their strength and control during this action. By focusing on your grip and aiming for evenness in using both arms, you\'ll improve your ability to execute the maneuver more effectively.nnStart by pulling yourself up while gripping only the front edge of the wall. This helps you to avoid what is known as an \"over grip,\" which can lead to strain or imbalance. Focus on pulling as high as you can, and remember that it’s okay if you can’t reach the top right away. The goal is to get comfortable with the pulling motion and to engage both arms simultaneously. As you practice, pay attention to your form and try to keep your movements symmetrical—this ensures you\'re building strength evenly on both sides of your body.nnIn conclusion, the essence of this technique is consistent practice and proper form. By avoiding an over grip and working towards a balanced pull with both arms, you’re setting yourself up for success. The more you practice this, the more natural it will feel, and you’ll likely see significant improvement in your overall performance. Keep challenging yourself, and remember that each little step gets you closer to your goal.nn- Focus on gripping the front edge of the wall for better control.n- Practice pulling up as high as possible, even if you can\'t reach the top right away.n- Ensure that you’re using both arms equally to build balanced strength.\",\n                    \"refusal\": null\n                },\n                \"logprobs\": null,\n                \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"usage\": {\n            \"prompt_tokens\": 328,\n            \"completion_tokens\": 318,\n            \"total_tokens\": 646,\n            \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n                \"cached_tokens\": 0\n            },\n            \"completion_tokens_details\": {\n                \"reasoning_tokens\": 0\n            }\n        },\n        \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_0ba0d124f1\"\n    },\n    \"response\": {\n        \"code\": 200,\n        \"message\": \"OK\"\n    },\n    \"cookies\": [\n        {\n            \"name\": \"__cf_bm\",\n            \"value\": \"GeyyC_p_a8xcv7l0i.5A8wBcviByFeb84eEoTuuRmzU-1730721353-1.0.1.1-ibxSFVILwVj467kzsvxkldAsEK.6TNXpOYyglL3NYHLADqKuMqzA3SoPa4fBpdCw7YYem8_DGmtaZ7i2i.Ohlg\",\n            \"expires\": 1730723153,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        },\n        {\n            \"name\": \"_cfuvid\",\n            \"value\": \"3xHoW3nVJ4ovaMRPACX7ItnoZRRSKEmISVCcOlNQKNo-1730721353131-0.0.1.1-604800000\",\n            \"expires\": null,\n            \"path\": \"/\",\n            \"domain\": \"api.openai.com\",\n            \"port\": null,\n            \"host_only\": false\n        }\n    ],\n    \"filename\": null,\n    \"http_response\": {\n        \"data\": null,\n        \"headers\": null,\n        \"status\": null\n    }\n}'),(78383,55988,'_video_transcript_chatgpt_json','field_67224645b19c3'),(78384,55988,'video_transcript_chatgpt','When it comes to mastering a particular technique, whether it\'s in climbing or a similar physical task, practice and form are key. This tutorial addresses a technique that involves pulling your body upwards efficiently. It’s designed for those who are looking to enhance their strength and control during this action. By focusing on your grip and aiming for evenness in using both arms, you\'ll improve your ability to execute the maneuver more effectively.\n\nStart by pulling yourself up while gripping only the front edge of the wall. This helps you to avoid what is known as an \"over grip,\" which can lead to strain or imbalance. Focus on pulling as high as you can, and remember that it’s okay if you can’t reach the top right away. The goal is to get comfortable with the pulling motion and to engage both arms simultaneously. As you practice, pay attention to your form and try to keep your movements symmetrical—this ensures you\'re building strength evenly on both sides of your body.\n\nIn conclusion, the essence of this technique is consistent practice and proper form. By avoiding an over grip and working towards a balanced pull with both arms, you’re setting yourself up for success. The more you practice this, the more natural it will feel, and you’ll likely see significant improvement in your overall performance. Keep challenging yourself, and remember that each little step gets you closer to your goal.\n\n- Focus on gripping the front edge of the wall for better control.\n- Practice pulling up as high as possible, even if you can\'t reach the top right away.\n- Ensure that you’re using both arms equally to build balanced strength.'),(78385,55988,'_video_transcript_chatgpt','field_67221db82655c'),(78386,55988,'portrait_thumnbail_filename',''),(78387,55988,'_portrait_thumnbail_filename','field_682053ef408b7'),(78388,55988,'landscape_thumnbail_filename',''),(78389,55988,'_landscape_thumnbail_filename','field_68205408408b8'),(78390,55989,'_thumbnail_id','14016'),(78391,55989,'video_id','VX4kdy51-dk'),(78392,55989,'video_type','tutorial'),(78393,55989,'order_position','11'),(78394,55989,'originalJSON','{\"kind\":\"youtube#playlistItem\",\"etag\":\"3y4ySIjOmJUV1RkE22QnQPvHLe4\",\"id\":\"UExsSWQ4SDdOa3hUTktJVUhMckdzRTkwTXU5VXJZaERxTC40NzZCMERDMjVEN0RFRThB\",\"snippet\":{\"publishedAt\":\"2019-06-25T09:08:34Z\",\"channelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\",\"title\":\"How to Climbup #12 - Overgrip Pull Parkour Tutorial. [051]\",\"description\":\"ud83cudf93 A Tutorial on how to Climbup (Beginner) in Parkour - Overgrip Pull.nud83eudd77 The Parkour Climbup Series. Episode #12nu25e3u25e5 https://LONDONPARKOUR.comnnu2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2571u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572u2572nu2003u2003#Climbing #OvergripPull #---nnClimbups - Overgrip Pull!nnToday we are just trying to pull without the negative. Using an overgrip and no slope now.nn1. Try to keep close to the wall as much as possible. You shouldn\'t be going away and then back in again. This is an indication that you\'re not using your arms enough to keep you towards the wall.nn2. Don\'t worry if you can\'t do this yet, there are plenty of progressions we\'ve gone through that will help you.nn3. ABSOLUTELY make sure both elbows are going up together. Remember correctly technique trumps EVERYTHING ELSE. It\'s better to not be able to get on than do it badly. We will be working strength exercises at some point, so don\'t worry. Just focus on technique!nn4. Remember to keep that foot high and I\'ve realised that it should be close to level with your hip, which logically makes sense if you want to push towards rather than down. n nn--- Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fX1d8knStep Vault Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3wV3ckvnBalancing Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cdWrlTnPullups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3g3cX9hnTurn Vaults Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3yUnM6cnCat Pass Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fG9qyEnClimbups Tutorial u25b6 https://bit.ly/2Rcs8VenCat Leaps Tutorial u25b6 https://bit. Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/2S1yBTinUnderbar Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/34FM7yInSwinging Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3cbcAbKnRolling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIuogAnJumping Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3fIfbMjnCrawling Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vIT7qpnClimbing Demo u25b6 https://bit.ly/3vNB6YinBalancing Demo u25b6 https://bit. to channel: https://youtube.com/londonparkour?sub_  https://LONDONPARKOUR.COMnnud83dudd39 Practical movement that will transform the way you think, move and feel.nnu26a1ufe0f https://pulse.localhost:8443nnud83euddea https://labs.localhost:8443nnud83dudd38 https://www.Instagram.com/london_parkournnud83dudd38 https://www.youtube.com/londonparkournnud83dudd38 contact@localhost:8443nnu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nu2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u25e3 u25e5u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588u2588nnu3000u3000   video features Parkour performed either by professionals or under the supervision of professionals. All members of LondonParkour have been training since 2005 and insist that no one attempt to re-create or re-enact any ROOFTOP activities or movement AT HEIGHT performed in these videos. Do not use private property.nnu2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501u2501n{{Tag:beginner}}{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Climbing}}{{Series:Climbups Tips}}\"\",\"thumbnails\":{\"default\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VX4kdy51-dk/default.jpg\",\"width\":120,\"height\":90},\"medium\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VX4kdy51-dk/mqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":320,\"height\":180},\"high\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VX4kdy51-dk/hqdefault.jpg\",\"width\":480,\"height\":360},\"standard\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VX4kdy51-dk/sddefault.jpg\",\"width\":640,\"height\":480},\"maxres\":{\"url\":\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VX4kdy51-dk/maxresdefault.jpg\",\"width\":1280,\"height\":720}},\"channelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"playlistId\":\"PLlId8H7NkxTNKIUHLrGsE90Mu9UrYhDqL\",\"position\":11,\"resourceId\":{\"kind\":\"youtube#video\",\"videoId\":\"VX4kdy51-dk\"},\"videoOwnerChannelTitle\":\"London Parkour\",\"videoOwnerChannelId\":\"UCufdBIaXEIQwIX0CMGbXdAw\"},\"index\":11}'),(78395,55989,'scrapeId','Tutorial - Climbups'),(78396,55989,'authId','Auth1'),(78397,55989,'searchId','Tutorials - Climbups'),(78398,55989,'filterId','none'),(78399,55989,'mapperId','YouTube - Playlist Items - Tutorial Videos'),(78400,55989,'importId','Tutorial Videos - Climbups'),(78401,55989,'_edit_last','1'),(78402,55989,'video_transcript_chatgpt','In this tutorial, we’re going to focus on enhancing your climbing technique by transitioning from using a slope to efficiently pulling yourself up without any aids. This shift will help improve your strength and body positioning, making you more capable on various climbing surfaces. If you’ve been reliant on slopes or tools like a flyboard, this is the perfect opportunity to challenge yourself and refine your skills.\n\nTo get started, you\'ll want to ensure your footing is correct. Place your feet firmly on the wall, focusing on aligning your body for optimal pulling. Begin with an over grip on the holds above you and utilize your legs effectively to push yourself upwards. As you climb, aim to engage your core by bringing your belly closer to the wall, shifting your weight into a dip position similar to a push-up. This technique emphasizes pulling from your arms while maintaining strength in your legs and core, allowing for a smooth ascent without the aid of slopes.\n\nBy practicing this method, you\'ll develop greater upper body strength and enhance your climbing efficiency. The transition from slope use to pulling techniques can be challenging, but with consistency, you’ll notice significant improvements in your climbing performance. Remember, focusing on your grip, foot placement, and body alignment are key to mastering this climbing style.\n\n- Transition to climbing without slopes to enhance strength and technique.\n- Focus on foot placement and proper grip when climbing.\n- Engage your core and shift your wei