:52:38','',0,'http://localhost:8102/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/G5ghqaCQGMo.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(14599,1,'2024-10-21 17:01:38','2024-10-21 17:01:38','🎓 A Tutorial on Step Vaults for Beginners\n🥷 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 001\n◣◥ https://LONDONPARKOUR.com\n\n#Vaulting #Step-Vault #Parkour\n\n## Step Vault 1 - Basics\n---\n### Intro\n\nThis is called the **Step Vault** or also known as the Safety Vault\nThe step vault\'s primary purpose is to allow maximum confidence overcoming an obstacle, \nMoving both safely and securely.\n\n### The TL;DR\n\nIt is usually the vault that is taught first to beginners because of it\'s simplicity. \nHowever this vault is hugely versatile and can have a huge range of variations.\n\n### The goal\n\nThe key is control and security, so the more confidence you feel with this vault, the better because it will always be your fall-back vault. \nAdvanced practitioners will also use this vault, especially when they\'re feeling uncomfortable or want maximum reliability. So nailing this down to perfection is in your best interest.\n\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nYou do not need any previous experience and very little movement ability to complete this vault at its basic level.\nHowever, having the mobility to be able to get a foot and hand on the obstacle will be nessesary.\n\n### Getting started\n\n1. General Joint Mobility should be encouraged. It\'ll loosen up the joints and help with being able to reach better ranges of motion under control\n2. Thorough warmups should always be done. Unless you are a top-level athlete who monitors their performance, you should start slow but gradually move into going hard on the warmups.\n3. Shoulders and arms with QM / Diving Monkeys / Pushups\n4. Hip mobility with squats / lunges / burpees / jumps.\n\n\n\n\n\n## Basics\n\n### The setup\n\nYou need to find a wall roughly thigh to hip height. This is the general height for starting vaults.\n\nWe are going to do everything with as much control as possible, which means nice and slow.\n\n\n### Both Hands & Foot\n\nStart with both hands and a single foot to create space under you.\nYou want them a good distance apart to create a large space.\n\n### Hand position\n\nStart with placing both hands on the wall first. Hands facing forward, have a good grip, flat, with the whole palm on the wall.\nThe pressure should be in the middle of the hand to ball of thumb.\n\n### Foot\n\nPlace one of your feet onto the wall, with the ball of the foot. Facing forwards. \n\n### Foot trajectory\n\nTry to place the foot up and over the wall, not straight at it. This is to prevent clipping the foot on the corner. As your accuracy improves, this can be refined, but for the beginning, over exaggerate the movement.\n\n### Hips\n\nPush your hips up high behind you. \nAgain, you are trying to create space, so less mobility means higher hips.\n\n### Inside leg\n\nThis is the Tricky part - Remove the inside arm and push the back leg through the gap between the stabilising hand and foot. \n\n### Landing\n\nPlace the ball of that foot down on the ground, controlled and slow.\n\n### Direction\n\nMove forward in a straight line, removing the hand/foot on the wall behind you.\n\n### Cue 1 - straight\n\nStraight line - For now, remember that you are travelling in a straight line, so pick a point in front of you and head towards it.\n\n### Cue 2 - space\n\nThe amount of space under you is key. Either you have good mobility, or you create more space to get the trailing leg through.\n\n### Cue 3 - weight distribution\n\nAt the beginning, try to move your weight so that it\'s more towards your arms.\n\n---\n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}} Step Vault','Step Vault Basics - Parkour Tutorials - 001 image','','inherit','open','closed','','step-vault-basics-parkour-tutorials-001-image','','','2024-10-21 17:01:38','2024-10-21 17:01:38','',0,'http://localhost:8102/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/2g9XgPf5Bg.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(14601,1,'2024-10-21 17:01:38','2024-10-21 17:01:38','🎓 A Tutorial on Step Vault Progressions for Beginners\n🥷 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 002\n◣◥ https://LONDONPARKOUR.com\n\n#Vaults #StepVault #Parkour\n\n## Variations\n---\nOrdered by difficulty, in this video we are going to look at over a dozen variations on the step vault. \n\n### Beginner 1 - Both hands\n\nThis is the one done in the basics video. Use two hands and one foot to get yourself over an obstacle.\n\n### Beginner 2 - One hand\n\nThis is the standard step vault that everyone will recognise - the one hand, one foot version.\nSame as the two foot one, but without using the inside hand on the wall at all. \nYou may need a little hop to get the foot up on the wall now.\n\n### Beginner 3 - floor\n\nThis is safer, but harder. Do the one foot, one handed version on the floor. This will help with confidence, mobility and balance.\n\n### Beginner 4 - bad side\n\nThe one handed version of the vault but performed with the less other side.\nThis is a variation that you should always do to maximise versatility - sometimes the environment will dictate that you can only do a move on one side - so best get comfortable using either.\n\n### Beginner 5 - momentum\n\nStart with one step back and move into the step vault with a constant speed (not fast). Once happy, switch to the other side.\nNext take two steps and repeat. Keep increasing steps as you feel comfortable with the amount of momentum generated.\nHowever - be aware of wasting the momentum by pausing or slowing before the wall.\n\n### Intermediate 1 - Different surfaces\n\nStart investigating the use of different surfaces. I\'m talking about shapes and materials. From concrete brick to metal railings and everything else in between.\nYou want to start being able to distinguish how different materials are going to behave and react. Wood, metal, types of brick; all have to be treated slightly differently.\nAs an addition to this one, you can use the weather to your advantage and use it as another variable to change the surfaces. The same obstacle in the wet, cold, hot, dry, icy, all make things different.\nFor instance, we know that wet wood will no-doubt be slippery. Crumbly dry brick with dust may also need care...\n\n### Intermediate 2 - Chaining\n\nStart to practice chaining multiple step vaults together with two or three steps in between. \nLearning how to set yourself up with the correct hand and foot placement from one to another will help create flow.\nThis will also get you to start understanding the importance of being  accurate with those positions.\n\n### Intermediate 3 - Consistency\n\nThis one can be misunderstood for going fast. What we are looking for is a consistent movement speed, which can be slow, but has less fluctuations of that speed.\nFor instance, the entry and exit of the vault are usually easier and quicker than the middle part of getting over the wall. Try to remain active and contracted the whole way, without any relaxing of the muscles at any point.\nFilm yourself and watch it to see when any of the \"sticking\" points are. \n\n### Intermediate 4 - Slow and Controlled\n\nPerform the step vault as slow as you can and then hold the position where the back leg has passed through, but not touched the ground yet.\nExtend the leg straight, hold your balance for 10seconds and reverse the movement to the beginning again.\n\n### Advanced 1 - Speed\n\nThe \"Speed Step\" is a slightly different variation of the step vault and deserves its own video. However, performing the normal step vault at speed is a good skill to acquire.\nSlowly practice increasing your speed from walking pace to jogging into / out of it, to having the confidence of going at sprinting speed.\n\n### Advanced 2 - Flow\n\nGoing fast, over multiple obstacles, of different types, without planning your foot placement and keep a consistent speed is good practice for when the terrain and environment is unknown.\nBeing able to adapt to what is coming up without preparation will make you more capable and practical.\n\n### Advanced 3 - Height\n\nThis is obviously dependent of your own height, but try to use you arms to pull you into a much higher step vault. \nHelpful on higher railings and the beginnings of climb-ups.\n\n### Advanced 4 - Isometric\n\nThe same as the intermediate variation of the slow and controlled, however, this is at height and on a railing. A goal post, top of a swing set, scaffolding or anything else. \nThis will really tax your muscular control, adrenaline and fear. A great way of fine-tuning those positions and body awareness.\n\n### Advanced 5 - Embrace the weird\n\nThe last variation is to go out and challenge yourself to find any weird and strange obstacles that you don\'t know whether you can step vault or not.\nCan you step vault through small gaps? At slanted angles? With backpacks on? Barefoot? In the dark? Up high? Underground? On a chain? A moving gate? The list is endless... Try challenge yourself with everything.\n---\n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}} Step Vault','Step Vault Progressions - Parkour Tutorials - 002 image','','inherit','open','closed','','step-vault-progressions-parkour-tutorials-002-image','','','2024-10-21 17:01:38','2024-10-21 17:01:38','',0,'http://localhost:8102/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/8LR6aA2JzsY.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(14603,1,'2024-10-21 17:01:38','2024-10-21 17:01:38','🎓 A Tutorial on Step Vault Common Faults for Beginners\n🥷 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 003\n◣◥ https://LONDONPARKOUR.com\n\n#Vaults #StepVault #Parkour\n\n## Common Faults\n---\nIn this video we\'ll discuss and demonstrate some of the common faults made when starting out with the step vault.\n\nYou can use this as a troubleshooting guide to help any difficulties you may be having.\n\n\n### Fault 1 - Steepled Fingers\n\nThis happens If you cannot create the correct body structure to create enough space under yourself. \n\nWithout thinking about it, the fingers are sometimes steepled up to increase the torso height.\n\nThis leaves the fingers susceptible to stubbing them and too much body weight on the finger tendons. Not to mention over bending if they\'re unable to hold you up.\n\nThis can also be an indicator of strength / mobility issues. And it also means that you\'ll have difficulty when you want to move onto vaulting railings and thinner obstacles.\n\n#### Fix\n\nHave flat hands and increasing hip height or mobility.\n\n\n### Fault 2 - Weight Distribution towards the leg\n\nMoving too much towards the leg usually means you\'re feeling uncomfortable taking your bodyweight on the arms and upper-body. \n\n#### Fix\n\nStart practicing on the floor to create the structure for confidence.\n\nYou can also practice using two arms to get into the position. Do NOT move your feet and hands once they\'re placed on the wall.\n\n\n### Fault 3 - Foot & Hand too close\n\nWith the foot and hand being too close, you will have to create enough space under yourself by raising hips far too high. Alternatively, you\'ll have to have great mobility. \n\n#### Fix\n\nIf this is the case,  spread the feet/hands out more and make visible marks on the wall as targets.\n\n\n#### Fault 4 - Space underneath\n\nIf the space underneath is still too small, it means your mobility needs work or your feet and hands are too close.\n\n#### Fix\n\nSpend more time at the bottom of a squat position. Try practicing deep lunges, crane positions and sideways monkeys.\n\n\n### Fault 5 - Knee collapse\n\nThe knee can collapse and all weight placed onto the hands because you don\'t want to raise your hips higher (which, in turn, leans them forwards - which won\'t feel comfortable.)\n\n#### Fix\n\nGet the knee pointing upwards and use both the arms and leg to push the bodyweight higher.\n\nSometimes it\'s hard to even realise what the problem is, so film yourself to spot the mistake.\n\nMobility work will also help to open up the hips. Deep squat position and pushing knees out will start to work this.\n\n\n### Fault 6  - Foot facing outwards\n\nThe foot position is key to where the hips will be facing and where the weight will be distributed. If the foot is pointed at an angle, the hips will have difficulty getting high enough.\n\nKeep the foot facing fowards, pressure towards the ball of the foot and keep it stable, active, tense, and solid.\n\n### Fault 7 - Wrong motivations\n\nHaving the right motivations is key. \n\nObtaining clean and refined technique is what will help you become better when other variables are introduced. (Usually speed, flow, chaining, height, variations, etc...) However, most advanced practitioners usually wish they had spent time refining the basics when they first started - don\'t fall into the trap of introducing more complicated things before being able to do the simple ones.\n\n### Fix\n\nCreate clear objectives and goals for yourself to benchmark your progress. Write down what you can do and what you struggle with.  Identifying, highlighting and fixing those weaknesses will pay tenfold in the future.\n\n### Fault 8 - Rotation\n\nYou are rotating around the arm and moving in a curve, rather than going in a straight line. This means you are using the arm as a pivot point too much.\n\nThis is not to say that you can\'t move in this way, it\'s just that it should come as a later variation - and when the situation arises. Nailing down the straight line should be the priority.\n\n### Fix\n\nDraw a line perpendicular to the wall on both sides with chalk. Try to take off and land alond the line.\n\n\n### Fault 9 - Two Foot Landings\n\nUnless the environment dictates a two-foot landing, or you are landing in a precision, you should be able to keep the momentum going in the same direction. \n\nA two foot landing will stop the momentum.\n\n#### Fix\n\nLand one foot only and then step to the next, moving in a straight line.\n\n\n### Fault 10 - Granularity\n\nIf you are unable to do the current movement then the step between the previous version and the one you\'re currently trying to do is too great.\n\n#### Fix\n\nFind a mid-way point on the difficulty. Take a regression step back try something slightly different and then retry.\n\n---\n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}} Step Vault','Step Vault Common Faults - Parkour Tutorials - 003 image','','inherit','open','closed','','step-vault-common-faults-parkour-tutorials-003-image','','','2024-10-21 17:01:38','2024-10-21 17:01:38','',0,'http://localhost:8102/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/hKDhQaYRke4.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(14605,1,'2024-10-21 17:01:39','2024-10-21 17:01:39','🎓 A Tutorial on Step Vault Technical Details for Beginners\n🥷 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 004\n◣◥ https://LONDONPARKOUR.com\n\n#Vaults #StepVault #Parkour\n---\n### 🦾Biomechanics\n\nParkour movements are very complex biomechanically because they go through multiple phases and change constantly. In this video, we\'re going to try to dissect the movement down into its component parts.\n\nFirstly, The step-vault is a Contralateral movement - Meaning that opposite limbs are used.\n\nWe can break down this vault into three steps:\n1. Entry - This is generally a short concentric (shorten), then eccentric (lengthening of muscles) to get onto the wall.  \n2. Hold - Isometric hold to create a body structure that will allow us to balance on one hand / foot.\n3. Exit - Lastly, a concentric off the back limbs and eccentric on the front leg.\nThe main muscle groups will depend on situation and environment, but you will find that the triceps / deltoids for arms, quads / hip-flexors for legs.\n\n### 🧎‍♂️ R.O.M. And mobility\n\nThe Step Vault doesn\'t require a huge amount of range to be effective, which is one of the reasons for its low requirements to perform. \nHowever, having better range of motion gives you more adaptability to smaller spaces and awkward scenarios.\nMobility is the range of motion (flexibilty) of the limbs while having power and control. \nIf you cant get into the correct positions, then mobility is usually the issue.\n\n\n### ⚙️ Torque / Tension\n\nThe pushing action requires an internal torque (tension) on the shoulder. No rotation is required though.  The legs will also have an internal tension for stability.\nRemember, it matters what muscles are doing the work, not what LOOKS like it doing the work. Feel which muscles are working and contracting.\n\n### 🏹 Trajectory\n\nThe initial idea is to move in a straight line with minimal up/down motion. \"Efficiency\" really just means less wild, unnecessary, movement and using the power generated to do the job needed.\nThe position of the head is also a good indication of tension, contraction and efficiency. Try to minimalise the head moving up and down.\n\n### 👣 Body Position\n\nHip stability and control from the lower abs/erectors/glutes play a large part in positioning. Hips should always be working to be facing forwards and driving towards the straight line path. \nA strong and stable body structure will give you confidence in performing. \nThere should always be tension within the whole movement. Try not to release at any stage. A contract-release-contract action will take more time and loose power and control.\n\n### 🫁 Breathing\n\nIf you have issues holding the body structure / shape, then try holding your breath during the holding-phase. This will create inter-abdominal pressure and spinal stability.\nSimilar to power lifters, both the concentric and eccentric  phase needs full stability. You can breathe out on the eccentric part of the movement (exiting the vault).\nThis will also help remove the contract-release-contract mistake. \n\n### 🧘‍♂️ Recovery work\n\nMake sure you spend time on recovery work. The primary muscle groups to focus on for stretching (which is always done after the training), are:\n\n1.  Triceps - from the the arms are opening on the push. \n2.  Deltoids - from stabilisation\n3.  Forearms - from hold the body position while the hand is pulled backwards.\n4.  Calfs - From holding the foot position on the ball of the foot.\n5.  Quads - From the stabilisation of the body in the isometric position.\n\nYou can also use Myofacial Release with a foam roller or ball to also focus on these areas. \n\n### ⚖️ Imbalances\n\nMake sure you spend MORE time on the weaker side. Otherwise over time you\'ll build up more and more imbalances within your body. \nAt best, this can lead to being less functional within certain situations and at worst it can cause postural issues and difficulty on hard vaults when more stress is applied. \n(For instance, a lot of people start to notice these with cat-passes, climbups and muscle-ups - where the stronger, more dominant side of the body makes it really hard to stay straight and unilateral. ) \n\n\n### 🤕 Common injuries\n\nAn uncommon topic for people to acknowledge and talk about, but it\'s wise to be aware of the most common mistakes and injuries made.\n\n1.  Clipping the foot.\nThe foot clips the wall because it hasn\'t been raised high enough and the person falls forward over the wall. Injury to the shin and wrist (from catching themselves on the floor) are the most common.\n    \n2.  Hand slips off front of the obstacle.\nMisjudgement of the hand position accuracy, usually from going too fast or with reduced concentration can cause the person to slip and go head-first over the obstacle.\n    \n3.  Catching your knee.\nLastly, this comes from not raising the hips up high enough and not judging the required amount of space and mobility needed to get the back leg through. \n---\n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}} Step Vault','Step Vault Technical Details - Parkour Tutorials - 004 image','','inherit','open','closed','','step-vault-technical-details-parkour-tutorials-004-image','','','2024-10-21 17:01:39','2024-10-21 17:01:39','',0,'http://localhost:8102/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/PaRVcCoGrFQ.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(14607,1,'2024-10-21 17:01:41','2024-10-21 17:01:41','🎓 A Tutorial on Speed Step and Reverse Step\n🥷 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 005\n◣◥ https://LONDONPARKOUR.com\n\n#Vaults #StepVault #Parkour\n---\n##   Variations\n\n### Speed step\n\nThe variation that is used more frequently by more advanced practitioners because it requires a lot more momentum. \n\nEssentially, the foot leads the vault, rather than the hand. \n\nThe primary reason for this is because the leg muscles are a lot bigger and stronger than the upper body muscles of the arms. This means they can take a lot more impact and control the momentum much better.\n\nBy jumping into the step vault, with the foot landing first, you can be sure that you\'re able to take the increased force through the leg.\n\nSecondly, it means that the head is not leading the movement and this can be a lot more dangerous and non recommended in a number of scenarios that i\'ll demonstrate later in the video.\n\nLastly, with this added control, you\'ll be able to move faster with more confidence.\n\n\n### HowTo\n\nFirstly, you want to aim for a jump onto the wall, with a single foot landing slightly to the side and in-front of you. \n\nIt\'s important to note that the following leg now does not trail behind. Instead it will also lead ahead, but without making contact with the wall.\nAs the foot lands on the wall, using the ball of the foot, the second leg should already be ahead of the wall.\nThe opposite hand will then land last and help to keep positioning and direction correct. It\'s there to stabilise you and take some of the remaining downward body weight onto the wall.\n\n### Downwards\n\nAs you can see, this method requires the momentum and power to almost completely jump over the wall already and starts to look like a dash vault rather than a step vault.\n\nNot only does the speed step allow you to move much quicker over the wall, it also allows for the ability to go downwards. \n\nTraditionally, in this position of a level-to-level wall, a normal step vault, with the hand leading first, would require the arm to take a lot of impact and the head to go first. As I mentioned earlier, this is one of those situations where you really don\'t want to be going fast, downwards with the head first. \n\nThe speed step is much more useful vault here. With the foot leading and taking the impact, you can safely do the step vault downwards.\n\nThere is an extra added benefit of this movement in a specific scenario - where you have multiple downward levels. \n\nIt\'s much easier to chain speed steps together, staying on the same side and foot, hopping down as if you are doing multiple one-leg pistols, strides or cranes. Note that you can also alternate sides as well.  Because the leg is taking the impact you can slow yourself down and regulate the momentum much easier if needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Reverse Step Vault\n\nAs the name implies, the reverse step vault is performing the exact same movement, but in reverse.\n\nThis isn\'t as common or as useful as the speed step, but a good tool to have when you\'re working on flowing routes. \n\nThe reason this is helpful is because you don\'t need to turn around to perform a forwards based vault. By being able to accurately step vault backwards allows you to land from a previous vault and instantly perform a second wthout breaking the flow.\n\nLet me show you an example here:\n\nImagine you\'ve done a vault over the wall, landed two feet. But to continue the flow, you\'ll have to either turn or explosively return backwards, like this:\n\n### Learning\n\nThe thing about the reverse step vault is that you\'re blind to the obstacle and getting the foot up onto the wall will be a tricky thing to do. It\'s going to require more spacial awareness from feeling where the wall is with your hand.\n\nTo start training this, you can do the following things:\n\n### 1. Forwards First.\n\nDo a normal step vault, and without leaving the hand / foot position, reverse the movement.\n\n### 2. Use a low / wide obstacle.\n\nStart with your hand on the obstacle and you jump up onto the wall. Then step through and backwards. You can slowly increase the height as you feel more comfortable.\n\n### 3. Go slow, with no jump.\n\nTry without any momentum and slowly perform the reverse step vault without jumping into the position.\n\n### 3. Jump\n\nWhen you reversing backwards, jump much higher than the wall and come down onto it. The last thing you want is to clip you heel on the wall and go backwards. Jump up, over and down.\n\nThis will also give you more time to position the foot for the landing.\n\n### 4. Hand\n\nAlso rely more heavily on the arm with your bodyweight towards it. This means that there is less impact coming down onto the foot AND you\'re not having to commit your balance and position on correctly guessing the position of your landing.\n\nIf you misjudge, then the arm will more likely catch you rather than commiting all of your body to the leg and foot before landing.\n\n---\n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}} Step Vault','Step Vault Variations - Parkour Tutorials - 005 image','','inherit','open','closed','','step-vault-variations-parkour-tutorials-005-image','','','2024-10-21 17:01:41','2024-10-21 17:01:41','',0,'http://localhost:8102/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/G_-mMmQYy90.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(14614,1,'2024-10-21 17:03:11','2024-10-21 17:03:11','🎓 A Tutorial on Step Vaults for Beginners\n🥷 The Parkour Vaulting Series. Episode 001\n◣◥ https://LONDONPARKOUR.com\n\n#Vaulting #Step-Vault #Parkour\n\n## Step Vault 1 - Basics\n---\n### Intro\n\nThis is called the **Step Vault** or also known as the Safety Vault\nThe step vault\'s primary purpose is to allow maximum confidence overcoming an obstacle, \nMoving both safely and securely.\n\n### The TL;DR\n\nIt is usually the vault that is taught first to beginners because of it\'s simplicity. \nHowever this vault is hugely versatile and can have a huge range of variations.\n\n### The goal\n\nThe key is control and security, so the more confidence you feel with this vault, the better because it will always be your fall-back vault. \nAdvanced practitioners will also use this vault, especially when they\'re feeling uncomfortable or want maximum reliability. So nailing this down to perfection is in your best interest.\n\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nYou do not need any previous experience and very little movement ability to complete this vault at its basic level.\nHowever, having the mobility to be able to get a foot and hand on the obstacle will be nessesary.\n\n### Getting started\n\n1. General Joint Mobility should be encouraged. It\'ll loosen up the joints and help with being able to reach better ranges of motion under control\n2. Thorough warmups should always be done. Unless you are a top-level athlete who monitors their performance, you should start slow but gradually move into going hard on the warmups.\n3. Shoulders and arms with QM / Diving Monkeys / Pushups\n4. Hip mobility with squats / lunges / burpees / jumps.\n\n\n\n\n\n## Basics\n\n### The setup\n\nYou need to find a wall roughly thigh to hip height. This is the general height for starting vaults.\n\nWe are going to do everything with as much control as possible, which means nice and slow.\n\n\n### Both Hands & Foot\n\nStart with both hands and a single foot to create space under you.\nYou want them a good distance apart to create a large space.\n\n### Hand position\n\nStart with placing both hands on the wall first. Hands facing forward, have a good grip, flat, with the whole palm on the wall.\nThe pressure should be in the middle of the hand to ball of thumb.\n\n### Foot\n\nPlace one of your feet onto the wall, with the ball of the foot. Facing forwards. \n\n### Foot trajectory\n\nTry to place the foot up and over the wall, not straight at it. This is to prevent clipping the foot on the corner. As your accuracy improves, this can be refined, but for the beginning, over exaggerate the movement.\n\n### Hips\n\nPush your hips up high behind you. \nAgain, you are trying to create space, so less mobility means higher hips.\n\n### Inside leg\n\nThis is the Tricky part - Remove the inside arm and push the back leg through the gap between the stabilising hand and foot. \n\n### Landing\n\nPlace the ball of that foot down on the ground, controlled and slow.\n\n### Direction\n\nMove forward in a straight line, removing the hand/foot on the wall behind you.\n\n### Cue 1 - straight\n\nStraight line - For now, remember that you are travelling in a straight line, so pick a point in front of you and head towards it.\n\n### Cue 2 - space\n\nThe amount of space under you is key. Either you have good mobility, or you create more space to get the trailing leg through.\n\n### Cue 3 - weight distribution\n\nAt the beginning, try to move your weight so that it\'s more towards your arms.\n\n---\n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Tax:Vaulting}}{{Tax:Step Vault}}{{Tag:beginner}} Step Vault','Step Vault Basics - Parkour Tutorials - 001 image','','inherit','open','closed','','step-vault-basics-parkour-tutorials-001-image-2','','','2024-10-21 17:03:11','2024-10-21 17:03:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/2g9XgPf5Bg-1.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(14620,1,'2024-10-21 17:05:16','2024-10-21 17:05:16','🎓 A Tutorial on Quadrupedal Movement for Beginners\n🥷 The Parkour Crawling Series. Episode 006\n◣◥ https://LONDONPARKOUR.com\n\n#Vaults #QM #Parkour\n\n{{PostType:Tutorial}}{{Crawling}}{{Tax:QM}}{{Tag:beginner}} QM Foundational','QM Basics. Parkour Tutorials - 006 image','','inherit','open','closed','','qm-basics-parkour-tutorials-006-image-2','','','2024-10-21 17:05:16','2024-10-21 17:05:16','',0,'http://localhost:8102/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/12k8cp3f2E-1.jpg',0,'attachment','i