\";s:13:\"new_item_name\";s:21:\"New Tutorial Tag Name\";s:12:\"search_items\";s:20:\"Search Tutorial Tags\";s:13:\"popular_items\";s:21:\"Popular Tutorial Tags\";s:26:\"separate_items_with_commas\";s:34:\"Separate tutorial tags with commas\";s:19:\"add_or_remove_items\";s:27:\"Add or remove tutorial tags\";s:21:\"choose_from_most_used\";s:39:\"Choose from the most used tutorial tags\";s:9:\"most_used\";s:0:\"\";s:9:\"not_found\";s:22:\"No tutorial tags found\";s:8:\"no_terms\";s:16:\"No tutorial tags\";s:22:\"name_field_description\";s:0:\"\";s:22:\"slug_field_description\";s:0:\"\";s:22:\"desc_field_description\";s:0:\"\";s:21:\"items_list_navigation\";s:29:\"Tutorial Tags list navigation\";s:10:\"items_list\";s:18:\"Tutorial Tags list\";s:13:\"back_to_items\";s:23:\"← Go to tutorial tags\";s:9:\"item_link\";s:17:\"Tutorial Tag Link\";s:21:\"item_link_description\";s:24:\"A link to a tutorial tag\";}s:11:\"description\";s:0:\"\";s:12:\"capabilities\";a:4:{s:12:\"manage_terms\";s:17:\"manage_categories\";s:10:\"edit_terms\";s:17:\"manage_categories\";s:12:\"delete_terms\";s:17:\"manage_categories\";s:12:\"assign_terms\";s:10:\"edit_posts\";}s:6:\"public\";i:1;s:18:\"publicly_queryable\";i:1;s:12:\"hierarchical\";i:0;s:7:\"show_ui\";i:1;s:12:\"show_in_menu\";i:1;s:17:\"show_in_nav_menus\";i:1;s:12:\"show_in_rest\";i:1;s:9:\"rest_base\";s:0:\"\";s:14:\"rest_namespace\";s:5:\"wp/v2\";s:21:\"rest_controller_class\";s:24:\"WP_REST_Terms_Controller\";s:13:\"show_tagcloud\";i:1;s:18:\"show_in_quick_edit\";i:1;s:17:\"show_admin_column\";i:0;s:7:\"rewrite\";a:3:{s:17:\"permalink_rewrite\";s:12:\"taxonomy_key\";s:10:\"with_front\";s:1:\"1\";s:20:\"rewrite_hierarchical\";s:1:\"0\";}s:9:\"query_var\";s:13:\"post_type_key\";s:14:\"query_var_name\";s:0:\"\";s:12:\"default_term\";a:1:{s:20:\"default_term_enabled\";s:1:\"0\";}s:4:\"sort\";i:0;s:8:\"meta_box\";s:7:\"default\";s:11:\"meta_box_cb\";s:0:\"\";s:20:\"meta_box_sanitize_cb\";s:0:\"\";s:15:\"allow_ai_access\";b:0;s:14:\"ai_description\";s:0:\"\";s:8:\"modified\";i:1786001410;}','Tutorial Tags','tutorial-tags','publish','closed','closed','','taxonomy_6a7437f050205','','','2026-08-06 11:52:42','2026-08-06 11:52:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=acf-taxonomy&p=55155',0,'acf-taxonomy','',0),(55156,1,'2021-11-26 13:44:32','2021-11-26 13:44:32','Perform two speed-steps in a row with a maximum of three steps in between both movements. The walls must be hip-height or greater and no wider than a standard brick-length.\n\nEach speed-step must have the foot make contact with the wall first with the hand second. \n\nYou can perform either an alternating speed-step where the second movement is the opposite of the first, or both are the same side. However, you must demonstrate the same number of vaults on each side. (I.e. there must be a minimum of 12 vaults in total, 6 with the left-foot and right-hand. 6 with the right-foot and left-hand.)\n\nThe hand must be a flat palm facing forwards.\n','Two in a Row','','publish','closed','closed','','two-in-a-row','','','2021-11-26 13:44:32','2021-11-26 13:44:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55156',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55157,1,'2021-11-26 13:51:01','2021-11-26 13:51:01','Perform any vault (Cat-Pass / slide / step / side / turn / etc... ) on a hip-height obstacle, but the landing must be a speed-step where one-foot lands first, followed by the hand, vaulting out.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate you can perform a speed-step exit from a previous vault that allows you to continue the flow of movement.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate three reps of both vaults on both sides in a constant momentum.','Vault Landing','','publish','closed','closed','','vault-landing','','','2021-11-26 13:51:01','2021-11-26 13:51:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55157',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55158,1,'2021-11-26 14:50:27','2021-11-26 14:50:27','Precede the speed-step with a broad-jump of a minimum of six steps (heel-to-toe) or greater. You must land in the speed-step with one foot and the opposite hand. \r\n\r\nThe broad jump must be a two-foot standing jump onto a wall that that is at least hip-height or greater and a maximum width of brick-length or less.\r\nThe take-off can be from any position or obstacle. The broad jump must be onto a hip-height wall for the speed-step.\r\n\r\nThe speed step can be downwards off the target wall to facilitate an easier wall-to-wall broad jump environment.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate three speed-steps on each side. The palm of the hand must be facing forwards and be flat.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n','Jump Step','','publish','closed','closed','','jump-step','','','2021-11-26 14:50:27','2021-11-26 14:50:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55158',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55159,1,'2021-11-26 15:04:06','2021-11-26 15:04:06','You must demonstrate two speed-steps with a maximum of three paces in-between on rails rather than walls. This must be performed at jogging speed or faster.\n\nThe double speed step vaults can bee either the same hand and foot combination on both or alternate sides. However you must demonstrate three combinations on each side for a total of twelve vaults (six on each side).\n\nThe speed steps must be performed at jogging-speed or faster and have the palm facing forwards and around the rail.\n\nThe rails must be hip-height or greater, circular, and a maximum of scaffolding width or less.','Rail Doubles','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-doubles','','','2021-11-26 15:04:06','2021-11-26 15:04:06','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55159',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55160,1,'2021-11-26 15:11:51','2021-11-26 15:11:51','Perform two or more speed steps straight after each other, landing in the next speed-step on the opposite side.\r\n\r\nFor example, if the first vault is a right-foot/left-hand movement, the second foot (left) is the one you will land on. You will land on this left foot and directly drop the right-hand into the second speed-step.\r\n\r\nYou will need to demonstrate this on multiple obstacles going downwards to allow you to move into the subsequent vault.\r\n\r\nThere are no parameters for wall-heights or widths. You can perform this going down large steps or any other setup as long as you land into a second speed-step.\r\n\r\nYou must perform the double movement three times on each side.','Alternating Down','','publish','closed','closed','','alternating-down','','','2021-11-26 15:11:51','2021-11-26 15:11:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55160',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55161,1,'2021-11-26 15:22:13','2021-11-26 15:22:13','Replicating the level-9 award, you must perform two or more speed-steps in a row going downwards. However, instead of switching to the opposite side on each vault, you will now switch and stay on the same side, performing two of the exact same vault.\r\n\r\nYou must perform the two vaults in a row for three reps on both sides.\r\n\r\nThere are no parameters of wall heights or widths. You can perform this going down large steps or any other setup as long as you land into a second speed-step.\r\n\r\nYou must perform this at normal movement speed.','Continuous Down','','publish','closed','closed','','continuous-down','','','2021-11-26 15:22:13','2021-11-26 15:22:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55161',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55162,1,'2021-12-04 16:24:10','2021-12-04 16:24:10','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a curb, jumping from the ground with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. \n\nYou must jump from a minimum of five heel-to-toe steps or greater. Mark the distance on the ground with a marker/chalk/item.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Curb Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','curb-jump','','','2021-12-04 16:24:10','2021-12-04 16:24:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55162',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55163,1,'2021-12-04 16:29:23','2021-12-04 16:29:23','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a curb/wall/block that is over ankle-height, jumping from another wall/block/obstacle of the same height. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \n\nYou must jump from a minimum of five heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Level Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','level-jump','','','2021-12-04 16:29:23','2021-12-04 16:29:23','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55163',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55164,1,'2021-12-04 16:31:42','2021-12-04 16:31:42','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a curb/wall/block that is over hip-height, jumping from another wall/block/obstacle of the same height. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \n\nYou must jump from a minimum of five heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Height Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','height-jump','','','2021-12-04 16:31:42','2021-12-04 16:31:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55164',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55165,1,'2021-12-04 16:36:12','2021-12-04 16:36:12','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a curb/wall/block that is over hip-height, jumping from another wall/block/obstacle that is over head height. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \n\nThis is to demonstrate a downwards trajectory jump from head-height to hip-height.\n\nYou must jump from a minimum of five heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Downwards Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','downwards-jump','','','2021-12-04 16:36:12','2021-12-04 16:36:12','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55165',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55166,1,'2021-12-04 16:39:43','2021-12-04 16:39:43','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a curb/wall/block that is over chest-height, jumping from another wall/block/obstacle that is under hip-height. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate an upwards trajectory jump from hip-height to chest height.\r\n\r\nYou must jump from a minimum of five heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping.\r\n\r\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\r\n\r\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\r\n\r\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\r\n\r\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat five times.','Upwards Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','upwards-jump','','','2021-12-04 16:39:43','2021-12-04 16:39:43','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55166',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55167,1,'2021-12-05 09:02:19','2021-12-05 09:02:19','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a hip-height wall jumping from another wall of the same height. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \n\nYou must jump from a minimum of seven heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping. Walls can only be standard brick length or less.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Wall Distance','','publish','closed','closed','','wall-distance','','','2021-12-05 09:02:19','2021-12-05 09:02:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55167',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55168,1,'2021-12-05 09:07:20','2021-12-05 09:07:20','Demonstrate a good landing on the end of a hip-height wall jumping from a perpendicular wall of the same height. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \r\n\r\nYou must jump from a minimum of six heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping.\r\n\r\nThe end of the wall should be a maximum width of a single standard brick-length or less. Enough for two feet to land together.\r\n\r\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\r\n\r\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\r\n\r\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\r\n\r\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat five times.','Perpendicular Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','perpendicular-wall','','','2021-12-05 09:07:20','2021-12-05 09:07:20','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55168',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55169,1,'2021-12-05 09:11:10','2021-12-05 09:11:10','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a hip-height wall jumping from another wall that is head-height or greater. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \n\nYou must jump from a minimum of seven heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping. Walls can only be standard brick length or less.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Distance Down','','publish','closed','closed','','distance-down','','','2021-12-05 09:11:10','2021-12-05 09:11:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55169',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55170,1,'2021-12-05 09:22:09','2021-12-05 09:22:09','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a chest-height wall jumping up from another wall that is hip-height or greater. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \n\nYou must jump from a minimum of six heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping. Walls can only be standard brick length or less.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Distance Up','','publish','closed','closed','','distance-up','','','2021-12-05 09:22:09','2021-12-05 09:22:09','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55170',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55171,1,'2021-12-05 09:32:30','2021-12-05 09:32:30','Demonstrate a good landing on the edge of a head-height wall jumping from another wall of the same height. Jump with both feet at the same time and land both feet at the same time. \n\nYou must jump from a minimum of eight heel-to-toe steps or greater. Pace out the distance with your feet before jumping. Landing Wall can only be standard brick length or less. Your take-off can be any width wall.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Full Distance','','publish','closed','closed','','full-distance','','','2021-12-05 09:32:30','2021-12-05 09:32:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55171',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55172,1,'2021-12-05 11:49:54','2021-12-05 11:49:54','Demonstrate a wall-supported handstand position for twenty seconds.\n\nYour hands must be within two steps (heel-to-toe) of the base of the wall.\n\nYou must perform both orientations of the handstand facing towards the wall and away from the wall three times for twenty seconds.\n\nYour legs must be straight and you must maintain the position without moving the body.\n\nThis is a static (isometric) hold that used to test you arm pushing capability.','Wall Handstand','','publish','closed','closed','','wall-handstand','','','2021-12-05 11:49:54','2021-12-05 11:49:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55172',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55173,1,'2021-12-05 12:07:02','2021-12-05 12:07:02','The second level is another pushing action and very similar to a yoga downwards-dog to upwards-dog.\n\nDemonstrate five diving-monkeys in a row without stopping. \n\nExample of the diving-monkey:\n\n1. Start in a push-up position with hands flat and body straight.\n2. Push the hips up high as they can go and push the head back to look at the toes. (Downward dog position).\n3. Softly drop both elbows onto the ground at the same time.\n4. Dive your head and body downwards so you are in a low-plank position on your forearms.\n5. Lift the elbows off the ground onto your hands while keeping the torso low to the ground. \n6. You will now be in a low-plank position (bottom of a push-up) on your hands.\n7. Push your arms straight and lift the torso up. (Upwards dog position).\n8. Now reverse every step.\n\nYou must touch your forearms onto the ground in both directions.\n\nYour hands must remain flat on the ground.\n\nYour hips must return to their highest point.\n\nOnly your hands, forearms and toes must touch the ground. Absolutely nothing else can touch the ground.','Diving Monkey','','publish','closed','closed','','diving-monkey','','','2021-12-05 12:07:02','2021-12-05 12:07:02','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55173',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55174,1,'2021-12-05 12:17:45','2021-12-05 12:17:45','Demonstrate three strict pull-ups on a scaffolding bar or less. You must be able to touch your throat to the bar, with your chin *over* the bar every rep. \n\nThis is to demonstrate your ability of pulling strength. You must start in a dead-hang position with the arms straight and no contraction in the muscles.\n\nYou must return to that bottom position every-time to show a full-range-of-motion pull-up.\n\nYou cannot do partial range of motion pull-ups for this to count.\n\nYou must perform all three pull-ups without leaving the bar.\n\nYour hands must be in pronated grip (facing away), as if you were gripping the top of a wall.\n\nYour hands must be shoulder-width apart or less.','Pull-ups','','publish','closed','closed','','pull-ups','','','2021-12-05 12:17:45','2021-12-05 12:17:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55174',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55175,1,'2021-12-05 12:27:13','2021-12-05 12:27:13','Demonstrate twenty full range-of-motion air-squats where your hips must go lower than your knee. \n\nThe angle of the knee must be less than 90 degrees.\n\nYou feet can be facing in any direction, but you must have the heel and whole foot on the floor.\n\nYou must perform twenty (20x) air squats in a row without stopping or reducing quality or range of motion.\n\nThis is a leg pushing exercise primarily focusing on the hamstrings, glutes, quads and hip flexors.\n\n\nGood technique tips:\n\n1. Your knee should be facing the direction of the big-toe. \n2. Knees should not be collapsing inwards.\n3. You chest should be up and you should be facing forwards at the bottom of the squat.\n4. You should have an external tension going down, internal up.\n5. Contraction of the lower abs the entire time.\n\nYou cannot use your hands or arms to help push up','Air Squats','','publish','closed','closed','','air-squats','','','2021-12-05 12:27:13','2021-12-05 12:27:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55175',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55176,1,'2021-12-05 12:38:48','2021-12-05 12:38:48','Demonstrate thirty (30) hip raises in a row, with your heels on a knee-height block/wall/obstacle or higher.\n\nLay on your back next to the knee-height obstacle and rest your heels on top of the block, toes facing up.\n\nStart with your knees bent. Now by squeezing your glutes and hamstrings, straighten your hips in a complete line with your knees and shoulders.\n\nThe top position should be completely flat and straight for full range-of-motion.\n\nRelease the position, lowering your glutes back down to the ground.\n\nThis is to demonstrate a hamstring based, pulling action with the legs.\n\nDemonstrate all reps without loosing quality and go at a slow pace, holding at the top position for 1 second.\n\nHands can remain on the floor, but must not be used to help the exercise.\n\nYour shoulders and upper back (thoracic) with the heels should be the only parts of the body doing the exercise.','Hip Raises','','publish','closed','closed','','hip-raises','','','2021-12-05 12:38:48','2021-12-05 12:38:48','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55176',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55177,1,'2021-12-05 12:59:40','2021-12-05 12:59:40','Perform one hundred reps broken into five different abdominal exercises in six minutes.\n\nYou must demonstrate 20 reps of the following exercises without the legs touching the floor or using the hands to support the legs.\n\n1. 20x Reps of in-and-out. Legs straight to legs bent.\n2. 20x Reps of cycling the legs. 1 rep per full circular cycle.\n3. 20x Reps of both palms of the hands touching to the side of you. One rep is both sides.\n4. 20x Reps of Sit-ups with back completely off the ground. Feet not under anything.\n\nThe last set of twenty reps should be in a plank position with forearms and toes.\n5. 20x Reps of knees touching your elbows. (10 per side).\n\nYou must complete the first four exercises back-to-back with not legs touching the ground. You must then finish by going into the last exercise and complete before the 6 minutes are up.','Abdominals','','publish','closed','closed','','abdominals','','','2021-12-05 12:59:40','2021-12-05 12:59:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55177',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55178,1,'2021-12-05 13:02:51','2021-12-05 13:02:51','Complete a 2 Kilometre run under 12 minutes. This can be on a running machine, a running track with a known distance, or with a fitness app and GPS.\n\nThis is to test your short-term fast endurance.','Running','','publish','closed','closed','','running','','','2021-12-05 13:02:51','2021-12-05 13:02:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55178',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55179,1,'2021-12-05 13:12:19','2021-12-05 13:12:19','Demonstrate your grip strength by holding the cat-leap position statically on the side of a wall for 30 seconds or longer.\r\n\r\nYour arms must be stay bent the whole time, with no more than 120 degree angle. (Try to stay at 90 degrees)\r\n\r\nYou must keep both of your feet on the wall at all times. \r\n\r\nYou must only grip the front edge of the wall. No over-grip.\r\n\r\nOnly hands and feet should be in contact with the wall.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.','Grip','','publish','closed','closed','','grip','','','2021-12-05 13:12:19','2021-12-05 13:12:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55179',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55180,1,'2021-12-05 13:18:17','2021-12-05 13:18:17','Standing on a wall, bollard, block or railing; demonstrate five repetitions of a full range-of -motion leg pistol without any help from the other leg or arms.\n\nYou should demonstrate both sides / legs five times (10 reps in total).\n\nThe hanging leg must not drag up the wall or be used to aid stability of balance.\n\nPerform all reps without coming out of position.\n\nDo not use the hands or arms to help push into the standing position.\n\nYou must go into the full bottom position where you are sitting on your heel. There should be no active contraction in the leg to keep you up.\n\nYou must have a complete flat foot on the wall, no pushing from just the toes.','Pistols','','publish','closed','closed','','pistols','','','2021-12-05 13:18:17','2021-12-05 13:18:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55180',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55181,1,'2021-12-05 13:23:32','2021-12-05 13:23:32','Perform three complete unassisted muscle-ups on a scaffolding rail or wider. \n\nYou may start in the top or bottom position of the muscle-up. But there must not be any kipping or butterfly-swing motion.\n\nYou can must perform three reps on the bar without coming off.\n\nYou can use chalk for grip if needed.\n\nYou must perform full range-of-motion at both the bottom and top of the movement. The arms must be straight at the bottom position and at the top position.\n\nYou cannot use a thinner bar than scaffolding-width.','Muscle-up','','publish','closed','closed','','muscle-up','','','2021-12-05 13:23:32','2021-12-05 13:23:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55181',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55182,1,'2021-12-05 16:15:43','2021-12-05 16:15:43','Demonstrate any method of getting over a wall that is head-height or greater.\r\n\r\nWall width must be of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYou can use any body-part or grip but must finish standing on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Struggle-up','','publish','closed','closed','','struggle-up','','','2021-12-05 16:15:43','2021-12-05 16:15:43','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55182',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55183,1,'2021-12-05 16:23:42','2021-12-05 16:23:42','Demonstrate any method of getting over a wall that is head-height or greater using only your hands and feet. \r\n\r\nNo forearms, elbows, knees, shins, or any other body-part can be used other than the palm of the hands and the feet. Over-grip on the opposite of the wall is allowed.\r\n\r\nWall width must be of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYou must finish standing on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.\r\n','Low Climbup','','publish','closed','closed','','low-climbup','','','2021-12-05 16:23:42','2021-12-05 16:23:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55183',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55184,1,'2021-12-05 16:27:15','2021-12-05 16:27:15','Demonstrate any method of getting over a wall that is full-stretch height or greater. (Height of you standing with arm straight up above you at full stretch).\r\n\r\nWall width must be of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYou can use any body-part or grip but must finish standing on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','High Struggle','','publish','closed','closed','','high-struggle','','','2021-12-05 16:27:15','2021-12-05 16:27:15','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55184',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55185,1,'2021-12-05 16:31:39','2021-12-05 16:31:39','Demonstrate any method of getting over a wall that is full-stretch height or greater using only your hands and feet. \r\n\r\nNo forearms, elbows, knees, shins, or any other body-part can be used other than the palm of the hands and the feet. Over-grip on the opposite of the wall is allowed.\r\n\r\nWall width must be of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYou must finish standing on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','High Climbup','','publish','closed','closed','','high-climbup','','','2021-12-05 16:31:39','2021-12-05 16:31:39','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55185',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55186,1,'2021-12-05 16:38:47','2021-12-05 16:38:47','Starting in a cat-leap position on the side of a head-height wall, demonstrate a climb-up method of getting over a wall using only your hands and feet. \r\n\r\nNo forearms, elbows, knees, shins, or any other body-part can be used other than the palm of the hands and the feet. Over-grip on the opposite of the wall is allowed.\r\n\r\nWall width must be of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYou must finish standing on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Cat to Climbup','','publish','closed','closed','','cat-to-climbup','','','2021-12-05 16:38:47','2021-12-05 16:38:47','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55186',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55187,1,'2021-12-05 16:42:49','2021-12-05 16:42:49','Starting in a cat-leap position on the side of a head-height wall, demonstrate a climb-up method of getting over a wall using only your hands and feet. Both elbows must raise up above the wall in parallel and simultaneously.\r\n\r\nNo forearms, elbows, knees, shins, or any other body-part can be used other than the palm of the hands and the feet. \r\n\r\nNo Over-grip on the opposite of the wall is allowed.\r\n\r\nYou cannot lift one elbow and then the other. Both at the same time must be raised.\r\n\r\nWall width must be of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYou must finish standing on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Strict Climbup','','publish','closed','closed','','strict-climbup','','','2021-12-05 16:42:49','2021-12-05 16:42:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55187',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55188,1,'2021-12-05 16:48:11','2021-12-05 16:48:11','Starting in a cat-leap position on the side of a head-height wall, demonstrate five strict climb-ups using only your hands and feet. Both elbows must raise up above the wall in parallel and simultaneously.\n\nYou must finish each repetition standing on top of the wall, returning back to the cat-leap position with arms straight every time. You must perform these with no rest in-between and no pausing. A constant movement should be evident.\n\nNo forearms, elbows, knees, shins, or any other body-part can be used other than the palm of the hands and the feet. \n\nNo Over-grip on the opposite of the wall is allowed.\n\nYou cannot lift one elbow and then the other. Both at the same time must be raised.\n\nWall width must be of standard brick-length or less.\n','Five Climbups','','publish','closed','closed','','five-climbups','','','2021-12-05 16:48:11','2021-12-05 16:48:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55188',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55189,1,'2021-12-05 16:55:01','2021-12-05 16:55:01','Start in a balance on a railing with no bars or obstacles underneath it, then climb under it and back up the other side. You cannot use any other parts of the bar other than the bar you start on that should be parallel to the ground.\n\nThe bar can be any height, but must be circular and high enough that you can climb under it.\n\nAny body-part can be used on the bar, but you must finish back standing on top of the bar.\n\nYou must demonstrate one repetition of going around the bar in both directions.\n\nBar must be scaffolding-width or wider.','Bar Struggle-up','','publish','closed','closed','','bar-struggle-up','','','2021-12-05 16:55:01','2021-12-05 16:55:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55189',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55190,1,'2021-12-05 17:02:28','2021-12-05 17:02:28','Demonstrate a roll-off under-hang and back up again. You will need a wall / climbing-frame / roof with an overhang to preform this.\n\n1. Start on top of the overhang, belly down, chest hanging over the edge.\n2. Hands on your side, facing backwards.\n3. Roll your legs overhead, until you are in a dead-hang position with your back to the wall.\n4. Now reverse the movement, bringing your legs up and back on top of the wall.\n5. Shuffle back until your entire body is back on top.\n\nRepeat twice.\n\nAny body-part can be used to get back on top of the wall.','Reverse Overhang','','publish','closed','closed','','reverse-overhang','','','2021-12-05 17:02:28','2021-12-05 17:02:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55190',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55191,1,'2021-12-05 17:12:01','2021-12-05 17:12:01','The batman-jump is a colloquial term referencing the movie \"The Dark-Knight Rises\" in which Batman has to climb out of a prison pit. But to complete the task he needs to jump to an overhang with just his hands.\n\nPerform an overhang catch from a minimum of 5-steps away and then climb-up on top of the overhang. The top of the overhang must be full-stretch height or greater from the floor. \n\nYou must repeat twice. \n\nYou can use any body-part to climb-up on top of the overhang. \n\n','Batman Climbup','','publish','closed','closed','','batman-climbup','','','2021-12-05 17:12:01','2021-12-05 17:12:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55191',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55192,1,'2021-12-06 08:52:11','2021-12-06 08:52:11','Even though this is a running-jump movement, the first level is a single-leg standing jump onto an ankle-height or greater target.\r\n\r\nThe target curb/wall/step should be 4-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point on the floor  or another step/curb/wall at ankle-height. \r\n\r\nStand facing the target and using the swing of one leg, propel yourself forwards to land on the target in a precision landing with both feet together.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.','One Foot Takeoff','','publish','closed','closed','','one-foot-takeoff','','','2021-12-06 08:52:11','2021-12-06 08:52:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55192',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55193,1,'2021-12-06 08:59:59','2021-12-06 08:59:59','The second level is to demonstrate a single-leg jump onto an ankle-height or greater target with one single step into the jump.\n\nThe target curb/wall/step should be 5-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point on the floor or another step/curb/wall. \n\nStand facing the target and take a single step backwards with one leg only. You should have one leg forwards, and one backwards now. When ready to jump, push forwards off the back-leg and use the momentum to propel forwards.\n\nLand in a precision on both feet on the target wall/curb/step.\n\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.','One Step Takeoff','','publish','closed','closed','','one-step-takeoff','','','2021-12-06 08:59:59','2021-12-06 08:59:59','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55193',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55194,1,'2021-12-06 09:09:31','2021-12-06 09:09:31','The third level is to demonstrate a single-leg jump onto an ankle-height or greater target with two steps into the jump.\n\nThe target curb/wall/step should be 6-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point on the floor or another step/curb/wall. \n\nStand facing the target and take a single step backwards with one leg only. You should have one leg forwards, and one backwards now. Now take another big step backwards with the front leg.\nWhen ready to jump, push forwards off the back-leg and take two step forwards using the momentum to propel the distance.\n\nLand in a precision on both feet on the target wall/curb/step.\n\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.','Two Step Takeoff','','publish','closed','closed','','two-step-takeoff','','','2021-12-06 09:09:31','2021-12-06 09:09:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55194',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55195,1,'2021-12-06 09:14:11','2021-12-06 09:14:11','The fourth level is to demonstrate a single-leg jump onto an ankle-height or greater target with as many steps into the jump that is necessary.\r\n\r\nThe target curb/wall/step should be 7-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point on the floor or another step/curb/wall. \r\n\r\nYou can now take as many steps into the running jump as required to cover the distance.\r\n\r\nLand in a precision on both feet on the target wall/curb/step.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.','Running Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','running-jump-11','','','2021-12-06 09:14:11','2021-12-06 09:14:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55195',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55196,1,'2021-12-06 09:18:45','2021-12-06 09:18:45','The fifth level is to demonstrate a single-leg jump onto a knee-height or greater target with as many steps into the jump that is necessary.\n\nThe target curb/wall/step should be 7-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point on the floor/ step/curb/wall that can be no higher than ankle-height.\n\nThis is to demonstrate an upwards trajectory in a running jump.\n\nYou can now take as many steps into the running jump as required to cover the distance.\n\nLand in a precision on both feet on the target wall/curb/step.\n\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.','Upwards Trajectory','','publish','closed','closed','','upwards-trajectory','','','2021-12-06 09:18:45','2021-12-06 09:18:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55196',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55197,1,'2021-12-06 09:26:35','2021-12-06 09:26:35','The sixth level is to demonstrate a single-leg running jump onto an hip-height or greater target with as many steps into the jump that is necessary from a same-level hip-height takeoff point.\n\nThe target curb/wall/step should be 7-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point.\n\nYou can now take as many steps into the running jump as required to cover the distance.\n\nAny landing is allowed on the target wall/curb/step.\n\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.\n\nThis is to demonstrate that you can jump a 7-step distance over a hip-height gap.','Hip-Height Distance','','publish','closed','closed','','hip-height-distance','','','2021-12-06 09:26:35','2021-12-06 09:26:35','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55197',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55198,1,'2021-12-06 09:31:09','2021-12-06 09:31:09','The seventh level is to demonstrate a single-leg running jump onto an head-height or greater target with as many steps into the jump that is necessary from a same-level head-height takeoff point.\n\nThe target curb/wall/step should be 7-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point.\n\nYou can now take as many steps into the running jump as required to cover the distance.\n\nAny landing is allowed on the target wall/curb/step.\n\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.\n\nThis is to demonstrate that you can jump a 7-step distance over a head-height gap.','Head-Height Distance','','publish','closed','closed','','head-height-distance','','','2021-12-06 09:31:09','2021-12-06 09:31:09','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55198',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55199,1,'2021-12-06 09:44:23','2021-12-06 09:44:23','The eighth level is to demonstrate a single-leg running jump from the end of a hip-height or greater perpendicular wall onto an hip-height or greater target with as many steps into the jump that is necessary.\n\nYou should be running along a wall that is a maximum of one standard brick-length wide.\n\nThe target curb/wall/step should be 7-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point. It must also be a maximum of a single brick-length width or less.\n\nYou can now take as many steps into the running jump as required to cover the distance.\n\nAny landing is allowed on the target wall/curb/step.\n\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.\n\nThis is to demonstrate that you can jump a 7-step distance over a hip-height gap from the end of a wall that is one-brick length wide.','Thin-Wall Running','','publish','closed','closed','','thin-wall-running','','','2021-12-06 09:44:23','2021-12-06 09:44:23','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55199',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55200,1,'2021-12-06 09:50:28','2021-12-06 09:50:28','The ninth level is to demonstrate a single-leg running jump to a rail precision.\n\nThe target rail should be 7-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater from the takeoff point. It must also be a maximum of a standard circular scaffolding bar or less.\n\nThere is no height restriction on the takeoff or landing.\n\nYou can now take as many steps into the running jump as required to cover the distance.\n\nYou must land in a precision on the rail and stick the landing.\n\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.','Running Rail-Pre','','publish','closed','closed','','running-rail-pre','','','2021-12-06 09:50:28','2021-12-06 09:50:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55200',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55201,1,'2021-12-06 09:53:39','2021-12-06 09:53:39','The tenth level is to demonstrate a single-leg running jump over a hip-height gap that is nine steps (heel-to-toe) away.\r\n\r\nYou can now take as many steps into the running jump as required to cover the distance.\r\n\r\nAny landing is accepted.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on each leg.','Long Distance','','publish','closed','closed','','long-distance','','','2021-12-06 09:53:39','2021-12-06 09:53:39','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55201',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55202,1,'2021-12-07 17:23:17','2021-12-07 17:23:17','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) onto a knee-height block/wall/obstacle with any number of steps into the movement.\r\n\r\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land on top of the obstacle with two feet at the same time.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.','Knee-Height','','publish','closed','closed','','knee-height','','','2021-12-07 17:23:17','2021-12-07 17:23:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55202',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55203,1,'2021-12-07 17:27:52','2021-12-07 17:27:52','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) onto a knee-height block/wall/obstacle with no run-up or steps into the movement that generate momentum. This includes shuffling the feet from the standing position.\n\nYou can start from any distance away from the wall, but must not move position once starting the jump\n\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land on top of the obstacle with two feet at the same time.\n\nRepeat three times.','Standing Knee','','publish','closed','closed','','standing-knee','','','2021-12-07 17:27:52','2021-12-07 17:27:52','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55203',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55204,1,'2021-12-07 17:29:32','2021-12-07 17:29:32','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) onto a hip-height block/wall/obstacle with any number of steps into the movement.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land on top of the obstacle with two feet at the same time.\n\nRepeat three times.','Hip-Height','','publish','closed','closed','','hip-height','','','2021-12-07 17:29:32','2021-12-07 17:29:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55204',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55205,1,'2021-12-07 17:33:42','2021-12-07 17:33:42','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) **over** a knee-height rail/bar/wall/obstacle with any number of steps into the movement.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land past the obstacle with two feet at the same time on the opposite side.\n\nRepeat three times.','Over Knee','','publish','closed','closed','','over-knee','','','2021-12-07 17:33:42','2021-12-07 17:33:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55205',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55206,1,'2021-12-07 17:35:49','2021-12-07 17:35:49','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) **over** a hip-height rail/bar/wall/obstacle with any number of steps into the movement.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land past the obstacle with two feet at the same time on the opposite side.\n\nRepeat three times.','Over Hip','','publish','closed','closed','','over-hip','','','2021-12-07 17:35:49','2021-12-07 17:35:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55206',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55207,1,'2021-12-07 17:38:19','2021-12-07 17:38:19','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) onto a hip-height block/wall/obstacle with no run-up or steps into the movement that generate momentum. This includes shuffling the feet from the standing position.\n\nYou can start from any distance away from the wall, but must not move position once starting the jump\n\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land on top of the obstacle with two feet at the same time.\n\nRepeat three times.','Standing Hip','','publish','closed','closed','','standing-hip','','','2021-12-07 17:38:19','2021-12-07 17:38:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55207',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55208,1,'2021-12-07 17:41:36','2021-12-07 17:41:36','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) onto a belly-button height block/wall/obstacle with any number of steps into the movement.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land on top of the obstacle with two feet at the same time.\n\nRepeat three times.','Belly-button','','publish','closed','closed','','belly-button','','','2021-12-07 17:41:36','2021-12-07 17:41:36','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55208',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55209,1,'2021-12-07 17:50:18','2021-12-07 17:50:18','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) **over** a hip-height rail/bar/wall/obstacle with any number of steps into the movement where the floor on the opposite site cannot be seen. The target is a landing point a maximum of one step (heel-to-toe) away from the wall.\n\nTo clarify, you must box-jump over a wall where the landing point cannot be seen until on the decent of the jump. No railings to see through.\n\nThe target can be a marker / brick / chalk circle, etc...\n\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land past the obstacle with two feet at the same time on the opposite side. You must stick your landing.\n\nRepeat three times.','Close Blind','','publish','closed','closed','','close-blind','','','2021-12-07 17:50:18','2021-12-07 17:50:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55209',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55210,1,'2021-12-07 17:54:20','2021-12-07 17:54:20','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) **over** a hip-height rail/bar/wall/obstacle with any number of steps into the movement. The wall must have a chest-height drop on the far side. \r\n\r\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land past the obstacle with two feet at the same time on the opposite side.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.','Box Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','box-drop','','','2021-12-07 17:54:20','2021-12-07 17:54:20','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55210',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55211,1,'2021-12-07 18:08:10','2021-12-07 18:08:10','Demonstrate a Box jump (vertical) **over** a hip-height rail/bar/wall/obstacle with any number of steps into the movement. land on another obstacle that is on ankle-height or greater and no wider than a standard brick-length.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet simultaneously and land past the obstacle with two feet at the same time on the opposite side, landing on the target wall in a precision.\n\nRepeat three times.','Box Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','box-precision','','','2021-12-07 18:08:10','2021-12-07 18:08:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55211',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55212,1,'2021-12-08 08:56:05','2021-12-08 08:56:05','Plyometric jumping is about combining force with speed in rapid succession. The speed of each explosive jump should help build more momentum up.\n\nDemonstrate five plyometric jumps over five lines on the ground which are four steps (heel-to-toe) apart from each other.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nUse visible lines on the ground - chalk lines / string / etc...\n\nRepeat three times.','Ground Plyos','','publish','closed','closed','','ground-plyos','','','2021-12-08 08:56:05','2021-12-08 08:56:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55212',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55213,1,'2021-12-08 08:59:30','2021-12-08 08:59:30','Plyometric jumping is about combining force with speed in rapid succession. The speed of each explosive jump should help build more momentum up.\n\nDemonstrate five plyometric jumps over five ankle-height obstacles on the ground which are four steps (heel-to-toe) apart from each other.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nUse visible markers to jump over. You can use plastic bottles, walls, bricks, etc..\n\nRepeat three times.','Ankle-height','','publish','closed','closed','','ankle-height','','','2021-12-08 08:59:30','2021-12-08 08:59:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55213',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55214,1,'2021-12-08 09:05:08','2021-12-08 09:05:08','Plyometric jumping is about combining force with speed in rapid succession. The speed of each explosive jump should help build more momentum up.\n\nDemonstrate two plyometric jumps over two ankle-height obstacles on the ground which are four steps (heel-to-toe) apart from each other. On the second landing, you will then do a third plyo in a broad jump onto ankle-height curb/wall/step that is six steps (heel-to-toe) away from the second line.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nUse visible markers to jump over. You can use plastic bottles, walls, bricks, etc..\n\nRepeat three times.','Plyo to Broad','','publish','closed','closed','','plyo-to-broad','','','2021-12-08 09:05:08','2021-12-08 09:05:08','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55214',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55215,1,'2021-12-08 09:15:20','2021-12-08 09:15:20','Plyometric jumping is about combining force with speed in rapid succession. The speed of each explosive jump should help build more momentum up.\n\nDemonstrate three plyometric jumps **onto** three ankle-height obstacles on the ground which are four steps (heel-to-toe) apart from each other.\n\nEach obstacle must be no wider that a standard brick-length or less. It can be walls, steps, bricks, etc... Each landing must be a precision landing on the ball of the foot, but with plyometric force into the next jump.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nRepeat three times.','Plyo Precisions','','publish','closed','closed','','plyo-precisions','','','2021-12-08 09:15:20','2021-12-08 09:15:20','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55215',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55216,1,'2021-12-08 09:19:56','2021-12-08 09:19:56','Plyometric jumping is about combining force with speed in rapid succession. The speed of each explosive jump should help build more momentum up.\n\nDemonstrate two plyometric precision jumps **onto** two ankle-height obstacles which are four steps (heel-to-toe) apart from each other. On the second landing, you will then do a third plyo in a broad jump precision onto ankle-height curb/wall/step that is six steps (heel-to-toe) away from the second line.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nRepeat three times.','Plyo Distance','','publish','closed','closed','','plyo-distance','','','2021-12-08 09:19:56','2021-12-08 09:19:56','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55216',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55217,1,'2021-12-08 09:32:07','2021-12-08 09:32:07','Use plyometric jumps up 30 stairs in the least number of jumps as possible. Every jump must be plyometric and you must not stop.\n\nYou must repeat three times in under 10 minutes.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.','Plyo Stairs','','publish','closed','closed','','plyo-stairs','','','2021-12-08 09:32:07','2021-12-08 09:32:07','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55217',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55218,1,'2021-12-08 09:44:49','2021-12-08 09:44:49','Demonstrate three plyometric jumps onto walls that are chest-height or higher. Walls must be brick-length or less in width. \n\nWalls must be four steps (heel-to-toe) distance apart or greater.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nRepeat three times.','Plyo at Height','','publish','closed','closed','','plyo-at-height','','','2021-12-08 09:44:49','2021-12-08 09:44:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55218',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55219,1,'2021-12-08 09:51:36','2021-12-08 09:51:36','Plyometric jumping is about combining force with speed in rapid succession. The speed of each explosive jump should help build more momentum up.\n\nDemonstrate two plyometric precision jumps **onto** two ankle-height obstacles which are four steps (heel-to-toe) or greater apart from each other. \n\nA third plyo jump is then performed at an eight-step distance from the second jump. You must land on a wall/curb/step in a precision.\n\nEach wall / obstacle can only be brick-length or less in width.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nRepeat three times.','Plyo Max','','publish','closed','closed','','plyo-max','','','2021-12-08 09:51:36','2021-12-08 09:51:36','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55219',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55220,1,'2021-12-08 09:57:31','2021-12-08 09:57:31','Demonstrate the three stages of this movement together:\n\n1. Drop from a wall at chest-height or greater.\n2. Land into one or more plyometric jumps towards a second wall.\n3. Plyometric box-jump up onto a hip-height or greater wall.\n\nPlyometrics can be on the floor and can be any number to get to the second wall.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nRepeat three times.','Plyo Drop Box','','publish','closed','closed','','plyo-drop-box','','','2021-12-08 09:57:31','2021-12-08 09:57:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55220',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55221,1,'2021-12-08 10:01:27','2021-12-08 10:01:27','Demonstrate three plyometric jumps in a row on three railings that are hip-height or higher and more than five-steps away from each other.\n\nChest should remain up, the jumps must be two-feet together, and you should not pause on any jump. The landing momentum of each jump should be redirected back up for the next jump.\n\nRail can be flat or circular, but no wider than standard scaffolding bar.\n\nRepeat three times.','Rail Plyos','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-plyos-2','','','2021-12-08 10:01:27','2021-12-08 10:01:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55221',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55222,1,'2021-12-09 09:15:41','2021-12-09 09:15:41','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a knee-height to floor drop.\r\n\r\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nLanding Position:\r\n\r\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\r\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\r\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\r\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\r\n5. Chest up and back straight.\r\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\r\n','First Contact','','publish','closed','closed','','first-contact','','','2021-12-09 09:15:41','2021-12-09 09:15:41','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55222',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55223,1,'2021-12-09 09:17:03','2021-12-09 09:17:03','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a hip-height to floor drop.\r\n\r\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nLanding Position:\r\n\r\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\r\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\r\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\r\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\r\n5. Chest up and back straight.\r\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\r\n','Hip-Height Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','hip-height-drop','','','2021-12-09 09:17:03','2021-12-09 09:17:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55223',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55224,1,'2021-12-09 09:18:05','2021-12-09 09:18:05','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a chest-height to floor drop.\n\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nLanding Position:\n\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\n5. Chest up and back straight.\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\n','Chest-Height Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','chest-height-drop','','','2021-12-09 09:18:05','2021-12-09 09:18:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55224',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55225,1,'2021-12-09 09:21:27','2021-12-09 09:21:27','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a dead-hang to floor drop. \n\nFind a bar or overhang that you can dead-hang from. Your feet must be hip-height or greater when in this position. So the bar will need to be full-stretch + hip-height.\n\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nLanding Position:\n\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\n5. Chest up and back straight.\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\n','Deadhang Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','deadhang-drop','','','2021-12-09 09:21:27','2021-12-09 09:21:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55225',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55226,1,'2021-12-09 09:24:19','2021-12-09 09:24:19','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a cat-leap position on a wall to floor drop. \n\nStart in a cat-leap / arm-jump position on a wall so that your feet are over hip-height. Push away and drop to the floor. You can land in any orientation away from the wall.\n\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nLanding Position:\n\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\n5. Chest up and back straight.\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\n','Cat to Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','cat-to-drop','','','2021-12-09 09:24:19','2021-12-09 09:24:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55226',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55227,1,'2021-12-09 09:32:07','2021-12-09 09:32:07','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a cat-leap position on a wall to floor drop. \r\n\r\nStart in a cat-leap / arm-jump position on a wall so that your feet are over chest-height. Push away and drop to the floor. You must land facing the wall.\r\n\r\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nLanding Position:\r\n\r\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\r\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\r\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\r\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\r\n5. Chest up and back straight.\r\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\r\n','High Cat to Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','high-cat-to-drop','','','2021-12-09 09:32:07','2021-12-09 09:32:07','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55227',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55228,1,'2021-12-09 09:50:40','2021-12-09 09:50:40','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a depth-jump off a chest-height or higher obstacle. \n\nYou will need a kids climbing-frame or similar obstacle to be able to drop directly downwards from chest-height through a gap into a landing on the floor.\n\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\n\nYou need to drop through a gap.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nLanding Position:\n\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\n5. Chest up and back straight.\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\n','Depth Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','depth-jump','','','2021-12-09 09:50:40','2021-12-09 09:50:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55228',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55229,1,'2021-12-09 09:54:29','2021-12-09 09:54:29','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a broad-jump off a chest-height or higher block / obstacle.\r\n\r\nYou can land on another obstacle or the floor, but the landing must be a chest-height drop or greater.\r\n \r\nThe landing target must be eight steps (heel to toe) away from the takeoff block.\r\n\r\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nLanding Position:\r\n\r\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\r\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\r\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\r\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\r\n5. Chest up and back straight.\r\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\r\n','Broad Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','broad-drop','','','2021-12-09 09:54:29','2021-12-09 09:54:29','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55229',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55230,1,'2021-12-09 09:58:53','2021-12-09 09:58:53','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a drop off a full-stretch height or higher block / obstacle onto a perpendicular hip-height or lower wall.\n\nMake sure you land in a stable position on both feet at the same time and there is no movement afterwards. No shuffling of feet or foot movement.\n\nYou will need to twist 90 degrees to land on the perpendicular wall.\n\nThe target wall must be standard brick-length or less in width.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nLanding Position:\n\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\n5. Chest up and back straight.\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\n','Drop Twist','','publish','closed','closed','','drop-twist','','','2021-12-09 09:58:53','2021-12-09 09:58:53','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55230',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55231,1,'2021-12-09 10:05:05','2021-12-09 10:05:05','Being able to correctly absorb impact without damage is essential for parkour. Demonstrate a good landing position from a drop off a full-stretch height or higher block / obstacle onto the ground and into an effective parkour roll.\r\n\r\nThe ground can be grass or concrete. \r\n\r\nYou must redirect the impact of the landing rather than absorb it. This means that you should not land first, take the impact, then perform a roll. The roll must take the impact and redirect the force. You must demonstrate this is what is happening. The roll must finish on your feet.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n\r\nLanding Position:\r\n\r\n1. You are landing both feet at the same time and on the ball of the foot (behind big toe)\r\n2. Your calfs, quads, hamstrings, glutes and lower abs are contracting hard.\r\n3. Your ankle, knee and hips are all at roughly 50%-70% bent position.\r\n4. You are actively contracting your toes and muscles.\r\n5. Chest up and back straight.\r\n6. Arms out in front for stability.\r\n','Drop Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','drop-roll','','','2021-12-09 10:05:05','2021-12-09 10:05:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55231',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55232,1,'2021-12-09 11:39:52','2021-12-09 11:39:52','Demonstrate six strides onto chalk marker-lines on the ground. Each line needs to be five steps (heel-to-toe) apart.\n\nBall of the foot must land on each line, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nRepeat twice.','Ground Strides','','publish','closed','closed','','ground-strides','','','2021-12-09 11:39:52','2021-12-09 11:39:52','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55232',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55233,1,'2021-12-09 11:46:41','2021-12-09 11:46:41','Demonstrate six strides over ankle-height obstacles on the ground. Each obstacle needs to be five steps (heel-to-toe) apart. \n\nUse water-bottles / cans / bags / improvised obstacles.\n\nBall of the foot must land on the ground, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nRepeat twice.','Six Obstacles','','publish','closed','closed','','six-obstacles','','','2021-12-09 11:46:41','2021-12-09 11:46:41','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55233',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55234,1,'2021-12-09 11:58:40','2021-12-09 11:58:40','Demonstrate three strides on three ankle-height obstacles. Each landing needs to be four steps (heel-to-toe) apart or greater. \n\nUse walls / blocks / steps / bricks / wood or improvised obstacles.\n\nBall of the foot must land on the obstacle, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can turn or move in different directions during the strides.\n \nRepeat twice.','Three Blocks','','publish','closed','closed','','three-blocks','','','2021-12-09 11:58:40','2021-12-09 11:58:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55234',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55235,1,'2021-12-09 12:02:24','2021-12-09 12:02:24','Demonstrate three strides on three ankle-height walls / thin obstacles. Each landing needs to be four steps (heel-to-toe) apart or greater. \n\nObstacle width must be standard brick-length or less.\n\nUse walls / blocks / steps / bricks / wood / bollards or improvised obstacles.\n\nBall of the foot must land on the obstacle, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can turn or move in different directions during the strides.\n \nRepeat twice.','Three Walls','','publish','closed','closed','','three-walls','','','2021-12-09 12:02:24','2021-12-09 12:02:24','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55235',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55236,1,'2021-12-09 16:10:02','2021-12-09 16:10:02','Demonstrate three strides on three knee-height walls / thin obstacles. Each landing needs to be four steps (heel-to-toe) apart or greater. \n\nObstacle width must be standard brick-length or less.\n\nUse walls / blocks / steps / bricks / wood / bollards or improvised obstacles.\n\nBall of the foot must land on the obstacle, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can turn or move in different directions during the strides.\n \nRepeat twice.','Knee-Height Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','knee-height-wall','','','2021-12-09 16:10:02','2021-12-09 16:10:02','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55236',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55237,1,'2021-12-09 16:15:48','2021-12-09 16:15:48','Demonstrate three strides on four knee-height walls / thin obstacles. Each landing needs to be six steps (heel-to-toe) apart or greater. \n\nUse walls / blocks / steps / bricks / wood / bollards or improvised obstacles.\n\nBall of the foot must land on the obstacle, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can turn or move in different directions during the strides.\n \nRepeat three times.','Six-step walls','','publish','closed','closed','','six-step-walls','','','2021-12-09 16:15:48','2021-12-09 16:15:48','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55237',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55238,1,'2021-12-09 16:18:53','2021-12-09 16:18:53','Demonstrate three strides on four hip-height walls that are no wider than on brick length. Each landing needs to be six steps (heel-to-toe) apart or greater. \n\nBall of the foot must land on the obstacle, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can turn or move in different directions during the strides.\n \nRepeat three times.','Four Walls','','publish','closed','closed','','four-walls','','','2021-12-09 16:18:53','2021-12-09 16:18:53','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55238',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55239,1,'2021-12-09 16:21:28','2021-12-09 16:21:28','Demonstrate four strides on four hip-height bollards that are no wider than on brick length and are rounded on top. Each landing needs to be four steps (heel-to-toe) apart or greater. \n\nBall of the foot must land on the obstacle, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can turn or move in different directions during the strides.\n \nRepeat three times.','Bollards','','publish','closed','closed','','bollards','','','2021-12-09 16:21:28','2021-12-09 16:21:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55239',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55240,1,'2021-12-09 16:24:31','2021-12-09 16:24:31','Demonstrate four strides on four knee-height rails that are no wider than on standard scaffolding-bar width and are circular. Each landing needs to be four steps (heel-to-toe) apart or greater. \n\nBall of the foot must land on the obstacle, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can turn or move in different directions during the strides.\n \nRepeat three times.','Rail Strides','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-strides','','','2021-12-09 16:24:31','2021-12-09 16:24:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55240',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55241,1,'2021-12-09 16:27:05','2021-12-09 16:27:05','Demonstrate four strides on four head-height walls that are no wider than on brick length. Each landing needs to be six steps (heel-to-toe) apart or greater. \n\nBall of the foot must land on the obstacle, alternating each leg.\n\nYou must be going at a consistent speed. No pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can turn or move in different directions during the strides.\n \nRepeat three times.','Height Strides','','publish','closed','closed','','height-strides','','','2021-12-09 16:27:05','2021-12-09 16:27:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55241',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55242,1,'2021-12-10 09:45:58','2021-12-10 09:45:58','Starting from 3-steps or greater away, demonstrate a crane landing on a hip-height or greater block.\r\n\r\nYou must takeoff two feet from the floor and land one foot on the top of the wall, with the other hanging along the side of the wall. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee. \r\n\r\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Basic Crane','','publish','closed','closed','','basic-crane','','','2021-12-10 09:45:58','2021-12-10 09:45:58','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55242',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55243,1,'2021-12-10 09:55:56','2021-12-10 09:55:56','Starting from 4-steps or greater away, demonstrate a crane landing on a hip-height or greater block.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet and land one foot on the top of the wall, with the other hanging along the side of the wall. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee. \n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Standing Further','','publish','closed','closed','','standing-further','','','2021-12-10 09:55:56','2021-12-10 09:55:56','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55243',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55244,1,'2021-12-10 10:02:10','2021-12-10 10:02:10','Starting from 4-steps or greater away, demonstrate a crane landing on a hip-height or greater block from a knee-height block or higher.\n\nHeight difference between takeoff to landing must be knee-height.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet and land one foot on the top of the wall, with the other hanging along the side of the wall. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee. \n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Knee-Height','','publish','closed','closed','','knee-height-2','','','2021-12-10 10:02:10','2021-12-10 10:02:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55244',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55245,1,'2021-12-10 10:05:43','2021-12-10 10:05:43','Starting from 4-steps or greater away, demonstrate a crane landing on a chest-height or greater block from a hip-height block or higher.\n\nHeight difference between takeoff to landing must be knee-height.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet and land one foot on the top of the wall, with the other hanging along the side of the wall. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee. \n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Higher Crane','','publish','closed','closed','','higher-crane','','','2021-12-10 10:05:43','2021-12-10 10:05:43','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55245',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55246,1,'2021-12-10 10:09:25','2021-12-10 10:09:25','Jump from the ground a 5-steps or greater distance and demonstrate a crane landing on a hip-height or greater block. You can now run into the jump take-off position to generate momentum.\n\nYou can takeoff two feet or one-foot from the floor and land one foot on the top of the wall, with the other hanging along the side of the wall. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee. \n\nYour foot must not cross the 5-step distance line. Clearly mark the line on the ground.\n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Running Crane','','publish','closed','closed','','running-crane','','','2021-12-10 10:09:25','2021-12-10 10:09:25','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55246',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55247,1,'2021-12-10 10:14:23','2021-12-10 10:14:23','Starting from 5-steps or greater away, demonstrate a crane landing on a head-height or greater block from a hip-height block/wall or higher.\n\nHeight difference between takeoff to landing must be hip-height. \n\nThe landing wall width must be one brick-length or less.\n\nYou must takeoff two feet and land one foot on the top of the wall, with the other hanging along the side of the wall. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee. \n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Standing Height','','publish','closed','closed','','standing-height','','','2021-12-10 10:14:23','2021-12-10 10:14:23','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55247',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55248,1,'2021-12-10 10:21:50','2021-12-10 10:21:50','Jump from the ground and demonstrate a crane landing on a chest-height or greater block. You can now run into the jump take-off position to generate momentum.\n\nYou can takeoff two feet or one-foot from the floor and land one foot on the top of the wall, with the other hanging along the side of the wall. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee. \n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Running Chest','','publish','closed','closed','','running-chest','','','2021-12-10 10:21:50','2021-12-10 10:21:50','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55248',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55249,1,'2021-12-10 10:26:15','2021-12-10 10:26:15','Demonstrate a standing level-to-level crane landing on hip-height wall or higher. The walls must be 6-steps away from each other. \n\nYou can takeoff two feet or one-foot and land one foot on the top of the wall, with the other hanging along the side of the wall. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee. \n\nYour trajectory must be downwards from a standing position. However, you can crouch and bend down on the takeoff wall.\n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Level-to-Level','','publish','closed','closed','','level-to-level','','','2021-12-10 10:26:15','2021-12-10 10:26:15','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55249',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55250,1,'2021-12-10 10:31:31','2021-12-10 10:31:31','Demonstrate a crane landing on hip-height railing or higher. The rail must be 6-steps away from the takeoff. The rail must not have any bars or obstructions underneath it for the hanging leg to touch.\n\nYou can takeoff two feet or one-foot and land one foot on the top of the rail, with the other hanging down. The hanging leg must be straight. \n\nYou can takeoff from the floor, but you must have a clearly marked line on the ground and your foot must not go past it.\n\nThe rail must be circular.\n\nYou can run into the jump.\n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.','Rail Crane','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-crane','','','2021-12-10 10:31:31','2021-12-10 10:31:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55250',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55251,1,'2021-12-10 10:36:27','2021-12-10 10:36:27','Demonstrate a running crane over a head-height gap that is eight steps or wider onto any landing height target.\n\nYou can takeoff two feet or one-foot and land one foot on the top of the target, with the other hanging down. The hanging leg must be straight, with the toes pulled back to protect the knee.\n\nDo not twist. Must be facing the same direction as the takeoff.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides, left &amp; right.\n','Big Crane','','publish','closed','closed','','big-crane','','','2021-12-10 10:36:27','2021-12-10 10:36:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55251',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55252,1,'2021-12-12 08:39:37','2021-12-12 08:39:37','Demonstrate a standing arm-jump (aka Cat-Leap) from 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away, onto a chest-height wall or higher.\r\n\r\nLanding with knees bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. Landing feet first, then hands.\r\n\r\nArms must be bent on landing. Full finger length over the top. No overgrip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nHold for 3 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each leg landing high, the other low.\r\n\r\nTakeoff two feet from the jump. You cannot lean in without a jump into the arm-jump.','Standing Arm Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','standing-arm-jump','','','2021-12-12 08:39:37','2021-12-12 08:39:37','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55252',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55253,1,'2021-12-12 08:51:03','2021-12-12 08:51:03','Demonstrate a running arm-jump (aka Cat-Leap) from 5-steps (heel-to-toe) away, onto a chest-height wall or higher.\n\nLanding feet first, then hands. Landing with knees bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. \n\nArms must be bent on landing. Full finger length over the top. No overgrip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\n\nHold for 3 seconds.\n\nRepeat three times on each leg landing high, the other low.\n\nTakeoff one or two feet from the jump. You can take as many steps before the jump as required.\n\nMake a mark on the floor for the distance.','Running Arm-Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','running-arm-jump','','','2021-12-12 08:51:03','2021-12-12 08:51:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55253',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55254,1,'2021-12-12 08:54:11','2021-12-12 08:54:11','Demonstrate a standing arm-jump (aka Cat-Leap) from 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away, onto a head-height wall or higher.\r\n\r\nLanding with knees bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. Landing feet first, then hands.\r\n\r\nArms must be bent on landing. Full finger length over the top. No overgrip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nHold for 3 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each leg landing high, the other low.\r\n\r\nTakeoff two feet from the jump. You cannot lean in without a jump into the arm-jump.','Upwards Arm-Jump','','publish','closed','closed','','upwards-arm-jump','','','2021-12-12 08:54:11','2021-12-12 08:54:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55254',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55255,1,'2021-12-12 09:00:44','2021-12-12 09:00:44','Demonstrate a standing arm-jump (aka Cat-Leap) from 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away onto a bar / pole / drainpipe / lamppost, that is within 1-step next to a wall.\r\n\r\nLanding with knees bent, feet must be each side of the pole. Both legs will be at same height.\r\n\r\nArms must be bent on landing.\r\n\r\nHold for 3 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nTakeoff two feet from the jump. You cannot lean in without a jump into the arm-jump.','Pole Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','pole-cat','','','2021-12-12 09:00:44','2021-12-12 09:00:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55255',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55256,1,'2021-12-12 09:32:07','2021-12-12 09:32:07','Demonstrate a standing waist-cat from 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away onto a hip-height or higher wall. You must land on top of the wall with the arms straight and legs still off the wall.\r\n\r\nHands facing forwards with arms straight. Your toes should be pulled back to avoid the knee from hitting the wall.\r\n\r\nHold for 3 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nTakeoff two feet from the jump. You cannot lean in without a jump into the waist-cat.','Waist Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','waist-cat','','','2021-12-12 09:32:07','2021-12-12 09:32:07','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55256',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55257,1,'2021-12-12 09:38:50','2021-12-12 09:38:50','Demonstrate an arm-jump where you are standing on a wall and the target wall is the same height. You trajectory will be downwards because the parallel wall will be no higher than where your takeoff feet are.\n\nThis is to demonstrate a downwards arm-jump. The height of both walls will need to be chest-height or greater.\n\nBoth walls must be 6-steps or further from each other.\n\nLanding with knees bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. Landing feet first, then hands.\n\nArms must be bent on landing. Full finger length over the top. No overgrip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\n\nHold for 3 seconds.\n\nRepeat three times, turning in both directions. Left and Right.','Level to Level','','publish','closed','closed','','level-to-level-cat','','','2021-12-12 09:38:50','2021-12-12 09:38:50','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55257',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55258,1,'2021-12-12 09:46:33','2021-12-12 09:46:33','Starting in an arm-jump (aka cat-leap) position, on one wall, Step up and kick away from the wall while turning 180 degrees to a second, parallel, wall. Landing in an arm-jump position.\r\n\r\nHeight is the key. Step up as high as you can on the wall while turning and then kicking the wall like a tic-tac.\r\n\r\nBoth walls must be 6-steps or further from each other. Both walls must be chest-height or greater.\r\n\r\nLanding with knees bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. Landing feet first, then hands.\r\n\r\nArms must be bent on landing. Full finger length over the top. No overgrip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nHold for 3 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times, turning in both directions. Left and Right.\r\n','180 Cat-to-Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','180-cat-to-cat','','','2021-12-12 09:46:33','2021-12-12 09:46:33','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55258',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55259,1,'2021-12-12 09:53:23','2021-12-12 09:53:23','Demonstrate a koala catch to a pipe / pole / drainpipe / lamppost. You must land feet and hands on the pole.\n\nTakeoff distance must be six-steps away from the pole.\n\nLanding with knees bent, feet must be on the pole. One foot higher than the other. You can land with alternating feet directions or both turned out facing the same direction.\n\nArms must be bent on landing.\n\nHold for 3 seconds.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nTakeoff two feet from the jump. You cannot lean in without a jump into the arm-jump.','Koala','','publish','closed','closed','','koala-11','','','2021-12-12 09:53:23','2021-12-12 09:53:23','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55259',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55260,1,'2021-12-12 10:12:44','2021-12-12 10:12:44','Demonstrate a standing arm-jump (aka Cat-Leap) from 6-steps (heel-to-toe) away, onto a chest-height or higher ridge, ledge, shallow edge or feature that only allows the first two phalanges (distal and intermediate) of your fingers to catch the wall.\r\n\r\nLanding with knees bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. Landing feet first, then hands.\r\n\r\nArms must be bent on landing. Must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nHold for 3 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nTakeoff two feet from the jump. You cannot lean in without a jump into the arm-jump.','Crimp','','publish','closed','closed','','crimp','','','2021-12-12 10:12:44','2021-12-12 10:12:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55260',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55261,1,'2021-12-12 10:23:35','2021-12-12 10:23:35','Demonstrate an arm-jump / cat-leap at height. You must jump over a gap that is one and a half times your head height and is six steps or further away.\r\n\r\nLanding with knees bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. Landing feet first, then hands.\r\n\r\nArms must be bent on landing. Full finger length over the top. No overgrip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nHold for 3 seconds, then climb-up onto wall.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nTakeoff two feet from the jump. You cannot lean in without a jump into the arm-jump.','High Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','high-cat','','','2021-12-12 10:23:35','2021-12-12 10:23:35','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55261',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55262,1,'2021-12-13 08:34:14','2021-12-13 08:34:14','Start by hanging in a cat-leap/arm-jump position on the side of a wall with your fingers on top of the wall, on a ridge or a railing next to a wall, where you have a higher target wall/ridge/ledge/rail/bar that you can grab.\n\nDemonstrate moving onto the higher ridge/ledge/bar by using one arm first, then join the other onto the higher part to ascend. Feet must stay on the side of the wall and cannot be used on anything else. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\n\nThe distance of the upper bar/ridge/ledge from the bottom one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides leading with each left or right arm. \n\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving upwards.\n\nThe upper target does not have to be parallel with the lower starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','One Arm &amp; Feet','','publish','closed','closed','','one-arm-feet','','','2021-12-13 08:34:14','2021-12-13 08:34:14','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55262',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55263,1,'2021-12-13 08:38:34','2021-12-13 08:38:34','Start by hanging in a cat-leap/arm-jump position on the side of a wall with your fingers on top of the wall, on a ridge or a railing next to a wall, where you have a higher target wall/ridge/ledge/rail/bar that you can grab.\n\nDemonstrate moving onto the higher ridge/ledge/bar by using **both** arms at the same time onto the higher point. Feet must stay on the side of the wall and cannot be used on anything else. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\n\nThe distance of the upper bar/ridge/ledge from the bottom one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\n\nRepeat three times. \n\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving upwards.\n\nThe upper target does not have to be parallel with the lower starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','Double Arms &amp; Feet','','publish','closed','closed','','double-arms-feet','','','2021-12-13 08:38:34','2021-12-13 08:38:34','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55263',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55264,1,'2021-12-13 08:43:10','2021-12-13 08:43:10','Start by hanging in a cat-leap/arm-jump position on the side of a wall with your fingers on top of the wall, on a ridge or a railing next to a wall, where you have a **lower** target wall/ridge/ledge/rail/bar that you lower yourself down to.\n\nDemonstrate moving onto the lower ridge/ledge/bar by using one arm first, then join the other onto the lower part to descend. Feet must stay on the side of the wall and cannot be used on anything else. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\n\nThe distance of the lower bar/ridge/ledge from the top one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides leading with each left or right arm. \n\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving downwards.\n\nThe lower target does not have to be parallel with the lower starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','Single Drop &amp; Feet','','publish','closed','closed','','single-drop-feet','','','2021-12-13 08:43:10','2021-12-13 08:43:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55264',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55265,1,'2021-12-13 08:45:41','2021-12-13 08:45:41','Start by hanging in a cat-leap/arm-jump position on the side of a wall with your fingers on top of the wall, on a ridge or a railing next to a wall, where you have a **lower** target wall/ridge/ledge/rail/bar that you lower yourself down to.\n\nDemonstrate moving onto the lower ridge/ledge/bar by using both arms at the same time. Feet must stay on the side of the wall and cannot be used on anything else. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\n\nThe distance of the lower bar/ridge/ledge from the top one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\n\nRepeat three times. \n\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving downwards.\n\nThe lower target does not have to be parallel with the lower starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','Double Drop &amp; Feet','','publish','closed','closed','','double-drop-feet','','','2021-12-13 08:45:41','2021-12-13 08:45:41','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55265',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55266,1,'2021-12-13 08:51:15','2021-12-13 08:51:15','Start by jumping into a cat-leap/arm-jump position on the side of a wall with your fingers on top of the wall, on a ridge or a railing next to a wall, where you have a higher target wall/ridge/ledge/rail/bar that you can grab. \r\n\r\nThe cat-leap must be 4-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater distance from the wall.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate moving onto the higher ridge/ledge/bar by using **both** arms at the same time onto the higher point. Feet must stay on the side of the wall and cannot be used on anything else. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\r\n\r\nThe distance of the upper bar/ridge/ledge from the bottom one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times. \r\n\r\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving upwards.\r\n\r\nThe upper target does not have to be parallel with the lower starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','Cat Double Tap','','publish','closed','closed','','cat-double-tap','','','2021-12-13 08:51:15','2021-12-13 08:51:15','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55266',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55267,1,'2021-12-13 08:55:55','2021-12-13 08:55:55','Start by hanging on a railing/bar/overhang in a dead-hang position. You must have a higher railing/bar/target above you to aim for.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate moving onto the higher bar by using one arm first, then join the other onto the higher part to ascend. Feet and legs must not touch anything. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\r\n\r\nYou are demonstrating a dyno/double-tap without the use of the legs.\r\n\r\nThe distance of the upper bar from the bottom one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on both sides leading with each left or right arm. \r\n\r\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving upwards.\r\n\r\nThe upper target does not have to be parallel with the lower starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','One Arm','London Parkour is a resource dedicated to the art of parkour, offering tutorials and insights into practical movement techniques that aim to transform participants’ physical capabilities and mindset. The organization\'s website, LondonParkour.com, serves as a hub for parkour enthusiasts, providing guidance on how to effectively practice and improve one\'s skills.\r\n\r\nThe channel encourages viewers to subscribe for ongoing tutorials and parkour-related content, helping users learn various movements and techniques. They maintain an active presence on social media platforms, including Instagram and YouTube, where they share parkour demonstrations and tips to inspire and engage the community.\r\n\r\nOverall, London Parkour emphasizes the importance of practical movement—how it influences not just physical abilities but also personal perception and emotional well-being. By participating in parkour, individuals can explore the freedom of movement in their environment, encouraging both physical fitness and a deeper connection to their surroundings.','publish','closed','closed','','one-arm','','','2021-12-13 08:55:55','2021-12-13 08:55:55','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55267',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55268,1,'2021-12-13 09:04:52','2021-12-13 09:04:52','Start by hanging on a railing/bar/overhang in a dead-hang position. You must have a higher railing/bar/target above you to aim for.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate moving onto the higher bar by using **both** arms at the same time onto the higher part to ascend. Feet and legs must not touch anything. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\r\n\r\nYou are demonstrating a dyno/double-tap without the use of the legs.\r\n\r\nThe distance of the upper bar from the bottom one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times. \r\n\r\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving upwards.\r\n\r\nThe upper target does not have to be parallel with the lower starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','Double Tap','The double-hand double-tap is an advanced parkour technique that requires precision and timing. It involves using both hands to tap or grip a surface as part of a fluid movement, allowing for greater upward momentum and control during tricks and jumps. Parkour enthusiasts often practice this technique to enhance their overall agility and capability when navigating urban environments, making it an essential skill for anyone serious about mastering parkour.\n\nTo execute the double-hand double-tap effectively, individuals need to focus on their hand placement and the timing of their movements. This technique typically starts with a jump or a vault towards a wall or a high surface. As the body is propelled upwards, both hands should make contact with the target area simultaneously, ensuring a strong grip that aids in maintaining balance and stability while transitioning to subsequent movements.\n\nPracticing the double-hand double-tap can lead to improved performance in various parkour maneuvers, such as wall runs, climbs, or flips. This technique not only improves physical strength but also boosts mental confidence in one\'s ability to navigate complex environments efficiently. By incorporating the double-hand double-tap into their training routines, parkour practitioners can elevate their skills and expand their range of movements in exciting ways.','publish','closed','closed','','double-tap','','','2021-12-13 09:04:52','2021-12-13 09:04:52','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55268',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55269,1,'2021-12-13 09:09:27','2021-12-13 09:09:27','Start by hanging on a railing/bar/overhang in a dead-hang position on a higher bar. You must have a lower railing/bar/target below you to aim for.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate moving onto the lower bar by using one arm first, then join the other onto the lower part to descend. Feet and legs must not touch anything. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\r\n\r\nYou are demonstrating a dyno/double-tap without the use of the legs going downwards.\r\n\r\nThe distance of the upper bar from the bottom one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on both sides leading with each left or right arm. \r\n\r\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving downwards.\r\n\r\nThe lower target does not have to be parallel with the upper starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','Single Drop','London Parkour offers a comprehensive platform for individuals interested in learning and practicing parkour. Their website, https://LONDONPARKOUR.com, provides a variety of resources, including tutorials and how-to guides, aimed at transforming the way participants think, move, and feel. Through the practice of parkour, individuals can experience personal growth in both physical and mental aspects of movement.\r\n\r\nThe London Parkour community encourages users to engage with their content via various social media platforms, including Instagram and YouTube. Subscribers can access video tutorials and demonstrations that cater to all skill levels, promoting a supportive environment for learning this dynamic and practical movement discipline. By following their channels, participants can stay updated on new content, events, and opportunities within the parkour community.\r\n\r\nFor those looking to connect or seek further information, London Parkour provides a direct contact option through email at contact@localhost:8443. Their commitment to fostering an inclusive and engaging parkour experience is evident through their emphasis on practical movement and community support, making it an ideal resource for anyone interested in enhancing their parkour skills.','publish','closed','closed','','single-drop','','','2021-12-13 09:09:27','2021-12-13 09:09:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55269',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55270,1,'2021-12-13 09:12:52','2021-12-13 09:12:52','Start by hanging on a railing/bar/overhang in a dead-hang position on a higher bar. You must have a lower railing/bar/target below you to aim for.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate moving onto the lower bar by using both arms at the same time onto the lower part to descend. Feet and legs must not touch anything. This is called the dyno or double-tap.\r\n\r\nYou are demonstrating a dyno/double-tap without the use of the legs going downwards.\r\n\r\nThe distance of the upper bar from the bottom one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times. \r\n\r\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving downwards.\r\n\r\nThe lower target does not have to be parallel with the upper starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','Drop Catch','London Parkour is dedicated to teaching practical movement skills and transforming individuals\' ability to think, move, and feel through the art of parkour. Their website, localhost:8443, serves as a hub for tutorials, resources, and inspiration related to parkour practice. The channel encourages newcomers and enthusiasts alike to explore the dynamic world of parkour by subscribing to their YouTube channel for informative content.\r\n\r\nThe organization emphasizes community engagement and offers a variety of tutorials and resources tailored for learners of all levels. Their social media presence on platforms like Instagram and YouTube helps to foster a supportive environment where practitioners can connect, share experiences, and refine their skills. London Parkour aims to make parkour accessible and enjoyable for everyone while promoting a healthy lifestyle and positive mindset.\r\n\r\nThe overarching goal of London Parkour is not just to teach movements but to instill a sense of confidence and creativity in practitioners. By emphasizing the physical and mental benefits of parkour, they hope to inspire individuals to embrace challenge and discover new ways to engage with their environment. With a focus on transformation and personal growth, London Parkour welcomes everyone to join and explore the exciting possibilities within this unique discipline.','publish','closed','closed','','drop-catch','','','2021-12-13 09:12:52','2021-12-13 09:12:52','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55270',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55271,1,'2021-12-13 09:17:27','2021-12-13 09:17:27','Start by hanging on a railing/bar/overhang in a dead-hang position. You must have a higher railing/bar/target above you to aim for.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate moving onto the higher bar by using **both** arms at the same time onto the higher part to ascend. Feet and legs must not touch anything. \r\n\r\nYou must then drop back down to the lower bar with both hands at the same time.\r\n\r\nThe distance of the upper bar from the bottom one must be one step length (heel-to-tow) or more. Use your shoe to measure the distance.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in a row without dropping off the bar.\r\n\r\nYou can change orientation and move to your left or right, as long as you are moving upwards.\r\n\r\nThe upper target does not have to be parallel with the lower starting position. You can use perpendicular rails, as long as the height requirement is met.','Triple Dyno','London Parkour offers a comprehensive platform for individuals interested in learning and mastering parkour techniques through its various online resources. Their website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a central hub for information, tutorials, and practical movement training that transforms how individuals think, move, and feel. Users are encouraged to subscribe to their YouTube channel for an array of parkour-related content, including tutorials and instructional videos.\r\n\r\nThe company emphasizes the importance of community and connection, providing different social media platforms where enthusiasts can engage with their content and with each other. By following their Instagram and YouTube channels, followers can stay updated on new techniques, events, and training tips to enhance their parkour skills. The company aims to create a supportive network for practitioners to learn and grow in their journey through parkour.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in direct communication or seeking additional information, London Parkour provides contact options, ensuring that their audience can easily reach out with inquiries or feedback. The incorporation of various digital platforms demonstrates their commitment to making parkour accessible and enjoyable for everyone, fostering a welcoming environment for both newcomers and experienced practitioners alike.','publish','closed','closed','','triple-dyno','','','2021-12-13 09:17:27','2021-12-13 09:17:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55271',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55272,1,'2021-12-13 11:01:56','2021-12-13 11:01:56','Demonstrate a skin-the-cat movement on a full-stretch bar or higher. Start in a dead-hang position on the bar, elevate your legs and hips, passing your feet over your head, under the bar, and between your hands. \n\nKeep turning over until your legs are straight and your feet are pointing to the ground.\n\nNow pull and return back to the dead-hang position.\n\nRepeat three times, returning back to the dead-hang position.\n\nHands must be in a pronated grip on the bar.','Skin the Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','skin-the-cat','','','2021-12-13 11:01:56','2021-12-13 11:01:56','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55272',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55273,1,'2021-12-13 11:18:47','2021-12-13 11:18:47','Using your hands and feet you must climb up a rope three times your head-height. If the rope is not long enough, you must descend the rope and climb again without touching the floor.\n\nRepeat only once. \n\nDistance is measured from where your top hand starts and finishes. Rope can be any width. ','Rope Climb','','publish','closed','closed','','rope-climb','','','2021-12-13 11:18:47','2021-12-13 11:18:47','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55273',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55274,1,'2021-12-13 11:22:57','2021-12-13 11:22:57','Climb up two parallel walls that are within two to tree steps of each other (heel-to-toe). You must use your back on one side and feet on the other. Pushing with your hands under your hips.\r\n\r\nYou must reach over twice head-height and back down from your starting position.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Bridging','London Parkour is an organization dedicated to teaching and promoting the art of parkour, emphasizing practical movement that enhances one\'s physical abilities and overall mindset. Their website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a hub for resources related to parkour training, tutorials, and community engagement. They encourage enthusiasts to connect with their content and participate in their activities, showcasing the transformative potential of parkour in everyday life.\r\n\r\nThe organization actively maintains a presence on various social media platforms, including Instagram and YouTube, where they share instructional videos and parkour-related content. Their YouTube channel features a range of parkour tutorials that cater to different skill levels, making it easier for individuals to learn at their own pace while engaging with a wider community of like-minded practitioners. The emphasis on practical movement reflects their commitment to making parkour accessible and enjoyable for everyone.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in further involvement or inquiries, London Parkour provides contact information through their website and social media channels. They invite followers and curious individuals to subscribe and stay updated on their latest offerings while promoting a supportive and inclusive atmosphere for learning and practicing parkour.','publish','closed','closed','','bridging','','','2021-12-13 11:22:57','2021-12-13 11:22:57','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55274',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55275,1,'2021-12-13 11:24:34','2021-12-13 11:24:34','Climb up two parallel walls that are within two to tree steps of each other (heel-to-toe). You must use one hand and foot on one side, with the opposite hand and foot on thee other. Alternating the hand/foot pressure.\r\n\r\nYou must reach over twice head-height and back down from your starting position.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Split climb','London Parkour is a dedicated platform that offers a range of resources and tutorials for individuals interested in the sport of parkour. The website, www.localhost:8443, serves as a comprehensive guide to practical movement, aiming to transform how participants think, move, and feel. By providing valuable information and training materials, it helps enthusiasts of all skill levels learn and enhance their parkour techniques.\r\n\r\nThe community is encouraged to engage with London Parkour through various online platforms. They can subscribe to their YouTube channel for parkour tutorials and related content, as well as follow the Instagram account for updates and inspiration. The goal is to foster a supportive and interactive environment for those looking to improve their parkour skills and connect with others who share the same passion.\r\n\r\nFor anyone looking to delve deeper into parkour, the website and associated social media channels offer numerous opportunities for learning and growth. By providing practical insights and community support, London Parkour aims to inspire individuals to embrace this dynamic form of movement and challenge themselves in new ways.','publish','closed','closed','','split-climb','','','2021-12-13 11:24:34','2021-12-13 11:24:34','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55275',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55276,1,'2021-12-13 11:29:47','2021-12-13 11:29:47','Using two parallel walls/obstacles/railings, use your feet on the one wall and your hands on the second wall. Create a stable structure using your abs and traverse upwards to over full-stretch height.\r\n\r\nReturn back down using the same method until you can step down.\r\n\r\nThe walls must be over six-steps from each other. You must be facing the floor.\r\n\r\nYou must not touch the ground.\r\n\r\nRepeat twice.','Vertical Lumbar','','publish','closed','closed','','vertical-lumbar','','','2021-12-13 11:29:47','2021-12-13 11:29:47','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55276',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55277,1,'2021-12-13 11:36:20','2021-12-13 11:36:20','Start in a dead-hang on a bar, bring your legs up while pulling with your arms. Raise your hips up over the front of the bar and swing your legs overhead. Spinning up on top of the bar in a waist-catch position.\r\n\r\nYou can use any thickness bar and use momentum to help. You must finish on top of the bar.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.','Pull-over','London Parkour offers a dynamic online platform focused on practical movement techniques designed to enhance physical abilities while transforming one\'s mindset and emotions. Through various online resources, including tutorials and instructional videos, the website aims to teach individuals the art of parkour, promoting both fitness and mental well-being.\r\n\r\nTo engage with content and keep up with the latest parkour training tips, enthusiasts are encouraged to subscribe to the London Parkour YouTube channel. This subscription provides access to a wealth of parkour-related tutorials and insights, allowing followers to improve their skills progressively. The community is dedicated to fostering a supportive environment for those interested in learning or enhancing their parkour techniques.\r\n\r\nIn addition to YouTube, London Parkour maintains an active presence on Instagram, providing followers with visual inspirations and updates on parkour activities. For inquiries or further information, individuals can reach out through the provided contact email. Overall, London Parkour is committed to transforming the way people think, move, and feel through the practice of parkour.','publish','closed','closed','','pull-over','','','2021-12-13 11:36:20','2021-12-13 11:36:20','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55277',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55278,1,'2021-12-13 11:41:05','2021-12-13 11:41:05','Using your hands only you must climb up a rope three times your head-height. If the rope is not long enough, you must descend the rope and climb again.\n\nYou must use a sit-start with your feet off the ground. You must finish in a seated position with your feet off the ground.\n\nYou may not use the legs at any point.\n\nRepeat twice without stopping and touching the ground between the start and finish. \n\nDistance is measured from where your top hand starts and finishes. Rope can be any width. ','Hands-only Rope','','publish','closed','closed','','hands-only-rope','','','2021-12-13 11:41:05','2021-12-13 11:41:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55278',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55279,1,'2021-12-13 11:46:24','2021-12-13 11:46:24','Starting in a dead-hang position on a full-stretch height scaffolding-bar (or wider) with your hands in pronated grip, you must climb up on top of the bar on the same side. You can use any body limb to climb up.\n\nYou can only touch the bar you are hanging on, no other item.\n\nYou cannot use a pull-over / spin-up to get up.\n\nRepeat three times.','Bar Climb-up','','publish','closed','closed','','bar-climb-up','','','2021-12-13 11:46:24','2021-12-13 11:46:24','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55279',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55280,1,'2021-12-13 11:50:46','2021-12-13 11:50:46','Standing under a full-stretch height bar, jump up grabbing the bar in an underbar grip (Hands crossed, one underneath, one on top). Using the momentum of the jump and pull, Muscle-up on the far side using the underbar to aid speed.\n\nRepeat twice on each side with hands in opposite underbar orientation.\n\nYou must start on thee ground and finish at your waist on the top of the bar.','Underbar Muscleup','','publish','closed','closed','','underbar-muscleup','','','2021-12-13 11:50:46','2021-12-13 11:50:46','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55280',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55281,1,'2021-12-13 11:55:15','2021-12-13 11:55:15','You can start at the top position or bottom position, but you must demonstrate a muscle-up with a complete bilateral movement of the elbows on a full-height bar that is scaffolding-width or thicker.\n\nStart from a hang or on the top at the waist position. \n\nYou must show the pulling action onto the top of the rail in a strict muscle-up.\n\nRepeat twice. \n\nHands must be pronated, but can be in a false-grip position.','Muscleup','','publish','closed','closed','','muscleup','','','2021-12-13 11:55:15','2021-12-13 11:55:15','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55281',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55282,1,'2021-12-16 15:32:16','2021-12-16 15:32:16','Demonstrate walking forwards on a circular railing that is no wider than a standard scaffolding pipe and turning back 180 degrees to face the opposite direction every 5-steps. You must travel forwards the entire time while walking along the same section of bar, turning at the ends.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three laps along the bar (there and back). You must turn both left and right.\r\n\r\nBar can be any height.\r\n\r\nRepeat the three laps (six lengths) Once without falling off.\r\n\r\nYou must not use anything to help balance you.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be straight, with no corners in it.\r\n\r\nTips on turning:\r\n1. Place both feet on the bar.\r\n2. Bend your knees 90 degrees.\r\n3. Using the ball of both feet, slowly turn with complete control.\r\n4. Do not rush the turn at any point.\r\n5. Slowly turn 180 degrees to face the way you came.','Turning','London Parkour offers a comprehensive online platform dedicated to the art of parkour, featuring tutorials and resources for individuals looking to enhance their movement skills. By subscribing to their YouTube channel, users gain access to a range of instructional videos that help to better understand parkour techniques. Additionally, the company\'s website serves as a hub for information and community engagement.\r\n\r\nThe website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, is designed to provide practical insights on physical movement, emphasizing a transformative approach that impacts not just how one moves but also how one thinks and feels. It invites learners, regardless of their skill level, to explore the philosophy and practice of parkour, combining physical activity with personal development. \r\n\r\nFurthermore, London Parkour maintains an active presence on social media platforms such as Instagram, enhancing its outreach and ability to connect with enthusiasts. For more information or inquiries, interested individuals can reach out via their contact email at contact@localhost:8443.','publish','closed','closed','','turning','','','2021-12-16 15:32:16','2021-12-16 15:32:16','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55282',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55283,1,'2021-12-16 15:36:41','2021-12-16 15:36:41','Demonstrate a two foot crouched or standing position, facing 90degrees away (perpendicular) to the rail. You must hold the position for 30 seconds.\r\n\r\nUse the ball of the foot to focus the downward pressure. Grip with the toes to contract the muscles harder and keep your balance.\r\n\r\nBar can be any height. You cannot use anything else to help balance you.\r\n\r\nRepeat twice.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be straight, with no corners in it.','Perpendicular','London Parkour is an organization that focuses on the art of practical movement, designed to reshape an individual\'s approach to physicality and self-expression. By incorporating elements of parkour into daily life, individuals can experience transformative changes in how they think, move, and feel. The company offers various resources, tutorials, and information related to parkour practices and techniques through their website and social media channels.\r\n\r\nThe organization actively encourages individuals to subscribe to their YouTube channel, providing a platform for parkour enthusiasts to access tutorials, tips, and new content regularly. Their social media presence on Instagram and YouTube fosters a community where members can connect and learn from one another, helping to promote the benefits of parkour as a holistic form of movement.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in parkour training or seeking guidance, London Parkour offers a wealth of resources, including instructional videos and tutorials. Users can visit their website for more information, subscribe to regular updates, and engage with the parkour community through their online platforms, enhancing their understanding and practice of this dynamic movement art.','publish','closed','closed','','perpendicular','','','2021-12-16 15:36:41','2021-12-16 15:36:41','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55283',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55284,1,'2021-12-16 15:43:03','2021-12-16 15:43:03','Demonstrate a two foot squat from crouched to standing position, facing 90degrees away (perpendicular) to the rail. You must complete 10 squats on the bar without falling off.\r\n\r\nUse the ball of the foot to focus the downward pressure. Grip with the toes to contract the muscles harder and keep your balance.\r\n\r\nBar can be any height. You cannot use anything else to help balance you.\r\n\r\nYou can take as long as you like to squat up and down.\r\n\r\nYour hips must drop down below your knee height. The knee angle must be less than 75 degrees.\r\n\r\nYou must stand up completely, with the knees in a straight line with the ankle and hip.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be straight, with no corners in it.','Balance Squats','London Parkour is an organization dedicated to teaching practical movement skills that enhance both physical and mental abilities. Their approach emphasizes transformation in how individuals think, move, and feel, promoting an active and engaged lifestyle. They provide tutorials and tips on parkour through various platforms, encouraging enthusiasts to better their skills and enjoy the benefits of this dynamic discipline.\r\n\r\nThe group maintains a presence on multiple social media channels, including Instagram and YouTube, where they post instructional videos under the tags #ParkourHowTo, #ParkourTutorial, and #ParkourPulse. These platforms serve as resources for both beginners and experienced practitioners looking to refine their techniques or learn new moves. Engaging with their content helps participants stay motivated and connected to the parkour community.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in exploring this unique form of movement, London Parkour encourages users to subscribe to their channels for the latest updates and tutorials. More information about their offerings can be found on their website, where visitors can learn about the philosophy behind parkour and how it can positively impact their lives. Whether through online tutorials or community engagement, London Parkour invites everyone to discover the transformative power of parkour.','publish','closed','closed','','balance-squats','','','2021-12-16 15:43:03','2021-12-16 15:43:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55284',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55285,1,'2021-12-16 15:51:07','2021-12-16 15:51:07','Staying in a crouched position, with the hips below the knees, walk along the bar as you would normally. Complete 5 steps in one direction, turn around and return back another 5 steps to complete a lap.\r\n\r\nRepeat three laps of 10-steps. (5-steps forward, 5-steps returning.)\r\n\r\nFace forwards the entire time, turning at the ends. Alternate the turns in opposite directions, so you should do three left turns, three right turns.\r\n\r\nComplete all laps without falling off the rail.\r\n\r\nYou cannot use anything to help you balance\r\n\r\nThe rail must be circular and no wider than standard scaffolding width. The rail must be straight, with no corners in it.','Crouch walk','London Parkour is a platform dedicated to the art of parkour, focusing on practical movement that transforms the way individuals think, move, and feel. By offering tutorials and resources, the organization aims to make expertise in parkour accessible to everyone, promoting both physical skills and mental confidence. \r\n\r\nThe team encourages community engagement through various online channels, including their YouTube channel where users can subscribe for updates and tutorials. Their social media presence on Instagram and YouTube reinforces their commitment to sharing knowledge and fostering a supportive environment for parkour enthusiasts.\r\n\r\nFor additional information and resources, visitors can explore their official website, which serves as a hub for learning and connecting with the parkour community. The platform also facilitates direct communication through their contact email, allowing individuals to inquire further about their services and opportunities.','publish','closed','closed','','crouch-walk','','','2021-12-16 15:51:07','2021-12-16 15:51:07','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55285',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55286,1,'2021-12-16 15:55:49','2021-12-16 15:55:49','Demonstrate the ability to cat-balance along a railing of standard scaffolding-width or less. The cat-balance must have both hands and feet on the railing in a crawl position. You must travel 10-steps along the rail forwards, and then return backwards without falling off.\r\n\r\nYou must stay on the rail the entire time, completing the entire 20 steps.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be straight and cannot contain any bends.\r\n\r\nRail can be any height.\r\n\r\nYou can take as long as you need.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Cat Balance','London Parkour is a platform dedicated to the practice and teaching of parkour, encouraging individuals to transform their physical abilities and mindset through movement. Their website, [LONDONPARKOUR.com](https://LONDONPARKOUR.com), offers resources and tutorials that aim to enhance the way participants think, move, and feel while mastering parkour techniques. \r\n\r\nThe organization actively engages users through various social media channels, including Instagram and YouTube, where they provide video content related to parkour tutorials and demonstrations. The YouTube channel, which invites subscriptions, features a range of instructional videos under hashtags like #ParkourHowTo and #ParkourTutorial, promoting a vibrant community of parkour enthusiasts and learners.\r\n\r\nFor further interaction and inquiries, London Parkour encourages users to connect via their email contact@localhost:8443. The mix of practical resources and an emphasis on community interactions positions London Parkour as a key destination for anyone interested in exploring the art of parkour.','publish','closed','closed','','cat-balance','','','2021-12-16 15:55:49','2021-12-16 15:55:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55286',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55287,1,'2021-12-16 16:02:52','2021-12-16 16:02:52','Start on standing on the rail with both feet at 90 degrees facing perpendicular to the bar. Cross one leg over in front of the other and then uncross with the opposite leg, travelling along the bar moving sideways.\r\n\r\nComplete five steps in one direction and then another five steps back to the start in the opposite direction.\r\n\r\nRepeat two times.\r\n\r\n## Part 2.\r\n\r\nNot repeat another two times, but with the foot crossing **behind** the other one. Completing five steps back and forth.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nBar must be straight and with no corners. Bar can be any height. Bar must be circular.','Sideways Walk','London Parkour offers tutorials and resources related to parkour, focusing on practical movement and its transformative effects on individuals\' thinking, movement, and overall well-being. The platform features a variety of instructional videos and tips, aimed at helping enthusiasts learn and master parkour techniques effectively.\r\n\r\nThe official London Parkour website, https://LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a comprehensive hub for parkour-related content, including training guides and community engagement. Additionally, users are encouraged to subscribe to the London Parkour YouTube channel for video content that enhances their learning experience and keeps them updated on parkour trends and events.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in connecting with the London Parkour community, social media platforms such as Instagram and YouTube provide an interactive way to engage with fellow parkour practitioners. The company emphasizes the importance of movement in everyday life and invites individuals to join their journey through various classes and tutorials.','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-walk','','','2021-12-16 16:02:52','2021-12-16 16:02:52','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55287',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55288,1,'2021-12-16 16:07:45','2021-12-16 16:07:45','Demonstrate a balance walk on a railing (square or circular) that is over full-stretch height off the ground.\r\n\r\nYou must complete 20 steps with two 180 degree turns.\r\n\r\nThe bar cannot have any corners in it and must not be close to any other obstacle within reaching distance.\r\n\r\nYou must complete the whole task without dropping off the rail.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Height Balance','London Parkour is a platform dedicated to teaching and promoting the art of parkour, a form of practical movement that emphasizes efficiency and fluidity in navigating one\'s environment. The website and associated social media channels, including YouTube and Instagram, provide tutorials, tips, and insights into the practice of parkour, helping individuals transform the way they think, move, and feel. \r\n\r\nVisitors to the London Parkour website can access a wealth of resources, including parkour how-tos and tutorials, aimed at practitioners of all skill levels. The focus is on practical movement, emphasizing both physical and mental aspects of training. The channel encourages viewers to subscribe for continuous updates and new content that aligns with their interests in parkour.\r\n\r\nTo connect with a community of like-minded enthusiasts and stay updated on the latest in parkour, individuals are encouraged to subscribe to the London Parkour YouTube channel, follow their Instagram account, and reach out through their contact email. The emphasis is on fostering a supportive environment for learning and sharing experiences related to parkour.','publish','closed','closed','','height-balance','','','2021-12-16 16:07:45','2021-12-16 16:07:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55288',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55289,1,'2021-12-17 14:08:32','2021-12-17 14:08:32','Standing from five-steps (heel-to-toe) away, jump and catch a hip-height railing with your hands and hip. Catch with your feet off the floor.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\n','Hip-Catch','','publish','closed','closed','','hip-catch','','','2021-12-17 14:08:32','2021-12-17 14:08:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55289',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55290,1,'2021-12-17 14:13:45','2021-12-17 14:13:45','Using only one hand on a different bar, consistently move for 15 seconds on a set of monkey-bars. Keep moving, including the use of turns. You cannot stop moving at any point.\n\nYour feet must not touch the ground or use any other obstacle to help you. \n\nRepeat once.','Monkey Bars','','publish','closed','closed','','monkey-bars','','','2021-12-17 14:13:45','2021-12-17 14:13:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55290',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55291,1,'2021-12-17 14:16:10','2021-12-17 14:16:10','Using some playground monkey-bars, demonstrate a full 360 degree turn in both directions using any of the bars.\n\nYour feet must not touch the ground or use any other obstacle to help you. \n\nRepeat once. ','Monkey Bar Turns','','publish','closed','closed','','monkey-bar-turns','','','2021-12-17 14:16:10','2021-12-17 14:16:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55291',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55292,1,'2021-12-17 14:27:56','2021-12-17 14:27:56','Hang from a bar and move along it facing the end of the bar the whole time. You can turn and return back in the opposite direction if needed. \n\nYou must traverse 20-steps (heel-to-toe) facing forwards.\n\nRepeat once.\n\nHands must be both on opposite sides of the bar overhead.','Forward Traverse','','publish','closed','closed','','forward-traverse','','','2021-12-17 14:27:56','2021-12-17 14:27:56','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55292',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55293,1,'2021-12-17 14:31:19','2021-12-17 14:31:19','Hang from a bar and move along it facing 90 degrees (perpendicular) from the end of the bar the whole time. You can turn and return back in the opposite direction if needed. \n\nYou must traverse 20-steps (heel-to-toe) facing outwards from the bar.\n\nRepeat once per side. (Traveling to your left, and then the right)','Sideways Traverse','','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-traverse','','','2021-12-17 14:31:19','2021-12-17 14:31:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55293',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55294,1,'2021-12-18 08:28:51','2021-12-18 08:28:51','Demonstrate traversing a bar for a continuous twenty seconds using the sideways swing technique.\nYou can use any railing, bar, scaffolding pole, overhang, pipe, of any thickness.\n\nIf the bar is not long enough for a continuous traversal for 20 seconds, then you can turnaround while on the bar and continue going back in the opposite direction.\n\nRepeat for both sides, going to your left and your right.\n\nRepeat once in each direction.\n\n## Example technique traversing to the right:\n1. Start hanging on the bar with both hands facing the same way.\n2. Pull up on the bar half way so your arms are bent.\n3. Keeping a stable core, swing your hips left and right to create momentum.\n4. As you swing the hips to the right, let go of the right hand and regrip further down the bar.\n5. On the back-swing to the left, the left hand joins the right hand down the bar.\n6. Repeat.','Sideways Swing','','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-swing','','','2021-12-18 08:28:51','2021-12-18 08:28:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55294',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55295,1,'2021-12-18 08:38:31','2021-12-18 08:38:31','Traverse in any direction on bars/pipe/overhang/scaffolding for twenty seconds while your lower-body, from the hip downwards, does not swing or move while the upper-body does all the movement.\n\nThis is to demonstrate core stability and control while swing traversing.\n\nIf the bar is not long enough for a continuous traversal for 20 seconds, then you can turnaround while on the bar and continue going back in the opposite direction. You can use multiple bars on a frame to move in any direction.\n\nRepeat twice.','Rigid Torso','','publish','closed','closed','','rigid-torso','','','2021-12-18 08:38:31','2021-12-18 08:38:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55295',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55296,1,'2021-12-18 08:47:25','2021-12-18 08:47:25','Traverse along any bar or rail from a hanging position while each movement forwards keeps your torso turning in the same direction (left or right). \n\nThe first movement will be a forwards grab, but the next will be a backwards one to keep the rotation.\n\nDemonstrate three 360-degree turns in total on the same bar while traversing along it.\n\nRepeat twice with the forward grab with the right hand. Then repeat twice with the forward grab on the left hand.\n\nYou cannot turn 360 degrees in the same location on the bar, you must be traveling along it while turning.','360 Turning','','publish','closed','closed','','360-turning','','','2021-12-18 08:47:25','2021-12-18 08:47:25','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55296',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55297,1,'2021-12-18 08:57:29','2021-12-18 08:57:29','Using overhead scaffolding, swing traverse on ten different overhead bars, moving along them constantly.\n\nYou must be over full-stretch height, and the bars must be standard scaffolding-width or greater. Railing must be circular.\n\nRepeat once.\n\nYou cannot drop or use your legs on any obstacle (making contact is ok, just not using the bar).\n\n','Scaffolding Traverse','','publish','closed','closed','','scaffolding-traverse','','','2021-12-18 08:57:29','2021-12-18 08:57:29','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55297',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55298,1,'2021-12-18 09:03:21','2021-12-18 09:03:21','On wide monkey bars / wide scaffolding bars / wide climbing frame, demonstrate a monkey bar traversal where one hand goes on each bar only. However, you must have to release the bar behind you before you grip the bar ahead.\n\nThere should be a moment between each bar where you are not touching either bar.\n\nTraverse for a total of four independent bars overhead. Each bar must be caught after you release the one behind it.\n\nRepeat three times.','Monkey Release','','publish','closed','closed','','monkey-release','','','2021-12-18 09:03:21','2021-12-18 09:03:21','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55298',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55299,1,'2021-12-20 08:40:48','2021-12-20 08:40:48','Demonstrate a full-stretch dead-hang on a bar that is scaffolding-width or greater for thirty seconds.\r\n\r\nYour hands must in be in a pronated grip on the bar.\r\n','Deadhang','','publish','closed','closed','','deadhang','','','2021-12-20 08:40:48','2021-12-20 08:40:48','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55299',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55300,1,'2021-12-18 16:04:00','2021-12-18 16:04:00','Demonstrate a basic Quadrupedal Movement (QM) or crawling pattern using opposite hand and foot for a distance of 10 paces. \r\n\r\nYou must perform both forwards and backwards directions one after the other without stopping.\r\n\r\nTotal distance should be 20 paces.\r\n\r\nYou must travel using opposite hand and foot.\r\n\r\nYou cannot stand-up, use your knees, crouch or only use the legs for weight-baring. \r\n\r\nYou are able to pause, but you must have weight distributed between three of your four limbs.\r\n\r\nYou must be facing the ground the entire time.\r\n\r\nRepeat once forwards, once backwards.','Basic QM','London Parkour is an initiative focused on practical movement, aiming to change the way individuals think, move, and feel. The program provides various resources, including tutorials and guides, to help practitioners learn and develop their parkour skills effectively. By subscribing to their YouTube channel, followers can access a wealth of content designed to enhance their understanding and execution of parkour techniques.\r\n\r\nThe organization\'s website, LondonParkour.com, serves as a hub for all things related to parkour in London, offering visitors essential information and insights. By engaging with their content, including videos and social media posts, users can immerse themselves in the parkour community and discover new ways to improve their movement capabilities. They also offer opportunities for personal growth through physical activity and the development of new skills.\r\n\r\nAdditionally, London Parkour maintains a presence on social media platforms such as Instagram and YouTube, enhancing their reach and connection with followers. Users are encouraged to join the community by subscribing and following their accounts for the latest tutorials, updates, and parkour-related content. For inquiries, people can contact them via their email address, fostering a supportive and engaging environment for parkour enthusiasts.','publish','closed','closed','','basic-qm','','','2021-12-18 16:04:00','2021-12-18 16:04:00','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55300',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55301,1,'2021-12-19 09:17:17','2021-12-19 09:17:17','Starting in a crouched position, place both hands flat out in front of you, lift your hips up and jump your feet in-between your hands. Keep repeating for 10-paces forwards and 10 backwards.\r\n\r\nYou must not stand up or place your knees on the ground. You must stay in a low-gait crouched position.\r\n\r\nYour knee must remain bent more than 90 degrees the entire time. You must takeoff two feet and land two feet with your hands flat.\r\n\r\nRepeat once forwards and once backwards.','Floor Cat-Pass','London Parkour offers a range of resources and tutorials focused on the practice of parkour, an athletic discipline that emphasizes efficient movement through urban environments. The organization has a dedicated website that provides insights and guidance on parkour techniques and philosophy, aiming to transform how individuals think, move, and feel through practical movement training.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in learning more about parkour, London Parkour invites enthusiasts to subscribe to their YouTube channel, where they release instructional videos and tutorials. Their content is designed to cater to both beginners and advanced practitioners, providing step-by-step guidance to improve skills and safety in parkour.\r\n\r\nAdditionally, London Parkour maintains an active presence on social media platforms like Instagram, further engaging with the community and sharing updates. Individuals can easily reach out for more information or inquiries through their provided contact email, reinforcing their commitment to spreading parkour culture and knowledge.','publish','closed','closed','','floor-cat-pass','','','2021-12-19 09:17:17','2021-12-19 09:17:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55301',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55302,1,'2021-12-19 09:25:40','2021-12-19 09:25:40','Demonstrate two sideways-monkey movements to the left and two movements to the right, repeating five times without stopping.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.\r\n\r\nYour hips must stay below your head and your leading leg must be straight while in the air.\r\n\r\nTry to keep your chest up and hands flat on the ground.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate a good movement ability and control in a low-gait position.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n1. Start in a crouch position.\r\n2. Place your left hand in front of your right foot.\r\n3. Place the right hand far out to the right of you so you are leaning on your right side.\r\n4. Jump both feet up in the air and drop the left foot past the left hand position.\r\n5. The right leg should extend straight out to the right and cover as much distance as possible.\r\n6. Push with both hands and return back to crouched position.','Sideways Monkey','London Parkour is an organization that focuses on practical movement, aiming to transform how individuals think, move, and feel. They offer resources and tutorials on Parkour, making it accessible and enhancing the learning experience for enthusiasts at all levels.\r\n\r\nThe organization promotes their content through various platforms, including their YouTube channel, where users can subscribe for tutorials and training tips. Their goal is to build a community passionate about Parkour, encouraging participation and engagement through social media channels such as Instagram.\r\n\r\nFor more information and resources, users can visit their official website, LondonParkour.com, which serves as a hub for Parkour-related content. Interested individuals can also reach out via their contact email for further inquiries or collaboration opportunities with the London Parkour community.','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-monkey','','','2021-12-19 09:25:40','2021-12-19 09:25:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55302',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55303,1,'2021-12-19 09:41:49','2021-12-19 09:41:49','With your hands behind you and your legs in-front of you, while keeping your hips up and belly in the air, move forwards for 10-paces and backwards for 10-paces.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate contraction of the hamstring and glute muscles while moving.\r\n\r\nMove opposite hand and foot while moving.\r\n\r\nYou must not allow the hip to drop more than the elbow height. Hips must stay up the entire time.\r\n\r\nYou cannot sit down or standup until completed.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Inverted Table','London Parkour offers resources and tutorials for individuals interested in learning parkour, a dynamic movement discipline. Their online presence includes a dedicated website, https://LONDONPARKOUR.com, which provides practical movement techniques aimed at enhancing how individuals think, move, and feel. The platform serves as a hub for both beginners and experienced practitioners to engage with the parkour community.\r\n\r\nThe organization encourages users to subscribe to their YouTube channel through the provided link to stay updated with the latest tutorials and parkour-related content. The social media presence on Instagram further reinforces their commitment to sharing parkour practices, insights, and community connections. Their tutorials are designed to be accessible, providing clear instructions and visual demonstrations to help users learn effectively.\r\n\r\nFor those looking to connect, London Parkour offers contact options via email and provides links to their social media platforms. By cultivating an active online community, they aim to inspire individuals to explore parkour as a form of self-expression and physical activity, enhancing their overall well-being through movement.','publish','closed','closed','','inverted-table','','','2021-12-19 09:41:49','2021-12-19 09:41:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55303',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55304,1,'2021-12-19 09:44:40','2021-12-19 09:44:40','Using the same movement technique as a ground cat-pass, this is the same except the feet land on the outside of the hands rather than inside.\r\n\r\nYou must complete 10 paces forwards and 10 backwards without stopping. Hands must be flat on the ground.\r\n\r\nHips must be lower than knee height.\r\n\r\nYou cannot standup or sit down.\r\n\r\nRepeat only once.\r\n\r\n','Ground Frog','London Parkour offers a wealth of resources and tutorials for those interested in the dynamic discipline of parkour. Through their YouTube channel, they provide a variety of instructional videos designed to help individuals at different skill levels learn the fundamentals and techniques of parkour. By subscribing, viewers can stay updated on the latest content and enhance their training experience.\r\n\r\nThe organization emphasizes the importance of practical movement, believing it can profoundly alter not only physical capabilities but also mental outlook and emotional well-being. Their website, https://LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a hub for information, resources, and community engagement related to the art of parkour.\r\n\r\nUsers can also engage with London Parkour on social media platforms like Instagram, where they can find additional content and connect with fellow enthusiasts. For inquiries or further information, individuals are encouraged to reach out via email at contact@localhost:8443.','publish','closed','closed','','ground-frog','','','2021-12-19 09:44:40','2021-12-19 09:44:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55304',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55305,1,'2021-12-19 09:49:11','2021-12-19 09:49:11','Start in a crouched position and place one hand on the ground to the side of you. The palm should be on the ground fully and the fingers facing backwards. Jump both feet up and rotate around the arm to land twisted around it and facing in the opposite direction.\r\n\r\nNow place the opposite hand down and repeat in the other direction.\r\n\r\nRepeat for 10 paces forwards and backwards.\r\n\r\nYou cannot sit down or stand up.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Ground twists','LONDON PARKOUR offers a comprehensive platform for those interested in the discipline of parkour. Through various channels, including their website and YouTube, they provide tutorials, tips, and insights into the art of movement, helping individuals transform their physical abilities and mindset. Their content encourages a practical approach to learning parkour, which emphasizes personal growth and skill development in the context of urban environments.\r\n\r\nThose interested in enhancing their parkour skills can subscribe to their YouTube channel for the latest tutorials and updates, ensuring they are part of a community that values both physical and mental agility. The channel hosts a variety of instructional videos tailored to different skill levels, promoting inclusivity and accessibility within the sport. Additionally, the company maintains an active Instagram presence, allowing followers to engage with parkour-related content and connect with fellow enthusiasts.\r\n\r\nFor inquiries or further engagement, London Parkour provides a dedicated contact email and encourages users to visit their website for additional resources. This supports their mission of fostering a vibrant parkour community, focused on empowering individuals through movement and encouraging a holistic view towards fitness and personal development.','publish','closed','closed','','ground-twists','','','2021-12-19 09:49:11','2021-12-19 09:49:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55305',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55306,1,'2021-12-19 09:54:08','2021-12-19 09:54:08','Start standing up and bend down to place your hands on the ground with your legs straight. Alternate moving your hands both at the same time and then hop your feet forwards at the same time.\r\n\r\nEither your hands or feet must move at any one time, but you cannot move the at the same time.\r\n\r\nMove forwards for 15 paces. Then repeat backwards for 15 paces.\r\n\r\nYou must be facing the ground the whole time with the legs straight. Your hips must stay at their highest.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.\r\n\r\nYou cannot stand up at any point.','Hinge','','publish','closed','closed','','hinge','','','2021-12-19 09:54:08','2021-12-19 09:54:08','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55306',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55307,1,'2021-12-19 09:58:24','2021-12-19 09:58:24','Bend down with the knees bent and place your hands at a 45 degree angle on the ground. Now \'gallop\' at a 45 degree angle with alternating hand and foot placement.\r\n\r\nTravel for 15 paces forwards. Repeat with the right shoulder leading, then the left shoulder leading.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.\r\n\r\nThis must be performed fast at jogging-speed or faster.','Gorilla Run','','publish','closed','closed','','gorilla-run','','','2021-12-19 09:58:24','2021-12-19 09:58:24','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55307',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55308,1,'2021-12-19 10:01:53','2021-12-19 10:01:53','Crouch down with your feet between your arms, closer than shoulder width. Now staying in a low-gait position with your hips lower than your knees, walk forwards for 15 paces. Repeat 15 steps backwards.\r\n\r\nYou cannot stop or stand up. You cannot sit down.\r\n\r\nYou must travel forwards and backwards without any stopping in between.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Duck Walk','','publish','closed','closed','','duck-walk','','','2021-12-19 10:01:53','2021-12-19 10:01:53','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55308',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55309,1,'2021-12-19 10:07:04','2021-12-19 10:07:04','The creeping monkey position is exactly the same as a normal basic QM (Level 1), but you should be stretched out a little more and every forwards movement should include a push-up in between where the body goes down to the ground and up again.\r\n\r\nYou must travel 10 paces forwards and 10 backwards.\r\n\r\nOnly the hands and toes can touch the ground. You must not rest on the ground or lay on the ground.  Your knees must also stay off the ground.\r\n\r\nEach pushup will have a leading arm ahead of the other. Each movement forwards will change that arm.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Creeping Monkey','Data refers to information collected for analysis, which informs understanding and decision-making.\r\n\r\nLondon Parkour is a creative movement discipline focused on efficient ways of navigating obstacles in urban environments. The official website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, offers tutorials, resources, and community engagement to enhance personal development in this practice. By emphasizing practicality and adaptability, parkour transforms not only physical skills but also mental perspectives on movement and problem-solving.\r\n\r\nThe London Parkour community encourages connection through various online platforms, including YouTube and Instagram. Subscribers can find videos that provide guidance and insights into developing parkour techniques tailored to different skill levels. This melding of community support and instructional content aims to foster growth and inspire individuals on their parkour journey while enriching their overall experience of movement.','publish','closed','closed','','creeping-monkey','','','2021-12-19 10:07:04','2021-12-19 10:07:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55309',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55310,1,'2021-12-19 16:03:49','2021-12-19 16:03:49','Demonstrate three underbars to the left and three to the right where you finish standing facing the rail, but on thee other side of it.\r\n\r\nYou must start with both hands in the correct position; One hand underneath, one on top, both crossed.\r\n\r\nYou must keep turning in the same direction with the head leading. As your torso gets through the rails, you can stop turning. Finish facing the rail with both hands on top of the bar.\r\n\r\nAs your legs come through, you should lead with the knees, not the heels of the foot.\r\n\r\nThis must be performed on a hip-height railing that has a mid-height railing at knee-height.\r\n\r\nYou cannot sit on the mid-bar. ','Basic Underbar','','publish','closed','closed','','basic-underbar','','','2021-12-19 16:03:49','2021-12-19 16:03:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55310',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55311,1,'2021-12-19 16:08:18','2021-12-19 16:08:18','Repeat the level-1 underbar but instead of stopping the turn once the torso is through, instead you keep turning until you\'ve rotated completely out of the underbar, facing the same direction as you started.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate three underbars to the left and three to the right where you finish away from the bar.\r\n\r\nYou must start with both hands in the correct position; One hand underneath, one on top, both crossed.\r\n\r\nYou must keep turning in the same direction with the head leading. \r\n\r\nAs your legs come through, you should lead with the knees, not the heels of the foot.\r\n\r\nThis must be performed on a hip-height railing that has a mid-height railing at knee-height.\r\n\r\nYou cannot sit on the mid-bar. ','Turning Underbar','','publish','closed','closed','','turning-underbar','','','2021-12-19 16:08:18','2021-12-19 16:08:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55311',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55312,1,'2021-12-19 16:12:12','2021-12-19 16:12:12','Repeat the level-1 underbar but instead of stopping the turn once the torso is through, instead you keep turning until you\'ve rotated completely out of the underbar, facing the same direction as you started. The difference to the level-2 is that you must increase the speed until both feet can leave the floor as they are passing through the underbar.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate three underbars to the left and three to the right where you finish away from the bar.\r\n\r\nYou must start with both hands in the correct position; One hand underneath, one on top, both crossed.\r\n\r\nYou must keep turning in the same direction with the head leading. \r\n\r\nAs your legs come through, you should lead with the knees, not the heels of the foot.\r\n\r\nThis must be performed on a hip-height railing that has a mid-height railing at knee-height.\r\n\r\nYou cannot sit on the mid-bar and the legs cannot touch the bar.','Speed Underbar','','publish','closed','closed','','speed-underbar','','','2021-12-19 16:12:12','2021-12-19 16:12:12','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55312',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55313,1,'2021-12-19 16:17:12','2021-12-19 16:17:12','Using a standard hip-height railing with a mid knee-height bar, use a pike-underbar with both legs straight to pass through the gap.\r\n\r\nStart with both hands on top of the rail. Jump the hips up high and pike the legs straight. Now thread the legs down and through the gap onto the other side.\r\n\r\nCrouch and pass your body through, with the arms trailing last.\r\n\r\nKeep in a straight line and land both feet together.\r\n\r\nYou cannot sit on the mid-bar and the legs cannot touch the bar.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.','Pike Underbar','','publish','closed','closed','','pike-underbar','','','2021-12-19 16:17:12','2021-12-19 16:17:12','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55313',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55314,1,'2021-12-19 16:21:13','2021-12-19 16:21:13','Repeat the level-1 underbar but now you must pass under the lower section of a two bar railing. \r\n\r\nDemonstrate three underbars to the left and three to the right where you finish away from the bar.\r\n\r\nStart with one foot forward and the opposite hand on top of the mid-bar. Pivot on the forward foot as you reach under the lower railing.\r\nGrab the higher railing and continue turning until you pass underneath.\r\n\r\nYou must keep turning in the same direction with the head leading. \r\n\r\nAs your legs come through, you should lead with the knees, not the heels of the foot.\r\n\r\nThis must be performed on a hip-height railing that has a mid-height railing at knee-height.\r\n\r\nYou must not lay or sit down on the ground or use the back on the ground.\r\n\r\nYou must perform the underbar in one movement.','Low Underbar','','publish','closed','closed','','low-underbar','','','2021-12-19 16:21:13','2021-12-19 16:21:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55314',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55315,1,'2021-12-19 16:24:49','2021-12-19 16:24:49','Perform any underbar (basic / turning / low / pike / speed) in a three movement route with two other vaults. One vault before, one vault after.\n\nYou must demonstrate a consistent speed both entering and exiting the underbar.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nThis must be performed on a hip-height railing that has a mid-height railing at knee-height.\n\nYou cannot sit on the mid-bar and the legs cannot touch the bar.','Underbar Route','','publish','closed','closed','','underbar-route','','','2021-12-19 16:24:49','2021-12-19 16:24:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55315',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55316,1,'2021-12-19 16:29:33','2021-12-19 16:29:33','Demonstrate a pike-underbar to a drop on the far side of the railing that is head-height or greater to the mid-height rail.\n\nUsing a standard hip-height railing with a mid knee-height bar, use a pike-underbar with both legs straight to pass through the gap.\n\nStart with both hands on top of the rail. Jump the hips up high and pike the legs straight. Now thread the legs down and through the gap onto the other side.\n\nCrouch and pass your body through, with the arms trailing last.\n\nKeep in a straight line and land both feet together on the ground from the drop.\n\nYou cannot sit on the mid-bar and the legs cannot touch the bar.\n\nRepeat three times. No twisting is allowed.','Pike to Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','pike-to-drop','','','2021-12-19 16:29:33','2021-12-19 16:29:33','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55316',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55317,1,'2021-12-19 16:36:14','2021-12-19 16:36:14','Demonstrate an underbar catch (one hand under, one hand over, crossed) on a bar where you have to dive **over** another obstacle / wall / rail / rope to catch it.\n\nThe obstacle to dive over must hip-height or greater and must be more than 3-steps in front of the catching bar. The catching bar can be any height.  \n\nYou cannot touch the first obstacle.\n\nRepeat three times, turning in any direction.','Diving Underbar','','publish','closed','closed','','diving-underbar','','','2021-12-19 16:36:14','2021-12-19 16:36:14','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55317',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55318,1,'2021-12-19 16:40:14','2021-12-19 16:40:14','Using a standard hip-height railing with a mid knee-height bar, use a dive-roll to pass through the upper gap of the railing.\n\nYou can land on grass or concrete. You must roll out of underbar and come back up to you feet with the momentum.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nYou must not touch the railings at any point.','Dive Roll Underbar','','publish','closed','closed','','dive-roll-underbar','','','2021-12-19 16:40:14','2021-12-19 16:40:14','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55318',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55319,1,'2021-12-19 16:44:51','2021-12-19 16:44:51','Using a standard hip-height railing with a mid knee-height bar, start facing away from the rail in a crouched position. Place both hands flat on the ground in front of you and perform a reverse ground cat-pass with your legs kicking up and passing through the rail behind you.\r\n\r\nPush with the hands and pass your torso through the rail once your feet have landed through the bars.\r\n\r\nYou must not touch the railings. \r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nNo twisting in the underbar. This is a bilateral movement backwards.','Reverse Pike','','publish','closed','closed','','reverse-pike','','','2021-12-19 16:44:51','2021-12-19 16:44:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55319',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55320,1,'2021-12-20 08:41:31','2021-12-20 08:41:31','Dead-Hang on a full height bar and start to generate swinging momentum by trying to get your hips to move forwards and backwards.\r\n\r\nYou must get your hips at a 45 degree angle to the ground on the forward and back swing. You can swing as much as you like until you get the angle.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nKick out in front of you with your legs, trying to get them as high as possible. On the back swing, try to hook your feet back by bending your knees. Repeat this process to get your hips moving back and forth.\r\n\r\nYour hands must be in a pronated grip.','Momentum','','publish','closed','closed','','momentum-2','','','2021-12-20 08:41:31','2021-12-20 08:41:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55320',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55321,1,'2021-12-20 08:53:17','2021-12-20 08:53:17','Dead-Hang on a full height bar and start to generate swinging momentum by trying to get your hips to move forwards and backwards.\r\n\r\nYou must get your hips at a 45 degree angle to the ground on the forward and back swing. You can swing as much as you like until you get the angle.\r\n\r\nOn the apex (highest point) of the back swing, just as you are returning forwards, perform a safe dismount from the bar to land on the ground two feet.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nKick out in front of you with your legs, trying to get them as high as possible. On the back swing, try to hook your feet back by bending your knees. Repeat this process to get your hips moving back and forth.\r\n\r\nYour hands must be in a pronated grip.\r\n\r\nYou must land two feet in a good stable and controlled landing. ','Dismount','','publish','closed','closed','','dismount','','','2021-12-20 08:53:17','2021-12-20 08:53:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55321',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55322,1,'2021-12-20 08:57:45','2021-12-20 08:57:45','Dead-Hang on a full height bar and start to generate swinging momentum by trying to get your hips to move forwards and backwards.\n\nYou must now dismount on the forward swing into a precision landing on the ground that is 5-steps (heel-to-toe) away from directly under the bar. \n\nRepeat three times.\n\nKick out in front of you with your legs, trying to get them as high as possible. On the back swing, try to hook your feet back by bending your knees. Repeat this process to get your hips moving back and forth.\n\nYour hands must be in a pronated grip.\n\nYou must land two feet in a good stable and controlled landing. ','Swing Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','swing-precision','','','2021-12-20 08:57:45','2021-12-20 08:57:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55322',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55323,1,'2021-12-20 09:00:52','2021-12-20 09:00:52','Dead-Hang on a full height bar and start to generate swinging momentum by trying to get your hips to move forwards and backwards.\r\n\r\nYou must get your hips at a 45 degree angle to the ground on the forward and back swing. You can swing as much as you like until you get the angle.\r\n\r\nOn the apex (highest point) of the back swing, demonstrate a re-grip of the hand hold with both hands. This means you will need to let go of the bar for a second and the re-grip your hands for a more sturdy grip.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n\r\nKick out in front of you with your legs, trying to get them as high as possible. On the back swing, try to hook your feet back by bending your knees. Repeat this process to get your hips moving back and forth.\r\n\r\nYour hands must be in a pronated grip.','Regrip','','publish','closed','closed','','regrip','','','2021-12-20 09:00:52','2021-12-20 09:00:52','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55323',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55324,1,'2021-12-20 09:04:06','2021-12-20 09:04:06','Demonstrate a one arm dead-hang for 10 seconds on each arm.\r\n\r\nRailing must be standard scaffolding-width or greater and allow you to hang full-stretch.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on both sides.','1-Arm Deadhang','','publish','closed','closed','','1-arm-deadhang','','','2021-12-20 09:04:06','2021-12-20 09:04:06','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55324',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55325,1,'2021-12-20 09:12:28','2021-12-20 09:12:28','Demonstrate a lachè from a bar to another bar. This is where you swing on the first bar with enough momentum to let go and catch the second bar.\n\nBoth bars must be 6-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater away from each other.\n\nBars must be full-stretch height or higher. Bars can be any width.\n\nYou must let go with both hands and catch with both hands.\n\nRepeat three times.','Bar to Bar','','publish','closed','closed','','bar-to-bar','','','2021-12-20 09:12:28','2021-12-20 09:12:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55325',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55326,1,'2021-12-20 09:18:04','2021-12-20 09:18:04','Demonstrate a lachè from a bar to another bar. This is where you swing on the first bar with enough momentum to let go and catch the second bar.\r\n\r\nOnce on the second bar, on the forward swing, you must now perform a 180 degree turn to re-grip facing back the way you came. You can turn in either direction.\r\n\r\nNow you must lache back to the first bar.\r\n\r\nBoth bars must be 6-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater away from each other.\r\n\r\nBars must be full-stretch height or higher. Bars can be any width.\r\n\r\nYou must let go with both hands and catch with both hands.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.','Bar Turning','','publish','closed','closed','','bar-turning','','','2021-12-20 09:18:04','2021-12-20 09:18:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55326',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55327,1,'2021-12-20 09:22:40','2021-12-20 09:22:40','Demonstrate a lachè from a bar to another bar. This is where you swing on the first bar with enough momentum to let go and catch the second bar. Once thee second bar has been caught, you must now repeat the process onto a third bar. \n\nYou are allowed a maximum of two swings on each bar.\n\nAll bars must be 6-steps (heel-to-toe) or greater away from each other.\n\nBars must be full-stretch height or higher. Bars can be any width.\n\nYou must let go with both hands and catch with both hands.\n\nRepeat three times.','Double lache','','publish','closed','closed','','double-lache','','','2021-12-20 09:22:40','2021-12-20 09:22:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55327',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55328,1,'2021-12-20 09:26:53','2021-12-20 09:26:53','Demonstrate a lachè from a bar to another bar. This is where you swing on the first bar with enough momentum to let go and catch the second bar. The second bar must be an adjacent one at a 90 degree angle to the first.\n\nBars must be full-stretch height or higher. Bars can be any width.\n\nYou must let go with both hands and catch with both hands.\n\nRepeat three times turning both sides.','Lache Twist','','publish','closed','closed','','lache-twist','','','2021-12-20 09:26:53','2021-12-20 09:26:53','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55328',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55329,1,'2021-12-20 14:59:57','2021-12-20 14:59:57','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 90 degrees from where you are starting from.\n\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\n\nYou must take off two feet and land two feet.\n\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','90 Degrees','','publish','closed','closed','','90-degrees','','','2021-12-20 14:59:57','2021-12-20 14:59:57','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55329',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55330,1,'2021-12-20 15:02:37','2021-12-20 15:02:37','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 180 degrees from where you are starting from.\n\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\n\nYou must take off two feet and land two feet.\n\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','180 degrees','','publish','closed','closed','','180-degrees','','','2021-12-20 15:02:37','2021-12-20 15:02:37','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55330',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55331,1,'2021-12-20 15:04:27','2021-12-20 15:04:27','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 270 degrees from where you are starting from.\n\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\n\nYou must take off two feet and land two feet.\n\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','270 Degrees','','publish','closed','closed','','270-degrees','','','2021-12-20 15:04:27','2021-12-20 15:04:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55331',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55332,1,'2021-12-20 15:05:47','2021-12-20 15:05:47','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 360 degrees from where you are starting from.\n\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\n\nYou must take off two feet and land two feet.\n\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','360 Degrees','','publish','closed','closed','','360-degrees','','','2021-12-20 15:05:47','2021-12-20 15:05:47','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55332',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55333,1,'2021-12-20 15:08:44','2021-12-20 15:08:44','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 180 degrees from a jump into the rotation 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away.\n\nYou must start 4-steps away from the pole and demonstrate and jump and grab as well as a rotational swing around the bar.\n\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\n\nYou must take off two feet and land two feet.\n\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','Jump and Grab','','publish','closed','closed','','jump-and-grab','','','2021-12-20 15:08:44','2021-12-20 15:08:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55333',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55334,1,'2021-12-20 15:26:03','2021-12-20 15:26:03','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 360 degrees from a jump into the rotation 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away.\n\nYou must start 4-steps away from the pole and demonstrate and jump and grab as well as a rotational swing around the bar.\n\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\n\nYou must take off two feet and land two feet in a precision landing on the ground on the marked line.\n\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','Jump Swing 360','','publish','closed','closed','','jump-swing-360','','','2021-12-20 15:26:03','2021-12-20 15:26:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55334',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55335,1,'2021-12-20 15:30:35','2021-12-20 15:30:35','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 180 degrees from a jump into the rotation 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away.\r\n\r\nYou must jump or dive over a hip-height obstacle/rail/wall as you grab the upright bar. You can use an artificial obstacle with rope / bands / chair / box / etc...\r\n\r\nYou must start 4-steps away from the pole and demonstrate and jump and grab as well as a rotational swing around the bar.\r\n\r\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\r\n\r\nYou must take off two feet and land two feet in a precision landing on the ground on the marked line.\r\n\r\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\r\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\r\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\r\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\r\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in both directions.','Perpendicular Bar','','publish','closed','closed','','perpendicular-bar','','','2021-12-20 15:30:35','2021-12-20 15:30:35','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55335',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55336,1,'2021-12-20 15:35:13','2021-12-20 15:35:13','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 180 degrees from a jump into the rotation 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away.\n\nYou must finish the rotation with a pike underbar through a gap that is knee to hip height. You can use an artificial obstacles or markers with rope / bands / string etc...\n\nYou must start 4-steps away from the pole and demonstrate and jump and grab as well as a rotational swing around the bar.\n\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\n\nYou must take off two feet and land two feet in a pike underbar landing on the ground on the marked line.\n\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','Jump Swing Pike','','publish','closed','closed','','jump-swing-pike','','','2021-12-20 15:35:13','2021-12-20 15:35:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55336',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55337,1,'2021-12-20 15:38:42','2021-12-20 15:38:42','Using an upright scaffolding bar / pole / lamppost of any width, rotate around the bar 180 degrees from a jump into the rotation 4-steps (heel-to-toe) away.\n\nYou must finish the rotation with a cat-leap onto a wall or obstacle of any height. You must have your hands and feet off the ground.\n\nYou must start 4-steps away from the pole and demonstrate and jump and grab as well as a rotational swing around the bar.\n\nUse markings on the ground or chalk to show the start position and landing.\n\nYou must take off two feet and land two hands and feet in a cat-leap position on a wall or obstacle. The wall can be any distance away.\n\nThis is to demonstrate the transition from rotational swing to cat-leap.\n\nExample If rotating around the bar to the right:\n- grab the bar as high as you can with the left hand.\n- grab the bar with the right hand underneath.\n- As you jump, contract both arms to hold your body in the air.\n- use the jump momentum to rotate you.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','Swing Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','swing-cat','','','2021-12-20 15:38:42','2021-12-20 15:38:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55337',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55338,1,'2021-12-20 15:47:10','2021-12-20 15:47:10','Start in a koala position on an upright pole / bar / lampposts / scaffolding that has another bar 5-steps or greater away from it. Using a tictac, kick away and turn 180 degrees to catch the second bar in a koala position.\n\nYou must demonstrate koala-to-koala tictac movement.\n\nYou must have both feet and both hands on the landing pole. You cannot touch the floor at any point.\n\nRepeat three times in any tictac direction.\n\nBar can be any shape. Circular or flat.','Koala to koala','','publish','closed','closed','','koala-to-koala','','','2021-12-20 15:47:10','2021-12-20 15:47:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55338',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55339,1,'2021-12-23 07:54:25','2021-12-23 07:54:25','Using the same movement technique as a ground cat-pass, this is the same except the feet land on the outside of the hands rather than inside and you are only using one hand rather than two.\r\n\r\nYou must complete 10 paces forwards and 10 backwards without stopping. Hand must be flat on the ground.\r\n\r\nHips must be lower than knee height.\r\n\r\nYou cannot standup or sit down.\r\n\r\nRepeat only once on each arm.','One Hand Frogs','','publish','closed','closed','','one-hand-frogs','','','2021-12-23 07:54:25','2021-12-23 07:54:25','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55339',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55340,1,'2021-12-23 08:03:54','2021-12-23 08:03:54','This movement is similar to a side-monkey movement with a 180 sweep included. Demonstrate ten sweeps in front of you and ten behind you.\r\n\r\nRepeat once forwards, once backwards.\r\n\r\nHands should be flat. Legs should be straight when not the pivot foot.\r\n\r\nYour hips must not be higher than your knee.\r\n\r\n(Forwards) Example to the right:\r\n- Start in a crouch position.\r\n- Extend you left leg straight out to the side with all weight on your right foot.\r\n- Lift the left leg and pivot forwards on the right foot.\r\n- You can use your hands in front of you to stabilise.\r\n- Rotate 180 degrees on the right foot keeping the left leg straight.\r\n- Drop the left foot once travelled the 180 degrees.\r\n- Shift your body-weight across to the left foot and make it the pivot foot.\r\n- Repeat the process on left foot.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n','180 Sweep','','publish','closed','closed','','180-sweep','','','2021-12-23 08:03:54','2021-12-23 08:03:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55340',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55341,1,'2021-12-23 08:12:28','2021-12-23 08:12:28','Half crouch down with your feet between your arms, closer than shoulder width. Now staying in a low-gait position with your hips at the same height as your knees.\nAs you move forwards, your torso and head must stay still and at the same height.\nwalk forwards for 15 paces.\n\nYou cannot stop or stand up. You cannot sit down.\n\nYou must travel forwards without any stopping in between.\n\nYour head must stay at the same height throughout the fifteen paces.\n\nRepeat once.','Swimming Duck','','publish','closed','closed','','swimming-duck','','','2021-12-23 08:12:28','2021-12-23 08:12:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55341',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55342,1,'2021-12-23 08:28:32','2021-12-23 08:28:32','Start in a crouched position with hands off the floor. Cross one leg behind the other, place the foot down and untwist to face the opposite direction.\n\nRepeat for ten steps once.\n\nYou should remain in a low gait position the entire time with the hips at knee height or less.\n\nYou should be alternating legs to move in one direction.\n\nYou cannot stop or stand up. You cannot sit down.\n\nYou must travel forwards without any stopping in between.\n\n\n','Crouch 180 Cross','','publish','closed','closed','','crouch-180-cross','','','2021-12-23 08:28:32','2021-12-23 08:28:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55342',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55343,1,'2021-12-23 08:34:38','2021-12-23 08:34:38','Start with one hand and the opposite foot in contact with the ground. Extend the inside leg out in front of you and plant it onto the ground. Transition onto your opposite hand while bringing the first foot up and through, similar to a step vault.\r\n\r\nRepeat for 10 forward movements switching onto each side.\r\nRepeat for another 10 movements backwards.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.\r\n\r\nHands must be flat on the ground. No steepling of the fingers.\r\n\r\nNo standing up.','Ground Step Vaults','','publish','closed','closed','','ground-step-vaults','','','2021-12-23 08:34:38','2021-12-23 08:34:38','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55343',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55344,1,'2021-12-23 09:10:21','2021-12-23 09:10:21','Demonstrate ten steps forwards and ten steps backwards in a saddle-position where your hips are at knee height and your feet are as wide as possible.\r\n\r\nMaintain the position while stepping forwards or backwards. \r\n\r\nYou cannot raise your hips higher than knee height. \r\n\r\nYour feet maintain the same distance apart from each other.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Saddle Walking','','publish','closed','closed','','saddle-walking','','','2021-12-23 09:10:21','2021-12-23 09:10:21','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55344',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55345,1,'2021-12-23 09:15:28','2021-12-23 09:15:28','Demonstrate a quadrupedal movement using the monkey-walk up thirty steps on some stairs going backwards. You head must be facing down, with your feet leading up the stairs.\n\nYou must not use any body part on the ground other than your hands and feet. \n\nYou must not stand up.\n\nYou can rest, but must have weight on three limbs as you rest the fourth.\n\nYour knees cannot touch the stairs at any point.\n\nRepeat once.','Backwards Stairs','','publish','closed','closed','','backwards-stairs','','','2021-12-23 09:15:28','2021-12-23 09:15:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55345',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55346,1,'2021-12-23 09:21:28','2021-12-23 09:21:28','This is a three phase movement that you must demonstrate five repetitions of.\n\nStart in a standing position and drop straight in front of you into a push-up position.\n\nSecond, dynamically push your torso up and drive your feet from behind you to in front of you like a kash vault on the ground. Landing on your hands and bum.\n\nThird, push yourself forwards into a standing position again. Repeat the whole process.\n\nThis is a completely bilateral movement.\n\nYour hands should be flat without any steepling of the fingers. \nYour feet should land ahead of your torso when pushing your legs through.','Drop, Push, Stand','','publish','closed','closed','','drop-push-stand','','','2021-12-23 09:21:28','2021-12-23 09:21:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55346',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55347,1,'2021-12-23 09:26:55','2021-12-23 09:26:55','Demonstrate a sideways-monkey where the hips are now allowed to raise up and be high in the air. As you transition your bodyweight from one side to the other, you must now pause mid-air with your feet off the ground for 1 second each time.\r\n\r\nRepeat both directions for ten movements each way.\r\n\r\nYou must pause for 1 second in a mini handstand position each time.\r\n\r\nArms can be bent or straight.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Sideways slow','','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-slow','','','2021-12-23 09:26:55','2021-12-23 09:26:55','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55347',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55348,1,'2021-12-23 09:31:21','2021-12-23 09:31:21','Start in the bottom of a push-up position with only your hands and feet touching the ground. \n\nYour chest, hips or knees must remain off the ground the entire time.\n\nNow remaining in that position you can walk or hop forwards for a distance of ten steps (heel-to-toe).\n\nYou cannot lie down or stand up.\n\nYour arms must remain completely bent the entire time.\n\nRepeat once.','Low Push-Up','','publish','closed','closed','','low-push-up','','','2021-12-23 09:31:21','2021-12-23 09:31:21','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55348',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55349,1,'2021-12-23 10:30:26','2021-12-23 10:30:26','The creeping monkey position is exactly the same as a normal basic QM (Level 1), but you should be stretched out a little more and every forwards movement should include a push-up in between where the body goes down to the ground and up again. \n\nYou must travel 10 steps forwards and 10 backwards but now with a shoe placed across your upper back to measure control of your movement. The shoe must remain on your back the entire way.\n\nOnly the hands and toes can touch the ground. You must not rest on the ground or lay on the ground. Your knees must also stay off the ground.\n\nEach pushup will have a leading arm ahead of the other. Each movement forwards will change that arm.\n\nRepeat once.\n\n','Creeping Control','','publish','closed','closed','','creeping-control','','','2021-12-23 10:30:26','2021-12-23 10:30:26','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55349',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55350,1,'2021-12-23 10:43:57','2021-12-23 10:43:57','The creeping monkey position is exactly the same as a normal basic QM (Level 1), but you should be stretched out a little more and every forwards movement should include a push-up in between where the body goes down to the ground. \n\nYou must travel 10 steps forwards and 10 backwards but you must now remain in the bottom position of the creeping-monkey.\n\nOnly the hands and toes can touch the ground. You must not rest on the ground or lay on the ground. Your knees must also stay off the ground.\n\nEach movement will have a leading arm ahead of the other. Each movement forwards will change that arm. To change to the opposite side, you will need to perform a sweeping arm movement to make it the lead one.\n\nRepeat once.\n\n','Creeping Low','','publish','closed','closed','','creeping-low','','','2021-12-23 10:43:57','2021-12-23 10:43:57','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55350',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55351,1,'2021-12-23 10:46:48','2021-12-23 10:46:48','The creeping monkey position is exactly the same as a normal basic QM (Level 1), but you should be stretched out a little more and every forwards movement should include a push-up in between where the body goes down to the ground. \n\nYou must travel 10 steps forwards and 10 backwards but you must now touch your knee onto your elbow and balance it there while your foot comes off the ground as you perform the pushup. Once you push back up, the foot can return to the ground.\n\nOnly the hands and toes can touch the ground. You must not rest on the ground or lay on the ground. Your knees must also stay off the ground.\n\nEach movement will have a leading arm ahead of the other. Each movement forwards will change that arm.\n\nRepeat once.\n\n','Knee to Elbow','','publish','closed','closed','','knee-to-elbow','','','2021-12-23 10:46:48','2021-12-23 10:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55351',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55352,1,'2021-12-23 12:54:22','2021-12-23 12:54:22','Similar to the level 3 specialist QM with an added kick.\nThe creeping monkey position is exactly the same as a normal basic QM (Level 1), but you should be stretched out a little more and every forwards movement should include a push-up in between where the body goes down to the ground. \n\nYou must travel 5 steps forwards per side and 5 backwards per side but you must now touch your knee onto your elbow and balance it there while your foot comes off the ground as you perform the pushup.\nAs the you perform the pushup, your knee that is resting on the elbow will now straighten and kick out. Once you push back up, the foot can return to the ground.\n\nOnly the hands and toes can touch the ground. You must not rest on the ground or lay on the ground. Your knees must also stay off the ground.\n\nEach movement will have a leading arm ahead of the other. Each movement forwards will change that arm.\n\nRepeat once.\n\n','Knee Elbow Kick','','publish','closed','closed','','knee-elbow-kick','','','2021-12-23 12:54:22','2021-12-23 12:54:22','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55352',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55353,1,'2021-12-23 13:16:13','2021-12-23 13:16:13','The creeping monkey position is exactly the same as a normal basic QM (Level 1), but you should be stretched out a little more and every forwards movement should include a push-up in between where the body goes down to the ground. \n\nEach time you move the back leg forwards, you need to move it in a \'S\' shape movement going outwards and then back inwards to the elbow.\n\nYou must travel 10 steps forwards and 10 backwards but you must now remain in the bottom position of the creeping-monkey.\n\nOnly the hands and toes can touch the ground. You must not rest on the ground or lay on the ground. Your knees must also stay off the ground.\n\nEach movement will have a leading arm ahead of the other. Each movement forwards will change that arm. To change to the opposite side, you will need to perform a sweeping arm movement to make it the lead one.\n\nRepeat once.\n\n','Creeping Scorpion','','publish','closed','closed','','creeping-scorpion','','','2021-12-23 13:16:13','2021-12-23 13:16:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55353',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55354,1,'2021-12-23 13:20:52','2021-12-23 13:20:52','Demonstrate two sideways-monkey movements to the left and two movements to the right, repeating five times without stopping. \nEvery time you move sideways and extend your leg straight, you must keep that leg off the ground for three seconds.\n\nRepeat once.\n\nYour hips must stay below your head and your leading leg must be straight while in the air.\n\nTry to keep your chest up and hands flat on the ground.\n\nThis is to demonstrate a good movement ability and control in a low-gait position while keeping the TFL Muscle group contracted.\n\nExample.\n\n- Start in a crouch position.\n- Place your left hand in front of your right foot.\n- Place the right hand far out to the right of you so you are leaning on your right side.\n- Jump both feet up in the air and drop the left foot past the left hand position.\n- The right leg should extend straight out to the right and cover as much distance as possible - while staying off the ground.\n- Push with both hands and return back to crouched position.\n','Sideways Hold','','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-hold','','','2021-12-23 13:20:52','2021-12-23 13:20:52','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55354',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55355,1,'2021-12-23 13:25:56','2021-12-23 13:25:56','Demonstrate on sideways-monkey movement to the left and one movement to the right, repeating five times without stopping. \nEvery time you move sideways and extend your leg straight, you must keep that leg off the ground.\nWhile the leg is off the ground you must now complete the following:\n1. 5x leg raises up and down.\n2. 3x circles with the leg forwards.\n3. 3x circles with the leg backwards.\n\nOnce that sequence is complete, you can drop the leg back to the ground and repeat back to the other side.\n\nRepeat once.\n\nYour hips must stay below your head and your leading leg must be straight while in the air.\n\nTry to keep your chest up and hands flat on the ground.\n\nThis is to demonstrate a good movement ability and control in a low-gait position while keeping the TFL Muscle group contracted.\n\nExample.\n\n- Start in a crouch position.\n- Place your left hand in front of your right foot.\n- Place the right hand far out to the right of you so you are leaning on your right side.\n- Jump both feet up in the air and drop the left foot past the left hand position.\n- The right leg should extend straight out to the right and cover as much distance as possible - while staying off the ground.\n- Now perform the sequence.\n- Push with both hands and return back to crouched position.\n','Sideways Circles','','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-circles','','','2021-12-23 13:25:56','2021-12-23 13:25:56','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55355',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55356,1,'2021-12-23 13:31:45','2021-12-23 13:31:45','Demonstrate one sideways-monkey movement to the left and one movement to the right, repeating five times without stopping. \nEvery time you move sideways and extend your leg straight, you must keep that leg off the ground.\nNow you must pivot on the inside leg and twist your body towards the extended leg while maintaining it off the ground.\nTurn back to facing sideways and repeat the twist three times.\n\nRepeat once.\n\nYour hips must stay below your head and your leading leg must be straight while in the air.\n\nTry to keep your chest up and hands flat on the ground.\n\nThis is to demonstrate a good movement ability and control in a low-gait position while keeping the TFL Muscle group contracted and twisting.\n\nExample.\n\n- Start in a crouch position.\n- Place your left hand in front of your right foot.\n- Place the right hand far out to the right of you so you are leaning on your right side.\n- Jump both feet up in the air and drop the left foot past the left hand position.\n- The right leg should extend straight out to the right and cover as much distance as possible - while staying off the ground.\n- Now pivot on the left foot to face the right foot.\n- Pivot back to the original position and repeat.\n- Push with both hands and return back to crouched position.\n','Sideways Forwards','','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-forwards','','','2021-12-23 13:31:45','2021-12-23 13:31:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55356',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55357,1,'2021-12-23 13:34:54','2021-12-23 13:34:54','Demonstrate one sideways-monkey movement to the left and one movement to the right, repeating five times without stopping. \nEvery time you move sideways and extend your leg straight, you must keep that leg off the ground.\nNow you must pivot on the inside leg and twist your body away from the extended leg while maintaining it off the ground.\nTurn back to facing sideways and repeat the twist three times.\n\nRepeat once.\n\nYour hips must stay below your head and your leading leg must be straight while in the air.\n\nTry to keep your chest up and hands flat on the ground.\n\nThis is to demonstrate a good movement ability and control in a low-gait position while keeping the TFL Muscle group contracted and twisting.\n\nExample.\n\n- Start in a crouch position.\n- Place your left hand in front of your right foot.\n- Place the right hand far out to the right of you so you are leaning on your right side.\n- Jump both feet up in the air and drop the left foot past the left hand position.\n- The right leg should extend straight out to the right and cover as much distance as possible - while staying off the ground.\n- Now pivot on the left foot to the left, away from the right foot.\n- Pivot back to the original position and repeat.\n- Push with both hands and return back to crouched position.\n','Sideways Backwards','','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-backwards','','','2021-12-23 13:34:54','2021-12-23 13:34:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55357',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55358,1,'2021-12-23 13:39:14','2021-12-23 13:39:14','Demonstrate one sideways-monkey movement to the left and one movement to the right, repeating five times without stopping. \nEvery time you move sideways and extend your leg straight, you must keep that leg off the ground.\nNow you must pivot on the inside leg and twist your body towards the extended leg while maintaining it off the ground. Once you are facing the extended leg you must perform three one-legged full range of motion squats (aka pistols).\nTurn back to facing sideways and repeat on the other side.\n\nRepeat once.\n\nYour hips must stay below your head and your leading leg must be straight while in the air.\n\nTry to keep your chest up and hands flat on the ground.\n\nThis is to demonstrate a good movement ability and control in a low-gait position while keeping the TFL and posterior chain Muscle group contracted and twisting.\n\nExample.\n\n- Start in a crouch position.\n- Place your left hand in front of your right foot.\n- Place the right hand far out to the right of you so you are leaning on your right side.\n- Jump both feet up in the air and drop the left foot past the left hand position.\n- The right leg should extend straight out to the right and cover as much distance as possible - while staying off the ground.\n- Now pivot on the left foot to face the right foot.\n- Keeping the right foot off the ground you must now perform three pistols.\n- Pivot back to the original position and repeat on the left.\n','Sideways Pistol','','publish','closed','closed','','sideways-pistol','','','2021-12-23 13:39:14','2021-12-23 13:39:14','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55358',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55359,1,'2021-12-24 09:05:06','2021-12-24 09:05:06','Demonstrate a basic parkour roll from a crouched position on grass. \r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\r\n\r\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the ground and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\r\n\r\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\r\n\r\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.\r\n\r\nBasic steps:\r\n1. Place hands flat on the ground at 45 degrees to your body.\r\n2. Open your knees wide.\r\n3. Drop your forward shoulder onto the ground while extending your legs.\r\n4. Roll diagonally across the back coming up from the opposite hip.\r\n5. Place your feet under you, making sure you do not roll onto your knees.','Basic Crouch Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','basic-crouch-roll','','','2021-12-24 09:05:06','2021-12-24 09:05:06','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55359',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55360,1,'2021-12-24 09:41:50','2021-12-24 09:41:50','Demonstrate a basic parkour roll from a standing position on grass. \r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\r\n\r\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the ground and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\r\n\r\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\r\n\r\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.\r\n\r\nBasic steps:\r\n1. Place hands flat on the ground at 45 degrees to your body.\r\n2. Open your knees wide.\r\n3. Drop your forward shoulder onto the ground while extending your legs.\r\n4. Roll diagonally across the back coming up from the opposite hip.\r\n5. Place your feet under you, making sure you do not roll onto your knees.','Basic Standing Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','basic-standing-roll','','','2021-12-24 09:41:50','2021-12-24 09:41:50','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55360',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55361,1,'2021-12-24 09:46:44','2021-12-24 09:46:44','Demonstrate a four-step (heel-to-toe) jump into basic parkour roll from a standing position on grass. \r\n\r\nStart in a standing position, jump the four steps onto your feet like a broad jump and instead of stopping the momentum, you must immediately drop into the roll.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\r\n\r\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the ground and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\r\n\r\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\r\n\r\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.\r\n\r\nBasic steps:\r\n1. Place hands flat on the ground at 45 degrees to your body.\r\n2. Open your knees wide.\r\n3. Drop your forward shoulder onto the ground while extending your legs.\r\n4. Roll diagonally across the back coming up from the opposite hip.\r\n5. Place your feet under you, making sure you do not roll onto your knees.','Basic Jump Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','basic-jump-roll','','','2021-12-24 09:46:44','2021-12-24 09:46:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55361',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55362,1,'2021-12-24 09:51:17','2021-12-24 09:51:17','Demonstrate a basic parkour roll from a crouched position on grass over an obstacle that is ankle high or greater.\n\nThis is a basic dive roll over the obstacle onto your hands and then into the roll. This is to demonstrate the mechanics of leading with the hands a little more.\n\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\n\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the ground and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\n\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\n\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.\n\nBasic steps:\n1. Place hands flat on the ground at 45 degrees to your body.\n2. Open your knees wide.\n3. Drop your forward shoulder onto the ground while extending your legs.\n4. Roll diagonally across the back coming up from the opposite hip.\n5. Place your feet under you, making sure you do not roll onto your knees.','Basic Dive Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','basic-dive-roll','','','2021-12-24 09:51:17','2021-12-24 09:51:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55362',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55363,1,'2021-12-24 09:55:50','2021-12-24 09:55:50','Demonstrate a basic parkour roll from a vault over a hip-height wall onto grass. \r\n\r\nYou can perform any vault you prefer, but you must seamlessly and immediately drop into the roll using the momentum from the vault. You cannot pause or stop after you have vaulted the wall.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate the ability of impact redirection into a roll.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\r\n\r\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the ground and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\r\n\r\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\r\n\r\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.\r\n\r\nBasic steps:\r\n1. Place hands flat on the ground at 45 degrees to your body.\r\n2. Open your knees wide.\r\n3. Drop your forward shoulder onto the ground while extending your legs.\r\n4. Roll diagonally across the back coming up from the opposite hip.\r\n5. Place your feet under you, making sure you do not roll onto your knees.','Basic Vault Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','basic-vault-roll','','','2021-12-24 09:55:50','2021-12-24 09:55:50','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55363',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55364,1,'2021-12-24 10:06:54','2021-12-24 10:06:54','Demonstrate three parkour rolls from a standing position on concrete in each of the four directions:\n- Forwards\n- Backwards\n- Left\n- Right\n\nRepeat the entire process once over the right shoulder (12x rolls - 3 in each direction) and then the left shoulder (12x rolls - 3x in each direction). Total of 24 rolls.\n\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the ground and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\n\nYou must be on concrete / tarmac or any hard stone surface.\n\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\n\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.','Directional Rolls','','publish','closed','closed','','directional-rolls','','','2021-12-24 10:06:54','2021-12-24 10:06:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55364',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55365,1,'2021-12-24 10:11:49','2021-12-24 10:11:49','Demonstrate a parkour roll over a wide obstacle that is hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nPlacing your hands on the obstacle, jump up and dive into the roll on the obstacle. Your feet must roll off the obstacle on the other side.\r\n\r\nWall can be any width, but wider would help the distance of the roll.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\r\n\r\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the obstacle and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\r\n\r\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\r\n\r\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.','Obstacle Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','obstacle-roll','','','2021-12-24 10:11:49','2021-12-24 10:11:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55365',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55366,1,'2021-12-24 10:26:09','2021-12-24 10:26:09','Demonstrate a parkour roll from a hip-height or greater drop onto concrete. You must seamlessly redirect the energy of the drop into the roll.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\r\n\r\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the obstacle and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\r\n\r\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\r\n\r\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.','Drop Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','drop-roll-2','','','2021-12-24 10:26:09','2021-12-24 10:26:09','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55366',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55367,1,'2021-12-24 10:30:18','2021-12-24 10:30:18','Demonstrate a parkour roll through a gap that is over knee height and under hip-height. It can be artificially created or a structure like railings. You must roll on concrete / hard stone surface.\r\n\r\nYou must not touch any part of the obstacles while diving through. You can start in any position; crouched or standing.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\r\n\r\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the obstacle and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\r\n\r\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\r\n\r\nYou should travel in a straight line and you must finish on your feet.','Dive Gap Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','dive-gap-roll','','','2021-12-24 10:30:18','2021-12-24 10:30:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55367',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55368,1,'2021-12-24 10:36:23','2021-12-24 10:36:23','Demonstrate a parkour roll from a hip-height or greater drop onto concrete. Land on your feet and you must seamlessly redirect the energy of the drop back up into a dive roll to cover 5-steps (heel-to-toe) distance. You must be on your feet and jogging out of the roll to finish.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times over each shoulder.\r\n\r\nYour head and knees must not come in contact with the obstacle and you must roll across one shoulder and the opposite hip.\r\n\r\nYou cannot perform a sideways roll or a gymnastics roll. This must be a diagonal roll across the back from the shoulder to the hip.\r\n\r\nYou should travel in a straight line.','Drop Rebound Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','drop-rebound-roll','','','2021-12-24 10:36:23','2021-12-24 10:36:23','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55368',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55369,1,'2021-12-24 14:52:51','2021-12-24 14:52:51','Demonstrate a full cartwheel.\r\n\r\nYour arms and legs must be straight when performing the cartwheel.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate cartwheeling onto the left hand and onto the right hand.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n\r\nYou must land on your feet.','Cartwheel','','publish','closed','closed','','cartwheel','','','2021-12-24 14:52:51','2021-12-24 14:52:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55369',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55370,1,'2021-12-24 15:13:34','2021-12-24 15:13:34','Demonstrate a palm spin over the 90degree corner of a knee-height (or higher) obstacle / wall / block.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n\r\nYou must land two feet on the ground without touching the obstacle with your feet.\r\n\r\nYou must take off two feet and make it over the corner and turning 90 degrees.','Palm Spin','','publish','closed','closed','','palm-spin','','','2021-12-24 15:13:34','2021-12-24 15:13:34','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55370',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55371,1,'2021-12-24 15:19:54','2021-12-24 15:19:54','The leg sweep is used to seamlessly transition under a hip-height (or lower) bar, fence, \r\nobstacle or rail while still moving. and keeping your momentum.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate three full sweeps in each direction going under a bar at hip-height or lower.\r\n\r\nYou need the mobility to sweep the leg under the bar and move your hips onto that foot in one move.\r\n\r\nYou must maintain speed and must keep your hips lower than your head.\r\n\r\nYour hands can touch the ground in needed','Leg Sweep','','publish','closed','closed','','leg-sweep','','','2021-12-24 15:19:54','2021-12-24 15:19:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55371',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55372,1,'2021-12-25 11:18:13','2021-12-25 11:18:13','Demonstrate a waist-cat position on a hip-height or higher wall. You must be on the wall with the arms straight and legs straight off the wall.\r\n\r\nHands facing forwards with arms straight. Your toes should be pulled back to avoid the knee from hitting the wall.\r\n\r\nFrom this position, using only the hands on the top of the wall, you must now turn 360 degrees all the way around. You can travel up or down the wall while rotating.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in each direction.','Waist-Cat 360','','publish','closed','closed','','waist-cat-360','','','2021-12-25 11:18:13','2021-12-25 11:18:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55372',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55373,1,'2021-12-25 11:25:05','2021-12-25 11:25:05','The cavalier is more of an aesthetic vault that combines a lazy-vault on a hip-height thin wall or rail with a sitting rotation 180 degrees and then lazy vault out again.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n\r\nYou must not stop the momentum and your lower legs must not touch the obstacle or floor.\r\n\r\nUse your hands for stabilisation and watch out for falling backwards.\r\n\r\nNo mats can be used.','Cavalier','London Parkour offers a variety of resources aimed at teaching parkour techniques through engaging tutorials and practical movement training. Their website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a hub for individuals interested in transforming their physical capabilities, mindset, and overall experience with movement.\r\n\r\nThe initiative encourages followers to subscribe to their YouTube channel, where they can access instructional videos and stay updated on new content. The hashtag #ParkourHowTo and various social media platforms, including Instagram, help the organization reach a wider audience and foster a community of parkour enthusiasts.\r\n\r\nOverall, London Parkour not only focuses on the physical aspects of movement but also emphasizes the mental transformation that comes with mastering parkour skills. With a strong online presence and various resources available, they aim to support practitioners of all levels in their journey to enhance their movement abilities.','publish','closed','closed','','cavalier','','','2021-12-25 11:25:05','2021-12-25 11:25:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55373',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55374,1,'2021-12-25 13:20:46','2021-12-25 13:20:46','Demonstrate a handstand with your legs against a wall. Then rotate your entire body 360 degrees around so that your hands and body finish in the exact same position.\n\nRepeat once in each direction.\n\nYou cannot fall off the wall and your feet cannot touch the ground. \n\nYou must use a flat wall with no ledges or edges to catch your feet on.\n','Handstand 360','','publish','closed','closed','','handstand-360','','','2021-12-25 13:20:46','2021-12-25 13:20:46','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55374',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55375,1,'2021-12-25 13:34:32','2021-12-25 13:34:32','Demonstrate a full 180 degree palm spin over a hip-height (or higher) obstacle / wall / block.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.\n\nYou must land two feet on the ground without touching the obstacle with your feet.\n\nYou must take off two feet and make it over the obstacle and turning 180 degrees to finish facing the opposite direction.\n\nNo mats can be used.','Full Palm Spin','','publish','closed','closed','','full-palm-spin','','','2021-12-25 13:34:32','2021-12-25 13:34:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55375',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55376,1,'2021-12-25 13:39:43','2021-12-25 13:39:43','Demonstrate a full 180 degree palm spin in reverse over a knee-height (or higher) obstacle / wall / block. Instead of jumping forwards in a normal palm spin, you\'ll be jumping backwards.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.\n\nYou must land two feet on the ground without touching the obstacle with your feet.\n\nYou must take off two feet and make it over the obstacle and turning 180 degrees to finish facing the opposite direction.\n\nNo mats can be used.','Reverse Palm Spin','','publish','closed','closed','','reverse-palm-spin','','','2021-12-25 13:39:43','2021-12-25 13:39:43','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55376',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55377,1,'2021-12-25 13:47:44','2021-12-25 13:47:44','On a hip-height (or higher) railing, demonstrate an open split-leg swing from on top to underneath the rail while both hands (on the inside) and both feet (on the outside) are on the rail.\n\nYou can push out of the swing into any landing on your feet.\n\nYou must start on top of the rail and swing underneath in position. Your legs must be straight.\n\nHands should be pronated on the rail.\n\nBeing a bilateral movement, both feet should leave the rail at the same time.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nNo mats can be used.','Crab Rail Swing','','publish','closed','closed','','crab-rail-swing','','','2021-12-25 13:47:44','2021-12-25 13:47:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55377',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55378,1,'2021-12-25 13:57:04','2021-12-25 13:57:04','Demonstrate a full wall-spin on a flat, upright, wall in both directions.\n\nSimilar to a palm-spin, however the hands are placed flat against a full-height wall, rather than on top of a low wall/rail. You must run and jump into the rotation to get enough height.\n\nYou feet must go over your head.\n\nYou must repeat three times in both directions.\n\nYou must land on your feet.\n\nNo mats can be used.  ','Wall Spin','','publish','closed','closed','','wall-spin','','','2021-12-25 13:57:04','2021-12-25 13:57:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55378',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55379,1,'2021-12-26 09:51:34','2021-12-26 09:51:34','Demonstrate the use of a 360 rotation to keep the momentum of the movements. Start with a step-vault over a hip-height wall/rail/obstacle and land straight into a 360 turn. Exit the 360 turn with another step-vault back over the obstacle further down.\n\nAfter the first step-vault, you must land on one foot to dynamically move into the 360 turn. \n\nAs you exit the 360 degree turn, the momentum should make the entry of the step vault easier to perform.\n\nThe landing of the second step-vault can be of any type.\n\nUse your hands to guide your position through the 360 turn.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','Step 360 Step','','publish','closed','closed','','step-360-step','','','2021-12-26 09:51:34','2021-12-26 09:51:34','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55379',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55380,1,'2021-12-26 10:11:22','2021-12-26 10:11:22','Demonstrate a normal one-hand and one-foot TicTac over a hip-height wall which transitions into a full momentum 360 turn. The exit of the 360 turn becomes a step-vault back over the wall.\r\n\r\nAfter the first TicTac, you must land on one foot to dynamically move into the 360 turn. \r\n\r\nAs you exit the 360 degree turn, the momentum should make the entry of the step vault easier to perform.\r\n\r\nThe landing of the second step-vault can be of any type.\r\n\r\nUse your hands to guide your position through the 360 turn.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in both directions.','TicTac 360 Step','','publish','closed','closed','','tictac-360-step','','','2021-12-26 10:11:22','2021-12-26 10:11:22','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55380',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55381,1,'2021-12-26 10:16:31','2021-12-26 10:16:31','Demonstrate a seamless connection between a step-vault on a hip-height railing to an underbar over a knee-height middle bar.\n\nAs you exit the step vault on one foot, pivot on the hand on the rail and drop down into the underbar position. Keep turning and exit the underbar while turning.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.\n\nYou must maintain a consistent speed while performing the entire route.\n\nYou cannot sit on the middle bar while performing the underbar.\n\nYou must finish on both feet turned 180 degrees back the way you came.\n','Step Underbar','','publish','closed','closed','','step-underbar','','','2021-12-26 10:16:31','2021-12-26 10:16:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55381',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55382,1,'2021-12-26 10:24:27','2021-12-26 10:24:27','Demonstrate any vault over a hip-height (or greater) obstacle into a two-foot landing that transitions into a plyometric jump and landing in a precision on the front edge on an ankle-height wall.\n\nThe vault can be any vault you wish to perform.\n\nThe plyometric jump must be a minimum of 5-steps (heel-to-toe) into the precision.\n\nYou must land on the front edge of an ankle-height (or greater) wall / block / step / etc...\n\nYou must land in a good precision position on the ball of the foot and stick the landing. Holding in a controlled (non-moving position) for 3 seconds.\n\nNo pausing or stopping, especially on the plyometric jump. Keep a consistent speed throughout.\n\nRepeat three times. ','Vault Plyo Pre','','publish','closed','closed','','vault-plyo-pre','','','2021-12-26 10:24:27','2021-12-26 10:24:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55382',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55383,1,'2021-12-26 10:34:05','2021-12-26 10:34:05','Demonstrate a jump into a grab of an upright bar which allows you to spin around the bar 90 degrees and clear an obstacle next to the bar that is hip-height or greater.\n\nStart away from the upright bar and jump to grab it with two hands. If you are rotating around the pole to the right, the left hand should be highest.\n\nAfter you have swung around the bar for a minimum of 90-degrees, you must raise your legs into a box-jump position to clear a hip-height obstacle perpendicular from the bar. You can use any artificial obstacle like a chair, rope, mobility-band, etc... To designate the height.\n\nThe upright bar can be any type or width. Scaffolding bar would be most appropriate.\n\nLand two feet in control and not falling.\n\nYou must start with your hands off the bar and jump into the grab.\n\nThe ninety degrees should be measured from the position you grab the bar, not from your jumping point.\n\nRepeat three times in both directions.','Jump Spin Box','','publish','closed','closed','','jump-spin-box','','','2021-12-26 10:34:05','2021-12-26 10:34:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55383',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55384,1,'2021-12-27 09:20:56','2021-12-27 09:20:56','Demonstrate six individual vaults with a maximum of only two paces between each one. You can do the same vault only twice meaning that a minimum of three different vaults are required.\r\n\r\nYou cannot loose momentum or fluctuate in your speed of the route. You can go slow, but you must remain consistent in speed. It will not count if you have to slow down to perform a vault or prepare for a vault.\r\n\r\nYou can use any obstacles or combinations of obstacles to create your route. You can use the same obstacles multiple times.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in total.\r\n\r\nAfter you land with one or two feet, you can only take a maximum of two steps (paces) to the next vault.\r\n\r\nPerforming the same vault on the opposite side counts as a duplication.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate the ability to chain multiple movements together with a seamless flow between each by using prepared, accurate and planned hand and foot placements.\r\n\r\nYou cannot pause or stop at any point in the route.','Six Vaults','','publish','closed','closed','','six-vaults','','','2021-12-27 09:20:56','2021-12-27 09:20:56','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55384',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55385,1,'2021-12-27 17:26:22','2021-12-27 17:26:22','Demonstrate a minimum of four individual vaults with a maximum of only two paces between each one. You must include (in any order) the following movements:\n\n- A Reverse Step-vault\n- A Slide Vault\n- A Lazy Vault\n- A TicTac\n\nYou cannot loose momentum or fluctuate in your speed of the route. You can go slow, but you must remain consistent in speed. It will not count if you have to slow down to perform a vault or prepare for a vault.\n\nYou can include more vaults as long as you do not loose momentum.\n\nYou can use any obstacles or combinations of obstacles to create your route. You can use the same obstacles multiple times.\n\nRepeat three times in total.\n\nAfter you land with one or two feet, you can only take a maximum of two steps (paces) to the next vault.\n\nThis is to demonstrate the ability to chain multiple specific movements together with a seamless flow between each by using prepared, accurate and planned hand and foot placements.\n\nYou cannot pause or stop at any point in the route.','Four Vaults','','publish','closed','closed','','four-vaults','','','2021-12-27 17:26:22','2021-12-27 17:26:22','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55385',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55386,1,'2021-12-27 17:35:14','2021-12-27 17:35:14','Demonstrate three duplicate vaults performed after each other in a flow over hip-height obstacles (or greater). You can choose any vault you wish to perform, but there must only be a maximum of two paces between the landing and the next vault.\n\nEach vault must be repeated on the same side, or on bilateral movements.\n\nYou cannot loose momentum or fluctuate in your speed of the route. You can go slow, but you must remain consistent in speed. It will not count if you have to slow down to perform a vault or prepare for a vault.\n\nYou can include more vaults as long as you do not loose momentum.\n\nYou can use any obstacles or combinations of obstacles to create your route.\n\nRepeat three times in total.\n\nAfter you land with one or two feet, you can only take a maximum of two steps (paces) to the next vault.\n\nThis is to demonstrate the ability to chain multiple of the same movements together with a seamless flow between each by using prepared, accurate and planned hand and foot placements.\n\nYou cannot pause or stop at any point in the route.','Triple Duplication','','publish','closed','closed','','triple-duplication','','','2021-12-27 17:35:14','2021-12-27 17:35:14','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55386',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55387,1,'2021-12-27 17:42:04','2021-12-27 17:42:04','Demonstrate a minimum of four individual movements with a maximum of only two paces between each one. You must include (in any order) the following things:\n\n- A Vault at hip-height (or greater)\n- A 7-step Jump (heel-to-toe) (Box / Broad / Precision) \n- A head-height Drop \n- A Knee-height rail or higher\n\nYou cannot loose momentum or fluctuate in your speed of the route. You can go slow, but you must remain consistent in speed. It will not count if you have to slow down to perform a vault or prepare for a vault.\n\nYou can include more vaults as long as you do not loose momentum.\n\nYou can use the same obstacles multiple times.\n\nRepeat three times in total.\n\nAfter you land with one or two feet, you can only take a maximum of two steps (paces) to the next vault.\n\nThis is to demonstrate the ability to chain multiple specific movements together with a seamless flow between each by using prepared, accurate and planned hand and foot placements.\n\nYou cannot pause or stop at any point in the route.','Specific Four','','publish','closed','closed','','specific-four','','','2021-12-27 17:42:04','2021-12-27 17:42:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55387',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55388,1,'2021-12-27 17:48:49','2021-12-27 17:48:49','Demonstrate ten different vaults in thirty seconds with a maximum of only two paces between each one and all vaults must be over hip-height. You can only use the same vault twice (regardless of side you use) and the same obstacle twice.\n\nYou cannot loose momentum or fluctuate in your speed of the route. You can go slow, but you must remain consistent in speed. It will not count if you have to slow down to perform a vault or prepare for a vault.\n\nYou can include more vaults as long as you do not loose momentum and do not duplicate more than twice.\n\nRepeat three times in total.\n\nAfter you land with one or two feet, you can only take a maximum of two steps (paces) to the next vault.\n\nThis is to demonstrate the ability to chain many movements together, fast; with a seamless flow between each by using prepared, accurate and planned hand and foot placements.\n\nYou cannot pause or stop at any point in the route.\n','Ten in Thirty','','publish','closed','closed','','ten-in-thirty','','','2021-12-27 17:48:49','2021-12-27 17:48:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55388',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55389,1,'2021-12-29 09:19:42','2021-12-29 09:19:42','Demonstrate a turn-vault over a hip-height rail that has a hip-height drop on the far side. The turn vault then flows into a drop to the ground, followed be a parkour roll for impact dispersion.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on both sides for the turn vault and roll.\r\n\r\nAs you land in the turn vault, you must turn 180 degrees and drop onto the ground for the roll.\r\n\r\n\r\n','Turn to Drop Roll','','publish','closed','closed','','turn-to-drop-roll','','','2021-12-29 09:19:42','2021-12-29 09:19:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55389',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55390,1,'2021-12-29 09:29:46','2021-12-29 09:29:46','Demonstrate an arm-jump (cat-leap) from 4-steps away onto a head-height wall or higher. Transition into a climb-up onto the top of the wall and finish standing on top. \r\n\r\nYou can use any climb-up grip (including over-grip) to get up. You must not use your knees at any point.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times. \r\n\r\n','Cat to Climbup','','publish','closed','closed','','cat-to-climbup-2','','','2021-12-29 09:29:46','2021-12-29 09:29:46','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55390',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55391,1,'2021-12-29 09:39:43','2021-12-29 09:39:43','Demonstrate an arm-jump (cat-leap) onto a chest-height wall or higher from 4-steps away off the ground (or higher). Once you\'ve landed into the cat-leap position you must then step up with the lower foot and perform a tictac back and precision land on your original take-off point. \r\n\r\nYou must land in a precision and stick the landing. You cannot step forwards or backwards or shuffle your feet after landing. You must also land on the balls of your foot.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in each direction for the TicTac.\r\n\r\nYou must catch the arm-jump on the front of the wall.','Cat to Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','cat-to-precision','','','2021-12-29 09:39:43','2021-12-29 09:39:43','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55391',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55392,1,'2021-12-29 09:56:16','2021-12-29 09:56:16','Demonstrate an arm-jump onto a wall that then immediately transitions into a dyno (double-tap) onto a higher rail on top that then allows you to perform a climb-up over the rail.\n\nHeight of the wall can be any height.\n\nYou must perform the arm-jump from 4-steps away.\n\nThe rail can be any distance above the wall and of any orientation. You must perform a two-handed dyno onto the rail from the wall.\n\nYou must finish on top of the wall.\n\nYou must climb-up onto the rail with only your hands and feet. No knees or elbows allowed.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nRail can be any width.','Cat Dyno Climbup','','publish','closed','closed','','cat-dyno-climbup','','','2021-12-29 09:56:16','2021-12-29 09:56:16','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55392',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55393,1,'2021-12-29 10:02:49','2021-12-29 10:02:49','Perform a broad-jump of 6-steps or greater and land on a single foot (and opposite hand) to drop into a speed step-vault.\n\nYou will need to use a higher block that is hip-height or greater to start on so that the speed step will be going downwards as you exit.\n\nRepeat three times on each side of the speed step.\n\nYou must land one foot and not pause or stop the momentum.\n','Jump Speed Step','','publish','closed','closed','','jump-speed-step','','','2021-12-29 10:02:49','2021-12-29 10:02:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55393',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55394,1,'2021-12-29 10:53:51','2021-12-29 10:53:51','Demonstrate a cat-pass (kong) over a hip-height obstacle onto an ankle-height obstacle that is 4-steps or greater away landing in a precision.\n\nYou must stick the landing with two feet and not step forwards or backwards or shuffle your feet once landed. You must land in a precision on the front edge of the target. \nThe target wall must be less than one brick-length in width.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nObstacle can be a wall or rail.\n','Kong Pre','','publish','closed','closed','','kong-pre','','','2021-12-29 10:53:51','2021-12-29 10:53:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55394',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55395,1,'2021-12-29 11:01:19','2021-12-29 11:01:19','Demonstrate a cat-pass (kong) over a hip-height obstacle into an arm-jump (cat-leap) position on a parallel wall that is 4-steps or greater away.\n\nYou must land on the front-face of the cat-leap wall with both hands on the front edge. You cannot use an over-grip. You must land the cat-leap with you feet and hands.\n\nYou must stick the landing and hold the cat-leap position for three seconds or more.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nObstacle that you vault over can be a wall or rail.\n','Kong Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','kong-cat','','','2021-12-29 11:01:19','2021-12-29 11:01:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55395',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55396,1,'2021-12-29 11:08:13','2021-12-29 11:08:13','Perform a cat-pass (kong) over a hip-height rail or wall and catch underneath a second railing that is parallel to it.\nBoth rails / bars can be any width and circular or flat. \n\nYou must catch the second bar with the rest of your body off the ground. You cannot touch the floor after the cat-pass.\n\nThe distance of the second rail can be any distance you prefer. The height of the second railing can be any height.\n\nThis can also lead into a climb-up or muscle-up, but is not necessary.\n\nrepeat three times.\n\nYou cannot catch in a waist-position and then drop. Your chest must be under the rail as you catch it.','Kong Rail Catch','','publish','closed','closed','','kong-rail-catch','','','2021-12-29 11:08:13','2021-12-29 11:08:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55396',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55397,1,'2021-12-29 11:14:43','2021-12-29 11:14:43','Demonstrate a Lache to an arm-jump (cat-leap) on a wall that is 6 steps or greater away from the bar. You must stay off the ground for the entire movement.\r\n\r\nThe lache bar must be over full-stretch height and the cat-leap target can be any height.\r\n\r\nLand in a correct arm-jump position with feet staggered and hands on the front edge of the wall. You must stay in position for three seconds.\r\n\r\nThe lache bar must be scaffolding-width or greater in width.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.\r\n \r\n','Lache Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','lache-cat','','','2021-12-29 11:14:43','2021-12-29 11:14:43','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55397',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55398,1,'2021-12-29 11:19:34','2021-12-29 11:19:34','Demonstrate a Lache to a precision landing at knee-height (or less) that is 6 steps or greater away from the bar. You must stay off the ground for the entire movement.\n\nThe lache bar must be over full-stretch height and the precision target must be under knee-height. However, the landing cannot be on the ground.\n\nThe target must have a width of brick-length or less. It can also be a railing.\n\nLand in a correct precision and you must not step forwards or backwards or shuffle after your landing.\n\nThe lache bar must be scaffolding-width or greater in width.\n\nRepeat three times.\n \n','Lache Pre','','publish','closed','closed','','lache-pre','','','2021-12-29 11:19:34','2021-12-29 11:19:34','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55398',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55399,1,'2021-11-18 20:02:11','2021-11-18 20:02:11','The safety-vault, or step-vault is generally the first movement to learn within parkour.\r\nInitially, you should start with *both* hands on the wall or obstacle. Place one foot (i.e. right foot) on the outside of the hands and drive the hips up.\r\nRemove the inner hand (i.e. right hand) off the wall and bring your back leg (i.e. left leg), up and through the gap.\r\n\r\nThe obstacle must be hip height or above and your landing can be of any type. Landing both feet or one, in any orientation.','Two Hands, One-foot.','','publish','closed','closed','','step-vault-01','','','2021-11-18 20:02:11','2021-11-18 20:02:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55399',1,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55400,1,'2021-11-21 16:03:04','2021-11-21 16:03:04','This should be performed with one Hand, that is placed on the wall first, followed by the opposite side foot only on the wall.\r\n\r\nThe wall should be at hip-height or above and only a normal house-brick length or less, so that you can effectively pass over it and reach the ground on the other side.  \r\n\r\nLanding can be facing in any direction and with both feet.','One Hand, One-foot.','','publish','closed','closed','','step-vault-02','','','2021-11-21 16:03:04','2021-11-21 16:03:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55400',2,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55401,1,'2021-11-22 09:40:49','2021-11-22 09:40:49','With a one or two steps before the vault, step into the movement using the momentum to place the hand, then foot onto the wall without pausing before the obstacle. \r\n\r\nThe obstacle should be hip-height or above and only a single brick wide or less.\r\n\r\nThis level is looking at you ability to move into the vault with a consistent speed (which doesn\'t have to be fast) but without any pausing to prepare the movement.','With Momentum','','publish','closed','closed','','with-momentum','','','2021-11-22 09:40:49','2021-11-22 09:40:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55401',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55402,1,'2021-11-22 09:46:44','2021-11-22 09:46:44','This movement drill is used for enhancing stability within the step vault. Repeat the standard step-vault from level 2, but this time doing it on the ground.\r\n\r\nExtend the leg out in front of you and hold it for 5 seconds before moving it behind you and holding for 5 seconds again. Repeat this 3 times without the leg touching the ground.\r\n\r\nRepeat on both sides. ','Ground Level','','publish','closed','closed','','ground-level','','','2021-11-22 09:46:44','2021-11-22 09:46:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55402',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55403,1,'2021-11-22 09:54:01','2021-11-22 09:54:01','This is to demonstrate you can successfully climb up onto a wall at chest-height or greater and perform a step-vault on the top, dropping to the other side. You can perform the level-1 step vault with both hands and one foot.\n\nThe wall must be of standard brick-length or less. No knees, forearms or elbows can be used. Only hands and foot are allowed contact with the top of the wall.\n\nLanding on the other side can be of any type and orientation.','High Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','high-wall','','','2021-11-22 09:54:01','2021-11-22 09:54:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55403',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55404,1,'2021-11-22 10:02:26','2021-11-22 10:02:26','Using the same step-vault technique as level-2, using one hand and one foot, you must demonstrate entering and exiting the vault at speed.\r\n\r\nSpeed must be at jogging-pace or more and there must be minimal slow-down during the vault. A consistency in flowing over the obstacle is key.\r\n\r\nYou must land one-foot and run out of the vault.\r\n\r\nYou must start more than 5-paces away (a standard walking-pace) and finish 5-paces away on the other side of the obstacle.\r\n\r\nWall must be hip-height or greater, with a standard brick-length width.','At speed.','','publish','closed','closed','','at-speed','','','2021-11-22 10:02:26','2021-11-22 10:02:26','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55404',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55405,1,'2021-11-22 10:33:20','2021-11-22 10:33:20','Similar to level-6, this has all the same parameters, except that it must be performed on a railing of scaffolding diameter or less (48mm).\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate accuracy of hand and foot placement while at speed.\r\n\r\nSpeed must be at jogging-pace or more and there must be minimal slow-down during the vault. A consistency in flowing over the obstacle is key.\r\n\r\nYou must land one-foot on the ground and run out of the vault.\r\n\r\nYou must start more than 5-paces away (a standard walking-pace) and finish 5-paces away on the other side of the obstacle.\r\n\r\nRailing must be hip-height or greater, with a standard scaffolding rail width or less. It can be round or flat. If it is flat, it cannot be wider than 48mm.','Railing Speed','','publish','closed','closed','','railing-speed','','','2021-11-22 10:33:20','2021-11-22 10:33:20','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55405',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55406,1,'2021-11-22 10:43:35','2021-11-22 10:43:35','You must perform two step-vaults in a row with only two steps in between each. Each vault must be performed on opposite sides. So if the first vault uses the left-hand and the right foot, the second vault must use the right-hand and the left-foot.\n\nThis is on standard walls at hip-height or greater. \n\nAfter the first vault you must land one foot, take another step, then perform the second vault on the other hand.\n\nYou can land in any position after the second vault.\n\nThis must be performed at a jogging-speed or greater.','Doubles','','publish','closed','closed','','doubles','','','2021-11-22 10:43:35','2021-11-22 10:43:35','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55406',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55407,1,'2021-11-22 10:51:45','2021-11-22 10:51:45','You must demonstrate a slow and controlled one-hand, one-foot step-vault on a round railing at full-stretch height or greater. \r\n\r\nThis is similar to level-4, but on a railing at height. It is testing your control of movement and ability of fine motor-control of your muscles.\r\n\r\nYou must hold the forward position for 10 seconds and the back position for 10 seconds without touching the bar with the second hand or dropping. \r\n\r\nYou must repeat 3 times on each side. This is a total of 1 minute per side.','Slow and High','','publish','closed','closed','','slow-and-high','','','2021-11-22 10:51:45','2021-11-22 10:51:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55407',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55408,1,'2021-11-22 11:05:00','2021-11-22 11:05:00','This is to demonstrate you can perform a step-vault and move into a cat-leap (arm-jump) position on the other side of a wall. You should have the control and confidence to vault the obstacle with control.\n\nThe wall must be over hip-height from where your feet are in the start position. The wall on the opposite side must be over full-stretch height to the top.\n\nYou can do this on a wall or a wall with a rail.\n\nThe take-off can be a standing or running position.\n\nRepeat three times on both sides of the step vault and turn into the cat-position.','Step Turn Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','step-turn-cat','','','2021-11-22 11:05:00','2021-11-22 11:05:00','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55408',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55409,1,'2021-11-22 17:20:16','2021-11-22 17:20:16','Using a low wall, bench or obstacle, under knee-height or less; demonstrate a step-vault in reverse. \r\nUsing one hand and the opposite foot, you can finish on the wall or past it. This level is to show that you can control going backwards.\r\n\r\nWall can be of any width but you must repeat the exercise 3 times, on both sides.','Low Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','low-wall','','','2021-11-22 17:20:16','2021-11-22 17:20:16','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55409',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55410,1,'2021-11-23 08:32:15','2021-11-23 08:32:15','Perform a regular step-vault, but pause after your leg passes over the wall and is half-way to the floor. Now stop and reverse the movement to perform a reverse-step-vault all the way back to your starting position.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be hip-height or more and a standard brick-length or less in width.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate three repetitions using left-hand with the right foot and three repetitions on the right-hand with the left foot.','Step-to-Reverse','','publish','closed','closed','','step-to-reverse','','','2021-11-23 08:32:15','2021-11-23 08:32:15','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55410',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55411,1,'2021-11-23 08:56:54','2021-11-23 08:56:54','Starting with your foot and hand in the correct position on the wall, push your hips up and place the second leg over the wall to land on the ground on the other side.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate you can perform a full reverse-step-vault with no momentum while starting in the correct position.','No Momentum','','publish','closed','closed','','no-momentum','','','2021-11-23 08:56:54','2021-11-23 08:56:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55411',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55412,1,'2021-11-23 09:10:38','2021-11-23 09:10:38','Starting off the wall, demonstrate that you can move into a reverse-step vault, place both feet on the ground and then perform a regular step vault back to your starting position. \r\n\r\nUse the same hand and foot for both reverse-step and step vault.\r\n\r\nYou must perform three reverse-step to step-vault in a row without stopping. Repeat for the other hand and foot. (Six in total)\r\n','Back &amp; Forth','','publish','closed','closed','','back-forth','','','2021-11-23 09:10:38','2021-11-23 09:10:38','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55412',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55413,1,'2021-11-23 09:14:46','2021-11-23 09:14:46','Demonstrate that you can move into a reverse-step-vault and pause in the middle position where your second leg has left the ground.\r\n\r\nPause for 5-seconds in the middle position and then continue the reverse-step vault until completed.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate you have control of your body while moving through the position.\r\n\r\nThe landing can be of any type, but repeat three times on each side.','Reverse and Stop','','publish','closed','closed','','reverse-and-stop','','','2021-11-23 09:14:46','2021-11-23 09:14:46','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55413',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55414,1,'2021-11-23 09:21:50','2021-11-23 09:21:50','Starting with your hand on the wall, you must jump up into the landing of the foot position. The foot should go higher than the wall and come down onto it. Try not to just go straight onto the wall - this is to prevent clipping of the heel.\r\n\r\nYour foot must land on the ball of the foot on the wall. The second leg must land on the floor first.\r\n\r\nPerform three reverse-step vaults, jumping into the foot position, on each side. ','Hop up','','publish','closed','closed','','hop-up','','','2021-11-23 09:21:50','2021-11-23 09:21:50','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55414',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55415,1,'2021-11-23 09:46:48','2021-11-23 09:46:48','This is a replication of level 6, demonstrating that you can hop into the foot position, but with this level you must start with your hands off the wall. \r\n\r\nPlace the hand down as you are jumping into the reverse-step-vault. Also focusing on landing on the ball of the foot as it comes down onto the wall.\r\n\r\nThe hand must be flat and not with steepled fingers or thumb tucked behind.\r\n\r\nThere must not be any stopping during the vault - this is a normal-speed vault with no pausing.','Hands Off','','publish','closed','closed','','hands-off','','','2021-11-23 09:46:48','2021-11-23 09:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55415',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55416,1,'2021-11-23 09:54:30','2021-11-23 09:54:30','Start *Facing* the wall, take two steps towards it, gaining momentum, turn 180 degrees by placing your hand on the wall on the opposite side of your body. Turn, away from the wall and jump into the reverse step-vault.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate that you can move into the reverse step with momentum and control the position of your hand and foot with speed. \r\n\r\nThere must be a continuous movement with no pausing.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate both sides, turning away from the wall in both directions.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n1. The Right-hand goes onto the wall.\r\n2. You will turn away from the wall to your left. \r\n3. The left-foot will then hop onto the wall. \r\n4. The right-leg passes through the gap under you and onto the ground.\r\n\r\nThis is then performed in the complete opposite direction:\r\n\r\n1. The Left-hand goes onto the wall.\r\n2. You will turn away from the wall to your right. \r\n3. The right-foot will then hop onto the wall. \r\n4. The left-leg passes through the gap under you and onto the ground.','With Momentum','','publish','closed','closed','','with-momentum-2','','','2021-11-23 09:54:30','2021-11-23 09:54:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55416',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55417,1,'2021-11-23 09:59:02','2021-11-23 09:59:02','Perform level-8, with momentum and turning into the reverse-step vault, but this time on a railing.\r\n\r\nThe railing must be standard scaffolding-width or less and hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate accuracy of both the hand and foot placement while moving with momentum.\r\n','On a Rail','','publish','closed','closed','','on-a-rail','','','2021-11-23 09:59:02','2021-11-23 09:59:02','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55417',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55418,1,'2021-11-23 10:53:01','2021-11-23 10:53:01','Demonstrate that you can perform a reverse-step vault within a parkour route that contains three or more different vaults. The reverse-step vault must have a vault before it and after it.\r\n\r\nEach vault must be a maximum of three steps away from each other and the route must be performed at a consistent speed with minimal fluctuations or changes in the flow. There should be no pausing or stopping during the route.\r\n\r\nThe reverse-step can be on a wall or rail, but must be entered with momentum. \r\n\r\nYou must be moving at a jogging-pace or faster. This must not be performed at walking pace.\r\n\r\nThe reverse-step vault must be performed at hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nAll other vaults can be any vault you wish to perform.\r\n\r\nThe route must be completed three times.  There is no requirement to perform the route using the other hand and foot.','In a Route','','publish','closed','closed','','in-a-route','','','2021-11-23 10:53:01','2021-11-23 10:53:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55418',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55419,1,'2021-11-23 11:14:06','2021-11-23 11:14:06','The first mounting level requires you to perform a two-handed step-vault and then reposition into a mounted position on a wall. This requires mobility at the bottom position.\r\n\r\nThis can be performed on a wall no wider than a standard brick-length. Wall should be hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nThe following steps should be performed.\r\n\r\n1. Face the wall, place both hands on top.\r\n2. Place the right-foot on the outside of your hands on the wall.\r\n3. Push your hips up high and remove the inside (right) hand.\r\n4. Bring your back (left) leg up and place it on the wall in between the left hand and right foot.\r\n5. Now push on your left hand to shift your weight over both feet.\r\n6. Remove your left hand, finishing in a crouched position.\r\n\r\nRepeat on the other side using the other hand and foot to start.\r\n\r\nEach side must be repeated three times.\r\n','Step-Vault Reposition','London Parkour is an online platform dedicated to teaching the art of parkour through tutorials, tips, and community engagement. They offer various resources including a website, social media channels, and a YouTube channel where subscribers can learn practical movements that can transform their physical abilities and mindset. The focus is on making parkour accessible and enjoyable for everyone, regardless of skill level.\r\n\r\nTo stay updated with their content and community, London Parkour encourages users to subscribe to their YouTube channel and follow them on Instagram. They provide a variety of tutorials that guide viewers through essential parkour techniques, promoting a fit and active lifestyle. The platform aims to foster a supportive environment for parkour enthusiasts and newcomers alike.\r\n\r\nFor inquiries or further information, users can reach out via email or explore their website, which serves as a hub for all parkour-related content and community events. By engaging with London Parkour, individuals can enhance their understanding and practice of parkour, connecting with fellow enthusiasts in the process.','publish','closed','closed','','step-vault-reposition','','','2021-11-23 11:14:06','2021-11-23 11:14:06','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55419',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55420,1,'2021-11-23 11:29:53','2021-11-23 11:29:53','This mounting technique is useful because on the stability and many points of contact.\r\n\r\n1. Using a wall at hip-height or higher, place the left foot on top.\r\n2. Place a hand on each side of the foot. \r\n3. Slowly bring the back (right) leg up onto the wall outside the right hand.\r\n4. Use your left hand to push and shift your weight in between both feet.\r\n5. Move your left hand inside both feet, next to your right hand.\r\n6. Remove both hands.\r\n\r\nRepeat this movement three times and then demonstrate using the opposite side. You must stay on the wall for 10-seconds or more.','Assassins Creed','','publish','closed','closed','','assassins-creed','','','2021-11-23 11:29:53','2021-11-23 11:29:53','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55420',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55421,1,'2021-11-23 11:40:11','2021-11-23 11:40:11','This level is the same as level 1, however you must now perform the mount technique on a rail instead of a wall. The rail must be circular and no bigger than a standard scaffolding width (48mm) and hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nThe following steps should be performed.\r\n\r\n1. Face the rail, place both hands on top.\r\n2. Place the right-foot on the outside of your hands on the rail.\r\n3. Push your hips up high and remove the inside (right) hand.\r\n4. Bring your back (left) leg up and place it on the rail in between the left hand and right foot.\r\n5. Now push on your left hand to shift your weight over both feet.\r\n6. Remove your left hand, finishing in a crouched position.\r\n7. You must stay in the crouched position on the rail for 10 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat on the other side using the other hand and foot to start.\r\n\r\nEach side must be repeated three times.','Rail Reposition','London Parkour provides resources and tutorials focused on the art of parkour, designed to help individuals enhance their physical movement and mindset. Their approach emphasizes practical techniques that can transform how participants think, move, and experience their environment. The company is dedicated to making parkour accessible and beneficial for everyone, regardless of their skill level.\r\n\r\nTo stay updated with the latest parkour tutorials and content, users are encouraged to subscribe to their YouTube channel, which features a variety of instructional videos and demonstrations. The channel serves as a platform for parkour enthusiasts to learn new skills, improve existing ones, and connect with the broader parkour community. Engaging with the material can broaden one’s understanding of parkour as not only a physical discipline but also a form of personal expression and growth.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in exploring more about London Parkour, additional information can be found on their official website and social media platforms. Through these channels, users can follow their journey, access more resources, and contact the team for further inquiries. By focusing on the holistic development of individuals in the realm of parkour, London Parkour aims to foster an inclusive environment where everyone can thrive physically and mentally.','publish','closed','closed','','rail-reposition','','','2021-11-23 11:40:11','2021-11-23 11:40:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55421',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55422,1,'2021-11-23 17:01:36','2021-11-23 17:01:36','This level is used to demonstrate that you can push up on a rail from one leg.\r\n \r\n1. Place a single foot inline with the rail, so you are facing along the bar. \r\n2. Start in a crouched position.\r\n3. Both hands on the rail in front of you. \r\n4. Make sure the heel is on the rail.\r\n5. Push upwards, extending the leg and straightening it.\r\n6. Finish standing in a single-foot balance.\r\n\r\nYour second leg can brush along the rail and help keep balance.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater and of standard scaffolding width or less.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each leg.','Parallel Pistol','London Parkour offers a comprehensive online platform dedicated to the art of parkour, providing tutorials and resources to help individuals learn and improve their skills in practical movement. Their website, https://LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a hub for various training materials, encouraging users to revolutionize their physical capabilities and mindset through parkour techniques. The aim is to enhance both mental and physical fitness, making it approachable for people of all levels.\r\n\r\nThe organization actively promotes engagement through social media channels, including Instagram and YouTube. They encourage users to subscribe to their YouTube channel for regular updates and tutorials, which are categorized under various hashtags like #ParkourHowTo, #ParkourTutorial, and #LondonParkour. These platforms allow followers to stay connected with the parkour community and access valuable content that can aid in their journey of mastering parkour moves.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in learning more about their offerings or seeking guidance, London Parkour provides an email contact for inquiries. The organization emphasizes a transformation in movement and overall well-being, making it a promising resource for anyone looking to explore the dynamic discipline of parkour. Potential learners are invited to connect through their website and social media to begin their journey in this exciting physical activity.','publish','closed','closed','','parallel-pistol','','','2021-11-23 17:01:36','2021-11-23 17:01:36','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55422',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55423,1,'2021-11-23 17:07:02','2021-11-23 17:07:02','Similar to the start of the Level 2 assassins-creed position, start with a single foot on a hip-height rail facing out from the rail (not along it, but at 90degrees perpendicular to it).\r\n\r\nPlace a hand on both sides of the leg. \r\n\r\nNow, push the leg straight while keeping the back leg off the ground. There should be no assistance from the second leg.\r\n\r\nFinish in a half-crouched or standing position while joining the second leg on the bar.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each leg.','Perpendicular Pistol','London Parkour is an online platform that provides tutorials and resources on Parkour, encouraging movement and creativity in urban environments. Their website offers a range of practical movement techniques designed to enhance physical abilities and promote a transformative mindset. The community is actively engaged through various social media channels.\r\n\r\nThe organization encourages individuals to subscribe to their YouTube channel to stay updated on new content related to Parkour. With their unique approach, London Parkour aims to connect enthusiasts and beginners alike, fostering a supportive environment for anyone interested in improving their movement skills. \r\n\r\nIn addition to their main website, London Parkour is present on Instagram, providing visual content and updates that enhance the Parkour learning experience. Interested parties can reach out via their contact email for further inquiries, ensuring accessible communication for those looking to delve deeper into the Parkour community.','publish','closed','closed','','perpendicular-pistol','','','2021-11-23 17:07:02','2021-11-23 17:07:02','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55423',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55424,1,'2021-11-23 17:16:03','2021-11-23 17:16:03','Starting next to a hip-height circular rail, place both hands on the bar, close together.\r\nNext, jump up and land both feet on the rail on the outside of the hands.\r\nYou can crouch into this position with four points of contact, or take your hands off the rail and finish in a crouched position.\r\n\r\nThis is a bilateral movement where both sides of the body are moving identically and at the same time. Therefore, only three total reps need to be completed. \r\n\r\nBoth feet need to land on the bar at the same time.','Leap Frog','London Parkour is a dedicated platform focusing on the art of parkour, offering tutorials and guides to help individuals develop their skills in this dynamic movement discipline. The website, https://LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as both an instructional resource and a community hub for enthusiasts. Followers can access a range of parkour-related content and learn how to improve their physical abilities through practical movement.\r\n\r\nFor ongoing updates and visual content, London Parkour encourages interested individuals to subscribe to their YouTube channel, which provides step-by-step tutorials and showcases various parkour techniques. By visiting their social media platforms, particularly Instagram, followers can stay connected with the latest trends and community events surrounding parkour in London. The overarching goal is to inspire transformation in one\'s thinking, movement, and overall physical experience.\r\n\r\nTo engage with the community or seek additional information, users are invited to reach out via the provided contact email. By participating in London Parkour, members can expect to enhance their parkour skills while also connecting with like-minded individuals who share a passion for this physical art form.','publish','closed','closed','','leap-frog','','','2021-11-23 17:16:03','2021-11-23 17:16:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55424',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55425,1,'2021-11-23 17:30:40','2021-11-23 17:30:40','Perform the level-2 assassins creed mounting technique but on a circular railing.\n\n1. Using a rail at hip-height or higher, place the left foot on top.\n2. Place a hand on each side of the foot. \n3. Slowly bring the back (right) leg up onto the rail outside the right hand.\n4. Use your left hand to push and shift your weight in between both feet.\n5. Move your left hand inside both feet, next to your right hand.\n6. Remove both hands.\n\nRepeat this movement three times and then demonstrate using the opposite side. You must stay on the rail for 10-seconds or more in the finished position.\n\nThis should demonstrate a good ability for muscle control and balance.','Rail Assassin','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-assassin','','','2021-11-23 17:30:40','2021-11-23 17:30:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55425',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55426,1,'2021-11-23 17:44:35','2021-11-23 17:44:35','This requires a railing with nothing underneath it. No secondary bars or perpendicular bars, since your legs will need to swing underneath.\r\n\r\n1. Start facing the hip-height rail.\r\n2. Place both hands on the rail at shoulder-width apart in front of you.\r\n3. Rest the front of your hips on the railing.\r\n4. Both legs should be off the ground.\r\n5. Swing your legs forward to generate momentum.\r\n6. On the back swing of the legs, open your hips and \'flick\' your legs up behind.\r\n7. At the apex of the back swing, quickly bring your feet back in and onto the railing.\r\n8. Finish by releasing the rail with the hands as the feet land.\r\n\r\nYou must stay on the railing for at least 10 seconds once you\'ve finished the movement.\r\n\r\nThis is a bilateral movement, so only three repetitions in total are needed to be done. \r\n\r\nBoth feet must land on the railing at the same time.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be circular and no wider than a scaffolding bar width.','Danish Flick','London Parkour offers a variety of resources and tutorials for individuals interested in learning the art of parkour. Their website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a hub for practical movement techniques that aim to enhance both physical abilities and mindset. By focusing on their approach to parkour, they strive to transform the way participants think, move, and feel, making it accessible for everyone.\r\n\r\nThe channels available for engagement include Instagram and YouTube, where followers can find additional content such as tutorials and demonstration videos. These platforms are designed to inspire and educate newcomers, as well as providing continuing support for more experienced practitioners. The community is encouraged to subscribe to their YouTube channel to receive updates and new content regularly.\r\n\r\nFor further information,\r\ninterested parties are invited to reach out via email at contact@localhost:8443. By fostering a strong online presence and interactivity, London Parkour aims to build a supportive network that caters to all levels of parkour enthusiasts, promoting growth and development within the discipline.','publish','closed','closed','','danish-flick','','','2021-11-23 17:44:35','2021-11-23 17:44:35','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55426',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55427,1,'2021-11-23 17:52:06','2021-11-23 17:52:06','This movement is to demonstrate that you can mount a rail at an angle with momentum.\r\n\r\n1. Start facing the rail.\r\n2. Place both hands further down the rail to your right. The left hand should be past the right hip bone.\r\n3. Pull the rail as you jump along the rail to the right.\r\n4. Both feet should land on the rail to the right of both hands.\r\n5. Release the rail with the hands and finish in a crouched position.\r\n\r\nYou must stay in the crouch for 10 seconds on the rail.\r\n\r\nYou should have travelled along the rail more than 2-steps.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be circular and no greater than standard scaffolding bar width.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times, moving on both sides.','Slide-Pull','London Parkour offers a comprehensive platform for individuals interested in learning parkour through online tutorials and resources. Their content is designed to enhance not only the physical abilities of practitioners but also to reshape their mental approach to movement. By subscribing to their YouTube channel, viewers can access a wealth of tutorials and inspiration to foster their parkour journey.\r\n\r\nThe website serves as a hub for enthusiasts, providing practical movement insights that can significantly improve one\'s overall experience with parkour. With a focus on transforming the way individuals think, move, and feel, London Parkour aims to build a community where learners can connect and grow together. Their social media presence, particularly on Instagram, allows followers to engage with their content and keep up with the latest updates.\r\n\r\nFor those looking to join the Parkour movement, London Parkour encourages interaction through their various online platforms. Interested individuals can visit their website for resources or reach out via email for any inquiries. The emphasis on community and personal development makes London Parkour an inviting space for both beginners and experienced athletes alike.','publish','closed','closed','','slide-pull','','','2021-11-23 17:52:06','2021-11-23 17:52:06','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55427',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55428,1,'2021-11-23 18:02:41','2021-11-23 18:02:41','This mounting technique is used to land on the railing facing back in the opposite way to normal.\r\n\r\nIf you started facing the rail, you\'ll finish turning 180 degrees back and facing the opposite direction.\r\n\r\n1. Start facing the railing.\r\n2. Place the left-hand in a supinated grip (under the bar, palm facing up)\r\n3. Place the right-hand in a pronated grip (over the bar, palm facing down)\r\n4. Jump upwards, letting go of the railing with the right hand.\r\n5. As the right hand releases, the left foot must replace it on the railing.\r\n6. Pivot on the left foot and the left hand. The right leg will swing over the bar and behind you.\r\n7. You should turn around 180 degrees facing back the way you came.\r\n8. The right foot should land on the railing on the other side of the left hand.\r\n9. As the right foot lands, the right hand should also grab the bar on the inside of the right foot.\r\n10. You should finished crouched on the railing with both hands between you legs.\r\n\r\nYou must finish in the crouched position for 10 seconds.','Jump and Twist','London Parkour is an engaging platform dedicated to the discipline of parkour, offering a variety of resources and tutorials for enthusiasts. Their website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a central hub where users can access practical movement techniques that aim to transform their physicality and mental approach to movement. The site is equipped with numerous instructional materials designed to cater to both novice and experienced practitioners.\r\n\r\nThe organization also encourages users to subscribe to their YouTube channel, where they can find additional parkour tutorials and demonstrations. The use of hashtags such as #ParkourHowTo, #ParkourTutorial, and #ParkourPulse further emphasizes their focus on community engagement and the sharing of knowledge within the parkour enthusiast demographic. By promoting their social media presence, especially through platforms like Instagram and YouTube, London Parkour aims to broaden its reach and provide a space for connection among practitioners.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in learning more and enhancing their parkour skills, the London Parkour team actively invites contact through email at contact@localhost:8443. This open line of communication supports a community-oriented approach, ensuring that participants feel connected and supported in their parkour journey. Their emphasis on transformative movement not only focuses on physical skills but also on the mental aspects of parkour, promoting a holistic development experience for all practitioners.','publish','closed','closed','','jump-and-twist','','','2021-11-23 18:02:41','2021-11-23 18:02:41','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55428',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55429,1,'2021-11-25 08:19:22','2021-11-25 08:19:22','To achieve this level, you will require a wall at hip-height or greater connected to a second,  perpendicular wall. \n\nWith this set-up, stand in the corner of where the two walls meet and place one hand on each wall.\n\nPush with both hands, creating a stable structure and lift both feet off the ground. Pass both legs over the wall and land two feet on the other side of the wall.\n\nYou must repeat this three times in both directions making sure you do three reps with the left hand on the hip-height wall, and then three reps with the right hand on the wall.','Hands Only','','publish','closed','closed','','hands-only','','','2021-11-25 08:19:22','2021-11-25 08:19:22','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55429',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55430,1,'2021-11-25 08:39:39','2021-11-25 08:39:39','Using the same setup as level-1, you will require a wall at hip-height or greater connected to a second, perpendicular wall.\n\nStand in the corner of where the two walls meet and place one hand only on the hip-height wall.\n\nPlace your opposite foot into the corner where the two walls meet - just as you would with a step-vault. Move your hand a little further and try to extend your body a little further away from the wall, slightly more than a regular step-vault.\n\nNow try to move the foot on the wall onto the perpendicular wall, even if you\'re still resting the foot on the hip-height wall. \n\nCreate a stable position using both hand and foot. Bring the back leg off the floor and pass it over the wall and landing on this same leg.\n\nYou must repeat this three times in both directions making sure you do three reps with the left hand on the hip-height wall, and then three reps with the right hand on the wall.','Hand &amp; Foot','','publish','closed','closed','','hand-foot','','','2021-11-25 08:39:39','2021-11-25 08:39:39','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55430',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55431,1,'2021-11-25 08:56:45','2021-11-25 08:56:45','Repeat the same movement as level-3 using momentum and at least 2 paces away from the vault to jump into the tictac with one hand and one foot.\n\nDraw a target on the ground with chalk or using any method appropriate and visible in the landing spot on the other side of the wall. The target should be a line on the ground that corresponds to how you will be landing.\n\nOn exiting the vault, instead of landing one foot, you will now land two feet together on your target. Your hand that was on the wall must no longer be in contact on landing.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.','Two Foot Target','','publish','closed','closed','','two-foot-target','','','2021-11-25 08:56:45','2021-11-25 08:56:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55431',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55432,1,'2021-11-25 09:04:14','2021-11-25 09:04:14','You must demonstrate the same movement as the level-3 tictac with momentum, with one or more steps into the vault, placing the foot onto the perpendicular wall higher than the corner.\r\n\r\nHowever, the hip-height obstacle should now be a railing or bar instead of a wall. This is to test you hand position accuracy. The railing can be flat or circular, but no wider than a standard scaffolding bar.\r\n\r\nMake sure your foot is placed in-line with the hip-height wall.\r\n\r\nYou must take-off on one foot, finishing with one or more steps out of the vault in any landing.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.','Rail TicTac','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-tictac','','','2021-11-25 09:04:14','2021-11-25 09:04:14','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55432',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55433,1,'2021-11-25 12:46:50','2021-11-25 12:46:50','This is a repeat on the regular TicTac movement on a hip-height wall with a perpendicular wall. This must be done with momentum as you must now remove the hand completely and only use the foot on the wall.\n\nTarget the foot inline with the hip-height wall. Do not kick the wall before or after the position as this will lead to slipping.\n\nYou must repeat three times using both sides and both feet.','Foot Only','','publish','closed','closed','','foot-only','','','2021-11-25 12:46:50','2021-11-25 12:46:50','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55433',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55434,1,'2021-11-25 12:52:42','2021-11-25 12:52:42','This is a repeat on the regular TicTac movement on a hip-height wall with a perpendicular wall using only the foot and no hands. \n\nYou must land on one foot only and with a maximum of one extra step, move straight into a second vault of your choosing.\n\nThere must not be any pausing or stopping and minimal fluctuation of speed.\n\nTarget the foot inline with the hip-height wall. Do not kick the wall before or after the position as this will cause slipping.\n\nYou must repeat three times using both sides and both feet. The second vault must *ALSO* be performed on the opposite side too.','Second Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','second-vault','','','2021-11-25 12:52:42','2021-11-25 12:52:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55434',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55435,1,'2021-11-25 13:00:04','2021-11-25 13:00:04','You must demonstrate the foot-only TicTac on a wall, over a hip-height wall or obstacle, to a precision onto a target of brick-width or less.\n\nUsing a stable brick or piece of wood, place it onto your target landing area on the far-side of the TicTac. \n\nYou must land on the target with both feet and stick the landing. This means that there is no shuffling of the feet and no falling off.\n\nThe target can be any distance away from the wall.\n\nThis must be demonstrated 3 times on each side.','To Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','to-precision','','','2021-11-25 13:00:04','2021-11-25 13:00:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55435',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55436,1,'2021-11-25 13:08:27','2021-11-25 13:08:27','In a similar scenario as a normal TicTac, you must demonstrate a the move with two steps on the wall rather than a single foot.\r\n\r\nThe takeoff will now be switched and the inside leg (closest to the hip-height wall) must go onto the perpendicular wall first. Step up higher onto the wall with the second foot and ricochet off the wall in a normal TicTac.\r\n\r\n\r\nBoth feet must come in contact with the wall and no hands can be used. You must go over a hip-height wall.\r\n\r\nGenerating a lot of momentum will be key as well as finding the right angle to approach and exit the movement.','Two Steps','','publish','closed','closed','','two-steps','','','2021-11-25 13:08:27','2021-11-25 13:08:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55436',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55437,1,'2021-11-25 13:13:15','2021-11-25 13:13:15','You must demonstrate a single-foot only TicTac over a gap of full-stretch height or more. \n\nThe gap must be five-steps or greater.\n\nYou do not need to go over an obstacle.\n\nYou must repeat three times on each side.\n\nAny landing is acceptable - one or two foot. Running or Precision.','Over a Gap','','publish','closed','closed','','over-a-gap-2','','','2021-11-25 13:13:15','2021-11-25 13:13:15','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55437',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55438,1,'2021-11-26 09:19:03','2021-11-26 09:19:03','Demonstrate the cat-pass (aka Kong) movement on the ground.\r\n\r\nStart in a crouched position and place both hands, palms flat, on the ground in front. Now shift you weight onto your arms, pick both feet up off the ground and land them in-line and between your hands.\r\n\r\nAs your feet are mid-air, and approaching the landing, lift your hands off the ground so that you have enough mobility and space to land in a crouched position again.\r\n\r\nThis is a bilateral movement, so repeat five times on the ground.\r\n\r\nYou feet should not drag on the ground and your hands must not steeple onto the fingers. The base of the palm must be on the ground. Also, the thumb should be next to the fingers facing forwards, not facing backwards and behind the palm, lifting it up.\r\n\r\nLand on the ball of the foot and you are aiming to get the front of your shoe to where the back of the palm was on the ground; or further. Minimum range of distance should be 1 foot step forward.\r\n\r\nNo twisting onto one side. This is a bilateral movement where both sides of your body should be doing the same motion at the same time.','Floor Kong','','publish','closed','closed','','floor-kong','','','2021-11-26 09:19:03','2021-11-26 09:19:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55438',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55439,1,'2021-11-26 09:35:27','2021-11-26 09:35:27','Demonstrate that you can perform a cat-pass up onto a higher level. You can use some stairs, a bench, short wall, block or raised platform that is higher that knee height.\n\nPerform the cat-pass placing your hands on the higher level and then landing your feet on the same level.\n\nYou must repeat five times at the same height or greater.\n\nPalms must be flat with no steepling of the fingers and the thumb facing forwards.\n\nNo twisting onto one side. This is a bilateral movement where both sides of your body should be doing the same motion at the same time.','Upwards','','publish','closed','closed','','upwards','','','2021-11-26 09:35:27','2021-11-26 09:35:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55439',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55440,1,'2021-11-26 09:49:46','2021-11-26 09:49:46','Demonstrate a cat-pass over a wall that is over knee-height and landing on the ground the other side.\r\n\r\nThe wall width must be standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYou must use flat hands with only the palms. The fingers must be facing forwards and no steepling of the fingers.\r\n\r\nFive repetitions or more.\r\n\r\nNo twisting onto one side. This is a bilateral movement where both sides of your body should be doing the same motion at the same time.','Over a Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','over-a-wall','','','2021-11-26 09:49:46','2021-11-26 09:49:46','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55440',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55441,1,'2021-11-26 09:55:32','2021-11-26 09:55:32','Demonstrate a cat-pass over a wall with two steps of momentum before the movement. \r\n\r\nFrom your start position you must take two steps to generate more momentum over the the wall. You can take-off split-foot or two-foot.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be at hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nPalms must be flat on the wall, no steepling of fingers.\r\n\r\nAny landing on the other side is acceptable.\r\n\r\nFive repetitions are required.\r\n\r\nNo twisting onto one side. This is a bilateral movement where both sides of your body should be doing the same motion at the same time.','2-Step Momentum','','publish','closed','closed','','2-step-momentum','','','2021-11-26 09:55:32','2021-11-26 09:55:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55441',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55442,1,'2021-11-26 10:04:44','2021-11-26 10:04:44','Demonstrate the ability to do a cat-pass with a split-foot / staggered take-off over a hip-height wall.\r\n\r\nOne foot must be ahead of the other by at least a foot-length and you will be taking off the front foot last.\r\n\r\nAny landing is acceptable, but palms must be flat on the wall with no steepling of fingers.\r\n\r\nYou can use any run-up or momentum you require.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate that you can use a more efficient staggered take-off.\r\n\r\nNo twisting onto one side. This is a bilateral movement where both sides of your body should be doing the same motion at the same time.','Split-foot','','publish','closed','closed','','split-foot','','','2021-11-26 10:04:44','2021-11-26 10:04:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55442',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55443,1,'2021-11-26 10:11:35','2021-11-26 10:11:35','Perform a standing cat-pass with no momentum, steps in, or run-up over a hip-height wall or greater.\r\n\r\nThis is a standard cat-pass that can be split-foot or parallel foot takeoff position. However, you cannot move your feet from the take-off position unless its to perform the cat-pass. Any shuffling or repositioning during the movement will not be acceptable.\r\n\r\nPerform five repetitions over the wall.\r\n\r\nNo twisting of the torso and the hands must be flat on the wall with no steepling of the fingers.','Standing','','publish','closed','closed','','standing','','','2021-11-26 10:11:35','2021-11-26 10:11:35','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55443',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55444,1,'2021-11-26 10:25:54','2021-11-26 10:25:54','Demonstrate a cat-pass on a hip-height or higher railing. The railing must be of scaffolding-width or less and can be circular or flat.\n\nAny take-off or landing is acceptable, however you must start away from the railing and not with your hands on it before the movement starts.\n\nYou must perform five repetitions without any twisting of the torso.','Rails','','publish','closed','closed','','rails','','','2021-11-26 10:25:54','2021-11-26 10:25:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55444',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55445,1,'2021-11-26 10:32:32','2021-11-26 10:32:32','Using a bag or any item that is knee-height or greater, place it three steps in front of a hip-height wall. \r\n\r\nYou need to demonstrate a diving cat-pass over the lower item and over the main wall. You entire body needs to be in the air for a moment. As you jump and your feet leave the ground, your hands must not be touching the wall yet.\r\n\r\nYou hands must land flat on the palm and there must not be any twisting in the movement. \r\n\r\nRepeat five times, not touching the knee-height item. ','Diving','','publish','closed','closed','','diving','','','2021-11-26 10:32:32','2021-11-26 10:32:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55445',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55446,1,'2021-11-26 10:40:35','2021-11-26 10:40:35','Perform a cat-pass to precision. The precision landing must not be on flat ground - It needs to be an item that is higher than the floor - a curb, brick, wood, railing, etc.\n\nYou must demonstrate a cat-pass over a hip-height obstacle (wall / rail / block / etc) and into a two-foot precision landing with the balls of your feet. You must stick the landing and hold it for 5 seconds.\n\nThe landing must be at least three-steps away from the vaulting wall.\n\nYou must demonstrate five repetitions with no twisting of the body.','Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','precision','','','2021-11-26 10:40:35','2021-11-26 10:40:35','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55446',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55447,1,'2021-11-26 10:46:20','2021-11-26 10:46:20','Demonstrate a double cat-pass over two walls. Both walls must be over three steps apart and at hip-height or greater.\n\nFor a double cat-pass, the first vault must lift your hips up higher than the wall so that the hands can shift across to the second wall, ready for the landing position.\n\nHands must be flat and the feet must not touch either wall. There must not be any twisting of the body.\n\nFive repetitions must be demonstrated.','Double','','publish','closed','closed','','double','','','2021-11-26 10:46:20','2021-11-26 10:46:20','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55447',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55448,1,'2021-11-26 11:48:28','2021-11-26 11:48:28','Demonstrate a speed-step up onto a higher platform, block, step or level.\r\n\r\nYou must lead the vault with your foot and it must make contact onto the block before the hand does.\r\n\r\nThe block must be knee-height or greater and can just have a flat continuous top, unlike a wall with a drop on the furthest side.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n\r\nYou landing and takeoff does not have any parameters.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate your can vault while leading with your foot rather than the hand.\r\n\r\nThe hand must land flat with the palm and no steepled fingers.\r\n\r\nAny speed or momentum can be used.','Foot First','','publish','closed','closed','','foot-first','','','2021-11-26 11:48:28','2021-11-26 11:48:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55448',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55449,1,'2021-11-26 13:06:18','2021-11-26 13:06:18','Demonstrate a full speed-step at jogging-pace or more over a hip-height or greater wall that has a width that is standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYour foot must land and make contact with the wall first, followed by the opposite hand afterwards, facing forwards.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate both sides, three times.\r\n\r\nThe hand must land palm down, facing forwards.','Over a Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','over-a-wall-2','','','2021-11-26 13:06:18','2021-11-26 13:06:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55449',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55450,1,'2021-11-26 13:13:51','2021-11-26 13:13:51','Perform a speed-step downwards to a drop that is chest-height or greater. Start in a standing position at the top of a block or wide wall and hop into the speed-step, landing foot-first and then the opposite hand.\r\n\r\nYou must drop downwards from the speed-step at least chest-height or greater.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate three times on each side (Left &amp; Right).\r\n\r\nAny landing is acceptable, however the palm of the hand must be facing forwards and flat.','Downwards','','publish','closed','closed','','downwards','','','2021-11-26 13:13:51','2021-11-26 13:13:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55450',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55451,1,'2021-11-26 13:19:29','2021-11-26 13:19:29','Perform a speed-step at jogging speed on a hip-height (or greater) wall. This is to demonstrate you can move into the speed-step the way it is intended. \r\n\r\nYou must jump into the one-foot landing, where your entire body is off the ground at a single moment. I.e. your back foot should have left the floor before the front foot lands on the wall.\r\n\r\nHand must land facing forwards on the palm.\r\n\r\nThree repetitions on each side (left &amp; right) must be shown.\r\n\r\nThe wall width must be standard brick-length or less.','With Speed','','publish','closed','closed','','with-speed','','','2021-11-26 13:19:29','2021-11-26 13:19:29','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55451',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55452,1,'2021-11-27 15:05:43','2021-11-27 15:05:43','Using a knee-height or higher block / obstacle / wall, you will demonstrate a side-vault up onto the higher obstacle.\r\n\r\nStart standing next to the higher block facing along the wall, not towards it. (So the wall is on your side).\r\nPlace both hands flat on top of the block and keep both feet together. \r\n\r\nNow jump both feet up together behind you and land on the balls of the foot on top of the block.\r\n\r\nMake sure both feet land together.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on each side.','Up and On','','publish','closed','closed','','up-and-on','','','2021-11-27 15:05:43','2021-11-27 15:05:43','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55452',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55453,1,'2021-11-27 15:19:55','2021-11-27 15:19:55','Perform a side-vault up onto a hip-height wall that has a maximum width of a standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nStand with the wall to your side, place both hands onto the wall, one on each side of the wall. \r\n\r\nJump your feet up together behind you, lean forwards onto your hands and land both feet onto the top of the wall together. Drop both feet down onto the floor on the other side of the wall using the balls of the feet.\r\n\r\nMake sure you repeat three times going in both directions so that you are side-vaulting both to your left and your right.','Onto a Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','onto-a-wall','','','2021-11-27 15:19:55','2021-11-27 15:19:55','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55453',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55454,1,'2021-11-27 15:25:04','2021-11-27 15:25:04','Perform the side-vault exactly the same as level-2, however your feet should only touch the top of the wall for a maximum of one second or less.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate a faster movement and flow from takeoff to landing.\r\n\r\nStand with the wall to your side, place both hands onto the wall, one on each side of the wall. \r\n\r\nJump your feet up together behind you, lean forwards onto your hands and land both feet onto the top of the wall together for a maximum *one* second. Drop both feet down onto the floor on the other side of the wall using the balls of the feet.\r\n\r\nMake sure you repeat three times going in both directions so that you are side-vaulting both to your left and your right.','On and Off','','publish','closed','closed','','on-and-off','','','2021-11-27 15:25:04','2021-11-27 15:25:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55454',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55455,1,'2021-11-27 15:37:48','2021-11-27 15:37:48','Demonstrate the ability to side-vault completely over a railing of hip-height or greater. Using a railing will mean you have less width to jump over and will therefore be easier than a wall.\r\n\r\nYou feet must not touch the top of the rail and should clear the entire bar and land on the ground the other side.\r\n\r\nYour chest, torso and hips must be facing the same direction on the takeoff and landing position. \r\n\r\nWhen you grab the bar with your hands, the closest hand will grab the far side of the rail and the other will grab the near-side.\r\n\r\nThe bar can be circular or flat, however it must be of standard scaffolding width or less.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate three reps on each side.','Over a Rail','','publish','closed','closed','','over-a-rail','','','2021-11-27 15:37:48','2021-11-27 15:37:48','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55455',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55456,1,'2021-11-27 15:44:54','2021-11-27 15:44:54','Demonstrate the ability to side-vault completely over a wall of hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nYou feet must not touch the top of the wall and should clear the entire obstacle and land on the ground the other side.\r\n\r\nYour chest, torso and hips must be facing the same direction on the takeoff and landing position. \r\n\r\nWhen you grab the wall with your hands, the closest hand will grab the far side of the wall and the other will grab the near-side.\r\n\r\nThe wall must have a maximum width of a standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate three reps on each side.','Over a Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','over-a-wall-3','','','2021-11-27 15:44:54','2021-11-27 15:44:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55456',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55457,1,'2021-11-27 15:50:07','2021-11-27 15:50:07','Chain ten (x10) side-vaults back-and-forth in a row without pausing or stopping. Your landing position should also be your take-off position on the next vault.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate this on a hip-height wall or rail.\r\n\r\nYour chest, torso and hips must be facing the same direction on the takeoff and landing position. \r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate ten reps on each side (20 in total).','Chaining','','publish','closed','closed','','chaining-11','','','2021-11-27 15:50:07','2021-11-27 15:50:07','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55457',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55458,1,'2021-11-27 16:12:36','2021-11-27 16:12:36','Perform a side-vault over a higher wall or railing that is chest-height or greater. This is to demonstrate that you can vault higher obstacles in one movement.\n\nYour chest, torso and hips must be facing the same direction on the takeoff and landing position. \n\nYou must repeat three times on each side.','Higher Obstacle','','publish','closed','closed','','higher-obstacle','','','2021-11-27 16:12:36','2021-11-27 16:12:36','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55458',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55459,1,'2021-11-27 16:22:11','2021-11-27 16:22:11','Using momentum you must perform a side-vault over a hip-height wall and land more than 5-steps away from the wall on the other side. \r\n\r\nYou can land on the inside foot only (closest to wall) and keep moving out or in a stop, but the foot must clear 5-steps away from the wall. Use a marker to indicate the distance of the 5-steps.\r\n\r\nOn the exit of the side-vault, you can use the trailing hand to push you away from the wall.\r\n\r\nYour chest, torso and hips must be facing the same direction on the takeoff and landing position. \r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on each side.\r\n ','Cover Distance','','publish','closed','closed','','cover-distance','','','2021-11-27 16:22:11','2021-11-27 16:22:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55459',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55460,1,'2021-11-27 16:28:31','2021-11-27 16:28:31','Perform a side-vault over a height-drop that is at head-height or greater to the base of your takeoff point. The gap must be more than 4-steps wide. \n\nStarting on the opposite side of the gap, you need to dive over the gap, landing the hands on the wall and clear both feet over the wall - landing on the far side of the wall.\n\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and the width to be of standard brick-length or less.\n\nYour chest, torso and hips must be facing the same direction on the takeoff and landing position. \n\nYou must repeat three times on each side. ','Over a Gap','','publish','closed','closed','','over-a-gap-3','','','2021-11-27 16:28:31','2021-11-27 16:28:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55460',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55461,1,'2021-11-27 16:42:01','2021-11-27 16:42:01','Demonstrate a side-vault over a hip-height wall / rail into a rail precision that you cannot see until you have committed to the vault and are already jumping. \r\n\r\nRailing can be any height, but must be 4-steps or greater away,\r\n\r\nThe target width must be scaffolding-rail width or less. \r\n\r\nYou must stick your landing with both feet and not bounce off or miss it.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on each side. \r\n','Side to Rail Pre','','publish','closed','closed','','side-rail-pre','','','2021-11-27 16:42:01','2021-11-27 16:42:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55461',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55462,1,'2021-11-28 13:43:30','2021-11-28 13:43:30','Demonstrate a two-handed step-vault on a hip-height railing with the outside hand facing the opposite way in a supinated grip. \r\nOnce you are on top of the rail and the back leg is passing over the wall, twist towards the hand 180 degrees and bring the foot down onto the ground facing back the way you came.\r\nAs the foot comes down, the inside hand re-grips the bar on the outside of the main hand.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n1. Grab the bar with Left-hand facing up, right hand facing down.\r\n2. Right-foot is placed into a step-vault position on the rail.\r\n3. Push up and remove the right hand off the bar.\r\n4. Bring the left foot up and over the rail.\r\n5. As the left leg is dropping on the other side, turn it back towards the left-hand.\r\n6. Turn the foot so it\'s facing back the way you came as it lands.\r\n7. The right hand grabs the rail on the outside of the left hand.\r\n8. finish with the right foot landing next to the left foot.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or above.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on both sides.','Step Turn','','publish','closed','closed','','step-turn','','','2021-11-28 13:43:30','2021-11-28 13:43:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55462',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55463,1,'2021-11-28 13:51:18','2021-11-28 13:51:18','Very similar to the level-1, this is the same movement, however you need to land on both feet at the same time, rather than one and then the other.\r\n\r\nStart with a two-handed step-vault on a hip-height railing with the outside hand facing the opposite way in a supinated grip. \r\nOnce you are on top of the rail and the back leg is passing over the wall, twist towards the hand 180 degrees and bring both feet down onto the ground facing back the way you came.\r\nAs the feet come down, the inside hand re-grips the bar on the outside of the main hand.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n1. Grab the bar with Left-hand facing up, right hand facing down.\r\n2. Right-foot is placed into a step-vault position on the rail.\r\n3. Push up and remove the right hand off the bar.\r\n4. Bring the left foot up and over the rail.\r\n5. As the left leg is dropping on the other side, turn it back towards the left-hand.\r\n6. Turn the foot so it\'s facing back the way you came and join the second foot next to it.\r\n7. The right hand grabs the rail on the outside of the left hand.\r\n8. Both feet need to land together on the ball of the foot facing the rail.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or above.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on both sides.','Step Turn Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','step-turn-drop','','','2021-11-28 13:51:18','2021-11-28 13:51:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55463',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55464,1,'2021-11-28 14:01:03','2021-11-28 14:01:03','Demonstrate a side-vault over a hip-height railing and turn towards the rail ninety degrees so you finish towards the bar.\r\n\r\nBoth feet must remain together on the take-off and landing. \r\n\r\nFor example.\r\n1. Stand with the rail on your right side.\r\n2. Your right hand grabs the bar on the far-side.\r\n3. Your left hand grabs a little further away on the bar, but on the near-side.\r\n4. Jump up with both feet together\r\n5. pass over the railing with both of the feet together and behind you.\r\n6. Let go of the right hand and move it ahead of the left hand.\r\n7. Grab the bar with the right hand on top in a pronated grip. \r\n8. Turn ninety degrees to your left as you are landing.\r\n9. Land two-feet together facing the railing using the ball of the foot.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater.','Side Turn','','publish','closed','closed','','side-turn','','','2021-11-28 14:01:03','2021-11-28 14:01:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55464',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55465,1,'2021-11-28 14:11:24','2021-11-28 14:11:24','Very similar to level-3 side vault to turn vault, but the slight difference is that both feet need to land under the railing on the ground. You can draw a line with chalk or crayons, or use a natural curb. The curb can be within half a step away from under the rail. \r\n\r\nThis is an accuracy test to show your ability to land correctly.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate a side-vault over a hip-height railing and turn towards the rail ninety degrees so you finish towards the bar.\r\n\r\nBoth feet must remain together on the take-off and landing. \r\n\r\nYou must land under the railing with both feet together and at the same time.\r\n\r\nFor example.\r\n1. Stand with the rail on your right side.\r\n2. Your right hand grabs the bar on the far-side.\r\n3. Your left hand grabs a little further away on the bar, but on the near-side.\r\n4. Jump up with both feet together\r\n5. pass over the railing with both of the feet together and behind you.\r\n6. Let go of the right hand and move it ahead of the left hand.\r\n7. Grab the bar with the right hand on top in a pronated grip. \r\n8. Turn ninety degrees to your left as you are landing.\r\n9. Land two-feet together facing the railing using the ball of the foot.\r\n10. Land underneath the railing.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater.','Side Turn Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','side-turn-precision','','','2021-11-28 14:11:24','2021-11-28 14:11:24','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55465',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55466,1,'2021-11-28 14:19:08','2021-11-28 14:19:08','As an extension of the level-4 requirements, you must now perform the same movement but now start facing the rail, rather than in a side vault.\r\nTherefore, you will be turning 180 degrees rather than 90 degrees.\r\n\r\nAll other parts are the same as level-4.\r\n\r\nBoth feet must remain together on the take-off and landing. \r\n\r\nFor example.\r\n1. Stand with the rail in front of you.\r\n2. Your right hand grabs the bar on top in a pronated grip facing down.\r\n3. Your left hand grabs in a supinated grip facing up.\r\n4. Jump up with both feet together over the rail to your right and turn 90 degrees as you do.\r\n5. pass over the railing with both of the feet together and behind you.\r\n6. Let go of the right hand and move it around the left hand.\r\n7. Grab the bar with the right hand on top in a pronated grip next to the left hand. \r\n8. Turn another 90 degrees to your left as you are landing.\r\n9. Land two-feet together facing the railing using the ball of the foot.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater.','Turn Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','turn-vault-11','','','2021-11-28 14:19:08','2021-11-28 14:19:08','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55466',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55467,1,'2021-11-28 14:25:33','2021-11-28 14:25:33','As an extension of the level-5 requirements, you must now perform the same movement but now finish with both feet landing underneath the rail on a curb or line.\r\n\r\nAll other parts are the same as level-5.\r\n\r\nBoth feet must remain together on the take-off and landing. \r\n\r\nYou must land under the railing with both feet together and at the same time.\r\n\r\nFor example.\r\n1. Stand with the rail in front of you.\r\n2. Your right hand grabs the bar on top in a pronated grip facing down.\r\n3. Your left hand grabs in a supinated grip facing up.\r\n4. Jump up with both feet together over the rail to your right and turn 90 degrees as you do.\r\n5. pass over the railing with both of the feet together and behind you.\r\n6. Let go of the right hand and move it around the left hand.\r\n7. Grab the bar with the right hand on top in a pronated grip next to the left hand. \r\n8. Turn another 90 degrees to your left as you are landing.\r\n9. Land two-feet together facing the railing using the ball of the foot.\r\n10. Land underneath the railing.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater.','Turn Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','turn-precision','','','2021-11-28 14:25:33','2021-11-28 14:25:33','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55467',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55468,1,'2021-11-28 14:35:18','2021-11-28 14:35:18','Perform the full turn-vault with precision and with a height on the far-side greater than hip-height.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate a useful turn-vault that has a small drop on the other side of the railing.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate a level-6 turn-vault, with both feet landing perfectly on the edge of a wall that is hip-height or greater and no more than half a step away from being under the rail. (The rail must be within half a step to the edge of the wall).\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater. \r\n\r\nYou must show three repetitions on each side going in both directions.','Small Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','small-drop','','','2021-11-28 14:35:18','2021-11-28 14:35:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55468',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55469,1,'2021-11-28 14:46:41','2021-11-28 14:46:41','The set-up for this vault is slightly different than just a normal railing, this will be a railing that is mounted closely on-top of a wall. The rail should be connected to the wall with a small gap underneath so that the wall is acting as a barrier, not the rail.\r\n\r\nWith the turn-vault to cat-leap position, you need to demonstrate performing a turn vault over the wall, using the rail to grip. However, instead of landing on top of a wall, you will now be landing on the other side of the wall in a cat-leap position.\r\n\r\nYour arms must be bent, Both feet must be on the wall and in a bent-knee position. \r\n\r\nYou must hold thee cat-leap position for 5 seconds.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate the turn-vault to cat-leap in both directions.\r\n\r\nAlternatively, you can perform this on a narrow wall, without a rail if needed.','Turn to Cat','','publish','closed','closed','','turn-to-cat','','','2021-11-28 14:46:41','2021-11-28 14:46:41','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55469',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55470,1,'2021-11-28 14:53:17','2021-11-28 14:53:17','You must demonstrate a rail precision to turn vault precision. You can take-off from any obstacle, but must land on a railing from a two-footed broad jump. Land both feet on the railing and transition into a turn-vault on the far side of the railing.\r\nLand in a precision under the rail from the turn-vault.\r\n\r\nThe distance of the rail-precision must be 5-steps or greater.\r\n\r\nThis is to show that you can transition into a turn-vault from a rail precision - which is good as a bailing technique to save yourself from over-powering the jump.\r\n\r\nThe railing must be hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate doing the turn-vault in both directions.\r\n','Rail Precision Turn','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-precision-turn','','','2021-11-28 14:53:17','2021-11-28 14:53:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55470',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55471,1,'2021-11-28 15:02:26','2021-11-28 15:02:26','Perform a full turn-vault to precision landing or cat-leap position, but with a full-stretch drop on the far side from where your feet will land.\r\n\r\nThe turn-vault is usually used when there is a large gap on the far side of the rail (which is usually the barrier for the edge) - this is demonstrating you can vault the rail and land correctly on the edge with control.\r\n\r\nThe rail should be hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nYou must demonstrate three reps on each side.','At height','','publish','closed','closed','','at-height','','','2021-11-28 15:02:26','2021-11-28 15:02:26','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55471',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55472,1,'2021-11-28 15:16:30','2021-11-28 15:16:30','The slide-vault (AKA Thief-vault or Slide-monkey) is a movement that allows you to vault a wall at an angle with full momentum. \r\n\r\nThe level-1 is to demonstrate that you can hold the correct position on the floor for 10 seconds.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Sit on the ground, both legs straight out in front of you.\r\n2. You right leg will bend 45 degrees and you place it tucked underneath your left leg.\r\n3. Both hands are place on the ground behind you with the fingers pointing outwards (thumbs pointing towards each other)\r\n4. Your chest must be facing forwards towards your left foot and your hips must be square on facing your foot. (No twisting on one side)\r\n5. Keep both legs in the same position, but now lift them both up using your abs. \r\n6. Both legs must remain off the ground for 10 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat on the opposite side with the left-leg tucked under and the right-leg straight.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side holding for 10seconds each.','Ground Position','','publish','closed','closed','','ground-position','','','2021-11-28 15:16:30','2021-11-28 15:16:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55472',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55473,1,'2021-11-28 15:39:59','2021-11-28 15:39:59','Perform a standard step-vault with either one or two hands and one foot. However, you must drop the foot on top of the wall onto the ground before the normal foot lands on the floor.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Place left hand and right foot onto the hip-height wall. \r\n2. Lift hips up and raise left leg up over the wall, but do not drop it onto the far side.\r\n3. As the leg passes the wall, hop the right foot off the wall and land with that foot instead.\r\n4. You can place the right hand on the wall behind you to correctly position you as you drop.\r\n5. Do not twist off the wall. You must finish facing the same direction you started.\r\n\r\nYou must perform this on a hip-height wall or greater. The wall width must be of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on both sides.\r\n','Step Irregular','','publish','closed','closed','','step-irregular','','','2021-11-28 15:39:59','2021-11-28 15:39:59','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55473',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55474,1,'2021-11-28 15:55:17','2021-11-28 15:55:17','Perform a slide-vault into a seated position and then off the other side.\r\n\r\nUse a hip-height or greater block or obstacle that you can sit on and also vault off the far-side. A bench or wide wall for example.\r\n\r\nStart with the obstacle to your side and swing the *outside* leg up onto the wall (as if doing a step vault from a twisted position).\r\n\r\nSit down on your bum facing along the wall. Tuck the opposite leg underneath the first leg, similar to the ground position in level-1.\r\n\r\nPlace both hands behind you and make sure your hips are facing down the wall. \r\n\r\nHold the position for 5 seconds.\r\n\r\nNow rock the first leg off the wall and land on the ground with that foot, keeping the other one tucked behind and bent.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n\r\nThe wall will be easier if it\'s wider to sit on, however it can be a normal width if required.','Wide Seated','','publish','closed','closed','','wide-seated','','','2021-11-28 15:55:17','2021-11-28 15:55:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55474',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55475,1,'2021-11-28 16:02:56','2021-11-28 16:02:56','Perform a full slide-vault over a hip-height wall without making contact other than your hands behind you.\r\n\r\nA standard brick-length width wall or less.\r\n\r\nStart with the obstacle to your side and swing the *outside* leg up over the wall (as if doing a step vault from a twisted position).\r\n\r\nTuck the opposite leg underneath the first leg, similar to the ground position in level-1.\r\n\r\nPlace both hands behind you and make sure your hips are facing down the wall. \r\n\r\nKeep sweeping the first leg over the wall and down on the opposite side with that foot first.\r\n\r\nKeep the back leg tucked behind you until you\'ve landed the first foot.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n','Slide Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','slide-vault','','','2021-11-28 16:02:56','2021-11-28 16:02:56','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55475',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55476,1,'2021-11-28 17:50:50','2021-11-28 17:50:50','Perform a full slide-vault over a hip-height rail without making contact other than your hands behind you.\r\n\r\nA standard scaffolding-rail width bar diameter or less. A flat railing is acceptable.\r\n\r\nStart with the obstacle to your side and swing the *outside* leg up over the wall (as if doing a step vault from a twisted position).\r\n\r\nTuck the opposite leg underneath the first leg, similar to the ground position in level-1.\r\n\r\nPlace both hands behind you and make sure your hips are facing down the wall. The second hand will need to grab the rail without seeing the bar.\r\n\r\nKeep sweeping the first leg over the rail and down on the opposite side with that foot first.\r\n\r\nKeep the back leg tucked behind you until you\'ve landed the first foot.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n','Rail Slide Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-slide-vault','','','2021-11-28 17:50:50','2021-11-28 17:50:50','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55476',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55477,1,'2021-11-28 18:02:23','2021-11-28 18:02:23','Perform two slide-vaults on the same hip-height wall with a maximum of three steps on the ground between the two.\r\n\r\nYou must keep momentum going and there must not be any pausing or stopping. Minimal fluctuations in speed.\r\n\r\nThis is the same as standard level-4 slide-vault, but joining multiple together in a flow.\r\n\r\nThe second vault can be on thee same side or switching to use the opposite hand and foot.\r\n\r\nYou must perform both movements three times and then do the complete opposite another three times. You should demonstrate a total of 12 vaults (6 on the left, 6 on the right). ','Two in a Row','','publish','closed','closed','','two-in-a-row-2','','','2021-11-28 18:02:23','2021-11-28 18:02:23','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55477',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55478,1,'2021-11-28 18:09:50','2021-11-28 18:09:50','Slide-vault over a hip-height obstacle (wall, block or rail) at a constant speed, with no slowing down or pausing. You must be going at a jogging-pace or faster.\r\n\r\nThe must also be a greater drop on the far slide of the vault that is hip-height or more to the bas of the obstacle. (Effectively making the height of the floor to the top of the obstacle twice hip-height.\r\n\r\nLand on one foot and still run out of the vault at a continuous speed.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on each side.','Slide Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','slide-drop','','','2021-11-28 18:09:50','2021-11-28 18:09:50','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55478',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55479,1,'2021-11-28 18:17:04','2021-11-28 18:17:04','Demonstrate a slide-vault over two separate hip-height walls with a maximum of two steps between each. \r\n\r\nThere must be a consistent speed between both vaults and no pausing or stopping.\r\n\r\nYou can perform the second vault on the same side or opposite side. However, you must demonstrate 3 reps of each double vault on each side. Therefore you must demo a maximum of twelve vaults.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n1. 3x Left-leg over first on first vault. Right leg over first on the second vault.\r\n2. 3x Right leg over first on the first vault. Left-leg over first on second vault.','Two Walls','','publish','closed','closed','','two-walls','','','2021-11-28 18:17:04','2021-11-28 18:17:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55479',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55480,1,'2021-11-28 18:20:07','2021-11-28 18:20:07','Slide-vault over a chest-height or higher obstacle. The obstacle can be a wall, block or rail of any width.\n\nYou can approach with speed or perform a standing slide-vault. Nothing but your hands must make contact with the obstacle.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.','High Slide','','publish','closed','closed','','high-slide','','','2021-11-28 18:20:07','2021-11-28 18:20:07','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55480',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55481,1,'2021-11-28 18:27:45','2021-11-28 18:27:45','Perform four slide-vaults in a row on the same hip-height or higher wall. You must land on one leg and use a plyometric to redirect back out to the second slide-vault. No more than one foot should land after each vault.\r\n\r\nRepeat the four vaults (two left, two right) three times.\r\n\r\nYou must have momentum the entire time and there should not be any pausing or stopping.\r\n\r\nEach vault should be redirected to angle back towards the same wall, rather than away from it.\r\n\r\nYou should be travelling down the wall in a zig-zag pattern, switching from left to right side.','Multiple Flow','','publish','closed','closed','','multiple-flow','','','2021-11-28 18:27:45','2021-11-28 18:27:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55481',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55482,1,'2021-11-29 12:45:06','2021-11-29 12:45:06','Demonstrate a lazy-vault around the end of a hip-height railing or wall. This is to show that you have the correct movement mechanics without including the height.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start with the rail to your left side and face the end of the rail.\r\n2. Grab the rail with your left hand in a pronated grip (facing down)\r\n3. Swing the left leg around the rail (no need to go over the rail).\r\n4. The right leg will follow around the rail.\r\n5. Turn around the end 180 degrees and facing the opposite direction to the start.\r\n6. Land on the left foot first.\r\n\r\nYou must show three repetitions in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater.','Lazy Around','','publish','closed','closed','','lazy-around','','','2021-11-29 12:45:06','2021-11-29 12:45:06','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55482',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55483,1,'2021-11-29 12:53:58','2021-11-29 12:53:58','Demonstrate a lazy-vault over a hip-height railing, landing on the balls of the foot with two feet at the same time. \r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start with the rail to your left side.\r\n2. Grab the rail with your left hand in a pronated grip (facing down)\r\n3. Swing the left leg over the rail.\r\n4. The right leg will follow around the rail.\r\n5. Turn around the end 180 degrees and facing the opposite direction to the start.\r\n6. Land on both feet at the same time on the other side.\r\n\r\nYou must show three repetitions in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\r\n\r\nYou must land two feet at the same time.','Lazy Rail Stop','','publish','closed','closed','','lazy-rail-stop','','','2021-11-29 12:53:58','2021-11-29 12:53:58','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55483',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55484,1,'2021-11-29 12:59:27','2021-11-29 12:59:27','Demonstrate a lazy-vault over a hip-height railing, landing on the first foot and move out of the vault at jogging pace.\r\n\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start with the rail to your left side.\r\n2. Grab the rail with your left hand in a pronated grip (facing down)\r\n3. Swing the left leg over the rail.\r\n4. The right leg will follow around the rail.\r\n5. Turn around 180 degrees and facing the opposite direction to the start.\r\n6. Land on the left foot and step the right foot further forward and keep moving in that direction.\r\n\r\nYou must show three repetitions in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\r\n\r\nYou must land one foot first and continue at jogging pace.','Lazy Rail Run','','publish','closed','closed','','lazy-rail-run','','','2021-11-29 12:59:27','2021-11-29 12:59:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55484',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55485,1,'2021-11-29 13:04:40','2021-11-29 13:04:40','Demonstrate a lazy-vault over a hip-height wall that is of standard brick-length or less, turning 180 degrees.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start with the wall to your left side.\r\n2. Place your left hand flat facing down on the wall using the palm.\r\n3. Swing the left leg over the wall.\r\n4. The right leg will follow around the wall.\r\n5. Turn around 180 degrees and facing the opposite direction to the start.\r\n6. Land on both feet at the same time on the other side of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must show three repetitions in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\r\n\r\nYou must land two feet at the same time.','Wall Lazy','','publish','closed','closed','','wall-lazy','','','2021-11-29 13:04:40','2021-11-29 13:04:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55485',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55486,1,'2021-11-29 13:11:10','2021-11-29 13:11:10','Demonstrate a lazy-vault over a hip-height wall that is of standard brick-length or less, turning on 90 degrees so that you end facing away from the wall.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start with the wall to your left side.\r\n2. Place your left hand flat facing down on the wall using the palm.\r\n3. Swing the left leg over the wall.\r\n4. The right leg will follow around the wall.\r\n5. Turn around 90 degrees and face away from the wall.\r\n6. Place your right hand on the wall behind you as you pass over the wall for stability.\r\n7. Land on the left foot first, followed by the right.\r\n\r\nYou must show three repetitions in each direction.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and of standard brick-length or less.\r\n\r\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\r\n\r\nYou must land on one foot at a time and both hands must contact the wall.','Half Turn Lazy','','publish','closed','closed','','half-turn-lazy','','','2021-11-29 13:11:10','2021-11-29 13:11:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55486',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55487,1,'2021-11-29 13:18:44','2021-11-29 13:18:44','Demonstrate a complete lazy-vault over a hip-height wall that is of standard brick-length or less with no turning or twisting. You can enter and exit the vault at an angle, but you must be travelling in a straight line.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start with the wall to your left side.\n2. Place your left hand flat facing down on the wall using the palm.\n3. Swing the left leg over the wall.\n4. The right leg will follow around the wall.\n5. Push forwards and keep your hips facing the same direction.\n6. Place your right hand on the wall behind you as you pass over the wall for stability.\n7. Any landing is acceptable.\n\nYou must show three repetitions in each direction.\n\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and of standard brick-length or less.\n\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\n\nYou must travel in a straight line with both hands landing on the wall.','Full Lazy','','publish','closed','closed','','full-lazy','','','2021-11-29 13:18:44','2021-11-29 13:18:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55487',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55488,1,'2021-11-29 13:23:30','2021-11-29 13:23:30','Demonstrate a complete lazy-vault over a hip-height railing that is standard scaffolding-bar width or less with no turning or twisting. You can enter and exit the vault at an angle, but you must be travelling in a straight line.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start with the rail to your left side.\n2. Place your left hand on the rail facing down (pronated grip).\n3. Swing the left leg over the rail.\n4. The right leg will follow around the rail.\n5. Push forwards and keep your hips facing the same direction.\n6. Place your right hand on the rail behind you as you pass over the rail for stability.\n7. Any landing is acceptable.\n\nYou must show three repetitions in each direction.\n\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater and of standard scaffolding bar width or less.\n\nYou must travel in a straight line with both hands landing on the rail.\n\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\n\nAny landing is acceptable.','Full Lazy Rail','','publish','closed','closed','','full-lazy-rail','','','2021-11-29 13:23:30','2021-11-29 13:23:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55488',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55489,1,'2021-11-29 13:34:01','2021-11-29 13:34:01','Demonstrate a complete lazy-vault over a chest-height railing or wall that is standard brick-length width or less. You can enter and exit the vault at an angle, and can finish out of the vault at any angle.\n\nThis is to demonstrate you can lazy vault over higher obstacles.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start with the rail to your left side.\n2. Place your left hand on the rail facing down (pronated grip).\n3. Swing the left leg over the rail.\n4. The right leg will follow around the rail.\n5. Push forwards and keep your hips facing the same direction.\n6. Place your right hand on the rail behind you as you pass over the rail for stability.\n7. Any landing is acceptable.\n\nYou must show three repetitions in each direction.\n\nThe rail/wall must be chest-height or greater and of standard brick-length width or less.\n\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\n\nAny landing is acceptable.','Lazy High','','publish','closed','closed','','lazy-high','','','2021-11-29 13:34:01','2021-11-29 13:34:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55489',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55490,1,'2021-11-29 13:42:49','2021-11-29 13:42:49','Demonstrate a complete lazy-vault over a hip-height wall that is standard brick-length width or less. After you land, you can take two steps or less before you must perform a second lazy-vault over the same wall while maintaining momentum.\n\nThis is to demonstrate you can lazy vault over the same wall multiple times while keeping momentum.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start with the wall to your left side.\n2. Place your left hand on the wall facing down (pronated grip).\n3. Swing the left leg over the wall.\n4. The right leg will follow around the wall.\n5. Push forwards and keep your hips facing the same direction.\n6. Place your right hand on the wall behind you as you pass over the wall for stability.\n7. Land on your left foot and step forwards with the right foot.\n8. Once on thee right foot, step again to the left foot.\n9. Swing the right leg into the second lazy vault and perform the entire movement again back over the wall.\n\nYou must show three repetitions of the two vaults in each direction. (12 Vaults in total)\n\nThe wall must be chest-height or greater and of standard brick-length width or less.\n\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\n\nYou must land on the same foot that is doing the take-off.\n\nYou must not stop or pause - keep momentum while demonstrating both vaults.','Double Lazy','','publish','closed','closed','','double-lazy','','','2021-11-29 13:42:49','2021-11-29 13:42:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55490',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55491,1,'2021-11-29 13:54:18','2021-11-29 13:54:18','Demonstrate a three lazy-vaults over three separate hip-height walls/rails that are of standard brick-length or less with no turning or twisting while performing the vault. You can enter and exit each vault at an angle, but you must be travelling in a straight line when passing the obstacle.\n\nYou can only take a maximum of three steps between each lazy-vault and must maintain a consistent jogging-pace or faster.\n\nThe vaults must alternate from one side to the other and back again. So left-right-left vaults or right-left-right vaults.\n\nThis is to demonstrate you can lazy-vault over multiple obstacles while keeping momentum and changing sides.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start with the wall to your left side.\n2. Place your left hand on the wall facing down (pronated grip).\n3. Swing the left leg over the wall.\n4. The right leg will follow around the wall.\n5. Push forwards and keep your hips facing the same direction.\n6. Place your right hand on the wall behind you as you pass over the wall for stability.\n7. Land on your left foot and step forwards with the right foot towards the next wall.\n8. Once on the right foot, step again to the left foot.\n9. Swing the right leg into the second lazy vault and perform the entire movement again over the second wall.\n10. Repeat the process onto the third wall.\n\nYou must show two repetitions of all three vaults in each direction. (12 Vaults in total)\n\nThe walls/rails must be hip-height or greater and of standard brick-length width or less.\n\nThe inside leg must takeoff first.\n\nYou must land on the same foot that is doing the take-off.\n\nYou must not stop or pause - keep momentum while demonstrating both vaults, with a maximum of three steps between each vault.','Lazy Chain','','publish','closed','closed','','lazy-chain','','','2021-11-29 13:54:18','2021-11-29 13:54:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55491',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55492,1,'2021-11-29 14:03:19','2021-11-29 14:03:19','Demonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height block / obstacle or higher. Land a single foot on the outside of the hands.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n\r\n1. Take two steps towards a chest-height block.\r\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall and use it to lever your body higher.\r\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\r\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring your right foot up on top of the wall on the outside of the hands.\r\n\r\nYou must perform three times on both sides. You must alternate the kicking foot and the foot on top of the wall. Do not kick the wall with one foot and place the same foot on top of the wall.\r\n\r\n','Pop Foot Outside','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-foot-outside','','','2021-11-29 14:03:19','2021-11-29 14:03:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55492',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55493,1,'2021-11-29 14:35:30','2021-11-29 14:35:30','Demonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height block / obstacle or higher. Land a single foot on the inside of both hands.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n\r\n1. Take two steps towards a chest-height block.\r\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall and use it to lever your body higher.\r\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\r\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring your right foot up on top of the wall in between your arms and hands.\r\n\r\nYou must perform three times on both sides. You must alternate the kicking foot and the foot on top of the wall. Do not kick the wall with one foot and place the same foot on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must land the foot on top of the wall in between both hands without twisting or removing one of the hands.\r\n\r\nYou can take any number of steps into the kick.\r\n\r\n','Pop Foot Inside','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-foot-inside','','','2021-11-29 14:35:30','2021-11-29 14:35:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55493',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55494,1,'2021-11-29 15:02:47','2021-11-29 15:02:47','Demonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height block / obstacle or higher. Land both feet on the inside of both hands.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n\r\n1. Take two steps towards a chest-height block.\r\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall using the ball-of-the-foot and use it to lever your body higher.\r\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\r\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring both feet up on top of the wall in between your arms and hands.\r\n5. As your feet are tucked underneath you, remove your hands off the wall to generate more space for your knees. Push with your hands gain more area.\r\n\r\nYou must perform three times on each foot kicking the wall. \r\n\r\nYou must land both feet on top of the wall in between both hands without twisting or removing one of the hands.\r\n\r\nYou can take any number of steps into the kick.','Pop Both Feet','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-both-feet','','','2021-11-29 15:02:47','2021-11-29 15:02:47','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55494',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55495,1,'2021-11-29 15:07:28','2021-11-29 15:07:28','Demonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height or higher wall that is brick-length width or less. Land both feet on the inside of both hands.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate that you can pop onto a wall with a drop on the other side of the wall.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n\r\n1. Take two steps towards a chest-height wall.\r\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall using the ball-of-the-foot and use it to lever your body higher.\r\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\r\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring both feet up on top of the wall in between your arms and hands.\r\n5. As your feet are tucked underneath you, remove your hands off the wall to generate more space for your knees. Push with your hands gain more area.\r\n\r\nYou must perform three times on each foot kicking the wall. \r\n\r\nYou must land both feet on top of the wall in between both hands without twisting or removing one of the hands.\r\n\r\nYou can take any number of steps into the kick.','Pop Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-wall','','','2021-11-29 15:07:28','2021-11-29 15:07:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55495',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55496,1,'2021-11-29 15:15:34','2021-11-29 15:15:34','Demonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height or higher wall that is brick-length width or less. Land with one foot on the outside of the hands. You can pause and stop before you remove the inside hand and move into a step-vault off the wall. No consistent speed is required.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate that you can pop onto a wall with a drop on the other side and move into a step-vault. \r\n\r\nExample.\r\n\r\n1. Take two steps towards a chest-height wall.\r\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall using the ball-of-the-foot and use it to lever your body higher.\r\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\r\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring the right foot up on top of the wall on the outside of your right hand.\r\n5. Remove the right hand off the wall as the foot lands.\r\n6. Bring the left leg up and underneath you, passing over the wall to perform a step-vault.\r\n7. Any landing is acceptable.\r\n\r\nYou must perform three times on each side. \r\n\r\nYou must land one foot on top of the wall, on the outside of the hands, then move into the step vault position, finishing on the opposite side. \r\n\r\nYou can take any number of steps into the kick.','Pop Step','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-step','','','2021-11-29 15:15:34','2021-11-29 15:15:34','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55496',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55497,1,'2021-11-29 15:27:05','2021-11-29 15:27:05','Demonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height or higher wall that is brick-length width or less. Land with one foot on the outside of the hands and move directly into the step-vault with no pausing or stopping. Momentum and consistent speed are required.\n\nThis is to demonstrate that you can pop onto a wall with a drop on the other side and move into a step-vault without loosing momentum or speed.\n\nExample.\n\n1. Take two steps towards a chest-height wall.\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall using the ball-of-the-foot and use it to lever your body higher.\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring the right foot up on top of the wall on the outside of your right hand.\n5. Remove the right hand off the wall as the foot lands.\n6. Bring the left leg up and underneath you, passing over the wall to perform a step-vault.\n7. Any landing is acceptable.\n\nYou must perform three times on each side. \n\nYou must land one foot on top of the wall, on the outside of the hands, then move into the step vault position, finishing on the opposite side without loosing pace.\n\nYou can take any number of steps into the kick.\n\nYou must be going at a jogging-pace or faster.','Pop Step Over','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-step-over','','','2021-11-29 15:27:05','2021-11-29 15:27:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55497',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55498,1,'2021-11-29 15:33:11','2021-11-29 15:33:11','The traditional understanding of the pop-vault was to **not** land any feet on top of the wall. Perform the pop-vault to slide-vault or lazy-vault with only the hands touching the top of the wall.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height or higher wall that is brick-length width or less. Land with no feet on top of the wall and move directly into the lazy-vault or slide-vault with no pausing or stopping. Momentum and consistent speed are required.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate that you can pop onto a wall with a drop on the other side and move into a lazy-vault or slide-vault without loosing momentum or speed.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n\r\n1. Take two steps towards a chest-height wall.\r\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall using the ball-of-the-foot and use it to lever your body higher.\r\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\r\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring the right foot up and swing it over the top of the wall.\r\n5. Remove the right hand from the wall as the leg passes over the wall.\r\n6. Bring the left leg up, bend the knee and tuck it underneath you, passing over the wall to perform a slide-vault.\r\n7. Place the right hand behind you onto the wall again to re-orientate your hips and control the drop.\r\n7. Any landing is acceptable.\r\n\r\nYou must perform three times on each side. \r\n\r\nYou can take any number of steps into the kick.\r\n\r\nYou must be going at a jogging-pace or faster.\r\n\r\nOnly the hands must touch the top of the wall.','Pop Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-vault','','','2021-11-29 15:33:11','2021-11-29 15:33:11','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55498',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55499,1,'2021-11-29 15:38:19','2021-11-29 15:38:19','The traditional understanding of the pop-vault was to **not** land any feet on top of the wall. Perform the pop-vault to cat-pass with only the hands touching the top of the wall.\n\nDemonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height or higher wall that is brick-length width or less. Land with no feet on top of the wall and move directly into the cat-pass with no pausing or stopping. Momentum and consistent speed are required.\n\nThis is to demonstrate that you can pop onto a wall with a drop on the other side and move into a cat-pass without loosing momentum or speed.\n\nExample.\n\n1. Take two steps towards a chest-height wall.\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall using the ball-of-the-foot and use it to lever your body higher.\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring both knees up underneath you and lift your hips up.\n5. Lean you head forwards and push into the cat-pass position.\n6. Any landing is acceptable.\n\nYou must perform three times kicking the wall on each foot. \n\nYou can take any number of steps into the kick.\n\nYou must be going at a jogging-pace or faster.\n\nOnly the hands must touch the top of the wall.','Pop Cat-Pass','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-cat-pass','','','2021-11-29 15:38:19','2021-11-29 15:38:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55499',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55500,1,'2021-11-29 15:43:54','2021-11-29 15:43:54','The traditional understanding of the pop-vault was to **not** land any feet on top of the wall. Perform the pop-vault to cat-pass with only the hands touching the top of the wall with the additional parameter of only having two-steps run-up into the pop-vault.\n\nDemonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height or higher wall that is brick-length width or less. Land with no feet on top of the wall and move directly into the cat-pass with no pausing or stopping. Momentum from two-steps only and consistent speed are required.\n\nThis is to demonstrate that you can generate enough momentum with only two-steps to pop-vault into a cat-pass.\n\nExample.\n\n1. Take **two steps only** towards a chest-height wall.\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall using the ball-of-the-foot and use it to lever your body higher.\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring both knees up underneath you and lift your hips up.\n5. Lean you head forwards and push into the cat-pass position.\n6. Any landing is acceptable.\n\nYou must perform three times kicking the wall on each foot. \n\nYou can take only two steps into the kick, starting from a standing position.\n\nOnly the hands must touch the top of the wall.','Restricted Pop','','publish','closed','closed','','restricted-pop','','','2021-11-29 15:43:54','2021-11-29 15:43:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55500',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55501,1,'2021-11-29 15:55:08','2021-11-29 15:55:08','The traditional understanding of the pop-vault was to **not** land any feet on top of the wall. Perform the pop-vault to cat-pass with only the hands touching the top of the wall. You must land in a precision on an ankle-high or greater second obstacle or wall.\n\nDemonstrate a pop-vault on a chest-height or higher wall that is brick-length width or less. Land with no feet on top of the wall and move directly into the cat-pass with no pausing or stopping.\n\nLand in a precision on a second wall or obstacle that is ankle-high or greater. This can be a permanent structure (curb / wall / block) or artificial (Using a brick, wood, etc...)\n\nYou must land with both feet on the ball of the foot on the edge of the precision target and stick the landing. No movement after the landing from the feet and you must hold the position until you are in-balance and in control.\n\nThis is to demonstrate that you can control the landing out of the pop-vault cat-pass onto an accurate target.\n\nExample.\n\n1. Move towards a chest-height wall.\n2. Kick the left foot onto the side of the wall using the ball-of-the-foot and use it to lever your body higher.\n3. As you kick the foot on the wall, place both hands on top of the wall.\n4. Straighten your arms as you bring both knees up underneath you and lift your hips up.\n5. Lean you head forwards and push into the cat-pass position.\n6. Drive both feet forward together and land on a target obstacle / wall / rail / curb.\n7. Land on the balls-of-the-foot and stick the landing.\n\nYou must perform three times kicking the wall on each foot. \n\nOnly the hands must touch the top of the wall.\n\nYou must stick the landing on the balls of the feet until you are in control of your balance.','Pop Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','pop-precision','','','2021-11-29 15:55:08','2021-11-29 15:55:08','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55501',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55502,1,'2021-11-30 15:50:45','2021-11-30 15:50:45','Demonstrate the first stage of a speed vault from a standing position, up on top of a hip-height block or wide wall. \r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start standing in front of the wall and place your right hand on top, palm down and flat.\r\n2. Jump up, leaning onto the right arm.\r\n3. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\r\n4. Land your right foot onto the wall only to the left of the hand.\r\n5. The left foot should stay off the wall and stay elevated.\r\n\r\nYou must perform this three times on each side.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater.','Standing Speed','','publish','closed','closed','','standing-speed','','','2021-11-30 15:50:45','2021-11-30 15:50:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55502',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55503,1,'2021-11-30 15:58:01','2021-11-30 15:58:01','Demonstrate a full speed vault over a railing or bar no wider than a standard scaffolding bar. The rail must be hip-height or greater.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start standing in front of the rail and place your right hand on top, palm down facing forwards.\n2. Jump up, leaning onto the right arm.\n3. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\n4. Pass both feet over the railing to the left of your right hand.\n5. Land on both feet the other side of the railing.\n6. You can be orientated at any angle.\n\nYou must perform this three times on each side.\n\nRail can be flat or circular.','Standing Rail','','publish','closed','closed','','standing-rail','','','2021-11-30 15:58:01','2021-11-30 15:58:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55503',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55504,1,'2021-11-30 16:07:00','2021-11-30 16:07:00','Demonstrate a standing full speed vault over a knee-high wall or greater that is a standard brick-length wide or less, landing on both feet. \n\nExample:\n\n1. Start standing in front of the wall and place your right hand on top, palm down facing forwards.\n2. Jump up, leaning onto the right arm.\n3. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\n4. Pass both feet over the wall to the left of your right hand.\n5. Land on both feet the other side of the wall.\n6. You can be orientated at any angle.\n\nYou must perform this three times on each side.\n\nHand must be flat and no steepled fingers.\n\nNo feet should touch the top of the wall.\n','Low Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','low-wall-2','','','2021-11-30 16:07:00','2021-11-30 16:07:00','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55504',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55505,1,'2021-11-30 16:10:59','2021-11-30 16:10:59','Demonstrate a standing speed vault over a hip-high wall or greater that is a standard brick-length wide or less, landing on both feet. Use both hands to generate more pulling momentum to get over the wall.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start standing in front of the wall and place both hands on top, palm down facing forwards.\r\n2. Jump up, leaning onto the right arm.\r\n3. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\r\n4. Remove the left hand off the wall.\r\n5. Pass both feet over the wall to the left of your right hand.\r\n6. Land on both feet the other side of the wall.\r\n7. You can be orientated at any angle.\r\n\r\nYou must perform this three times on each side.\r\n\r\nHands must be flat and no steepled fingers.\r\n\r\nNo feet should touch the top of the wall.\r\n\r\nWall must be hip-height or greater.','Two Handed Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','two-handed-wall','','','2021-11-30 16:10:59','2021-11-30 16:10:59','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55505',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55506,1,'2021-11-30 16:14:51','2021-11-30 16:14:51','Demonstrate a standing speed vault over a hip-high wall or greater that is a standard brick-length wide or less, landing on the inside foot. Use both hands to generate more pulling momentum to get over the wall.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start standing in front of the wall and place both hands on top, palm down facing forwards.\n2. Jump up, leaning onto the right arm.\n3. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\n4. Remove the left hand off the wall.\n5. Pass both feet over the wall to the left of your right hand.\n6. Land on the **right foot** only the other side of the wall.\n7. You can be orientated at any angle.\n\nYou must perform this three times on each side.\n\nHands must be flat and no steepled fingers.\n\nNo feet should touch the top of the wall.\n\nWall must be hip-height or greater.\n','One Foot Landing','','publish','closed','closed','','one-foot-landing','','','2021-11-30 16:14:51','2021-11-30 16:14:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55506',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55507,1,'2021-11-30 16:22:06','2021-11-30 16:22:06','Demonstrate a moving speed-vault over a hip-high wall or greater that is a standard brick-length wide or less, landing on the inside foot. Use a single hand on the wall. Perform with momentum.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start two steps away from the wall and take those two steps in, with the right foot ahead.\r\n2. Place the right hand on top, palm down facing forwards.\r\n3. Jump up, leaning onto the right arm.\r\n4. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\r\n5. Remove the left hand off the wall.\r\n6. Pass both feet over the wall to the left of your right hand.\r\n7. Land on the **right foot** only the other side of the wall.\r\n8. You should be orientated straight ahead.\r\n9. Step to the left foot with momentum and jog out.\r\n\r\nYou must perform this three times on each side.\r\n\r\nHands must be flat and no steepled fingers.\r\n\r\nNo feet should touch the top of the wall.\r\n\r\nWall must be hip-height or greater and no wider than a standard brick-length.','Standard Speed','','publish','closed','closed','','standard-speed','','','2021-11-30 16:22:06','2021-11-30 16:22:06','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55507',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55508,1,'2021-11-30 16:31:06','2021-11-30 16:31:06','Demonstrate a full running speed-vault over a hip-high wall or greater that is a standard brick-length wide or less, landing on the inside foot. Use a single hand on the wall. Perform with a running momentum.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start 10 paces away from the wall and take that run-in, \n2. The right foot should be the last step towards the wall.\n3. Place the right hand on top, palm down facing forwards.\n4. Jump up off the right foot, leaning onto the right arm.\n5. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\n6. Remove the left hand off the wall.\n7. Pass both feet over the wall to the left of your right hand.\n8. Land on the **right foot** only on the other side of the wall.\n9. You should be orientated straight ahead.\n10. Step to the left foot with momentum and continue to run out.\n\nThis should be performed at a running-speed or faster to demonstrate that you can perform the speed-vault as intended.\n\nYou must perform this three times on each side.\n\nHands must be flat and no steepled fingers.\n\nNo feet should touch the top of the wall.\n\nWall must be hip-height or greater and no wider than a standard brick-length.','Full Speed','','publish','closed','closed','','full-speed','','','2021-11-30 16:31:06','2021-11-30 16:31:06','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55508',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55509,1,'2021-11-30 16:37:54','2021-11-30 16:37:54','Demonstrate two moving speed-vaults in a row over two hip-high walls or greater that are a standard brick-length wide or less, landing on the inside foot. Use a single hand on the wall. Perform with momentum and a maximum of three paces in-between.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start 10 paces away from the wall and take that run-in, \n2. The right foot should be the last step towards the wall.\n3. Place the right hand on top, palm down facing forwards.\n4. Jump up off the right foot, leaning onto the right arm.\n5. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\n6. Remove the left hand off the wall.\n7. Pass both feet over the wall to the left of your right hand.\n8. Land on the **right foot** only on the other side of the wall.\n9. You should be orientated straight ahead.\n10. Take the second step to the left foot with momentum, followed by the third right foot.\n11. Perform the same speed-vault a second time over the second wall.\n\nWalls must be three paces apart. \n\nThis should be demonstrated at jogging-pace or faster.\n\nYou must perform the double vault three times on each side. (12 vaults in total).\n\nHands must be flat and no steepled fingers.\n\nNo feet should touch the top of the wall.\n\nWall must be hip-height or greater and no wider than a standard brick-length.','Two Walls','','publish','closed','closed','','two-walls-2','','','2021-11-30 16:37:54','2021-11-30 16:37:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55509',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55510,1,'2021-11-30 16:44:37','2021-11-30 16:44:37','During the standard speed-vault, you must lead with the wrong (outside) leg and switch in mid-air so that the correct (inside leg) is leading the landing.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate a moving speed-vault over a hip-high wall or greater that is a standard brick-length wide or less, landing on the inside foot. Use a single hand on the wall. Perform with a jogging momentum.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start 10 paces away from the wall and take that run-in, \r\n2. The right foot should be the last step towards the wall.\r\n3. Place the right hand on top, palm down facing forwards.\r\n4. Jump up off the right foot, leaning onto the right arm.\r\n5. Bring your hips up high and bring your knees up to your chest.\r\n6. Extend your left leg ahead of your body in mid-air.\r\n7. Kick your right leg forward and the left leg back (in a scissor action)\r\n8. Pass both feet over the wall to the left of your right hand.\r\n9. Land on the **right foot** only on the other side of the wall.\r\n10. You should be orientated straight ahead.\r\n11. Step to the left foot with momentum and continue to jog out.\r\n\r\nThis is to demonstrate an off-leg switch that is more of an aesthetic movement rather than a functional one.\r\n\r\nYou must perform this three times on each side.\r\n\r\nHands must be flat and no steepled fingers.\r\n\r\nNo feet should touch the top of the wall.\r\n\r\nWall must be hip-height or greater and no wider than a standard brick-length.','Scissor Kick','','publish','closed','closed','','scissor-kick','','','2021-11-30 16:44:37','2021-11-30 16:44:37','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55510',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55511,1,'2021-11-30 16:51:03','2021-11-30 16:51:03','Perform three speed-vaults on three railings that are a maximum of three paces apart. Rails must be hip-height or greater.\n\nYou must be going at running-speed or faster.\n\nRails must be scaffolding-width or less.\n\nThere should be no reduction of speed across the three railings / bars with no fluctuations in momentum.\n\nNo pausing or stopping.\n\nYou can use the same railing multiple times as long as the flow of the movement is not reduced.','Triple Flow','','publish','closed','closed','','triple-flow','','','2021-11-30 16:51:03','2021-11-30 16:51:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55511',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55512,1,'2021-12-01 15:34:55','2021-12-01 15:34:55','Demonstrate the exit of a dash-vault from a seated position on a chest-height or greater block / wall / obstacle.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start sitting on the edge of a block with your legs hanging off the edge.\r\n2. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you.\r\n3. Lift your hips up and push forwards off the wall.\r\n4. Extend legs forwards and land on both feet.\r\n\r\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands.\r\n\r\nYou must land on both feet at the same time.\r\n\r\nThe block must be chest-height or greater.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Push Off','','publish','closed','closed','','push-off','','','2021-12-01 15:34:55','2021-12-01 15:34:55','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55512',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55513,1,'2021-12-01 15:39:04','2021-12-01 15:39:04','Demonstrate the exit of a dash-vault from a crouched position on a chest-height or greater block / wall / obstacle.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start crouching on the edge of a block with your legs hanging off the edge.\r\n2. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you.\r\n3. Lift your hips up, as well as your knees in-front, and push forwards off the wall.\r\n4. Extend legs forwards and land on both feet.\r\n\r\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\r\n\r\nYou must land on both feet at the same time.\r\n\r\nThe block must be chest-height or greater.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Crouched Push','','publish','closed','closed','','crouched-push','','','2021-12-01 15:39:04','2021-12-01 15:39:04','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55513',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55514,1,'2021-12-01 15:46:18','2021-12-01 15:46:18','Demonstrate the entry of the dash-vault with a box-jump onto a hip-height or greater wall. Followed by an exit of a dash-vault from a crouched position that you landed in. \r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start standing in-front of the hip-height or greater wall.\r\n2. Box-jump onto the wall, aiming towards the back edge.\r\n3. Land both feet and in the crouched position.\r\n4. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you, slightly behind you.\r\n5. Lift your hips up, as well as your knees in-front, and push forwards off the wall.\r\n6. Extend legs forwards and land on both feet.\r\n\r\nThe box-jump can have momentum from additional steps and does not need to be a strict two-foot box-jump.\r\n\r\nYou must not move the feet or shuffle your position once on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\r\n\r\nYou must land on both feet at the same time.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and can be any width.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Box Push','','publish','closed','closed','','box-push','','','2021-12-01 15:46:18','2021-12-01 15:46:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55514',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55515,1,'2021-12-01 15:49:27','2021-12-01 15:49:27','Demonstrate the entry of the dash-vault with a cat-pass onto a hip-height or greater wall. Followed by an exit of a dash-vault from a crouched position that you landed in. \r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start standing in-front of the hip-height or greater wall.\r\n2. cat-pass onto the wall, aiming towards the back edge.\r\n3. Land both feet and in the crouched position.\r\n4. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you, slightly behind you.\r\n5. Lift your hips up, as well as your knees in-front, and push forwards off the wall.\r\n6. Extend legs forwards and land on both feet.\r\n\r\nThe cat-pass can have momentum from additional steps.\r\n\r\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\r\n\r\nYou must land on both feet at the same time.You must not move the feet or shuffle your position once on top of the wall.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and can be any width.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Cat Push','','publish','closed','closed','','cat-push','','','2021-12-01 15:49:27','2021-12-01 15:49:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55515',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55516,1,'2021-12-01 15:53:49','2021-12-01 15:53:49','Demonstrate a complete dash-vault with a box-jump/hurdle-jump over a knee-height or greater wall.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start standing in-front of the hip-height or greater wall.\r\n2. Box-jump or hurdle-jump over the wall, aiming past the wall.\r\n3. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you, slightly behind as you pass over.\r\n4. Extend legs forwards and land on both feet.\r\n\r\nThe box-jump can have momentum from additional steps and does not need to be a strict two-foot box-jump take-off.\r\n\r\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\r\n\r\nYou must land on both feet at the same time on the ground.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be knee-height or greater and can be any width.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Complete Dash','','publish','closed','closed','','complete-dash','','','2021-12-01 15:53:49','2021-12-01 15:53:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55516',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55517,1,'2021-12-01 15:58:26','2021-12-01 15:58:26','Demonstrate a complete dash-vault with a box-jump/hurdle-jump over a hip-height or greater wall finishing on one foot only and stepping out with momentum.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start standing in-front of the hip-height or greater wall.\r\n2. Box-jump or hurdle-jump over the wall, aiming past the wall.\r\n3. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you, slightly behind as you pass over.\r\n4. Extend legs forwards and land on a single foot first.\r\n5. Continue forward, stepping out of the vault and moving forwards.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe box-jump can have momentum from additional steps and does not need to be a strict two-foot box-jump take-off.\r\n\r\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\r\n\r\nYou must land on a single foot on the ground, stepping out and keep moving forwards.\r\n\r\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and can be any width. It must be standard brick-length width or less.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on each leg for the landing.','Full Dash','','publish','closed','closed','','full-dash','','','2021-12-01 15:58:26','2021-12-01 15:58:26','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55517',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55518,1,'2021-12-01 16:02:58','2021-12-01 16:02:58','Demonstrate a complete dash-vault with a box-jump/hurdle-jump over a hip-height or greater railing finishing on one foot only and stepping out with momentum.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start standing in-front of the hip-height or greater rail.\r\n2. Box-jump or hurdle-jump over the rail, aiming past it.\r\n3. Place both hands on top of the rail, on each side of you, slightly behind as you pass over.\r\n4. Extend legs forwards and land on a single foot first.\r\n5. Continue forward, stepping out of the vault and moving forwards.\r\n\r\nThe box-jump can have momentum from additional steps and does not need to be a strict two-foot box-jump take-off.\r\n\r\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\r\n\r\nYou must land on a single foot on the ground, stepping out and keep moving forwards.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be hip-height or greater and can be any width. It must be standard scaffolding width or less. It can also be circular or flat.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times.','Rail Dash','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-dash','','','2021-12-01 16:02:58','2021-12-01 16:02:58','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55518',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55519,1,'2021-12-01 16:27:57','2021-12-01 16:27:57','Demonstrate a complete dash-vault with a box-jump/hurdle-jump over a hip-height or greater wall that is twice your foot-length or greater. finishing on one foot only and stepping out with momentum.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start standing in-front of the hip-height or greater wall.\n2. Box-jump or hurdle-jump over the wall, aiming past the wall.\n3. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you, slightly behind as you pass over.\n4. Extend legs forwards and land on a single foot first.\n5. Continue forward, stepping out of the vault and moving forwards.\n\n\nThe box-jump can have momentum from additional steps and does not need to be a strict two-foot box-jump take-off.\n\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\n\nYou must land on a single foot on the ground, stepping out and keep moving forwards.\n\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and must be wider than two of your own steps. \n\nYou must repeat three times on each leg for the landing.','Wide Dash','','publish','closed','closed','','wide-dash','','','2021-12-01 16:27:57','2021-12-01 16:27:57','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55519',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55520,1,'2021-12-01 16:32:30','2021-12-01 16:32:30','Demonstrate a complete dash-vault with a box-jump/hurdle-jump over a hip-height or greater wall / rail but with a chest-height drop on the far side. \n\nExample:\n\n1. Start standing in-front of the hip-height or greater wall.\n2. Box-jump or hurdle-jump over the wall, aiming past the wall.\n3. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you, slightly behind as you pass over.\n4. Extend legs forwards and land on both feet or a single foot.\n5. Continue forward, stepping out of the vault and moving forwards.\n\nThe drop of the far side must be chest-height or greater.\n\nThe box-jump can have momentum from additional steps and does not need to be a strict two-foot box-jump take-off.\n\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\n\nYou must land on a single foot on the ground, stepping out and keep moving forwards.\n\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and can be any width. Can be a wall / rail or block.\n\nYou must repeat three times.','Dash Drop','','publish','closed','closed','','dash-drop','','','2021-12-01 16:32:30','2021-12-01 16:32:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55520',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55521,1,'2021-12-01 16:37:37','2021-12-01 16:37:37','Demonstrate two complete dash-vaults with a box-jump/hurdle-jump over two separate hip-height or greater walls finishing on one foot only and stepping out with momentum.\n\nA maximum of two paces only between both walls. You must be travelling at jogging-pace or faster.\n\nExample:\n\n1. Start standing in-front of the hip-height or greater wall.\n2. Box-jump or hurdle-jump over the wall, aiming past the wall.\n3. Place both hands on the corner of the wall, on each side of you, slightly behind as you pass over.\n4. Extend legs forwards and land on a single foot first.\n5. Continue forward, stepping out of the vault and moving forwards towards the second wall.\n6. Step towards the second wall and repeat dash vault.\n\n\nThe box-jump can have momentum from additional steps and does not need to be a strict two-foot box-jump take-off.\n\nYou must use the flat part of the palm of the hands. No steepling.\n\nYou must land on a single foot on the ground, stepping out and keep moving forwards.\n\nThe wall must be hip-height or greater and can be any width. It must be standard brick-length width or less.\n\nYou must repeat the double vault three times on each leg for the landing. (12 total)','Double Dash','','publish','closed','closed','','double-dash','','','2021-12-01 16:37:37','2021-12-01 16:37:37','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55521',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55522,1,'2021-12-02 07:38:32','2021-12-02 07:38:32','Demonstrate a seat-vault on a hip-height wall or higher. \r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start facing the wall and place your right hand palm down on the wall opposite the left hip.\r\n2. Turn 180 degrees to your left as you roll onto the outside of your right glute and TFL muscle.\r\n3. Roll across the glutes (bum) while lifting the left leg up first and sweeping over the wall.\r\n4. Leading with the left leg, keep turning to your left and sweep the right leg over too.\r\n5. Use the left hand on the wall to stabilise you.\r\n6. Land on the ground with the left foot, then the right.\r\n7. You should finish facing the direction you started after turning 360 degrees to the left.\r\n\r\nWall can be any width, but you must be able to get over to the floor on the opposite side.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on both sides.\r\n\r\nHands should be flat on the wall, using the palms.','Seat Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','seat-vault','','','2021-12-02 07:38:32','2021-12-02 07:38:32','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55522',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55523,1,'2021-12-02 07:49:05','2021-12-02 07:49:05','Demonstrate a barrel-vault on a hip-height wall or higher. Wall can be any width, but you must be able to get over to the floor on the opposite side. \r\n\r\nSimilar to the seat-vault except that your shoulders, upper back (thoracic) and glutes all now roll over the wall together.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start facing the wall and place your right hand down beside the wall opposite the left hip. As if you are going to touch the floor in front of the wall.\r\n2. Turn 180 degrees to your left as you roll onto the outside of your right shoulder (deltoid) on the top of the wall.\r\n3. Place the outer right glute (and TFL Muscle) onto the wall too.\r\n4. Roll across the glutes (bum), upper back and shoulder-blades.\r\n5. Lift the left leg up first and sweeping over the wall, without touching the wall.\r\n6. Leading with the left leg, keep turning to your left and sweep the right leg over.\r\n7. Use the left arm on the side of the wall on the opposite side to help stabilise your position.\r\n8. Land on the ground with the left foot, then the right.\r\n9. You should finish facing the direction you started after turning 360 degrees to the left.\r\n\r\nYou hands should not touch the wall at any time.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat three times on both sides.\r\n','Chest Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','chest-vault','','','2021-12-02 07:49:05','2021-12-02 07:49:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55523',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55524,1,'2021-12-02 07:55:17','2021-12-02 07:55:17','Demonstrate a hurdle-vault over a knee-height wall or greater. Wall width must be brick-length or more.\n\nYou must demonstrate leading with both sides and repeat three times.\n\nThe hurdle is very similar to a running box-jump, however one leg leads as the other trails behind. Also similar to a stride with an obstacle to pass over.\n\nYou must land one foot and keep momentum going. \n\nYou must be going at jogging pace or faster.','Hurdle Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','hurdle-vault','','','2021-12-02 07:55:17','2021-12-02 07:55:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55524',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55525,1,'2021-12-02 08:01:02','2021-12-02 08:01:02','Demonstrate a gate-vault on a hip-height or higher railing.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Start facing the hip-height railing.\r\n2. Lean hips (just under the hip bone - on the hip-flexor) onto the front of the railing.\r\n3. Lean over the railing grabbing the top with the left hand.\r\n4. Keeping the right arm straight, lean all the way over and place the right hand on the ground.\r\n5. Now cartwheel your legs over the railing landing one foot and then the other.\r\n6. You should finish standing.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.\r\n\r\nYou can perform on a wall as well, but will be harder to grip the top of the wall instead of a rail.\r\n\r\nYou can finish facing any direction as long as your are standing.','Gate Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','gate-vault','','','2021-12-02 08:01:02','2021-12-02 08:01:02','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55525',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55526,1,'2021-12-02 08:10:13','2021-12-02 08:10:13','Demonstrate the ability to do a reverse vault, turning 360 degrees, over a hip-height or greater wall. The wall can be any width, but you must land on the opposite side of the wall.\r\n\r\nYou must be at jogging-pace or faster.\r\n\r\nYou can land one or two feet, but must be facing the same direction you started.\r\n\r\nYou hand must be flat on the wall with the palm down and no steepling of the fingers.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n1. Jogging into the vault, start facing the wall.\r\n2. As you get to the wall, place your right hand flat on top of the wall in front of your left hip ( crossing your body)\r\n3. Start turning to your left and Box-jump with both feet together.\r\n4. As you are in the air, you will pass over the wall still turning to the left.\r\n5. Keep turning until you are 360 degrees as you land on the ground. \r\n\r\nYou can land one or two feet on the floor.\r\n\r\nThree repetitions on each side are required.','Reverse Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','reverse-vault','','','2021-12-02 08:10:13','2021-12-02 08:10:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55526',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55527,1,'2021-12-02 08:20:18','2021-12-02 08:20:18','The cat-pass is also commonly known as the \'kong\' vault. Combined with the exit of a dash vault, the kong-dash was shortened to be called the Kash Vault.\r\n\r\nDemonstrate a Kash vault on a hip-height or higher wall of any width. You must land two feet and your palms must be flat on top of the wall with no steepled fingers.\r\n\r\nThis can be performed with or without momentum.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n\r\n1. Standing in front of the wall, place both hands in the middle of the wall, flat and with the palms down.\r\n2. Jump into the air, performing the entry of a cat-pass, by tucking you knees and legs under you.\r\n3. As your tucked legs pass under you, your hands will leave the wall for a moment.\r\n4. As the legs start passing ahead of you, the hands will down back down onto the wall.\r\n5. With the legs ahead and you hands behind you, exit the vault in a dash vault.\r\n6. Land two feet together.\r\n\r\nYou must not twist or only have one-hand come off the wall. Both hands must leave the wall at the same time and land at the same time.\r\n\r\nThe Kash vault is a bilateral movement and therefore one side should not be different from the other at any time.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times.','Kash Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','kash-vault','','','2021-12-02 08:20:18','2021-12-02 08:20:18','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55527',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55528,1,'2021-12-02 08:30:13','2021-12-02 08:30:13','The push-back is performed standing between two rails or walls. You use the wall in-front of you to push backwards over the wall behind you.\n\nDemonstrate the push-back over hip-height or higher rails or walls that are greater than three steps apart. Public bike-racks are a good location for this vault.\n\nExample.\n\n1. Stand between two railings facing one and with your back to the other.\n2. Place both hands on top of the rail in front.\n3. Box-jump upwards and at the peak of the jump, push your hands on the railing.\n4. As your arms push you backwards, extend your legs and enter a pike position.\n5. Passing over the railing behind you, you can let go of the railing.\n6. As you descend, switch your grip onto the railing you are going over.\n7. Land two feet standing.\n\nYour legs or body must not touch the back railing or wall - only your hands can.\n\nRepeat three times.','Push-Back','','publish','closed','closed','','push-back','','','2021-12-02 08:30:13','2021-12-02 08:30:13','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55528',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55529,1,'2021-12-02 08:42:28','2021-12-02 08:42:28','Very similar to a <a href=\"/syllabus/hands-only/\">hands-only TicTac</a>, the swing vault moves the vaulting wall from being perpendicular to parallel.\n\nPerform three swing-vaults on a hip-height or greater wall or rail. You must demonstrate both sides.\n\nThe swing vault starts with a <a href=\"/syllabus/over-a-wall-3/\">side-vault</a> that is then caught into the hands-only TicTac.\n\nThe simplest setup for this can be a public bike-rack with two railings. \n \nExample:\n\n1. Stand in front of two parallel walls or railings that are slightly wider than shoulder-width. \n2. The second wall can the same height or higher. The first wall will be the one to vault over.\n3. Side-vault over the first wall to your right.\n4. As you are in the air, switch your right-hand onto the second wall.\n5. Catch your body-weight off the ground and keep the momentum swinging around the left arm.\n6. Keeping your legs tucked, rotate around the left arm and pass back over the front wall.\n7. Let go of the back wall with the right hand.\n8. Land on the ground with both feet facing away from the walls.\n\n','Swing Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','swing-vault','','','2021-12-02 08:42:28','2021-12-02 08:42:28','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55529',0,'lp_tutorial','',0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (55530,1,'2021-12-02 08:49:47','2021-12-02 08:49:47','Very similar to the swing vault in level 8, however, instead of swinging back over the wall you will keep the swing going and reverse the entire vault to finish in a side-vault back to where your started.\n\nPerform three reverse swing-vaults on a hip-height or greater wall or rail. You must demonstrate both sides.\n\nThe swing vault starts with a <a href=\"/syllabus/over-a-wall-3/\">side-vault</a> that is then caught into the hands-only TicTac that finishes in another side-vault.\n\nThe simplest setup for this can be a public bike-rack with two railings. \n \nExample:\n\n1. Stand to the side of two parallel walls or railings that are slightly wider than shoulder-width. \n2. The second wall can the same height or higher. The first wall will be the one to vault over.\n3. Side-vault over the first wall to your right.\n4. As you are in the air, switch your right-hand onto the second wall.\n5. Catch your body-weight off the ground and keep the momentum swinging forwards.\n6. As your legs swing in front of you, keep contracted and swing them back again behind you.\n7. When your legs are behind you, pull with the left hand and side-vault back out over the wall.\n8. Let go of the back wall with the right hand.\n9. Land on the ground with both feet facing away along the walls.\n','Reverse Swing Vault','','publish','closed','closed','','reverse-swing-vault','','','2021-12-02 08:49:47','2021-12-02 08:49:47','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55530',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55531,1,'2021-12-02 08:58:07','2021-12-02 08:58:07','Demonstrate a walk-over vault on a hip-height or higher wall, railing, bench or block. Repeat three times leading with opposite legs.\r\n\r\nThe walk-over is performed by going from a handstand position and leaning onto the obstacle, tipping your weight so that your legs pull you over the wall.\r\n\r\nExample.\r\n\r\n1. Start facing the wall about a pace away.\r\n2. Lean down and place both hands on the ground in-front of the wall.\r\n3. Kick up into a handstand and lean lower back (lumbar) onto the top of the wall.\r\n4. Slowly drop your legs past the wall, shifting your weight with momentum.\r\n5. Roll over into a sitting position or past the wall.\r\n6. Land your feet on the ground the other side.\r\n\r\nYour hands can transition from the floor to the wall for stability.\r\n\r\nYou must have continue momentum and consistent speed the whole time.','Walk Over','','publish','closed','closed','','walk-over','','','2021-12-02 08:58:07','2021-12-02 08:58:07','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55531',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55532,1,'2021-12-03 10:03:31','2021-12-03 10:03:31','Demonstrate a good precision landing on the edge of a curb, jumping from the ground with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. There is no distance requirement, which means you can jump from anywhere on the floor.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Floor-to-Curb','','publish','closed','closed','','floor-to-curb','','','2021-12-03 10:03:31','2021-12-03 10:03:31','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55532',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55533,1,'2021-12-03 13:16:19','2021-12-03 13:16:19','Demonstrate a good precision landing on the edge of a curb, jumping from a similar curb or low wall with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. There is no distance requirement, which means you can jump from any distance, as long as it\'s on an ankle-height or greater curb/wall/block.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Curb-to-Curb','','publish','closed','closed','','curb-to-curb','','','2021-12-03 13:16:19','2021-12-03 13:16:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55533',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55534,1,'2021-12-03 13:24:51','2021-12-03 13:24:51','Demonstrate a good precision landing on the edge of a block/wall/obstacle, jumping from a similar block/wall/obstacle with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. \r\n\r\nBoth block/wall/obstacle must be greater than hip-height of the practitioner.\r\n\r\nEach block must be further than participant 5-steps away from each other.\r\n\r\nThe blocks do not have to have a drop on the far side, like a wall does - they can continue to any width.\r\n\r\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\r\n\r\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\r\n\r\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\r\n\r\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\r\n\r\nYou must repeat five times.','Block-to-Block','','publish','closed','closed','','block-to-block','','','2021-12-03 13:24:51','2021-12-03 13:24:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55534',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55535,1,'2021-12-03 13:28:57','2021-12-03 13:28:57','Demonstrate a good precision landing on the edge of a wall, jumping from a similar wall with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. The wall width can only be brick-length or less.\n\nThis is to demonstrate accuracy of landing with a drop on the back side of the landing spot.\n\nBoth block/wall/obstacle must be greater than hip-height of the practitioner.\n\nEach block must be further than participant 5-steps away from each other.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nYou must repeat five times.','Wall-to-Wall','','publish','closed','closed','','wall-to-wall','','','2021-12-03 13:28:57','2021-12-03 13:28:57','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55535',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55536,1,'2021-12-03 13:34:10','2021-12-03 13:34:10','Demonstrate a good precision landing on the edge of a wall, jumping from a similar wall with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. The wall width can only be brick-length or less. \n\nYou must repeat six jumps in a row without falling off the walls. You only need to demonstrate this once.\n\nThis is to test your consistency of accuracy over multiple jumps.\n\nBoth block/wall/obstacle must be greater than hip-height of the practitioner.\n\nEach block must be further than participant 6-steps away from each other.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.','Consistency','','publish','closed','closed','','consistency','','','2021-12-03 13:34:10','2021-12-03 13:34:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55536',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55537,1,'2021-12-03 13:45:53','2021-12-03 13:45:53','Demonstrate a good precision landing on the edge of a wall, jumping from a similar wall with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. The wall width can only be brick-length or less. \n\nThe walls must be 8 heel-to-toe steps away from each other and hip-height or greater.\n\nThis is to test your accuracy and control over further distances and greater power.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nRepeat five times, sticking the landing.','Distance','','publish','closed','closed','','distance','','','2021-12-03 13:45:53','2021-12-03 13:45:53','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55537',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55538,1,'2021-12-03 13:53:16','2021-12-03 13:53:16','Demonstrate a good precision landing on a circular railing, jumping from a wall/block with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. The rail width can only be standard scaffolding width or less. \n\nThe rail must be 6 heel-to-toe steps away or greater from the wall and hip-height or greater.\n\nThis is to test your fine-accuracy on a landing.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nRepeat five times, sticking the landing.','Wall-to-Rail','','publish','closed','closed','','wall-to-rail','','','2021-12-03 13:53:16','2021-12-03 13:53:16','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55538',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55539,1,'2021-12-03 13:57:15','2021-12-03 13:57:15','Demonstrate a good precision landing on a circular railing, jumping from a circular railing in balance position with both feet at the same time and landing both feet at the same time. The rail width can only be standard scaffolding width or less. \r\n\r\nThe rails must be 6 heel-to-toe steps away or greater from each other and hip-height or greater.\r\n\r\nThis is to test your fine-accuracy on both take-off balance and landing position.\r\n\r\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\r\n\r\nYou must stick the landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\r\n\r\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\r\n\r\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\r\n\r\nRepeat five times, sticking the landing.','Rail-to-Rail','London Parkour is an organization focused on promoting and teaching the art of parkour, a movement discipline involving running, jumping, and climbing over obstacles in an urban environment. Their official website, https://LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a hub for resources, tutorials, and community engagement. Through their online presence, they aim to transform the way individuals think, move, and feel by providing practical movement techniques.\r\n\r\nThe organization maintains a strong social media presence, encouraging followers to engage with their content on platforms such as Instagram and YouTube, where they feature instructional videos and tutorials. Their YouTube channel, accessible through the link provided, offers a variety of parkour-related content aimed at both beginners and experienced practitioners. Subscribing to their channel allows individuals to stay updated on the latest tutorials and parkour tips.\r\n\r\nAs part of their outreach, London Parkour invites visitors to contact them through their listed email for inquiries, suggestions, or collaboration opportunities. Their commitment to fostering a community of parkour enthusiasts is evident in their efforts to create an accessible and informative space for anyone interested in the discipline.','publish','closed','closed','','rail-to-rail','','','2021-12-03 13:57:15','2021-12-03 13:57:15','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55539',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55540,1,'2021-12-03 14:04:43','2021-12-03 14:04:43','Perform three plyometrics jumps in a row on rails that over knee-height or greater. You must be moving consistently and stick the last landing.\n\nThis will require four rails to jump across. Start on the first, complete three plyos and finish in a stick on the last precision.\n\nThe rail width can only be standard scaffolding width or less. \n\nThe rails must be 5 heel-to-toe steps away or greater from each other and knee-height or greater.\n\nThis is to test your fine-accuracy over multiple jumps with momentum and power.\n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the last landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.\n\nRepeat five times, sticking the landing.','Rail Plyos','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-plyos','','','2021-12-03 14:04:43','2021-12-03 14:04:43','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55540',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55541,1,'2021-12-03 14:11:27','2021-12-03 14:11:27','Demonstrate a 90-degree turn onto a rail-precision that is hip-height or greater.\n\nYou can jump from a wall/block/step, but must land on a railing that is perpendicular to the takeoff point. The landing also needs to be greater than six heel-to-toe steps away from each other.\n\nThis is to test your ability to land accurately in awkward positions.\n\nYou must demonstrate three repetitions turning in each direction. \n\nThe rail width can only be standard scaffolding width or less. \n\nYou must Land on the ball of the big-toe as the impact point, on the front edge of the landing target.\n\nYou must stick the last landing, which requires you to have complete control of your balance once you have landed. This also means that after the initial contact of the feet, you cannot shuffle or move them from that point.\n\nA good bend on the ankle, knee and hip where muscle contraction is being used to maintain balance.\n\nYou cannot fall forwards or backwards after landing.','Rail 90 Degrees','','publish','closed','closed','','rail-90-degrees','','','2021-12-03 14:11:27','2021-12-03 14:11:27','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55541',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55542,1,'2021-12-14 11:03:03','2021-12-14 11:03:03','Demonstrate a straight-arm cat-leap / arm-jump hang position on the side of a wall. Wall can be any height that is comfortable.\r\n\r\nKnees should be bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. \r\n\r\nArms must be straight. Full finger length over the top. No over-grip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nHold for 20 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat once on each leg being high, the other low.\r\n','Straight Arm Cat','London Parkour is a platform dedicated to teaching and promoting the versatility and benefits of parkour, which is a practical movement discipline. Their website provides a range of resources and tutorials designed to help individuals transform the way they think, move, and feel through parkour training. By embracing this unique form of physical expression, participants can enhance not only their physical capabilities but also their mental resilience.\r\n\r\nThe London Parkour YouTube channel offers a variety of parkour tutorial videos with the hashtag #ParkourHowTo, allowing viewers to learn essential techniques and movements at their own pace. They encourage subscribers to stay updated with the latest content and develop their skills further. The presence of hashtags such as #LondonParkour and #ParkourPulse indicates a vibrant community around the discipline, fostering engagement and interaction among enthusiasts.\r\n\r\nIn addition to their YouTube channel, London Parkour is active on social media platforms, including Instagram, to share their journey and connect with followers. Interested individuals can easily subscribe to their resources or reach out via email for any inquiries, making it accessible for anyone looking to explore the world of parkour. Overall, London Parkour embodies a commitment to practical movement and community building within the parkour discipline.','publish','closed','closed','','straight-arm-cat','','','2021-12-14 11:03:03','2021-12-14 11:03:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55542',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55543,1,'2021-12-14 11:10:42','2021-12-14 11:10:42','Demonstrate a bent-arm cat-leap / arm-jump hang position on the side of a wall. Wall can be any height that is comfortable.\r\n\r\nKnees should be bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. \r\n\r\nArms must be bent at ninety degrees or less (chin closer to hands). Full finger length over the top. No over-grip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nHold for 20 seconds.\r\n\r\nRepeat once on each leg being high, the other low.\r\n','Bent Arm Cat','London Parkour offers a variety of resources and training for enthusiasts interested in mastering parkour techniques. Their website, LondonParkour.com, serves as a hub for those seeking practical movement methods to fundamentally enhance their physical abilities and mental perspective on movement. Through engaging tutorials and instructional content, London Parkour aims to provide comprehensive support for both beginners and advanced practitioners.\r\n\r\nAdditionally, London Parkour maintains a strong online presence through its YouTube channel, where viewers can subscribe and access a wealth of parkour tutorials and demonstrations. This platform not only showcases the skills of the practitioners but also serves as an educational tool for individuals looking to learn and improve their parkour abilities. Social media channels, particularly Instagram, further amplify the community\'s reach and connection, creating a vibrant network of parkour enthusiasts.\r\n\r\nFor inquiries or collaborations, individuals are encouraged to reach out via email. By building a community around parkour, London Parkour fosters an environment where enthusiasts can share their passion, learn from one another, and challenge their physical limits while enjoying the transformative journey of parkour practice.','publish','closed','closed','','bent-arm-cat','','','2021-12-14 11:10:42','2021-12-14 11:10:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55543',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55544,1,'2021-12-14 11:16:48','2021-12-14 11:16:48','Demonstrate a straight-arm cat-leap / arm-jump hang position on the side of a wall and then traverse 3-steps (heel-to-toe) to the left and to the right. Wall can be any height that is comfortable.\r\n\r\nKnees should be bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. \r\n\r\nArms must be straight. Full finger length over the top. No over-grip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nRepeat twice for both directions.\r\n\r\nThe distance of must be traversed by your entire body. You must pass the distance entirely before returning in the opposite direction\r\n','Straight Traverse','London Parkour is an organization dedicated to the practice and promotion of parkour as a transformative movement discipline. Their website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a platform for resources, tutorials, and information aimed at helping individuals improve their physical capabilities and change their mindset towards movement. They emphasize practical movement that can enhance both mental and physical well-being.\r\n\r\nThe organization leverages social media and video content to reach a broader audience. They encourage interested individuals to subscribe to their YouTube channel for parkour tutorials and insights. This approach not only fosters community engagement but also provides accessible training resources for practitioners at all skill levels.\r\n\r\nAdditionally, London Parkour maintains a presence on Instagram and offers direct contact via email for inquiries or further information. Their online platforms are designed to connect enthusiasts, share knowledge, and inspire individuals to explore the art of parkour.','publish','closed','closed','','straight-traverse','','','2021-12-14 11:16:48','2021-12-14 11:16:48','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55544',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55545,1,'2021-12-14 11:19:01','2021-12-14 11:19:01','Demonstrate a bent-arm cat-leap / arm-jump hang position on the side of a wall and then traverse 3-steps (heel-to-toe) to the left and to the right. Wall can be any height that is comfortable.\r\n\r\nKnees should be bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. \r\n\r\nArms must be bent at ninety degrees or less (chin closer to hands). Full finger length over the top. No over-grip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nRepeat twice for both directions.\r\n\r\nThe distance of must be traversed by your entire body. You must pass the distance entirely before returning in the opposite direction\r\n','Bent Traverse','London Parkour is a dedicated platform focused on teaching the art of parkour through various tutorials and instructional content. Their website, LONDONPARKOUR.com, serves as a central hub for information and resources related to practical movement, aimed at helping individuals transform their physical abilities and perspectives on movement. Users can subscribe to the YouTube channel to receive updates and access a wealth of parkour-related content.\r\n\r\nThe organization emphasizes the importance of functional movement, encouraging participants to rethink how they move through their environment. This practical approach not only promotes physical fitness but also enhances mental agility, promoting overall well-being. The tutorials are designed to cater to a wide range of skill levels, from beginners to more advanced practitioners.\r\n\r\nTo stay connected with the community, London Parkour maintains an active presence on social media platforms such as Instagram and YouTube, where they share tips, videos, and the latest news related to parkour. Interested individuals can reach out via email for further engagement and inquiries, ensuring a supportive and interactive environment for all parkour enthusiasts.','publish','closed','closed','','bent-traverse','','','2021-12-14 11:19:01','2021-12-14 11:19:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55545',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55546,1,'2021-12-14 11:26:42','2021-12-14 11:26:42','Using two parallel walls/obstacles/railings, use your feet on the one wall and your hands on the second wall. Create a stable structure using your abs and traverse to your side for over ten-steps (heel-to-toe)\r\n\r\nReturn back in the opposite direction. You can rest between directions.\r\n\r\nThe walls must be over six-steps from each other. You must be facing the floor.\r\n\r\nYou must not touch the ground.\r\n\r\nRepeat twice.','Lumbar Traverse','','publish','closed','closed','','lumbar-traverse','','','2021-12-14 11:26:42','2021-12-14 11:26:42','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55546',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55547,1,'2021-12-14 11:34:33','2021-12-14 11:34:33','Demonstrate a bent-arm cat-leap / arm-jump hang position on the side of a wall and then traverse 6-steps (heel-to-toe) to the left and to the right. Wall can be any height that is comfortable.\n\nEach reposition of the hand must be over 2-steps (heel-to-toe) away from the other hand. Same for the foot. For example, traversing to the right, as you move both right hand and right foot, they must both be further than 2-steps in distance.\n\nFor the 6-steps distance to cover, you must do this in three hand-reposition traversal movements.\n\nKnees should be bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you. \n\nArms must be bent at ninety degrees or less (chin closer to hands). Full finger length over the top. No over-grip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\n\nRepeat three times for both directions. You can rest between each direction.\n\nThe distance of must be traversed by your entire body. You must pass the distance entirely before returning in the opposite direction\n','Wide Traverse','','publish','closed','closed','','wide-traverse','','','2021-12-14 11:34:33','2021-12-14 11:34:33','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55547',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55548,1,'2021-12-15 08:26:39','2021-12-15 08:26:39','Demonstrate a bent-arm cat-leap / arm-jump hang position on the side of a wall and then traverse 10-steps (heel-to-toe) to the left and to the right without dropping off the wall until both directions are complete (20 steps distance in total). Wall can be any height that is comfortable.\r\n\r\nKnees should be bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you.\r\n\r\nArms must be bent at ninety degrees or less (chin closer to hands). Full finger length over the top. No over-grip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nRepeat twice for both directions.\r\n\r\nThe distance of must be traversed by your entire body. You must pass the distance entirely before returning in the opposite direction','Distance Traverse','London Parkour offers resources and guidance for those interested in the art of movement, prominently showcasing their offerings on their website and various social media platforms. With a focus on practical movement strategies, the organization aims to enhance the way individuals think, move, and feel through parkour training.\r\n\r\nThe company encourages viewers to subscribe to their YouTube channel for tutorials and tips related to parkour, thus fostering a community of enthusiasts eager to learn from their comprehensive video content. The use of hashtags such as #ParkourHowTo and #ParkourTutorial reflects the intent to reach a wider audience interested in this dynamic discipline.\r\n\r\nIn addition to their online presence, London Parkour actively engages users through Instagram, where followers can stay updated on their latest activities and insights. For more personalized inquiries, contact information is provided, ensuring that those interested in parkour have access to the expertise and support offered by the organization.','publish','closed','closed','','distance-traverse','','','2021-12-15 08:26:39','2021-12-15 08:26:39','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55548',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55549,1,'2021-12-15 08:39:10','2021-12-15 08:39:10','Using two parallel walls/obstacles/railings, use your feet flat against (not on top) of the wall and your hands flat against (not on top of) the second wall. Create a stable structure using your abs and traverse to your side for over ten-steps (heel-to-toe) and back again without dropping.\n\nYou must return back in the opposite direction. (20-Steps in total distance)\n\nThe walls must be over six-steps from each other. You must be facing the floor.\n\nYou must not touch the ground.\n\nRepeat twice.','Distance Lumbar','','publish','closed','closed','','distance-lumbar','','','2021-12-15 08:39:10','2021-12-15 08:39:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55549',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55550,1,'2021-12-15 08:48:14','2021-12-15 08:48:14','Demonstrate a full-hang traverse over a gap where your body and legs are not in contact with anything. You are effectively hanging over a gap where your feet are over hip-height from the ground.\r\n\r\nTraverse with your only your hands a full 15-steps in one direction and then climb-up at the end on top of the hang without dropping to the floor.\r\n\r\nRepeat only once in each direction.\r\n\r\nYour climb-up can use feet and hands and anything needed to get back up.\r\n\r\nYou must traverse on a concrete material with no over-grip. You cannot use railings or bars on the traverse - but you can for the climb-up.\r\n','Hang Traverse','','publish','closed','closed','','hang-traverse','','','2021-12-15 08:48:14','2021-12-15 08:48:14','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55550',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55551,1,'2021-12-15 08:29:44','2021-12-15 08:29:44','Demonstrate a bent-arm cat-leap / arm-jump hang position on the side of a wall and then traverse 10-steps (heel-to-toe) to the left and then climbup to your feet. Climb back down and then traverse back in the opposite direction. Climbup at the end again. \r\n\r\nYou can rest for 10 seconds between the climb-up and climb-down.\r\n\r\nWall can be any height that is comfortable.\r\n\r\nKnees should be bent, one leg higher than the other. One at hip-height, and underneath you.\r\n\r\nFull finger length over the top. No over-grip onto the back edge of the wall - must be on the front edge corner of the wall.\r\n\r\nRepeat twice for both directions.\r\n\r\nThe distance of must be traversed by your entire body. You must pass the distance entirely before returning in the opposite direction','Distance Climbup','London Parkour is an organization dedicated to teaching the art of parkour, focusing on practical movement techniques that can transform how individuals think, move, and feel. They offer a variety of resources, tutorials, and instructional videos to help practitioners at all levels improve their skills. The organization emphasizes community involvement and encourages people to engage with their content through social media and other platforms.\r\n\r\nTheir presence on social media, including Instagram and YouTube, helps reach a wider audience and connects enthusiasts of parkour around the world. By subscribing to their channel, viewers can access a wealth of tutorials and tips aimed at enhancing their abilities in parkour. This interactive approach fosters a supportive environment for both beginners and experienced practitioners.\r\n\r\nFor those interested in learning more about parkour and joining the community, further information can be found on their official website, LondoonParkour.com. There, individuals can explore various resources, sign up for classes, or contact the team for any inquiries. The organization seeks to inspire and empower people through the art of movement, making parkour accessible to all.','publish','closed','closed','','distance-climbup','','','2021-12-15 08:29:44','2021-12-15 08:29:44','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55551',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55552,1,'2021-12-15 09:30:54','2021-12-15 09:30:54','Demonstrate a wall-run to waist-cat position on a wall that is chest height or greater. Foot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot.\n\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on top of the wall.\n\nChange the foot that is kicking the wall and levering you up on top.\n\nYou can use as much momentum as needed.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.','Chest-Height','','publish','closed','closed','','chest-height','','','2021-12-15 09:30:54','2021-12-15 09:30:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55552',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55553,1,'2021-12-15 09:38:23','2021-12-15 09:38:23','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall with no top-edge. Foot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. Use chalk or post-it notes to mark height on the wall.\n\nYou must reach over 1.5x head height on the wall.\n\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\n\nYou can use as much momentum as needed.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.','Height Check','','publish','closed','closed','','height-check','','','2021-12-15 09:38:23','2021-12-15 09:38:23','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55553',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55554,1,'2021-12-15 09:44:03','2021-12-15 09:44:03','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall that is full-stretch height and catch the top edge - There is no requirement to hold the catch - Fingers must show a grip only for a second. Foot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. \r\n\r\nYou can use one hand or two hands over the top edge.\r\n\r\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\r\n\r\nYou can use as much momentum as needed.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.','Wall-run Full','','publish','closed','closed','','wall-run-full','','','2021-12-15 09:44:03','2021-12-15 09:44:03','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55554',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55555,1,'2021-12-15 09:48:21','2021-12-15 09:48:21','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall that is full-stretch height and catch the top edge - You must now climb-up on top of the wall using any means necessary. Foot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. \r\n\r\nYou can use one hand or two hands over the top edge. You can use any other object to help climb up.\r\n\r\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\r\n\r\nYou can use as much momentum as needed.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times on each side.','Wall-run Climbup','','publish','closed','closed','','wall-run-climbup','','','2021-12-15 09:48:21','2021-12-15 09:48:21','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55555',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55556,1,'2021-12-15 09:50:52','2021-12-15 09:50:52','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall that is full-stretch height and catch the top edge - There is no requirement to hold the catch - Fingers must show a grip only for a second. Foot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. \n\nYou only have three paced steps for momentum.\n\nYou can use one hand or two hands over the top edge.\n\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.','3-Step Wall-run','','publish','closed','closed','','3-step-wall-run','','','2021-12-15 09:50:52','2021-12-15 09:50:52','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55556',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55557,1,'2021-12-15 09:54:40','2021-12-15 09:54:40','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall that is full-stretch height and catch the top edge - You must now climb-up on top of the wall using any means necessary. Foot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. \n\nYou only have three paced steps for momentum.\n\nYou can use one hand or two hands over the top edge. You can use any other object to help climb up.\n\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.','3-Steps and up','','publish','closed','closed','','3-step-and-up','','','2021-12-15 09:54:40','2021-12-15 09:54:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55557',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55558,1,'2021-12-15 09:57:05','2021-12-15 09:57:05','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall that is 1.5x (150%) of your head-height and catch the top edge - You must now climb-up on top of the wall using any means necessary. \n\nFoot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. \n\nYou can use one hand or two hands over the top edge. You can use any other object to help climb up.\n\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.','High Wall-run','','publish','closed','closed','','high-wall-run','','','2021-12-15 09:57:05','2021-12-15 09:57:05','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55558',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55559,1,'2021-12-15 10:00:19','2021-12-15 10:00:19','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall that is 1.5x (150%) of your head-height and catch the top edge - You must now traverse 5-steps to the side and then climb-up on top of the wall.\n\nFoot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. \n\nTraverse can be bent arm or straight arm, but hands must only use front edge of the wall with no overgrip.\n\nYou can use one hand or two hands over the top edge. You can use any other object to help climb up.\n\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\n\nRepeat three times on each side.','High Traverse','','publish','closed','closed','','high-traverse','','','2021-12-15 10:00:19','2021-12-15 10:00:19','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55559',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55560,1,'2021-12-15 10:04:22','2021-12-15 10:04:22','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall that is 2x (200%) of your head-height and catch the top edge - You must now climb-up on top of the wall using any means necessary. \n\nFoot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. \n\nYou can use one hand or two hands over the top edge. You can use any other object to help climb up.\n\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\n\nRepeat three times on whatever side you prefer.','Double Height','','publish','closed','closed','','double-height','','','2021-12-15 10:04:22','2021-12-15 10:04:22','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55560',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55561,1,'2021-12-15 10:32:24','2021-12-15 10:32:24','Demonstrate a wall-run on a wall that is full-stretch height over a hip-height gap, that is 3-steps or wider, and catch the top edge - You must now climb-up on top of the wall using any means necessary. \n\nFoot should kick at roughly hip-height on the wall with the ball of the foot. \n\nThe height of the full-stretch must be from where you are taking-off from, not including the hip-height gap.\n\nYou can use one hand or two hands over the top edge. You can use any other object to help climb up.\n\nThis is to show correct foot placement and hand placement on the wall while jumping over a 3-step (heel-to-toe) gap. Use the same side foot and hand to kick the wall and reach up.\n\nRepeat three times on whatever side you prefer.','Gap Run','','publish','closed','closed','','gap-run','','','2021-12-15 10:32:24','2021-12-15 10:32:24','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55561',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55562,1,'2021-12-15 11:36:17','2021-12-15 11:36:17','Using a bar/drainpipe/scaffolding/lamppost next to a wall, place both feet onto the side of the wall from with the bar between your legs.\r\n\r\nPlace both hands onto the bar and pull yourself up. Hold yourself off the floor for 20 seconds.\r\n\r\nArms must be bent at 90 degrees or less (chin closer to hands).\r\n\r\nBar must be circular and any width.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times. ','Pipe Wall Hold','','publish','closed','closed','','pipe-wall-hold','','','2021-12-15 11:36:17','2021-12-15 11:36:17','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55562',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55563,1,'2021-12-15 11:39:45','2021-12-15 11:39:45','Using a bar/drainpipe/scaffolding/lamppost next to a wall, place both feet onto the side of the wall from with the bar between your legs.\r\n\r\nPlace both hands onto the bar and pull yourself up. Now climb up with both hands and feet 5-steps (heel-to-toe) up the pipe from where your feet started on the wall.\r\n\r\nDrop once at the top position.\r\n\r\nBar must be circular and any width.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times. ','Pipe Wall Climb','','publish','closed','closed','','pipe-wall-climb','','','2021-12-15 11:39:45','2021-12-15 11:39:45','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55563',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55564,1,'2021-12-15 11:42:00','2021-12-15 11:42:00','Using a bar/drainpipe/scaffolding/lamppost next to a wall, place both feet onto the side of the wall from with the bar between your legs.\n\nPlace both hands onto the bar and pull yourself up. Now climb up with both hands and feet above head-height from where your feet started on the wall.\n\nDrop once at the top position.\n\nBar must be circular and any width.\n\nRepeat three times. ','Pipe Climb Head','','publish','closed','closed','','pipe-climb-head','','','2021-12-15 11:42:00','2021-12-15 11:42:00','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55564',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55565,1,'2021-12-15 11:44:59','2021-12-15 11:44:59','Using a bar/drainpipe/scaffolding/lamppost next to a wall, place both feet onto the side of the wall from with the bar between your legs.\n\nPlace both hands onto the bar and pull yourself up. Now climb up with both hands and feet above head-height from where your feet started on the wall.\n\nNow return back down, stepping onto the ground softly and safely.\n\nBar must be circular and any width.\n\nRepeat three times. ','Pipe Climb Down','','publish','closed','closed','','pipe-climb-down','','','2021-12-15 11:44:59','2021-12-15 11:44:59','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55565',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55566,1,'2021-12-15 11:48:57','2021-12-15 11:48:57','Using a bar/drainpipe/scaffolding/lamppost next to a wall, start in a seated position and place both feet onto the side of the wall from a sitting position with the bar between your legs.\n\nPlace both hands onto the bar and pull yourself up. Now climb up with both hands and feet above head-height from where your feet started on the wall.\n\nNow return back down, finishing in a seated position without the feet touching the ground.\n\nBar must be circular and any width.\n\nRepeat three times. ','Sit Start Finish','','publish','closed','closed','','sit-start-finish','','','2021-12-15 11:48:57','2021-12-15 11:48:57','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55566',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55567,1,'2021-12-15 12:01:02','2021-12-15 12:01:02','Using a stand-alone bar/pole/lamppost/scaffolding with no wall next to it, place both feet onto the pole.\r\n\r\nPlace both hands onto the bar and pull yourself up. Now climb up with both hands and feet for 5 steps from where your feet started.\r\n\r\nNow return back down, but do not come off the bar. \r\nRepeat three laps without stopping and coming off the bar.\r\n\r\nBar must be circular and any width.\r\n\r\nRepeat once.','Pole Climb','','publish','closed','closed','','pole-climb','','','2021-12-15 12:01:02','2021-12-15 12:01:02','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55567',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55568,1,'2021-12-15 12:03:34','2021-12-15 12:03:34','Using a stand-alone lamppost with no wall next to it, climb up with both hands and feet and touch the lamp at the top.\n\nLamppost must be over twice head-height.\n\nBar must be circular and any width.\n\nRepeat once.','Lamppost Climb','','publish','closed','closed','','lamppost-climb','','','2021-12-15 12:03:34','2021-12-15 12:03:34','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55568',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55569,1,'2021-12-15 12:08:30','2021-12-15 12:08:30','Demonstrate a precision jump into a koala position on a scaffolding bar/rail/pole/lamppost with no walls near it.\n\nLand both feet and hands onto the bar from a 5-step distance jump and then continue to climb up 5-steps up the bar.\n\nNow return back down, stepping onto the ground softly and safely.\n\nYou can jump from the floor. Mark out thee distance to jump from on the ground.\n\nBar must be circular and any width.\n\nRepeat three times.','Koala Precision','','publish','closed','closed','','koala-precision','','','2021-12-15 12:08:30','2021-12-15 12:08:30','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55569',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55570,1,'2021-12-15 12:12:37','2021-12-15 12:12:37','Demonstrate a precision jump into a koala position on a scaffolding bar/rail/pole/lamppost with no walls near it.\n\nLand both feet and hands onto the bar from a 5-step distance jump and then rotate around the bar 360 degrees back to the starting point.\n\nNow return back to the jump takeoff point by performing a tictac on the bar and turning 180 degrees back. Land in a precision and stick it.\n\nYou can jump from the floor. Mark out thee distance to jump from on the ground.\n\nBar must be circular and any width.\n\nRepeat twice in each direction of the 360 rotation.','Koala TicTac Back','','publish','closed','closed','','koala-tictac-back','','','2021-12-15 12:12:37','2021-12-15 12:12:37','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55570',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55571,1,'2021-12-15 12:17:07','2021-12-15 12:17:07','Using a secure, metal, firmly fixed pipe/bar/drainpipe on the side of a building, that has no risk of damage, climb into the first-floor level of a balcony and return back down.\n\n**DO NOT TRESPASS**. Only use safe locations in public that you have permission to use.\n\nFirst floor balcony must be over twice head-height.\n\nRepeat once.','Building Pipe Climb','','publish','closed','closed','','building-pipe-climb','','','2021-12-15 12:17:07','2021-12-15 12:17:07','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55571',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55572,1,'2021-12-15 14:40:49','2021-12-15 14:40:49','Slide down a slope for a minimum distance of 5-steps in a splat-cat position with hands and feet touching the wall while keeping the position stable. Face down towards the wall. \n\nUse a hill, slope, concrete sloped wall, metal girder, etc... \n\nHands and feet should not move. You should be in a stable isometric hold.\n\nRepeat three times.\n\nThe splat-cat is used as a protective bail-technique to help control your fall if you fail a cat-leap.','Slope Slide','','publish','closed','closed','','slope-slide','','','2021-12-15 14:40:49','2021-12-15 14:40:49','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55572',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55573,1,'2021-12-16 10:59:24','2021-12-16 10:59:24','Starting from 3-steps away from a target wall, jump into a splat-cat position with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. Drop directly down the wall onto your feet without stumbling or falling.\r\n\r\nPerform a two-foot jump from the floor into the splat cat. \r\n\r\nYou can land at any height on the wall.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times. ','Small Splat','','publish','closed','closed','','small-splat','','','2021-12-16 10:59:24','2021-12-16 10:59:24','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55573',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55574,1,'2021-12-16 11:04:40','2021-12-16 11:04:40','Starting from 3-steps away from a target wall, jump into a splat-cat position with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. Drop directly down the wall onto your feet without stumbling or falling.\n\nPerform a one-foot jump from the floor into the splat cat. You can use momentum from multiple steps-in or not.\n\nRepeat three times on each foot leading. ','Step-in Splat','','publish','closed','closed','','step-in-splat','','','2021-12-16 11:04:40','2021-12-16 11:04:40','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55574',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55575,1,'2021-12-16 11:08:54','2021-12-16 11:08:54','Starting from 5-steps away from a target wall, jump into a splat-cat position with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. Drop directly down the wall onto your feet without stumbling or falling.\n\nPerform a two-foot jump from the floor into the splat cat. \n\nYou can land at any height on the wall.\n\nRepeat three times. ','Distance Splat','','publish','closed','closed','','distance-splat','','','2021-12-16 11:08:54','2021-12-16 11:08:54','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55575',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55576,1,'2021-12-16 11:14:00','2021-12-16 11:14:00','Starting from 5-steps away from a target wall, jump from an ankle-height block into a splat-cat with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. Drop directly down the wall onto your feet without stumbling or falling.\n\nPerform a two-foot jump from an ankle-height obstacle / wall / block into the splat cat. \n\nYou can land at any height on the wall.\n\nRepeat three times. ','Ankle Splat','','publish','closed','closed','','ankle-splat','','','2021-12-16 11:14:00','2021-12-16 11:14:00','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55576',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55577,1,'2021-12-16 11:21:10','2021-12-16 11:21:10','Demonstrate a splat-cat from a hip-height wall that is over 5-steps away from the target wall.\r\n\r\nStarting from 5-steps away from the target wall, jump from a knee-height block (or higher) into a splat-cat with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. Drop directly down the wall onto your feet without stumbling or falling.\r\n\r\nPerform a two-foot jump from an hip-height obstacle / wall / block into the splat cat. \r\n\r\nYou can land at any height on the wall.\r\n\r\nRepeat three times. ','Higher Splat','','publish','closed','closed','','heigher-splat','','','2021-12-16 11:21:10','2021-12-16 11:21:10','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55577',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55578,1,'2021-12-16 11:34:00','2021-12-16 11:34:00','Demonstrate a splat-cat from tow or more hip-height walls with a plyo jump between them that is over 5-steps away from each other.\n\nStarting from two walls away from the target wall, jump from a knee-height block (or higher) into a plyo jump which will then jump into a splat-cat with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. Drop directly down the wall onto your feet without stumbling or falling.\n\nThe plyo walls must be a single brick-length in width or less.\n\nYou can land at any height on the target wall.\n\nRepeat three times. ','Plyo Splat','','publish','closed','closed','','plyo-splat','','','2021-12-16 11:34:00','2021-12-16 11:34:00','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55578',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55579,1,'2021-12-16 11:41:51','2021-12-16 11:41:51','Starting from 5-steps away from the target wall, jump into a splat-cat position with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. Start from an ankle-height takeoff wall/block.\nNow push back and away from the wall into a precision back to the take-off position.  \n\nPerform a two-foot jump from the ankle-height wall/block into the splat cat. \n\nYou can use momentum with a run-in or not.\n\nYou can land at any height on the wall.\n\nRepeat three times turning in each direction.','Splat Back','','publish','closed','closed','','splat-back','','','2021-12-16 11:41:51','2021-12-16 11:41:51','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55579',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55580,1,'2021-12-16 11:46:01','2021-12-16 11:46:01','Starting standing directly next to (and facing) the target wall, box-jump up and into a splat-cat position with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. \nNow push back and away from the wall into a precision back to the take-off position.  \n\nYou can land at any height on the wall.\n\nRepeat three times turning in each direction.','Box Splat','','publish','closed','closed','','box-splat','','','2021-12-16 11:46:01','2021-12-16 11:46:01','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55580',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55581,1,'2021-12-16 11:54:57','2021-12-16 11:54:57','Starting from 5-steps away on a hip-height wall/block, from the target wall, jump into a splat-cat position with both hands and feet all on the wall at the same time. \nNow push back and away from the wall into a precision back to the take-off position.  \n\nPerform a two-foot jump from the hip-height wall/block into the splat cat. \n\nYou can use momentum with a run-in or not.\n\nYou can land at any height on the wall.\n\nRepeat three times turning in each direction.','Height Splat-back','','publish','closed','closed','','height-splat-back','','','2021-12-16 11:54:57','2021-12-16 11:54:57','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55581',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55582,1,'2021-12-16 14:42:02','2021-12-16 14:42:02','Demonstrate a one-foot standing position on a circular railing that is no wider than a standard scaffolding pipe. \r\n\r\nStay on the bar for 30seconds and switch to the other leg without falling off.\r\n\r\nYou must be facing along the bar with your entire foot inline with it.\r\n\r\nUse your arms and opposite leg to counter-balance you.\r\n\r\nBar can be any height.\r\n\r\nRepeat Twice.\r\n\r\nYou must not use anything to help balance you. The rail must be straight, with no corners in it.\r\n\r\nTips:\r\n1. Keep all muscles contracted &amp; bend your knee.\r\n2. Keep arms straight and at shoulder-height.\r\n3. Keep foot in line with the bar.\r\n4. Use opposite leg next to (not infront or behind) the balanced leg.','One Foot Standing','','publish','closed','closed','','one-foot-standing','','','2021-12-16 14:42:02','2021-12-16 14:42:02','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55582',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55583,1,'2021-12-16 14:48:36','2021-12-16 14:48:36','Demonstrate walking forwards on a circular railing that is no wider than a standard scaffolding pipe. Travel for 10 steps (heel-to-toe) without falling off the bar.\r\n\r\nYou must be facing along the bar with your entire foot inline with it.\r\n\r\nBar can be any height.\r\n\r\nRepeat Once.\r\n\r\nYou must not use anything to help balance you.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be straight, with no corners in it.\r\n\r\nTips:\r\n1. Keep all muscles contracted &amp; bend your knee.\r\n2. Keep arms straight and at shoulder-height.\r\n3. Keep foot in line with the bar.\r\n4. Use opposite leg next to (not infront or behind) the balanced leg.\r\n5. When stable, place the foot down ahead of you without shifting weight onto it.\r\n6. When both feet are on, shift weight to the front foot.\r\n7. Remove back foot.','Walking Forwards','','publish','closed','closed','','walking-forwards','','','2021-12-16 14:48:36','2021-12-16 14:48:36','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55583',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55584,1,'2021-12-16 14:51:37','2021-12-16 14:51:37','Demonstrate walking backwards on a circular railing that is no wider than a standard scaffolding pipe. Travel for 20 steps (heel-to-toe) without falling off the bar.\r\n\r\nSince you cannot see the bar behind you, use the toe to probe for the bar location.\r\n\r\nYou must be facing along the bar with your entire foot inline with it.\r\n\r\nBar can be any height.\r\n\r\nRepeat Once.\r\n\r\nYou must not use anything to help balance you.\r\n\r\nThe rail must be straight, with no corners in it.\r\n\r\nTips:\r\n1. Keep all muscles contracted &amp; bend your knee.\r\n2. Keep arms straight and at shoulder-height.\r\n3. Keep foot in line with the bar.\r\n4. Use opposite leg next to (not infront or behind) the balanced leg.\r\n5. When stable, place the foot down behind you without shifting weight onto it.\r\n6. When both feet are on, shift weight to the front foot.\r\n7. Remove front foot.','Walking Backwards','London Parkour offers a variety of resources and tutorials aimed at teaching practical movement techniques that can significantly enhance the way individuals think, move, and feel. Their online presence includes a dedicated website, LondonParkour.com, which serves as a hub for information about parkour and its various applications.\r\n\r\nThe organization encourages individuals to engage with their community and follow their content on social media platforms such as Instagram and YouTube. Through these channels, they share parkour tutorials, tips, and inspirational content, aimed at both beginners and advanced practitioners who are looking to improve their skills or learn new ones.\r\n\r\nAdditionally, London Parkour emphasizes the importance of subscribing to their YouTube channel for continuous updates and instructional videos. They are committed to fostering a space where enthusiasts of all levels can hone their abilities and gain insight into the world of parkour. For further inquiries, users can reach out via their provided contact email.','publish','closed','closed','','walking-backwards','','','2021-12-16 14:51:37','2021-12-16 14:51:37','',0,'http://localhost:8102/?post_type=lp_tutorial&p=55584',0,'lp_tutorial','',0),(55585,1,'2024-1